a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_2	event	\N	Jefferson Hope is summoned before a tribunal and dies after his aneurism bursts	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2675", "sentence_2676"], "communicatesContent": null}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_3	event	\N	Holmes remarks that Gregson and Lestrade will be upset about Jefferson Hope's death	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2677", "sentence_2678"], "communicatesContent": "Gregson and Lestrade will be wild about his death. Where will their grand advertisement be now?"}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_4	event	\N	Watson replies that Gregson and Lestrade had very little to do with Jefferson Hope's capture	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "john_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2679"], "communicatesContent": "I don't see that they had very much to do with his capture."}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_5	event	\N	Holmes states that worldly deeds matter less than what people are made to believe	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2680", "sentence_2681"], "communicatesContent": "What you do in this world is a matter of no consequence. The question is, what can you make people believe that you have done."}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_6	event	\N	Holmes says he would not have missed the investigation and calls it his best recent case	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2682", "sentence_2683", "sentence_2684", "sentence_2685"], "communicatesContent": "Never mind. I would not have missed the investigation for anything. There has been no better case within my recollection. Simple as it was, there were several most instructive points about it."}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_7	event	\N	Watson expresses surprise at Holmes's description of the case as simple	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "john_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2686", "sentence_2687"], "communicatesContent": "Simple!"}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_8	event	\N	Holmes explains that he was able to identify the criminal within three days by ordinary deductions	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2688", "sentence_2689"], "communicatesContent": "Well, really, it can hardly be described as otherwise. The proof of its intrinsic simplicity is, that without any help save a few very ordinary deductions I was able to lay my hand upon the criminal within three days."}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_9	event	\N	Watson agrees that Holmes's statement is true	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "john_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2690"], "communicatesContent": "That is true."}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_10	event	\N	Holmes explains reasoning backwards and contrasts analytic and synthetic reasoning	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2691", "sentence_2692", "sentence_2693", "sentence_2694", "sentence_2695", "sentence_2696", "sentence_2697", "sentence_2698", "sentence_2699", "sentence_2700", "sentence_2701", "sentence_2702", "sentence_2703"], "communicatesContent": "I have already explained to you that what is out of the common is usually a guide rather than a hindrance. In solving a problem of this sort, the grand thing is to be able to reason backwards. That is a very useful accomplishment, and a very easy one, but people do not practise it much. In the every-day affairs of life it is more useful to reason forwards, and so the other comes to be neglected. There are fifty who can reason synthetically for one who can reason analytically. I hardly expected that you would. Let me see if I can make it clearer. Most people, if you describe a train of events to them, will tell you what the result would be. They can put those events together in their minds, and argue from them that something will come to pass. There are few people, however, who, if you told them a result, would be able to evolve from their own inner consciousness what the steps were which led up to that result. This power is what I mean when I talk of reasoning backwards, or analytically."}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_75_11	event	\N	Holmes explains the steps of his reasoning from the cab tracks and garden path	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2704", "sentence_2705", "sentence_2706", "sentence_2707", "sentence_2708", "sentence_2709", "sentence_2710", "sentence_2711", "sentence_2712", "sentence_2713", "sentence_2714", "sentence_2715", "sentence_2716", "sentence_2717", "sentence_2718"], "communicatesContent": "Now this was a case in which you were given the result and had to find everything else for yourself. Now let me endeavour to show you the different steps in my reasoning. To begin at the beginning. I approached the house, as you know, on foot, and with my mind entirely free from all impressions. I naturally began by examining the roadway, and there, as I have already explained to you, I saw clearly the marks of a cab, which, I ascertained by inquiry, must have been there during the night. I satisfied myself that it was a cab and not a private carriage by the narrow gauge of the wheels. The ordinary London growler is considerably less wide than a gentleman's brougham. This was the first point gained. I then walked slowly down the garden path, which happened to be composed of a clay soil, peculiarly suitable for taking impressions. No doubt it appeared to you to be a mere trampled line of slush, but to my trained eyes every mark upon its surface had a meaning. There is no branch of detective science which is so important and so much neglected as the art of tracing footsteps. Happily, I have always laid great stress upon it, and much practice has made it second nature to me. I saw the heavy footmarks of the constables, but I saw also the track of the two men who had first passed through the garden. It was easy to tell that they had been before the others, because in places their marks had been entirely obliterated by the others coming upon the top of them. In this way my second link was formed, which told me that the nocturnal visitors were two in number, one remarkable for his height, and the other fashionably dressed."}
a-study-in-scarlet	event_76_1	event	\N	Holmes enters the house and confirms the dead man is present	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2719", "sentence_2720"], "communicatesContent": null}
