sign-of-the-four	event_34_11	event	\N	Jonathan Small keeps watch on efforts to find the treasure	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1432"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_34_12	event	\N	Jonathan Small leaves England and returns only at intervals	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1433"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_34_13	event	\N	Jonathan Small learns of the discovery of the garret	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1434"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_34_14	event	\N	A confederate in the household is traced again	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1435"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_34_15	event	\N	Jonathan Small, unable to reach Bartholomew Sholto's room, brings a curious associate who does so	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1436", "sentence_1437"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_34_16	event	\N	The associate dips a naked foot into creasote, leading to Toby and a limp	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1437"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_1	event	\N	Jonathan Small leaves his record, lowers the treasure-box to the ground, and follows it himself	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1443"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_2	event	\N	The speaker describes Jonathan Small's appearance as middle-aged, sunburned, bearded, and of calculated height	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1445", "sentence_1446", "sentence_1447"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_3	event	\N	The speaker asks whether the listener has a pistol	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1465"], "communicatesContent": "You have not a pistol, have you?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_4	event	\N	Watson answers that he has his stick	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1466"], "communicatesContent": "I have my stick."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_5	event	\N	Mrs. Smith says a wooden-legged man has been coming around and she dislikes him.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1560", "sentence_1561"], "communicatesContent": "I don’t like that wooden-legged man, wi’ his ugly face and outlandish talk. What did he want always knockin’ about here for?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_5	event	\N	The speaker says they may need a weapon if they reach the lair and that Jonathan will be left to the listener while the other man will be shot dead if he turns nasty	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1467", "sentence_1468"], "communicatesContent": "It is just possible that we may need something of the sort if we get to their lair. Jonathan I shall leave to you, but if the other turns nasty I shall shoot him dead."}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_6	event	\N	The speaker takes out his revolver, loads two chambers, and puts it back into his jacket pocket	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1469"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_7	event	\N	Watson and Holmes follow Toby toward the metropolis	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1470"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_8	event	\N	Watson and Holmes enter continuous streets where labourers, dockmen, slatternly women, and rough-looking men are out in the morning	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1471", "sentence_1472"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_9	event	\N	Toby leads the pursuit through Streatham, Brixton, Camberwell, and Kennington Lane	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1474"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_10	event	\N	The pursued men take a zigzag route to avoid observation	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1475", "sentence_1476"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_11	event	\N	The pursued men edge left through Bond Street and Miles Street	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1477"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_35_12	event	\N	Toby stops at Knight’s Place and runs backwards and forwards in indecision	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1478", "sentence_1479"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_36_1	event	\N	Toby darts away following the scent trail with increased energy	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1487", "sentence_1488"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_36_2	event	\N	Holmes, Watson, and Toby travel down Nine Elms and reach Broderick and Nelson's timber-yard	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1490", "sentence_1491"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_36_3	event	\N	Toby races through the timber-yard and springs upon a barrel on a hand-trolley	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1491", "sentence_1492"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_36_4	event	\N	Holmes and Watson look at each other and burst into laughter	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1495"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_37_1	event	\N	Holmes carries Toby out of the timber-yard	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1500"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_37_2	event	\N	Holmes and Watson follow the correct trail through the streets toward the river	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1505", "sentence_1506", "sentence_1507", "sentence_1508", "sentence_1509", "sentence_1511", "sentence_1516", "sentence_1517"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_37_3	event	\N	Toby leads them to the wharf and stops at the water's edge	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1518"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_37_4	event	\N	Holmes and Watson inspect the boats at the wharf, but Toby gives no sign at any of them	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1521", "sentence_1522"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_37_5	event	\N	Holmes examines the riverside house and reads the placard and boat-hire sign	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1523", "sentence_1524", "sentence_1525"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_37_6	event	\N	A little boy runs out of the house and his mother calls him back to be washed	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1531", "sentence_1532", "sentence_1533"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_37_7	event	\N	Holmes questions Jack and gives him money	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1534", "sentence_1535", "sentence_1536", "sentence_1537", "sentence_1538", "sentence_1539", "sentence_1540", "sentence_1541", "sentence_1542", "sentence_1543"], "communicatesContent": "Dear little chap! What a rosy-cheeked young rascal! Now, Jack, is there anything you would like?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_37_8	event	\N	Mrs. Smith says her husband has been away since yesterday morning	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1545", "sentence_1546", "sentence_1547", "sentence_1548", "sentence_1549", "sentence_1550", "sentence_1551"], "communicatesContent": "Lor' bless you, sir, he is that, and forward. He gets a'most too much for me to manage, 'specially when my man is away days at a time. He's been away since yesterday mornin', sir, and, truth to tell, I am beginnin' to feel frightened about him. But if it was about a boat, sir, maybe I could serve as well."}
sign-of-the-four	event_37_9	event	\N	Holmes says he wanted to speak to Mr. Smith and is disappointed that he is away	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1547", "sentence_1548", "sentence_1549"], "communicatesContent": "I am sorry for that, for I wanted to speak to Mr. Smith."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_1	event	\N	Mrs. Smith says the steam launch has already gone out.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1553"], "communicatesContent": "It is in the steam launch that he has gone."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_2	event	\N	Mrs. Smith explains that the launch has too little coal for a longer trip.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1554", "sentence_1556"], "communicatesContent": "There ain’t more coals in her than would take her to about Woolwich and back. What good is a steam launch without coals?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_3	event	\N	Holmes suggests that Mr. Smith might have bought coal at a wharf down the river.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1557"], "communicatesContent": "He might have bought some at a wharf down the river."}
silver-blaze	sentence_79	lexical	\N	\N	78	{"text": "'I understand that a stable-boy sleeps there alone every night.", "section": "silver-blaze-section_3", "entities": []}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_6	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Mrs. Smith means the wooden-legged man.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1562", "sentence_1563"], "communicatesContent": "A wooden-legged man?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_7	event	\N	Mrs. Smith identifies the man as a brown, monkey-faced chap who called for her husband.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1564"], "communicatesContent": "He is a brown, monkey-faced chap that’s called more’n once for my old man."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_8	event	\N	Mrs. Smith says the man roused her husband in the night and that the launch had steam up.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1565"], "communicatesContent": "It was him that roused him up yesternight, and my man knew he was comin’, for he had steam up in the launch."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_9	event	\N	Mrs. Smith says she is uneasy about the matter.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1566"], "communicatesContent": "I don’t feel easy in my mind about it."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_10	event	\N	Holmes reassures Mrs. Smith that she is frightening herself for nothing.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1567"], "communicatesContent": "You are frightening yourself about nothing."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_11	event	\N	Holmes asks how Mrs. Smith could tell the visitor was the wooden-legged man.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1568", "sentence_1569"], "communicatesContent": "How could you possibly tell that it was the wooden-legged man who came in the night?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_12	event	\N	Mrs. Smith explains that she recognized the man's voice.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1570", "sentence_1571"], "communicatesContent": "His voice. I knew his voice, which is kind o’ thick and foggy."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_13	event	\N	Mrs. Smith describes the night visitor tapping at the window.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "wooden_legged_man", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1572", "sentence_1573"], "communicatesContent": "Show a leg, matey; time to turn out guard."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_14	event	\N	Mrs. Smith says her husband woke Jim and they left without speaking to her.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1574"], "communicatesContent": "My old man woke up Jim, and away they went, without so much as a word to me."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_15	event	\N	Mrs. Smith says she heard the wooden leg clacking on the stones.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1575"], "communicatesContent": "I could hear the wooden leg clackin’ on the stones."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_16	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the wooden-legged man was alone.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1576"], "communicatesContent": "And was this wooden-legged man alone?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_17	event	\N	Mrs. Smith says she cannot say whether anyone else was present.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1577", "sentence_1578"], "communicatesContent": "Couldn’t say, I am sure. I didn’t hear no one else."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_18	event	\N	Holmes says he wanted a steam launch and had heard good reports of the Aurora.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1579", "sentence_1580"], "communicatesContent": "I wanted a steam launch, and I have heard good reports of the Aurora."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_19	event	\N	Holmes asks about the launch's appearance.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1581", "sentence_1582"], "communicatesContent": "She’s not that old green launch with a yellow line, very broad in the beam?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_20	event	\N	Mrs. Smith describes the Aurora as freshly painted and marked with two red streaks.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "mrs_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1583", "sentence_1584", "sentence_1585"], "communicatesContent": "She’s as trim a little thing as any on the river. She’s been fresh painted, black with two red streaks."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_21	event	\N	Holmes says he hopes to hear soon from Mr. Smith and will look for the Aurora down the river.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1586", "sentence_1587", "sentence_1588"], "communicatesContent": "I hope that you will hear soon from Mr. Smith. I am going down the river; and if I should see anything of the Aurora I shall let him know that you are uneasy."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_22	event	\N	Holmes asks about the launch's funnel color and Mrs. Smith corrects him.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1589", "sentence_1590", "sentence_1591", "sentence_1592", "sentence_1593"], "communicatesContent": "A black funnel, you say?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_23	event	\N	Holmes and Watson take a wherry to cross the river.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1594", "sentence_1595", "sentence_1596", "sentence_1597", "sentence_1598", "sentence_1599"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_24	event	\N	Watson says their course seems clear.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1600"], "communicatesContent": "Our course now seems pretty clear."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_25	event	\N	Holmes says he would hire a launch and search down the river for the Aurora.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1601", "sentence_1602"], "communicatesContent": "I would engage a launch and go down the river on the track of the Aurora."}
sign-of-the-four	event_38_26	event	\N	Watson says the search would be a colossal task.	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1603", "sentence_1604", "sentence_1605", "sentence_1606"], "communicatesContent": "It would be a colossal task. She may have touched at any wharf on either side of the stream between here and Greenwich. Below the bridge there is a perfect labyrinth of landing-places for miles. It would take you days and days to exhaust them, if you set about it alone."}
sign-of-the-four	event_39_1	event	\N	Holmes decides to call Athelney Jones later	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1608", "sentence_1609", "sentence_1610", "sentence_1611"], "communicatesContent": "I shall probably call Athelney Jones in at the last moment. He is not a bad fellow, and I should not like to do anything which would injure him professionally. But I have a fancy for working it out myself, now that we have gone so far."}
sign-of-the-four	event_39_2	event	\N	The speaker rejects advertising for information from wharfingers	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1612", "sentence_1613", "sentence_1614", "sentence_1615", "sentence_1616"], "communicatesContent": "Worse and worse! Our men would know that the chase was hot at their heels, and they would be off out of the country. As it is, they are likely enough to leave, but as long as they think they are perfectly safe they will be in no hurry. Jones’s energy will be of use to us there, for his view of the case is sure to push itself into the daily press, and the runaways will think that every one is off on the wrong scent."}
sign-of-the-four	event_39_3	event	\N	Watson asks what they are to do	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1617"], "communicatesContent": "What are we to do, then?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_39_4	event	\N	Holmes orders the cabman to stop at a telegraph-office and keep Toby	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1619", "sentence_1620", "sentence_1621", "sentence_1622"], "communicatesContent": "Take this hansom, drive home, have some breakfast, and get an hour’s sleep. It is quite on the cards that we may be afoot to-night again. Stop at a telegraph-office, cabby! We will keep Toby, for he may be of use to us yet."}
sign-of-the-four	event_39_5	event	\N	Holmes sends a telegram from the post-office	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1623"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_39_6	event	\N	Holmes explains that the wire was sent to Wiggins	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1624", "sentence_1625", "sentence_1627", "sentence_1629", "sentence_1630", "sentence_1631"], "communicatesContent": "You remember the Baker Street division of the detective police force whom I employed in the Jefferson Hope case? This is just the case where they might be invaluable. If they fail, I have other resources; but I shall try them first. That wire was to my dirty little lieutenant, Wiggins, and I expect that he and his gang will be with us before we have finished our breakfast."}
sign-of-the-four	event_39_7	event	\N	Watson arrives at Baker Street and refreshes himself	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1642", "sentence_1643"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_39_8	event	\N	Holmes shows Watson a newspaper notice about the Upper Norwood case	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1644", "sentence_1645", "sentence_1646", "sentence_1647", "sentence_1648", "sentence_1649", "sentence_1650", "sentence_1651", "sentence_1652", "sentence_1653", "sentence_1654", "sentence_1655", "sentence_1656", "sentence_1657"], "communicatesContent": "Here it is. The energetic Jones and the ubiquitous reporter have fixed it up between them. But you have had enough of the case. Better have your ham and eggs first."}
sign-of-the-four	event_40_1	event	\N	Mrs. Hudson rings and protests at the door while the Baker Street Irregulars arrive on the stairs	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1664", "sentence_1668", "sentence_1669"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_40_2	event	\N	Wiggins reports that he received a message and brought the boys quickly	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "wiggins", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1670", "sentence_1671", "sentence_1672"], "communicatesContent": "Got your message, sir, and brought ’em on sharp. Three bob and a tanner for tickets."}
sign-of-the-four	event_40_3	event	\N	Holmes gives the Baker Street Irregulars instructions to search for the launch Aurora and report back	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1673", "sentence_1674", "sentence_1675", "sentence_1676", "sentence_1677", "sentence_1678", "sentence_1679", "sentence_1680", "sentence_1681", "sentence_1682", "sentence_1683", "sentence_1684", "sentence_1685", "sentence_1686", "sentence_1687"], "communicatesContent": "In future they can report to you, Wiggins, and you to me. I cannot have the house invaded in this way. However, it is just as well that you should all hear the instructions. I want to find the whereabouts of a steam launch called the Aurora, owner Mordecai Smith, black with two red streaks, funnel black with a white band. She is down the river somewhere. I want one boy to be at Mordecai Smith’s landing-stage opposite Millbank to say if the boat comes back. You must divide it out among yourselves, and do both banks thoroughly. Let me know the moment you have news. Is that all clear? The old scale of pay, and a guinea to the boy who finds the boat. Here’s a day in advance. Now off you go!"}
sign-of-the-four	event_40_4	event	\N	Holmes pays the boys and sends them away	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1684", "sentence_1685", "sentence_1686", "sentence_1687"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_40_5	event	\N	Holmes states that the boys will search for the launch and that he and Watson must wait for results	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1688", "sentence_1689", "sentence_1690", "sentence_1691", "sentence_1692"], "communicatesContent": "If the launch is above water they will find her. They can go everywhere, see everything, overhear every one. I expect to hear before evening that they have spotted her. In the meanwhile, we can do nothing but await results. We cannot pick up the broken trail until we find either the Aurora or Mr. Mordecai Smith."}
sign-of-the-four	event_40_6	event	\N	Holmes remains up smoking and thinking over the case	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1694", "sentence_1695", "sentence_1696", "sentence_1697", "sentence_1698", "sentence_1699", "sentence_1700"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_40_7	event	\N	Holmes describes the data and asks Watson what he makes of it	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1701", "sentence_1702", "sentence_1703", "sentence_1704", "sentence_1705", "sentence_1706"], "communicatesContent": "That other man again! I have no wish to make a mystery of him,—to you, anyway. But you must have formed your own opinion. Now, do consider the data. Diminutive footmarks, toes never fettered by boots, naked feet, stone-headed wooden mace, great agility, small poisoned darts. What do you make of all this?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_40_8	event	\N	Watson identifies the figure as a savage and suggests an Indian associate of Jonathan Small	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1707", "sentence_1708", "sentence_1709"], "communicatesContent": "A savage! Perhaps one of those Indians who were the associates of Jonathan Small."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_12	event	\N	Watson says he will be back in an hour or two	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1779"], "communicatesContent": "I shall be back in an hour or two."}
valley-of-fear	section_89	section	\N	Section 89	88	{"wordCount": 600}
sign-of-the-four	event_41_1	event	\N	Holmes explains why the footmarks and darts indicate an Andaman islander	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1711", "sentence_1712", "sentence_1713", "sentence_1714", "sentence_1715", "sentence_1716", "sentence_1717"], "communicatesContent": "When first I saw signs of strange weapons I was inclined to think so; but the remarkable character of the footmarks caused me to reconsider my views. Some of the inhabitants of the Indian Peninsula are small men, but none could have left such marks as that. The Hindoo proper has long and thin feet. The sandal-wearing Mohammedan has the great toe well separated from the others, because the thong is commonly passed between. These little darts, too, could only be shot in one way. They are from a blow-pipe. Now, then, where are we to find our savage?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_41_2	event	\N	Watson suggests a South American origin	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1718"], "communicatesContent": "South American."}
sign-of-the-four	event_41_3	event	\N	Holmes consults a gazetteer and reads about the Andaman Islands	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1719", "sentence_1720", "sentence_1721", "sentence_1722", "sentence_1723", "sentence_1724", "sentence_1725", "sentence_1726", "sentence_1727", "sentence_1728", "sentence_1729", "sentence_1730", "sentence_1731", "sentence_1732", "sentence_1733", "sentence_1734", "sentence_1735", "sentence_1736", "sentence_1737", "sentence_1738", "sentence_1739", "sentence_1740"], "communicatesContent": "This is the first volume of a gazetteer which is now being published. It may be looked upon as the very latest authority. Andaman Islands, situated 340 miles to the north of Sumatra, in the Bay of Bengal. Moist climate, coral reefs, sharks, Port Blair, convict-barracks, Rutland Island, cottonwoods. The aborigines of the Andaman Islands may perhaps claim the distinction of being the smallest race upon this earth, though some anthropologists prefer the Bushmen of Africa, the Digger Indians of America, and the Terra del Fuegians. The average height is rather below four feet, although many full-grown adults may be found who are very much smaller than this. They are a fierce, morose, and intractable people, though capable of forming most devoted friendships when their confidence has once been gained. They are naturally hideous, having large, misshapen heads, small, fierce eyes, and distorted features. Their feet and hands, however, are remarkably small. So intractable and fierce are they that all the efforts of the British official have failed to win them over in any degree. They have always been a terror to shipwrecked crews, braining the survivors with their stone-headed clubs, or shooting them with their poisoned arrows. These massacres are invariably concluded by a cannibal feast. Nice, amiable people, Watson! If this fellow had been left to his own unaided devices this affair might have taken an even more ghastly turn. I fancy that, even as it is, Jonathan Small would give a good deal not to have employed him."}
sign-of-the-four	event_41_4	event	\N	Watson asks how the companion came to be with Small	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1741"], "communicatesContent": "But how came he to have so singular a companion?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_41_5	event	\N	Holmes says he cannot explain how the islander came to be with Small and that they will learn more later	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1742", "sentence_1743", "sentence_1744"], "communicatesContent": "Ah, that is more than I can tell. Since, however, we had already determined that Small had come from the Andamans, it is not so very wonderful that this islander should be with him. No doubt we shall know all about it in time."}
sign-of-the-four	event_41_6	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson to lie down on the sofa and tries to put him to sleep with violin music	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1745", "sentence_1746", "sentence_1747", "sentence_1748", "sentence_1749"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_1	event	\N	Watson wakes late in the afternoon, feeling strengthened and refreshed	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1751"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_2	event	\N	Holmes sits reading after setting aside his violin	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1752"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_3	event	\N	Holmes looks at Watson and his face appears dark and troubled	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1753"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_4	event	\N	Holmes says he feared their talk would wake Watson	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1754", "sentence_1755"], "communicatesContent": "You have slept soundly. I feared that our talk would wake you."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_5	event	\N	Watson says he heard nothing	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1756"], "communicatesContent": "I heard nothing."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_6	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Watson has had fresh news	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1757"], "communicatesContent": "Have you had fresh news, then?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_7	event	\N	Holmes says there is no fresh news and that Wiggins has reported no trace of the launch	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1758", "sentence_1759", "sentence_1760", "sentence_1761", "sentence_1762", "sentence_1763"], "communicatesContent": "Unfortunately, no. I confess that I am surprised and disappointed. I expected something definite by this time. Wiggins has just been up to report. He says that no trace can be found of the launch. It is a provoking check, for every hour is of importance."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_8	event	\N	Watson offers to help and proposes another night outing	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1764", "sentence_1765"], "communicatesContent": "Can I do anything? I am perfectly fresh now, and quite ready for another night’s outing."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_9	event	\N	Holmes says they can only wait and that he must remain on guard	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1766", "sentence_1767", "sentence_1768", "sentence_1769"], "communicatesContent": "No, we can do nothing. We can only wait. If we go ourselves, the message might come in our absence, and delay be caused. You can do what you will, but I must remain on guard."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_10	event	\N	Watson says he will visit Mrs. Cecil Forrester and Miss Morstan	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1770", "sentence_1771", "sentence_1772", "sentence_1774", "sentence_1775"], "communicatesContent": "Then I shall run over to Camberwell and call upon Mrs. Cecil Forrester. She asked me to, yesterday. Well, of course Miss Morstan too. They were anxious to hear what happened."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_11	event	\N	Holmes warns Watson not to tell them too much	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1776", "sentence_1777"], "communicatesContent": "I would not tell them too much. Women are never to be entirely trusted,—not the best of them."}
silver-blaze	sentence_394	lexical	\N	\N	393	{"text": "It shall all be done,\\" said he.", "section": "silver-blaze-section_11", "entities": []}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_13	event	\N	Holmes wishes Watson luck and asks him to return Toby	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1780", "sentence_1781", "sentence_1782"], "communicatesContent": "All right! Good luck! But, I say, if you are crossing the river you may as well return Toby, for I don’t think it is at all likely that we shall have any use for him now."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_14	event	\N	Watson takes Toby and leaves him with a half-sovereign at the old naturalist’s in Pinchin Lane	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1783"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_15	event	\N	Watson arrives at Camberwell and finds Miss Morstan and Mrs. Forrester waiting to hear the news	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1784", "sentence_1785"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_16	event	\N	Watson tells Miss Morstan and Mrs. Forrester what he and Holmes had done, omitting the worst details	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1786", "sentence_1787", "sentence_1788"], "communicatesContent": "I told them all that we had done, suppressing, however, the more dreadful parts of the tragedy. Thus, although I spoke of Mr. Sholto’s death, I said nothing of the exact manner and method of it."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_17	event	\N	Mrs. Forrester calls the affair a romance	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "mrs_cecil_forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1789", "sentence_1790"], "communicatesContent": "It is a romance!"}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_18	event	\N	Mrs. Forrester describes the story with an injured lady, treasure, a black cannibal, and a wooden-legged ruffian	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "mrs_cecil_forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1791", "sentence_1792"], "communicatesContent": "An injured lady, half a million in treasure, a black cannibal, and a wooden-legged ruffian. They take the place of the conventional dragon or wicked earl."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_19	event	\N	Miss Morstan says two knight-errants are involved in the rescue	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "mary_morstan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1793"], "communicatesContent": "And two knight-errants to the rescue."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_20	event	\N	Mrs. Forrester says Mary’s fortune depends on the search and comments on riches	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "mrs_cecil_forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1794", "sentence_1795", "sentence_1796"], "communicatesContent": "Why, Mary, your fortune depends upon the issue of this search. I don’t think that you are nearly excited enough. Just imagine what it must be to be so rich, and to have the world at your feet!"}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_21	event	\N	Watson notes Mary shows no elation at the prospect	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1797", "sentence_1798"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_22	event	\N	Miss Morstan says she is anxious for Mr. Thaddeus Sholto and wants him cleared of the charge	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "mary_morstan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1799", "sentence_1800", "sentence_1801"], "communicatesContent": "It is for Mr. Thaddeus Sholto that I am anxious. Nothing else is of any consequence; but I think that he has behaved most kindly and honourably throughout. It is our duty to clear him of this dreadful and unfounded charge."}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_23	event	\N	Watson leaves Camberwell and returns home in the dark	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1802"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_42_24	event	\N	Watson finds Holmes absent from home and sees his book and pipe left by the chair	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1803", "sentence_1804"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_1	event	\N	Mrs. Hudson reports that Holmes has gone to his room and worries about his health	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "mrs_hudson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1806", "sentence_1807", "sentence_1808"], "communicatesContent": "No, sir. He has gone to his room, sir. Do you know, sir, I am afraid for his health?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_2	event	\N	Mrs. Hudson describes Holmes walking restlessly and muttering to himself	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "mrs_hudson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1810", "sentence_1811", "sentence_1812", "sentence_1813"], "communicatesContent": "Well, he’s that strange, sir. After you was gone he walked and he walked, up and down, and up and down. Then I heard him talking to himself and muttering. And now he has slammed off to his room, but I can hear him walking away the same as ever."}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_3	event	\N	Mrs. Hudson says she suggested cooling medicine and Holmes reacted sharply	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "mrs_hudson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1815"], "communicatesContent": "I ventured to say something to him about cooling medicine, but he turned on me, sir, with such a look that I don’t know how ever I got out of the room."}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_4	event	\N	Watson reassures Mrs. Hudson about Holmes's condition	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1816", "sentence_1817", "sentence_1818"], "communicatesContent": "I don’t think that you have any cause to be uneasy, Mrs. Hudson. I have seen him like this before. He has some small matter upon his mind which makes him restless."}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_5	event	\N	Watson hears Holmes walking through the night and remains uneasy	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1819"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_6	event	\N	Holmes appears at breakfast worn and haggard	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1820"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_7	event	\N	Watson remarks that Holmes is knocking himself up and had been marching in the night	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1821", "sentence_1822"], "communicatesContent": "You are knocking yourself up, old man. I heard you marching about in the night."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_16	event	\N	Watson exclaims at seeing Holmes and asks where the old man is	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1974", "sentence_1975", "sentence_1976", "sentence_1977"], "communicatesContent": "Holmes! You here! But where is the old man?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_17	event	\N	Holmes reveals the old man's disguise	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1978", "sentence_1979", "sentence_1980"], "communicatesContent": "Here is the old man. Here he is,—wig, whiskers, eyebrows, and all. I thought my disguise was pretty good, but I hardly expected that it would stand that test."}
valley-of-fear	section_90	section	\N	Section 90	89	{"wordCount": 607}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_8	event	\N	Holmes says he could not sleep and explains that the problem is consuming him	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1823", "sentence_1824", "sentence_1825", "sentence_1826", "sentence_1827", "sentence_1828", "sentence_1829", "sentence_1830", "sentence_1831", "sentence_1832", "sentence_1833", "sentence_1834", "sentence_1835", "sentence_1836", "sentence_1837"], "communicatesContent": "No, I could not sleep. This infernal problem is consuming me. It is too much to be balked by so petty an obstacle, when all else had been overcome. I know the men, the launch, everything; and yet I can get no news. I have set other agencies at work, and used every means at my disposal. The whole river has been searched on either side, but there is no news, nor has Mrs. Smith heard of her husband. I shall come to the conclusion soon that they have scuttled the craft. But there are objections to that. No, I think that may be dismissed. I had inquiries made, and there is a launch of that description. I have considered that possibility too, and there is a search-party who will work up as far as Richmond. If no news comes to-day, I shall start off myself to-morrow, and go for the men rather than the boat. But surely, surely, we shall hear something."}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_9	event	\N	No news arrives from Wiggins or the other agencies	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1838", "sentence_1839"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_10	event	\N	Newspaper articles report on the Norwood tragedy and an inquest is scheduled	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1840", "sentence_1841", "sentence_1842"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_11	event	\N	Watson reports the ill success to the ladies and returns to find Holmes dejected and morose	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1843"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_43_12	event	\N	Holmes works all evening on an abstruse chemical analysis	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1844", "sentence_1845"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_1	event	\N	Watson wakes and finds Holmes standing by his bedside in sailor dress	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1846"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_2	event	\N	Holmes says he is going down the river	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1847", "sentence_1848", "sentence_1849"], "communicatesContent": "I am off down the river, Watson. I have been turning it over in my mind, and I can see only one way out of it. It is worth trying, at all events."}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_3	event	\N	Watson asks to come with Holmes	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1850", "sentence_1851"], "communicatesContent": "Surely I can come with you, then?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_4	event	\N	Holmes instructs Watson to remain as his representative and handle notes and telegrams	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1852", "sentence_1853", "sentence_1854", "sentence_1855", "sentence_1856", "sentence_1857", "sentence_1858", "sentence_1859"], "communicatesContent": "No; you can be much more useful if you will remain here as my representative. I am loath to go, for it is quite on the cards that some message may come during the day, though Wiggins was despondent about it last night. I want you to open all notes and telegrams, and to act on your own judgment if any news should come. Can I rely upon you? Most certainly. I am afraid that you will not be able to wire to me, for I can hardly tell yet where I may find myself. If I am in luck, however, I may not be gone so very long. I shall have news of some sort or other before I get back."}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_5	event	\N	Watson has not heard from Holmes by breakfast-time	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1860"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_6	event	\N	Watson opens The Standard and finds a fresh allusion to the Upper Norwood tragedy	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1861", "sentence_1862", "sentence_1863", "sentence_1864", "sentence_1865", "sentence_1866"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_7	event	\N	Watson tosses the paper onto the table and notices an advertisement in the agony column	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1870", "sentence_1871"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_8	event	\N	Watson identifies the advertisement as Holmes's doing	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1874", "sentence_1875", "sentence_1876"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_9	event	\N	Watson waits through a long day for Holmes or a reply to the advertisement	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1877", "sentence_1878", "sentence_1879"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_44_10	event	\N	Watson reflects on Holmes's reasoning and the possibility of error	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1880", "sentence_1881", "sentence_1882", "sentence_1883", "sentence_1884", "sentence_1885", "sentence_1886"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_45_1	event	\N	Athelney Jones arrives to speak with Watson	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1887", "sentence_1888", "sentence_1889"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_45_2	event	\N	Watson invites Athelney Jones to wait and offers him a chair, cigars, and whiskey-and-soda	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1892", "sentence_1893", "sentence_1894", "sentence_1895", "sentence_1896", "sentence_1897"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_45_3	event	\N	Athelney Jones explains that he has reconsidered his theory about the Norwood case	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1899", "sentence_1900", "sentence_1901", "sentence_1902", "sentence_1903", "sentence_1904", "sentence_1905", "sentence_1906", "sentence_1907"], "communicatesContent": "I have been obliged to reconsider it. I had my net drawn tightly round Mr. Sholto, sir, when pop he went through a hole in the middle of it. He was able to prove an alibi which could not be shaken. From the time that he left his brother’s room he was never out of sight of some one or other. So it could not be he who climbed over roofs and through trap-doors. It’s a very dark case, and my professional credit is at stake. I should be very glad of a little assistance."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_7	event	\N	Holmes looks through night-glasses toward the shore and reports seeing a sentry but no handkerchief	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2136", "sentence_2137"], "communicatesContent": "I see my sentry at his post, but no sign of a handkerchief."}
sign-of-the-four	event_45_4	event	\N	Athelney Jones praises Sherlock Holmes and describes receiving a wire from him	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1909", "sentence_1910", "sentence_1911", "sentence_1912", "sentence_1913", "sentence_1914", "sentence_1915", "sentence_1916"], "communicatesContent": "Your friend Mr. Sherlock Holmes is a wonderful man, sir. He’s a man who is not to be beat. I have known that young man go into a good many cases, but I never saw the case yet that he could not throw a light upon. He is irregular in his methods, and a little quick perhaps in jumping at theories, but, on the whole, I think he would have made a most promising officer, and I don’t care who knows it. I have had a wire from him this morning, by which I understand that he has got some clue to this Sholto business. Here is the message."}
sign-of-the-four	event_45_5	event	\N	Jones hands Watson a telegram from Holmes	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": "telegram", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1915", "sentence_1916", "sentence_1917", "sentence_1918", "sentence_1919", "sentence_1920"], "communicatesContent": "Go to Baker Street at once. If I have not returned, wait for me. I am close on the track of the Sholto gang. You can come with us to-night if you want to be in at the finish."}
sign-of-the-four	event_45_6	event	\N	Watson comments that Holmes has picked up the scent again	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1921", "sentence_1922"], "communicatesContent": "This sounds well. He has evidently picked up the scent again."}
sign-of-the-four	event_45_7	event	\N	Jones responds that Holmes has been at fault too and may be issuing a false alarm	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1923", "sentence_1924", "sentence_1925"], "communicatesContent": "Ah, then he has been at fault too. Even the best of us are thrown off sometimes. Of course this may prove to be a false alarm; but it is my duty as an officer of the law to allow no chance to slip."}
sign-of-the-four	event_45_8	event	\N	An elderly seafaring man arrives at Watson's door and enters	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1926", "sentence_1927", "sentence_1928", "sentence_1929", "sentence_1930", "sentence_1931", "sentence_1932", "sentence_1933", "sentence_1934", "sentence_1935"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_1	event	\N	Watson asks the visitor what is the matter	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1936", "sentence_1937"], "communicatesContent": "What is it, my man?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_2	event	\N	The visitor asks whether Sherlock Holmes is present	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "our_visitor", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1938", "sentence_1939", "sentence_1940"], "communicatesContent": "Is Mr. Sherlock Holmes here?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_3	event	\N	Watson says he is acting for Holmes and can receive the message	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1941", "sentence_1942"], "communicatesContent": "No; but I am acting for him. You can tell me any message you have for him."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_4	event	\N	The visitor says he was meant to tell Holmes himself	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "our_visitor", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1943"], "communicatesContent": "It was to him himself I was to tell it."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_5	event	\N	Watson insists he is acting for Holmes and asks about Mordecai Smith's boat	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1944", "sentence_1945"], "communicatesContent": "I am acting for him. Was it about Mordecai Smith’s boat?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_6	event	\N	The visitor says yes and claims to know the boat, the men, and the treasure	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "our_visitor", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1946", "sentence_1947", "sentence_1948", "sentence_1949", "sentence_1950"], "communicatesContent": "Yes. I knows well where it is. An’ I knows where the men he is after are. An’ I knows where the treasure is. I knows all about it."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_7	event	\N	Watson asks the visitor to tell him and promises to inform Holmes	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1951"], "communicatesContent": "Then tell me, and I shall let him know."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_8	event	\N	The visitor repeats that he was to tell Holmes himself	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "our_visitor", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1952"], "communicatesContent": "It was to him I was to tell it."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_9	event	\N	Watson tells the visitor to wait for Holmes	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1953"], "communicatesContent": "Well, you must wait for him."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_10	event	\N	The visitor refuses to wait and says he will not tell them	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "our_visitor", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1954", "sentence_1955", "sentence_1956"], "communicatesContent": "No, no; I ain’t goin’ to lose a whole day to please no one. If Mr. Holmes ain’t here, then Mr. Holmes must find it all out for himself. I don’t care about the look of either of you, and I won’t tell a word."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_11	event	\N	The visitor moves toward the door and Jones blocks him	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1957", "sentence_1958", "sentence_1959", "sentence_1960", "sentence_1961"], "communicatesContent": "Wait a bit, my friend. You have important information, and you must not walk off. We shall keep you, whether you like or not, until our friend returns."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_12	event	\N	The visitor protests that he has been seized and treated badly	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "our_visitor", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1962", "sentence_1963", "sentence_1964"], "communicatesContent": "Pretty sort o’ treatment this! I come here to see a gentleman, and you two, who I never saw in my life, seize me and treat me in this fashion!"}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_13	event	\N	Watson says the visitor will be recompensed and should sit on the sofa	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1965", "sentence_1966", "sentence_1967"], "communicatesContent": "You will be none the worse. We shall recompense you for the loss of your time. Sit over here on the sofa, and you will not have long to wait."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_14	event	\N	The visitor sits on the sofa	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1968"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_15	event	\N	Holmes speaks from close by and asks for a cigar	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1970", "sentence_1971", "sentence_1972", "sentence_1973"], "communicatesContent": "I think that you might offer me a cigar too."}
valley-of-fear	section_91	section	\N	Section 91	90	{"wordCount": 616}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_18	event	\N	Jones praises Holmes's disguise and says Holmes did not get away easily	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1981", "sentence_1982", "sentence_1983", "sentence_1984", "sentence_1985", "sentence_1986"], "communicatesContent": "Ah, You rogue! You would have made an actor, and a rare one. You had the proper workhouse cough, and those weak legs of yours are worth ten pounds a week. I thought I knew the glint of your eye, though. You didn’t get away from us so easily, You see."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_19	event	\N	Holmes says he has worked in disguise all day and asks about the wire	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1987", "sentence_1988", "sentence_1989"], "communicatesContent": "I have been working in that get-up all day. You see, a good many of the criminal classes begin to know me,—especially since our friend here took to publishing some of my cases: so I can only go on the war-path under some simple disguise like this. You got my wire?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_20	event	\N	Jones says the wire brought him there and reports his case has failed	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1990", "sentence_1991", "sentence_1992", "sentence_1993"], "communicatesContent": "Yes; that was what brought me here. It has all come to nothing. I have had to release two of my prisoners, and there is no evidence against the other two."}
sign-of-the-four	event_46_21	event	\N	Holmes says he will supply two others and puts Jones under his orders	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1994", "sentence_1995", "sentence_1996", "sentence_1997", "sentence_1998"], "communicatesContent": "Never mind. We shall give you two others in the place of them. But you must put yourself under my orders. You are welcome to all the official credit, but you must act on the line that I point out. Is that agreed?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_47_1	event	\N	A plan for a police-boat is arranged at Westminster Stairs for seven o'clock	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2000", "sentence_2001", "sentence_2002"], "communicatesContent": "I shall want a fast police-boat—a steam launch—to be at the Westminster Stairs at seven o’clock."}
sign-of-the-four	event_47_2	event	\N	Jones arranges for two stanch men to be available in the boat	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2003", "sentence_2004", "sentence_2005"], "communicatesContent": "I shall want two stanch men, in case of resistance."}
sign-of-the-four	event_47_3	event	\N	The treasure is to be obtained after the men are secured	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2006", "sentence_2007", "sentence_2008", "sentence_2009", "sentence_2010"], "communicatesContent": "When we secure the men we shall get the treasure."}
sign-of-the-four	event_47_4	event	\N	Jones proposes that Watson take the treasure box to the young lady	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2007", "sentence_2008", "sentence_2009", "sentence_2010", "sentence_2011", "sentence_2012", "sentence_2013", "sentence_2014", "sentence_2015"], "communicatesContent": "I think that it would be a pleasure to my friend here to take the box round to the young lady to whom half of it rightfully belongs."}
sign-of-the-four	event_47_5	event	\N	Jones says the treasure must be handed over to the authorities after the official investigation	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2011", "sentence_2012", "sentence_2013", "sentence_2014", "sentence_2015"], "communicatesContent": "The treasure must afterwards be handed over to the authorities until after the official investigation."}
sign-of-the-four	event_47_6	event	\N	Holmes requests an unofficial interview with Jonathan Small if he is efficiently guarded	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2016", "sentence_2017", "sentence_2018", "sentence_2019"], "communicatesContent": "I should much like to have a few details about this matter from the lips of Jonathan Small himself. There is no objection to my having an unofficial interview with him, either here in my rooms or elsewhere, as long as he is efficiently guarded?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_47_7	event	\N	Jones agrees to the interview if Jonathan Small is caught	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2020", "sentence_2021", "sentence_2022", "sentence_2023", "sentence_2024", "sentence_2025"], "communicatesContent": "However, if you can catch him I don’t see how I can refuse you an interview with him."}
sign-of-the-four	event_47_8	event	\N	Jones insists that Holmes and Watson dine with him	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2025", "sentence_2026", "sentence_2027", "sentence_2028", "sentence_2029"], "communicatesContent": "Only that I insist upon your dining with us. It will be ready in half an hour."}
sign-of-the-four	event_47_9	event	\N	Jones remarks that Watson has not yet recognised his merits as a housekeeper	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2028", "sentence_2029"], "communicatesContent": "Watson, you have never yet recognised my merits as a housekeeper."}
sign-of-the-four	event_48_1	event	\N	Holmes and the others eat a merry dinner	47	{"section": "section_48", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2031", "sentence_2032", "sentence_2033", "sentence_2034", "sentence_2035", "sentence_2036", "sentence_2037", "sentence_2038", "sentence_2039"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_48_2	event	\N	Holmes pours port and proposes a toast to the success of the expedition	47	{"section": "section_48", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2040", "sentence_2041"], "communicatesContent": "One bumper to the success of our little expedition."}
sign-of-the-four	event_48_3	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson to take a pistol and prepare to leave	47	{"section": "section_48", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2042", "sentence_2043", "sentence_2044", "sentence_2045", "sentence_2046", "sentence_2047", "sentence_2048"], "communicatesContent": "Have you a pistol, Watson? You had best take it, then. It is well to be prepared. I see that the cab is at the door. I ordered it for half-past six."}
sign-of-the-four	event_48_4	event	\N	Holmes, Watson, and Jones travel by cab to Westminster wharf and board the launch	47	{"section": "section_48", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2049", "sentence_2050", "sentence_2051", "sentence_2052", "sentence_2053", "sentence_2054", "sentence_2055", "sentence_2056"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_8	event	\N	Jones proposes lying in wait downstream for the men	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2138"], "communicatesContent": "Suppose we go down-stream a short way and lie in wait for them."}
sign-of-the-four	event_48_5	event	\N	Jones asks where to go and Holmes directs the launch to the Tower and Jacobson's Yard	47	{"section": "section_48", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2057", "sentence_2058", "sentence_2059", "sentence_2060"], "communicatesContent": "To the Tower. Tell them to stop opposite Jacobson’s Yard."}
sign-of-the-four	event_48_6	event	\N	The launch speeds past barges and overtakes a river steamer	47	{"section": "section_48", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2061", "sentence_2062", "sentence_2063", "sentence_2064", "sentence_2065", "sentence_2066", "sentence_2067"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_48_7	event	\N	Holmes explains how he reconsidered the Sholto problem after chemical analysis and reasoned about Small's likely movements	47	{"section": "section_48", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2068", "sentence_2069", "sentence_2070", "sentence_2071", "sentence_2072", "sentence_2073", "sentence_2074", "sentence_2075", "sentence_2076", "sentence_2077", "sentence_2078", "sentence_2079", "sentence_2080", "sentence_2081"], "communicatesContent": "I gave my mind a thorough rest by plunging into a chemical analysis. When I had succeeded in dissolving the hydrocarbon which I was at work at, I came back to our problem of the Sholtos, and thought the whole matter out again. ... I then reflected that since he had certainly been in London some time ... he could hardly leave at a moment’s notice, but would need some little time, if it were only a day, to arrange his affairs. That was the balance of probability, at any rate."}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_1	event	\N	Watson argues that Jonathan Small arranged his affairs before the expedition	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2082", "sentence_2083"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_2	event	\N	Watson reasons that Small kept his lair until he no longer needed it	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2084", "sentence_2085"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_3	event	\N	Watson reasons that Small feared gossip about his companion and the Norwood tragedy	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2086", "sentence_2087", "sentence_2088"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_4	event	\N	Small and his companion start from headquarters in darkness and return before daylight	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2089", "sentence_2090", "sentence_2091"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_5	event	\N	Watson infers that the travellers did not go very far	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2092"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_6	event	\N	Small and his party pay Smith, reserve the launch, and hurry to their lodgings with the treasure-box	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2093"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_7	event	\N	Watson predicts that Small's party will later travel by night to a ship for passage abroad	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2094"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_8	event	\N	Watson considers how Small could hide the launch and keep it available	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2095", "sentence_2096", "sentence_2097", "sentence_2098", "sentence_2099", "sentence_2100", "sentence_2101", "sentence_2102", "sentence_2103", "sentence_2104", "sentence_2105", "sentence_2106", "sentence_2107"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_9	event	\N	Watson visits the river yards in disguise and asks after the launch	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2108", "sentence_2109"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_10	event	\N	Watson learns that the Aurora was handed over to Jacobson's two days earlier by a wooden-legged man	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2110", "sentence_2111", "sentence_2112"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_11	event	\N	Mordecai Smith arrives at the yard and calls for the launch that night	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": "mordecai_smith", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2113", "sentence_2114", "sentence_2115", "sentence_2116"], "communicatesContent": "I want her to-night at eight o’clock; eight o’clock sharp, mind, for I have two gentlemen who won’t be kept waiting."}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_12	event	\N	Smith pays the men with shillings	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2117"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_13	event	\N	Watson follows Smith and then returns to the yard	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2118"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_49_14	event	\N	Watson stations a boy to watch the launch and signal when the departure begins	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2118", "sentence_2119", "sentence_2120"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_1	event	\N	Jones says he would have posted police in Jacobson's Yard and arrested the men when they came down	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2121"], "communicatesContent": "but if the affair were in my hands I should have had a body of police in Jacobson’s Yard, and arrested them when they came down."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_2	event	\N	Holmes says Small would send a scout ahead and delay if suspicious	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2123", "sentence_2124"], "communicatesContent": "This man Small is a pretty shrewd fellow. He would send a scout on ahead, and if anything made him suspicious lie snug for another week."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_3	event	\N	Watson suggests sticking to Mordecai Smith to be led to the hiding-place	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2125"], "communicatesContent": "But you might have stuck to Mordecai Smith, and so been led to their hiding-place."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_4	event	\N	The boat passes the bridges on the Thames while the conversation continues	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2131"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_5	event	\N	The boat passes the City and reaches twilight near the Tower	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2132", "sentence_2133"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_6	event	\N	Holmes identifies Jacobson's Yard and orders the boat to cruise under cover of lighters	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2134", "sentence_2135"], "communicatesContent": "That is Jacobson’s Yard. Cruise gently up and down here under cover of this string of lighters."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_9	event	\N	Holmes rejects taking anything for granted and instructs the group to stay put	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2140", "sentence_2141", "sentence_2142", "sentence_2143", "sentence_2144", "sentence_2145"], "communicatesContent": "We have no right to take anything for granted. It is certainly ten to one that they go down-stream, but we cannot be certain. From this point we can see the entrance of the yard, and they can hardly see us. It will be a clear night and plenty of light. We must stay where we are. See how the folk swarm over yonder in the gaslight."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_10	event	\N	Jones says the people are coming from work in the yard	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2146"], "communicatesContent": "They are coming from work in the yard."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_11	event	\N	Holmes speaks about the moral uncertainty of people and statistics	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2147", "sentence_2148", "sentence_2149", "sentence_2150", "sentence_2151", "sentence_2152", "sentence_2153", "sentence_2154", "sentence_2155", "sentence_2156"], "communicatesContent": "Dirty-looking rascals, but I suppose every one has some little immortal spark concealed about him. You would not think it, to look at them. There is no a priori probability about it. A strange enigma is man! Winwood Reade is good upon the subject. He remarks that, while the individual man is an insoluble puzzle, in the aggregate he becomes a mathematical certainty. You can, for example, never foretell what any one man will do, but you can say with precision what an average number will be up to. Individuals vary, but percentages remain constant. So says the statistician."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_12	event	\N	Holmes spots a white handkerchief in the distance	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2157", "sentence_2158"], "communicatesContent": "Surely there is a white flutter over yonder."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_13	event	\N	Watson identifies the flutter as Jones's boy	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2159", "sentence_2160"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, it is your boy. I can see him plainly."}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_14	event	\N	Holmes sees the Aurora racing downstream and orders pursuit	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2161", "sentence_2162", "sentence_2163", "sentence_2164"], "communicatesContent": "Full speed ahead, engineer. Make after that launch with the yellow light. By heaven, I shall never forgive myself if she proves to have the heels of us!"}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_15	event	\N	The Aurora slips unseen through the yard entrance and races down the stream	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2165", "sentence_2166"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_50_16	event	\N	Jones looks at the Aurora and shakes his head	49	{"section": "section_50", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2167"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_51_1	event	\N	Holmes urges the stokers to keep feeding the engines so they can catch the Aurora	50	{"section": "section_51", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2171", "sentence_2172", "sentence_2173", "sentence_2174"], "communicatesContent": "Heap it on, stokers! Make her do all she can! If we burn the boat we must have them!"}
sign-of-the-four	event_51_2	event	\N	The pursuing boat follows close behind the Aurora on the river	50	{"section": "section_51", "speakerId": "athleney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2175", "sentence_2176", "sentence_2177", "sentence_2178", "sentence_2179", "sentence_2180", "sentence_2181", "sentence_2182", "sentence_2183", "sentence_2184", "sentence_2185", "sentence_2186", "sentence_2187", "sentence_2188", "sentence_2191", "sentence_2192", "sentence_2193", "sentence_2194", "sentence_2195"], "communicatesContent": "I think we gain a little."}
sign-of-the-four	event_51_3	event	\N	A tug with three barges blunders between the pursuers and the Aurora, forcing them to avoid a collision	50	{"section": "section_51", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2189", "sentence_2190"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_51_4	event	\N	Jones turns the search-light on the Aurora and the crew on board become visible	50	{"section": "section_51", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2195", "sentence_2196", "sentence_2197", "sentence_2198"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_51_5	event	\N	Jones yells at the men on the Aurora to stop	50	{"section": "section_51", "speakerId": "athleney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2207", "sentence_2208", "sentence_2210", "sentence_2212", "sentence_2213", "sentence_2214", "sentence_2215", "sentence_2216", "sentence_2217"], "communicatesContent": "Stop."}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_1	event	\N	Holmes orders fire if the man raises his hand	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2218"], "communicatesContent": "Fire if he raises his hand."}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_2	event	\N	The pursuers close to within touch of their quarry by boat	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2219"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_3	event	\N	The white man and the dwarf stand in the boat	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2220"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_4	event	\N	The dwarf raises a wooden object to his lips	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2222"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_5	event	\N	Holmes and Watson fire their pistols	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2223"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_6	event	\N	The dwarf is struck, falls sideways into the stream, and the boatman turns the boat toward the southern bank	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2224", "sentence_2226", "sentence_2227"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_7	event	\N	The launch runs onto a mud-bank and the fugitive jumps ashore	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2229", "sentence_2230"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_8	event	\N	The fugitive struggles uselessly in the mud	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2231", "sentence_2232", "sentence_2233"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_9	event	\N	Holmes's party hauls the fugitive out and drags him aboard	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2234"], "communicatesContent": null}
silver-blaze	sentence_468	lexical	\N	\N	467	{"text": "\\"Exceedingly so.\\"", "section": "silver-blaze-section_12", "entities": []}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_11	event	\N	Holmes's party hauls the Aurora off and makes her fast to the stern	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2236"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_12	event	\N	A solid iron chest stands on the deck	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2237"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_13	event	\N	Holmes's party transfers the iron chest to their cabin	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2239"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_14	event	\N	Holmes and Watson steam upstream and search the river for the Islander	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2240", "sentence_2241"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_15	event	\N	Holmes points to the wooden hatchway	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2242"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_16	event	\N	Holmes remarks that they were hardly quick enough with their pistols	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2243"], "communicatesContent": "We were hardly quick enough with our pistols."}
sign-of-the-four	event_52_17	event	\N	A murderous dart is found stuck behind where the men had been standing	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2244", "sentence_2245", "sentence_2246"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_1	event	\N	Jonathan Small sits in the cabin opposite the iron box	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2248", "sentence_2253"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_2	event	\N	Jonathan Small appears handcuffed and seated with his head sunk on his breast	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2253"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_3	event	\N	Holmes speaks to Jonathan Small and says he is sorry it has come to this	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2256"], "communicatesContent": "I am sorry that it has come to this."}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_4	event	\N	Jonathan Small replies that he is sorry too	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "jonathan_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2257"], "communicatesContent": "And so am I, sir."}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_5	event	\N	Jonathan Small denies striking Mr. Sholto and blames Tonga for shooting him	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "jonathan_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2258", "sentence_2259", "sentence_2260", "sentence_2261", "sentence_2262", "sentence_2263"], "communicatesContent": "I don’t believe that I can swing over the job. I give you my word on the book that I never raised hand against Mr. Sholto. It was that little hell-hound Tonga who shot one of his cursed darts into him. I had no part in it, sir. I was as grieved as if it had been my blood-relation. I welted the little devil with the slack end of the rope for it, but it was done, and I could not undo it again."}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_6	event	\N	Holmes offers a cigar and flask and questions how Tonga could overpower Mr. Sholto	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2264", "sentence_2265"], "communicatesContent": "Have a cigar; and you had best take a pull out of my flask, for you are very wet. How could you expect so small and weak a man as this black fellow to overpower Mr. Sholto and hold him while you were climbing the rope?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_7	event	\N	Jonathan Small says he hoped to find the room clear and knew the house habits	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "jonathan_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2266", "sentence_2267", "sentence_2268", "sentence_2269", "sentence_2270", "sentence_2271", "sentence_2272", "sentence_2273"], "communicatesContent": "You seem to know as much about it as if you were there, sir. The truth is that I hoped to find the room clear. I knew the habits of the house pretty well, and it was the time when Mr. Sholto usually went down to his supper. I shall make no secret of the business. The best defence that I can make is just the simple truth. Now, if it had been the old major I would have swung for him with a light heart. I would have thought no more of knifing him than of smoking this cigar. But it’s cursed hard that I should be lagged over this young Sholto, with whom I had no quarrel whatever."}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_8	event	\N	Holmes states that Jonathan Small is under the charge of Athelney Jones and will be brought to his rooms	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2274", "sentence_2275", "sentence_2276", "sentence_2277"], "communicatesContent": "You are under the charge of Mr. Athelney Jones, of Scotland Yard. He is going to bring you up to my rooms, and I shall ask you for a true account of the matter. You must make a clean breast of it, for if you do I hope that I may be of use to you. I think I can prove that the poison acts so quickly that the man was dead before ever you reached the room."}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_9	event	\N	Jonathan Small agrees that the man was dead before he reached the room and describes seeing him	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "jonathan_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2278", "sentence_2279", "sentence_2280", "sentence_2281", "sentence_2282", "sentence_2283"], "communicatesContent": "That he was, sir. I never got such a turn in my life as when I saw him grinning at me with his head on his shoulder as I climbed through the window. It fairly shook me, sir. I’d have half killed Tonga for it if he had not scrambled off. That was how he came to leave his club, and some of his darts too, as he tells me, which I dare say helped to put you on our track; though how you kept on it is more than I can tell. I don’t feel no malice against you for it."}
sign-of-the-four	event_53_10	event	\N	Jonathan Small describes his claim to money and his connection with the Agra treasure	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "jonathan_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2284", "sentence_2285", "sentence_2286"], "communicatesContent": "It does seem a queer thing that I who have a fair claim to nigh upon half a million of money should spend the first half of my life building a breakwater in the Andamans, and am like to spend the other half digging drains at Dartmoor. It was an evil day for me when first I clapped eyes upon the merchant Achmet and had to do with the Agra treasure, which never brought anything but a curse yet upon the man who owned it. To him it brought murder, to Major Sholto it brought fear and guilt, to me it has meant slavery for life."}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_1	event	\N	Athelney Jones enters the cabin	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2287"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_6	event	\N	Watson returns to Baker Street with the empty box and meets Holmes, the prisoner, and the inspector	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2416", "sentence_2417"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_2	event	\N	Athelney Jones speaks about the capture and the pursuit	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2288", "sentence_2289", "sentence_2290", "sentence_2291", "sentence_2292", "sentence_2293"], "communicatesContent": "Quite a family party. I think I shall have a pull at that flask, Holmes. Well, I think we may all congratulate each other. Pity we didn’t take the other alive; but there was no choice. I say, Holmes, you must confess that you cut it rather fine. It was all we could do to overhaul her."}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_3	event	\N	Holmes replies	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2294", "sentence_2295"], "communicatesContent": "All is well that ends well. But I certainly did not know that the Aurora was such a clipper."}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_4	event	\N	Jones reports Smith’s claims about the Aurora and Small’s denial	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2296", "sentence_2297"], "communicatesContent": "Smith says she is one of the fastest launches on the river, and that if he had had another man to help him with the engines we should never have caught her. He swears he knew nothing of this Norwood business."}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_5	event	\N	Jonathan Small denies knowledge of the Norwood business and explains why he chose the launch	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "jonathan_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2298", "sentence_2299", "sentence_2300"], "communicatesContent": "Neither he did, not a word. I chose his launch because I heard that she was a flier. We told him nothing, but we paid him well, and he was to get something handsome if we reached our vessel, the Esmeralda, at Gravesend, outward bound for the Brazils."}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_6	event	\N	Jones speaks about spare treatment and condemnation	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2301", "sentence_2302"], "communicatesContent": "Well, if he has done no wrong we shall see that no wrong comes to him. If we are pretty quick in catching our men, we are not so quick in condemning them."}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_7	event	\N	Narrator observes Jones adopting a self-important manner	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2303", "sentence_2304"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_8	event	\N	Jones states that the party will reach Vauxhall Bridge and land Watson with the treasure-box	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2305", "sentence_2306", "sentence_2307", "sentence_2308", "sentence_2309", "sentence_2310"], "communicatesContent": "We will be at Vauxhall Bridge presently, and shall land you, Dr. Watson, with the treasure-box. I need hardly tell you that I am taking a very grave responsibility upon myself in doing this. It is most irregular; but of course an agreement is an agreement. I must, however, as a matter of duty, send an inspector with you, since you have so valuable a charge. You will drive, no doubt?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_9	event	\N	Jones asks about the key to the box and Small answers	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2311", "sentence_2312", "sentence_2313", "sentence_2314"], "communicatesContent": "It is a pity there is no key, that we may make an inventory first. You will have to break it open. Where is the key, my man?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_10	event	\N	Jones criticises the unnecessary trouble and instructs Watson to return with the box	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2315", "sentence_2316", "sentence_2317", "sentence_2318", "sentence_2319", "sentence_2320"], "communicatesContent": "Hum! There was no use your giving this unnecessary trouble. We have had work enough already through you. However, doctor, I need not warn you to be careful. Bring the box back with you to the Baker Street rooms. You will find us there, on our way to the station."}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_11	event	\N	Watson and the inspector land at Vauxhall with the iron box and travel by cab to Mrs. Cecil Forrester’s house	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2321", "sentence_2322"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_12	event	\N	The servant explains that Mrs. Cecil Forrester is out and Miss Morstan is in the drawing-room	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2323", "sentence_2324", "sentence_2325"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_13	event	\N	Watson enters the drawing-room carrying the box	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2325"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_14	event	\N	Watson finds Mary Morstan seated by the open window	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2326", "sentence_2327", "sentence_2328"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_54_15	event	\N	Mary Morstan rises at Watson’s footfall and flushes with surprise and pleasure	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2329"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_1	event	\N	A cab drives up	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2330"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_2	event	\N	Watson arrives at Mrs. Cecil Forrester’s house with the iron box	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2333", "sentence_2364"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_3	event	\N	Watson places the iron box on the table	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2333"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_4	event	\N	Mrs. Cecil Forrester asks what news Watson has brought	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "mrs_cecil_forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2331", "sentence_2332"], "communicatesContent": "What news have you brought me?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_5	event	\N	Watson says he has brought better than news and a fortune	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2333", "sentence_2334", "sentence_2335"], "communicatesContent": "I have brought something better than news. I have brought you something which is worth all the news in the world. I have brought you a fortune."}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_6	event	\N	Mrs. Cecil Forrester comments on the treasure and its value	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "mrs_cecil_forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2337", "sentence_2338", "sentence_2339", "sentence_2340", "sentence_2341", "sentence_2342", "sentence_2343", "sentence_2344", "sentence_2345"], "communicatesContent": "Is that the treasure, then?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_60_5	event	\N	The narrator keeps watch for two nights with the Sikh troopers	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2530", "sentence_2531"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_7	event	\N	Watson identifies the box as the great Agra treasure and explains the shares	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2339", "sentence_2340", "sentence_2341", "sentence_2342", "sentence_2343", "sentence_2344", "sentence_2345"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, this is the great Agra treasure. Half of it is yours and half is Thaddeus Sholto’s. You will have a couple of hundred thousand each. Think of that! An annuity of ten thousand pounds. There will be few richer young ladies in England. Is it not glorious?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_8	event	\N	Mrs. Cecil Forrester says she owes the treasure to Watson	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "mrs_cecil_forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2347"], "communicatesContent": "If I have it, I owe it to you."}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_9	event	\N	Watson says Sherlock Holmes found the clue and nearly lost the treasure	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2348", "sentence_2349", "sentence_2350"], "communicatesContent": "No, no, not to me, but to my friend Sherlock Holmes. With all the will in the world, I could never have followed up a clue which has taxed even his analytical genius. As it was, we very nearly lost it at the last moment."}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_10	event	\N	Mrs. Cecil Forrester asks Watson to sit down and tell her the story	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "mrs_cecil_forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2351"], "communicatesContent": "Pray sit down and tell me all about it, Dr. Watson."}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_11	event	\N	Watson recounts the search, the discovery of the Aurora, Athelney Jones, the expedition, and the chase	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2352", "sentence_2353", "sentence_2354", "sentence_2355", "sentence_2356", "sentence_2357", "sentence_2358", "sentence_2359", "sentence_2360", "sentence_2361", "sentence_2362", "sentence_2363", "sentence_2364"], "communicatesContent": "Holmes’s new method of search, the discovery of the Aurora, the appearance of Athelney Jones, our expedition in the evening, and the wild chase down the Thames."}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_12	event	\N	Mrs. Cecil Forrester reacts to the account of the dart and recovers after water is brought	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2353", "sentence_2354", "sentence_2355", "sentence_2356", "sentence_2357"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_13	event	\N	Mrs. Cecil Forrester says she was shocked to hear that her friends were in danger	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "mrs_cecil_forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2357"], "communicatesContent": "It was a shock to me to hear that I had placed my friends in such horrible peril."}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_14	event	\N	Watson asks to show the treasure first	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2362", "sentence_2363", "sentence_2364"], "communicatesContent": "There is the treasure. What could be brighter than that? I got leave to bring it with me, thinking that it would interest you to be the first to see it."}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_15	event	\N	Mrs. Cecil Forrester inspects the box and asks for the key and a poker	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "mrs_cecil_forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2365", "sentence_2366", "sentence_2368", "sentence_2369", "sentence_2370", "sentence_2371", "sentence_2372", "sentence_2373", "sentence_2374", "sentence_2375", "sentence_2376", "sentence_2377"], "communicatesContent": "It would be of the greatest interest to me. What a pretty box! This is Indian work, I suppose? And so heavy! The box alone must be of some value. Where is the key?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_16	event	\N	Watson says Small threw the key into the Thames	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2376"], "communicatesContent": "Small threw it into the Thames."}
sign-of-the-four	event_55_17	event	\N	Watson opens the iron box with Mrs. Forrester’s poker	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2378", "sentence_2379", "sentence_2380", "sentence_2381", "sentence_2382", "sentence_2383", "sentence_2384", "sentence_2385", "sentence_2386", "sentence_2387"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_56_1	event	\N	Miss Morstan says the treasure is lost	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": "mary_morstan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2388"], "communicatesContent": "The treasure is lost."}
sign-of-the-four	event_56_2	event	\N	The speaker reacts with relief to the treasure's loss	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2389", "sentence_2390", "sentence_2391", "sentence_2392", "sentence_2393"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_56_3	event	\N	Mary Morstan asks why the speaker says 'Thank God'	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": "mary_morstan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2394", "sentence_2395", "sentence_2396"], "communicatesContent": "Why do you say that?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_56_4	event	\N	The speaker takes Mary Morstan's hand and explains his love	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2397", "sentence_2398", "sentence_2399", "sentence_2400", "sentence_2401", "sentence_2402"], "communicatesContent": "Because you are within my reach again. Because I love you, Mary, as truly as ever a man loved a woman. Because this treasure, these riches, sealed my lips. Now that they are gone I can tell you how I love you. That is why I said, 'Thank God.'"}
sign-of-the-four	event_56_5	event	\N	Mary Morstan responds in agreement and the speaker draws her to his side	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": "mary_morstan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2403", "sentence_2404"], "communicatesContent": "Then I say, 'Thank God,' too."}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_1	event	\N	The inspector rides in a cab and waits until Watson rejoins him	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2406"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_2	event	\N	Watson shows the inspector the empty box	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2407"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_3	event	\N	The inspector reacts gloomily to the empty box	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2407", "sentence_2408", "sentence_2409"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_4	event	\N	Watson says Mr. Thaddeus Sholto is a rich man and will reward the inspector	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2412", "sentence_2413"], "communicatesContent": "Mr. Thaddeus Sholto is a rich man. He will see that you are rewarded, treasure or no."}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_5	event	\N	The inspector shakes his head despondently	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2414"], "communicatesContent": null}
silver-blaze	sentence_511	lexical	\N	\N	510	{"text": "roared the ring.", "section": "silver-blaze-section_13", "entities": []}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_7	event	\N	Holmes lounges in his arm-chair while Jonathan Small sits opposite him with his wooden leg cocked over his sound one	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2418"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_8	event	\N	Watson exhibits the empty box	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2419"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_9	event	\N	Athelney Jones accuses Small of arranging the matter	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2420"], "communicatesContent": "This is your doing, Small."}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_10	event	\N	Jonathan Small declares that he has hidden the treasure and that no one else shall have it	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "jonathan_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2421", "sentence_2422", "sentence_2423", "sentence_2424", "sentence_2425", "sentence_2426", "sentence_2427", "sentence_2428", "sentence_2429", "sentence_2430", "sentence_2431"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, I have put it away where you shall never lay hand upon it. It is my treasure; and if I can’t have the loot I’ll take darned good care that no one else does. I tell you that no living man has any right to it, unless it is three men who are in the Andaman convict-barracks and myself. I know now that I cannot have the use of it, and I know that they cannot. I have acted all through for them as much as for myself. It’s been the sign of four with us always. Well I know that they would have had me do just what I have done, and throw the treasure into the Thames rather than let it go to kith or kin of Sholto or of Morstan. It was not to make them rich that we did for Achmet. You’ll find the treasure where the key is, and where little Tonga is. When I saw that your launch must catch us, I put the loot away in a safe place. There are no rupees for you this journey."}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_11	event	\N	Athelney Jones accuses Small of deceiving them	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2432", "sentence_2433"], "communicatesContent": "You are deceiving us, Small. If you had wished to throw the treasure into the Thames it would have been easier for you to have thrown box and all."}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_12	event	\N	Jonathan Small replies that throwing the box would have made recovery easier	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "jonathan_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2434", "sentence_2435", "sentence_2436", "sentence_2437", "sentence_2438", "sentence_2439", "sentence_2440"], "communicatesContent": "Easier for me to throw, and easier for you to recover. The man that was clever enough to hunt me down is clever enough to pick an iron box from the bottom of a river. Now that they are scattered over five miles or so, it may be a harder job. It went to my heart to do it, though. I was half mad when you came up with us. However, there’s no good grieving over it. I’ve had ups in my life, and I’ve had downs, but I’ve learned not to cry over spilled milk."}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_13	event	\N	The detective says Small would have had a better chance at his trial if he had helped justice	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "athelney_jones", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2441", "sentence_2442"], "communicatesContent": "This is a very serious matter, Small. If you had helped justice, instead of thwarting it in this way, you would have had a better chance at your trial."}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_14	event	\N	Jonathan Small denounces justice and claims to have earned the treasure through suffering	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "jonathan_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2443", "sentence_2444", "sentence_2445", "sentence_2446", "sentence_2447", "sentence_2448", "sentence_2449", "sentence_2450", "sentence_2451"], "communicatesContent": "Justice! A pretty justice! Whose loot is this, if it is not ours? Where is the justice that I should give it up to those who have never earned it? Look how I have earned it! Twenty long years in that fever-ridden swamp, all day at work under the mangrove-tree, all night chained up in the filthy convict-huts, bitten by mosquitoes, racked with ague, bullied by every cursed black-faced policeman who loved to take it out of a white man. That was how I earned the Agra treasure; and you talk to me of justice because I cannot bear to feel that I have paid this price only that another may enjoy it! I would rather swing a score of times, or have one of Tonga’s darts in my hide, than live in a convict’s cell and feel that another man is at his ease in a palace with the money that should be mine."}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_15	event	\N	Small drops his mask of stoicism and speaks wildly while his handcuffs clank	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2452"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_57_16	event	\N	Watson concludes that Major Sholto's earlier terror was not groundless or unnatural	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2453"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_58_1	event	\N	Holmes says they know nothing of the matter and have not heard the story	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2454", "sentence_2455"], "communicatesContent": "You forget that we know nothing of all this. We have not heard your story, and we cannot tell how far justice may originally have been on your side."}
sign-of-the-four	event_58_2	event	\N	John Small responds that he bears no grudge and will tell his story	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": "john_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2456", "sentence_2457", "sentence_2458", "sentence_2459", "sentence_2460", "sentence_2461"], "communicatesContent": "Well, sir, you have been very fair-spoken to me, though I can see that I have you to thank that I have these bracelets upon my wrists. Still, I bear no grudge for that. It is all fair and above-board. If you want to hear my story I have no wish to hold it back. What I say to you is God’s truth, every word of it. Thank you; you can put the glass beside me here, and I’ll put my lips to it if I am dry."}
sign-of-the-four	event_58_3	event	\N	John Small states that he is a Worcestershire man born near Pershore	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": "john_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2462", "sentence_2463"], "communicatesContent": "I am a Worcestershire man myself,—born near Pershore. I dare say you would find a heap of Smalls living there now if you were to look."}
sign-of-the-four	event_58_4	event	\N	John Small says he had been no credit to his family and had been a rover	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": "john_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2464", "sentence_2465"], "communicatesContent": "I have often thought of taking a look round there, but the truth is that I was never much of a credit to the family, and I doubt if they would be so very glad to see me. They were all steady, chapel-going folk, small farmers, well-known and respected over the country-side, while I was always a bit of a rover."}
sign-of-the-four	event_60_6	event	\N	The troopers stand together and speak in their Sikh lingo all night	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2533"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_58_5	event	\N	John Small joins the 3rd Buffs and goes to India	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": "john_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2466"], "communicatesContent": "At last, however, when I was about eighteen, I gave them no more trouble, for I got into a mess over a girl, and could only get out of it again by taking the Queen’s shilling and joining the 3rd Buffs, which was just starting for India."}
sign-of-the-four	event_58_6	event	\N	John Small swims in the Ganges and is attacked by a crocodile	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": "john_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2467", "sentence_2468", "sentence_2469", "sentence_2470", "sentence_2471", "sentence_2472"], "communicatesContent": "I wasn’t destined to do much soldiering, however. I had just got past the goose-step, and learned to handle my musket, when I was fool enough to go swimming in the Ganges. Luckily for me, my company sergeant, John Holder, was in the water at the same time, and he was one of the finest swimmers in the service. A crocodile took me, just as I was half-way across, and nipped off my right leg as clean as a surgeon could have done it, just above the knee. What with the shock and the loss of blood, I fainted, and should have drowned if Holder had not caught hold of me and paddled for the bank. I was five months in hospital over it, and when at last I was able to limp out of it with this timber toe strapped to my stump I found myself invalided out of the army and unfitted for any active occupation."}
sign-of-the-four	event_58_7	event	\N	John Small says he became a cripple and was left unable to work actively	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": "john_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2473", "sentence_2474"], "communicatesContent": "I was, as you can imagine, pretty down on my luck at this time, for I was a useless cripple though not yet in my twentieth year. However, my misfortune soon proved to be a blessing in disguise."}
sign-of-the-four	event_58_8	event	\N	Abel White offers John Small an overseer post and the colonel recommends him	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": "john_small", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2475", "sentence_2476", "sentence_2477", "sentence_2478", "sentence_2479", "sentence_2480"], "communicatesContent": "A man named Abel White, who had come out there as an indigo-planter, wanted an overseer to look after his coolies and keep them up to their work. He happened to be a friend of our colonel’s, who had taken an interest in me since the accident. To make a long story short, the colonel recommended me strongly for the post and, as the work was mostly to be done on horseback, my leg was no great obstacle, for I had enough knee left to keep good grip on the saddle. What I had to do was to ride over the plantation, to keep an eye on the men as they worked, and to report the idlers. The pay was fair, I had comfortable quarters, and altogether I was content to spend the remainder of my life in indigo-planting. Mr. Abel White was a kind man, and he would often drop into my little shanty and smoke a pipe with me, for white folk out there feel their hearts warm to each other as they never do here at home."}
sign-of-the-four	event_59_1	event	\N	The mutiny breaks out suddenly	58	{"section": "section_59", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2482", "sentence_2483"], "communicatesContent": null}
