sign-of-the-four	event_61_1	event	\N	The watch is kept in dark, rainy weather	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2537", "sentence_2538"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_61_2	event	\N	The speaker tries to make the Sikhs talk	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2539"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_61_3	event	\N	The rounds pass by	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2540"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_61_4	event	\N	The speaker takes out his pipe and lays down his musket to strike a match	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2541"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_61_5	event	\N	The two Sikhs seize the speaker and threaten him with weapons	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2542", "sentence_2543"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_61_6	event	\N	Abdullah Khan demands loyalty or silence	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "abdullah_khan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2553", "sentence_2559"], "communicatesContent": "Listen to me, Sahib. You must either be with us now or you must be silenced forever. Either you are heart and soul with us on your oath on the cross of the Christians, or your body this night shall be thrown into the ditch and we shall pass over to our brothers in the rebel army. There is no middle way. Which is it to be, death or life? We can only give you three minutes to decide, for the time is passing, and all must be done before the rounds come again."}
sign-of-the-four	event_61_7	event	\N	The speaker refuses to help against the fort and offers to be stabbed	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2560", "sentence_2563"], "communicatesContent": "How can I decide? You have not told me what you want of me. But I tell you now that if it is anything against the safety of the fort I will have no truck with it, so you can drive home your knife and welcome."}
sign-of-the-four	event_61_8	event	\N	Abdullah Khan explains that they want the speaker to join them for loot	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "abdullah_khan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2564", "sentence_2569"], "communicatesContent": "It is nothing against the fort. We only ask you to do that which your countrymen come to this land for. We ask you to be rich. If you will be one of us this night, we will swear to you upon the naked knife, and by the threefold oath which no Sikh was ever known to break, that you shall have your fair share of the loot. A quarter of the treasure shall be yours. We can say no fairer."}
sign-of-the-four	event_61_9	event	\N	The speaker asks what the treasure is	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2570", "sentence_2572"], "communicatesContent": "But what is the treasure, then? I am as ready to be rich as you can be, if you will but show me how it can be done."}
sign-of-the-four	event_62_1	event	\N	A man demands an oath from another man before betrayal	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "abdullah_khan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2573", "sentence_2574", "sentence_2575", "sentence_2576", "sentence_2577"], "communicatesContent": "You will swear, then, by the bones of your father, by the honour of your mother, by the cross of your faith, to raise no hand and speak no word against us, either now or afterwards? ... My comrade and I will swear that you shall have a quarter of the treasure which shall be equally divided among the four of us. ... No; Dost Akbar must have his share."}
sign-of-the-four	event_62_2	event	\N	A speaker explains the plan to await a coming man at the gate	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "mahomet_singh", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2578", "sentence_2579", "sentence_2580", "sentence_2581", "sentence_2582", "sentence_2583"], "communicatesContent": "We can tell the tale to you while we await them. Do you stand at the gate, Mahomet Singh, and give notice of their coming. The thing stands thus, Sahib, and I tell it to you because I know that an oath is binding upon a Feringhee, and that we may trust you. Had you been a lying Hindoo, though you had sworn by all the gods in their false temples, your blood would have been upon the knife, and your body in the water. But the Sikh knows the Englishman, and the Englishman knows the Sikh. Hearken, then, to what I have to say."}
sign-of-the-four	event_62_3	event	\N	Mahomet Singh describes a rajah's wealth, concealment, and alliance choices	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "mahomet_singh", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2584", "sentence_2585", "sentence_2586", "sentence_2587", "sentence_2588", "sentence_2589", "sentence_2590", "sentence_2591", "sentence_2592"], "communicatesContent": "There is a rajah in the northern provinces who has much wealth, though his lands are small. Much has come to him from his father, and more still he has set by himself, for he is of a low nature and hoards his gold rather than spend it. When the troubles broke out he would be friends both with the lion and the tiger,—with the Sepoy and with the Company’s Raj. Soon, however, it seemed to him that the white men’s day was come, for through all the land he could hear of nothing but of their death and their overthrow. Yet, being a careful man, he made such plans that, come what might, half at least of his treasure should be left to him. That which was in gold and silver he kept by him in the vaults of his palace, but the most precious stones and the choicest pearls that he had he put in an iron box, and sent it by a trusty servant who, under the guise of a merchant, should take it to the fort at Agra, there to lie until the land is at peace. Thus, if the rebels won he would have his money, but if the Company conquered his jewels would be saved to him. Having thus divided his hoard, he threw himself into the cause of the Sepoys, since they were strong upon his borders. By doing this, mark you, Sahib, his property becomes the due of those who have been true to their salt."}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_9	event	\N	The merchant stops moving on the floor	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2668"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_10	event	\N	The man holds out his manacled hands for whiskey-and-water	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2672"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	section_93	section	\N	Section 93	92	{"wordCount": 615}
sign-of-the-four	event_62_4	event	\N	Mahomet Singh identifies the disguised merchant and describes the ambush plan	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "mahomet_singh", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2593", "sentence_2594", "sentence_2595", "sentence_2596", "sentence_2597", "sentence_2598", "sentence_2599"], "communicatesContent": "This pretended merchant, who travels under the name of Achmet, is now in the city of Agra, and desires to gain his way into the fort. He has with him as travelling-companion my foster-brother Dost Akbar, who knows his secret. Dost Akbar has promised this night to lead him to a side-postern of the fort, and has chosen this one for his purpose. Here he will come presently, and here he will find Mahomet Singh and myself awaiting him. The place is lonely, and none shall know of his coming. The world shall know of the merchant Achmet no more, but the great treasure of the rajah shall be divided among us. What say you to it, Sahib?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_62_5	event	\N	Watson reflects that Achmet's life does not matter to him and decides to accept	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2600", "sentence_2601", "sentence_2602", "sentence_2603"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_63_1	event	\N	Abdullah Khan urges the Sahib to let the group keep the jewels and asks whether he is with them	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": "abdullah_khan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2604", "sentence_2605", "sentence_2606", "sentence_2607", "sentence_2608", "sentence_2609", "sentence_2610"], "communicatesContent": "if this man is taken by the commandant he will be hung or shot, and his jewels taken by the government, so that no man will be a rupee the better for them. Now, since we do the taking of him, why should we not do the rest as well? The jewels will be as well with us as in the Company’s coffers. There will be enough to make every one of us rich men and great chiefs. No one can know about the matter, for here we are cut off from all men. What could be better for the purpose? Say again, then, Sahib, whether you are with us, or if we must look upon you as an enemy."}
sign-of-the-four	event_63_2	event	\N	The Sahib declares that he is with the group	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": "the_sahib", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2611"], "communicatesContent": "I am with you heart and soul."}
sign-of-the-four	event_63_3	event	\N	Abdullah Khan returns the firelock to the Sahib and says the group trusts him	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": "abdullah_khan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2612", "sentence_2613", "sentence_2614"], "communicatesContent": "It is well. You see that we trust you, for your word, like ours, is not to be broken. We have now only to wait for my brother and the merchant."}
sign-of-the-four	event_63_4	event	\N	The Sahib asks whether Abdullah Khan's brother knows the plan	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": "the_sahib", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2615", "sentence_2616"], "communicatesContent": "Does your brother know, then, of what you will do?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_63_5	event	\N	Abdullah Khan says the plan is his brother's and that they will share the watch with Mahomet Singh	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": "abdullah_khan", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2617", "sentence_2618", "sentence_2619"], "communicatesContent": "The plan is his. He has devised it. We will go to the gate and share the watch with Mahomet Singh."}
sign-of-the-four	event_63_6	event	\N	Rain falls steadily under heavy clouds	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2620", "sentence_2621"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_63_7	event	\N	A lantern appears beyond the moat and advances toward the gate	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2624", "sentence_2625"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_63_8	event	\N	The Sahib challenges the approaching men and shines his lantern on them	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": "the_sahib", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2626", "sentence_2627", "sentence_2628", "sentence_2629", "sentence_2630", "sentence_2631", "sentence_2632", "sentence_2633", "sentence_2634", "sentence_2635", "sentence_2636", "sentence_2637", "sentence_2638", "sentence_2639", "sentence_2640", "sentence_2641", "sentence_2642"], "communicatesContent": "Who goes there?"}
sign-of-the-four	event_63_9	event	\N	Merchant Achmet identifies himself and asks for protection at the fort of Agra	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": "merchant_achmet", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2643", "sentence_2644", "sentence_2645", "sentence_2646", "sentence_2647"], "communicatesContent": "Your protection, Sahib, your protection for the unhappy merchant Achmet. I have travelled across Rajpootana that I might seek the shelter of the fort at Agra. I have been robbed and beaten and abused because I have been the friend of the Company. It is a blessed night this when I am once more in safety,—I and my poor possessions."}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_1	event	\N	The merchant answers that the bundle contains an iron box and asks for shelter and a reward	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "little_merchant", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2648", "sentence_2650", "sentence_2651"], "communicatesContent": "An iron box, which contains one or two little family matters which are of no value to others, but which I should be sorry to lose. Yet I am not a beggar; and I shall reward you, young Sahib, and your governor also, if he will give me the shelter I ask."}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_2	event	\N	Watson orders the merchant taken to the main guard	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2655"], "communicatesContent": "Take him to the main guard."}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_3	event	\N	The Sikhs close in on the merchant and march him through the dark gateway	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2656"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_4	event	\N	Watson remains at the gateway with a lantern while the party moves inside	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2658", "sentence_2659"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_5	event	\N	A scuffle and blows break out in the corridors	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2660"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_6	event	\N	The merchant runs back down the passage with the Sikh pursuing him	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2661", "sentence_2662"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_7	event	\N	Watson casts his firelock between the merchant's legs and trips him	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2666"], "communicatesContent": null}
sign-of-the-four	event_64_8	event	\N	The Sikh stabs the fallen merchant twice in the side	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2667", "sentence_2668"], "communicatesContent": null}
