yellow-face	event_10_13	event	\N	Holmes removes the child's mask and reveals the woman underneath	9	{"section": "yellow-face-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_418", "sentence_419"], "communicatesContent": null}
yellow-face	event_10_14	event	\N	Watson laughs and Grant Munro stands stunned	9	{"section": "yellow-face-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_420"], "communicatesContent": null}
yellow-face	event_11_1	event	\N	Mrs. Hebron enters the room and explains the meaning of the situation	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": "mrs_hebron", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_424", "sentence_425", "sentence_426", "sentence_427"], "communicatesContent": "I will tell you the meaning of it. You have forced me, against my own judgment, to tell you, and now we must both make the best of it. My husband died at Atlanta. My child survived."}
yellow-face	event_11_2	event	\N	Mrs. Hebron shows a silver locket and opens it	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_429", "sentence_430", "sentence_431", "sentence_432", "sentence_433", "sentence_434"], "communicatesContent": null}
yellow-face	event_11_3	event	\N	Mrs. Hebron identifies the portrait in the locket as John Hebron of Atlanta	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": "mrs_hebron", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_434", "sentence_435"], "communicatesContent": "That is John Hebron, of Atlanta, and a nobler man never walked the earth. I cut myself off from my race in order to wed him, but never once while he lived did I for an instant regret it."}
yellow-face	event_11_4	event	\N	Mrs. Hebron explains that their child resembled the father's people and was named Lucy	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": "mrs_hebron", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_436", "sentence_437", "sentence_438"], "communicatesContent": "It was our misfortune that our only child took after his people rather than mine. It is often so in such matches, and little Lucy is darker far than ever her father was. But dark or fair, she is my own dear little girlie, and her mother's pet."}
yellow-face	event_11_5	event	\N	The child runs across the room and nestles against Mrs. Hebron's dress	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_439"], "communicatesContent": null}
yellow-face	event_11_6	event	\N	Mrs. Hebron explains why she left the child in America with a Scotch woman	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": "mrs_hebron", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_440", "sentence_441", "sentence_442"], "communicatesContent": "When I left her in America, it was only because her health was weak, and the change might have done her harm. She was given to the care of a faithful Scotch woman who had once been our servant. Never for an instant did I dream of disowning her as my child."}
yellow-face	event_11_7	event	\N	Mrs. Hebron explains that she feared to tell Jack about her child because she loved him	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": "mrs_hebron", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_443", "sentence_444", "sentence_445"], "communicatesContent": "But when chance threw you in my way, Jack, and I learned to love you, I feared to tell you about my child. God forgive me, I feared that I should lose you, and I had not the courage to tell you. I had to choose between you, and in my weakness I turned away from my own little girl."}
yellow-face	event_11_8	event	\N	Mrs. Hebron says she kept the child's existence secret for three years and later decided to bring the child over	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": "mrs_hebron", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_446", "sentence_447", "sentence_448", "sentence_449"], "communicatesContent": "For three years I have kept her existence a secret from you, but I heard from the nurse, and I knew that all was well with her. At last, however, there came an overwhelming desire to see the child once more. I struggled against it, but in vain. Though I knew the danger, I determined to have the child over, if it were but for a few weeks."}
stockbrokers-clerk	sentence_11	lexical	\N	\N	10	{"text": "I trust that Mrs. Watson has entirely recovered from all the little excitements connected with our adventure of the Sign of Four.\\"", "section": "stockbrokers-clerk-section_1", "entities": []}
yellow-face	event_11_9	event	\N	Mrs. Hebron sends money and instructions to the nurse about the cottage and concealment	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": "mrs_hebron", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_450", "sentence_451", "sentence_452"], "communicatesContent": "I sent a hundred pounds to the nurse, and I gave her instructions about this cottage, so that she might come as a neighbour, without my appearing to be in any way connected with her. I pushed my precautions so far as to order her to keep the child in the house during the daytime, and to cover up her little face and hands so that even those who might see her at the window should not gossip about there being a black child in the neighbourhood."}
yellow-face	event_11_10	event	\N	Mrs. Hebron explains that Jack discovered the occupied cottage, saw her leave, and later entered the cottage while the nurse and child escaped	10	{"section": "yellow-face-section_11", "speakerId": "mrs_hebron", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_453", "sentence_454", "sentence_455", "sentence_456", "sentence_457", "sentence_458", "sentence_459"], "communicatesContent": "It was you who told me first that the cottage was occupied. I should have waited for the morning, but I could not sleep for excitement, and so at last I slipped out, knowing how difficult it is to awake you. But you saw me go, and that was the beginning of my troubles. Next day you had my secret at your mercy, but you nobly refrained from pursuing your advantage. Three days later, however, the nurse and child only just escaped from the back door as you rushed in at the front one. And now to-night you at last know all, and I ask you what is to become of us, my child and me?"}
yellow-face	event_12_1	event	\N	Grant Munro breaks the silence and turns to leave with Effie and the child	11	{"section": "yellow-face-section_12", "speakerId": "grant_munro", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_460", "sentence_461", "sentence_462", "sentence_463"], "communicatesContent": "We can talk it over more comfortably at home. I am not a very good man, Effie, but I think that I am a better one than you have given me credit for being."}
yellow-face	event_12_2	event	\N	Holmes and Watson follow Grant Munro and Effie down the lane	11	{"section": "yellow-face-section_12", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_464"], "communicatesContent": null}
yellow-face	event_12_3	event	\N	Holmes speaks to Watson about returning to London	11	{"section": "yellow-face-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_465"], "communicatesContent": "I think that we shall be of more use in London than in Norbury."}
yellow-face	event_12_4	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson to whisper "Norbury" if Holmes becomes over-confident or less careful	11	{"section": "yellow-face-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_466", "sentence_467"], "communicatesContent": "Watson, if it should ever strike you that I am getting a little over-confident in my powers, or giving less pains to a case than it deserves, kindly whisper 'Norbury' in my ear, and I shall be infinitely obliged to you."}
