musgrave-ritual	event_1_4	event	\N	Holmes practices pistol shooting indoors with cartridges	0	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_7", "sentence_8", "sentence_9"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_1_5	event	\N	The room contains chemicals and criminal relics	0	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_10"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_1_6	event	\N	Holmes keeps papers in disordered accumulation	0	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_11", "sentence_12", "sentence_13"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_1_7	event	\N	Watson asks Holmes to make the room more habitable	0	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_14"], "communicatesContent": "as he had finished pasting extracts into his common-place book, he might employ the next two hours in making our room a little more habitable"}
musgrave-ritual	event_1_8	event	\N	Holmes goes to his bedroom and returns with a large tin box	0	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_15", "sentence_16"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_1_9	event	\N	Holmes opens the tin box and reveals bundles of papers	0	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_16", "sentence_17"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_1_10	event	\N	Holmes says the box contains cases and early work records	0	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_18", "sentence_19", "sentence_23", "sentence_25", "sentence_26", "sentence_27", "sentence_28"], "communicatesContent": "There are cases enough here, Watson. I think that if you knew all that I had in this box you would ask me to pull some out instead of putting others in. Yes, my boy, these were all done prematurely before my biographer had come to glorify me. They are not all successes, Watson. But there are some pretty little problems among them. Here's the record of the Tarleton murders, and the case of Vamberry, the wine merchant, and the adventure of the old Russian woman, and the singular affair of the aluminium crutch, as well as a full account of Ricoletti of the club-foot, and his abominable wife. And here—ah, now, this really is something a little recherché."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_1	event	\N	Holmes dives into the chest and brings up a small wooden box	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_29"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_2	event	\N	Holmes produces a crumpled piece of paper, a brass key, a peg of wood with string, and three rusty disks from the box	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_30"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_3	event	\N	Holmes arranges the objects along the edge of the table	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_38"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_4	event	\N	Holmes sits back and looks over the objects with satisfaction	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_39"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_5	event	\N	Holmes identifies the objects as all that he has left from the adventure of the Musgrave Ritual	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_40"], "communicatesContent": "These are all that I have left to remind me of the adventure of the Musgrave Ritual."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_6	event	\N	Watson asks Holmes to give an account of the case	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_42"], "communicatesContent": "I should be so glad if you would give me an account of it."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_7	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson whether he should leave the litter as it is	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_43", "sentence_44"], "communicatesContent": "And leave the litter as it is?"}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_8	event	\N	Holmes says Watson's tidiness will not bear much strain	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_45"], "communicatesContent": "Your tidiness won't bear much strain after all, Watson."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_9	event	\N	Holmes says the case should be added to Watson's annals and calls it unique	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_46", "sentence_47"], "communicatesContent": "I should be glad that you should add this case to your annals, for there are points in it which make it quite unique in the criminal records of this or, I believe, of any other country. A collection of my trifling achievements would certainly be incomplete which contained no account of this very singular business."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_10	event	\N	Holmes describes how earlier cases turned his attention to his profession	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_48", "sentence_49", "sentence_50", "sentence_51"], "communicatesContent": "The affair of the Gloria Scott and my conversation with the unhappy man whose fate I told you of first turned my attention in the direction of the profession which has become my life's work. You see me now when my name has become known far and wide."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_11	event	\N	Holmes says he had rooms in Montague Street and studied science there	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_52"], "communicatesContent": "When I first came up to London I had rooms in Montague Street, just round the corner from the British Museum, and there I waited, filling in my too abundant leisure time by studying all those branches of science which might make me more efficient."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_12	event	\N	Holmes says cases came to him through old fellow-students	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_53"], "communicatesContent": "Now and again cases came in my way, principally through the introduction of old fellow-students."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_13	event	\N	Holmes says the Musgrave Ritual was his third such case	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_54"], "communicatesContent": "The third of these cases was that of the Musgrave Ritual."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_14	event	\N	Holmes describes Reginald Musgrave as a college acquaintance	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_55"], "communicatesContent": "Reginald Musgrave had been in the same college as myself, and I had some slight acquaintance with him."}
musgrave-ritual	event_2_15	event	\N	Holmes and Reginald Musgrave converse occasionally	1	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_60"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_1	event	\N	Reginald Musgrave walks into Watson's room in Montague Street.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_61"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_2	event	\N	Reginald Musgrave and Watson cordially shake hands.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_63", "sentence_64"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_3	event	\N	Reginald Musgrave speaks to Watson about his father's death, his management of the Hurlstone estates, and his role as member for his district.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": "reginald_musgrave", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_65", "sentence_66"], "communicatesContent": "You probably heard of my poor father's death; he was carried off about two years ago. Since then I have had the Hurlstone estates to manage, and as I am member for my district as well, my life has been a busy one."}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_4	event	\N	Reginald Musgrave says that strange doings have occurred at Hurlstone and that the police have thrown no light on the matter.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": "reginald_musgrave", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_69", "sentence_70", "sentence_71"], "communicatesContent": "I am delighted to hear it, for your advice at present would be exceedingly valuable to me. We have had some very strange doings at Hurlstone, and the police have been able to throw no light upon the matter. It is really the most extraordinary and inexplicable business."}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_5	event	\N	Watson listens eagerly to Reginald Musgrave.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_72", "sentence_73"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_6	event	\N	Watson asks for the details.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_74"], "communicatesContent": "Pray, let me have the details."}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_7	event	\N	Reginald Musgrave sits down opposite Watson and lights a cigarette.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_75"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_9_1	event	\N	The detective measures from the shadow calculation and marks a spot by the house wall	8	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_9", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_282", "sentence_283"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_8	event	\N	Reginald Musgrave describes the staff at Hurlstone and names Brunton as the longest-serving servant.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": "reginald_musgrave", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_76", "sentence_77", "sentence_78", "sentence_79", "sentence_80"], "communicatesContent": "Though I am a bachelor, I have to keep up a considerable staff of servants at Hurlstone, for it is a rambling old place, and takes a good deal of looking after. I preserve, too, and in the pheasant months I usually have a house-party, so that it would not do to be short-handed. Altogether there are eight maids, the cook, the butler, two footmen, and a boy. The garden and the stables of course have a separate staff. Of these servants the one who had been longest in our service was Brunton the butler."}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_9	event	\N	Reginald Musgrave describes Brunton as a former schoolmaster who became valuable in the household and had worked there for twenty years.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": "reginald_musgrave", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_81", "sentence_82", "sentence_83", "sentence_84"], "communicatesContent": "He was a young schoolmaster out of place when he was first taken up by my father, but he was a man of great energy and character, and he soon became quite invaluable in the household. He was a well-grown, handsome man, with a splendid forehead, and though he has been with us for twenty years he cannot be more than forty now. With his personal advantages and his extraordinary gifts—it is wonderful that he should have been satisfied so long in such a position."}
musgrave-ritual	event_3_10	event	\N	Reginald Musgrave describes Brunton's romantic behavior, engagement to Rachel Howells, and later involvement with Janet Tregellis.	2	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_3", "speakerId": "reginald_musgrave", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_85", "sentence_86", "sentence_87", "sentence_88", "sentence_89", "sentence_90"], "communicatesContent": "This paragon has one fault. He is a bit of a Don Juan. When he was married it was all right, but since he has been a widower we have had no end of trouble with him. A few months ago we were in hopes that he was about to settle down again for he became engaged to Rachel Howells, our second housemaid; but he has thrown her over since then and taken up with Janet Tregellis, the daughter of the head gamekeeper. Rachel had a sharp touch of brain-fever, and goes about the house now—or did until yesterday—like a black-eyed shadow of her former self. That was our first drama at Hurlstone; but a second one came to drive it from our minds, and it was prefaced by the disgrace and dismissal of butler Brunton."}
musgrave-ritual	event_4_1	event	\N	The narrator cannot sleep after drinking strong café noir	3	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_95", "sentence_96"], "communicatesContent": null}
