musgrave-ritual	event_6_11	event	\N	Watson asks to see the paper that the butler consulted	5	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_6", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_189"], "communicatesContent": "I must see that paper, Musgrave, which this butler of yours thought it worth his while to consult, even at the risk of the loss of his place."}
musgrave-ritual	event_6_12	event	\N	Musgrave describes the ritual as absurd but ancient and offers a copy of the questions and answers	5	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_6", "speakerId": "reginald_musgrave", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_190", "sentence_191", "sentence_192"], "communicatesContent": "It is rather an absurd business, this ritual of ours. But it has at least the saving grace of antiquity to excuse it. I have a copy of the questions and answers here if you care to run your eye over them."}
musgrave-ritual	event_9_4	event	\N	The detective reaches the threshold of the old door and the indicated passage place	8	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_9", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_288", "sentence_289"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_9_5	event	\N	Musgrave checks the calculation with the manuscript and points out the omitted words	8	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_9", "speakerId": "reginald_musgrave", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_295", "sentence_296", "sentence_297"], "communicatesContent": "And under. You have omitted the \\"and under.\\""}
musgrave-ritual	event_7_1	event	\N	Holmes reads the Musgrave Ritual questions and answers aloud	6	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_194", "sentence_195", "sentence_196", "sentence_197", "sentence_198", "sentence_199", "sentence_200", "sentence_201", "sentence_202", "sentence_203", "sentence_204", "sentence_205", "sentence_206", "sentence_207", "sentence_208"], "communicatesContent": "Whose was it? His who is gone. Who shall have it? He who will come. Where was the sun? Over the oak. Where was the shadow? Under the elm. How was it stepped? North by ten and by ten, east by five and by five, south by two and by two, west by one and by one, and so under. What shall we give for it? All that is ours. Why should we give it? For the sake of the trust."}
musgrave-ritual	event_7_2	event	\N	Musgrave remarks on the ritual manuscript	6	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_7", "speakerId": "reginald_musgrave", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_209", "sentence_210"], "communicatesContent": "The original has no date, but is in the spelling of the middle of the seventeenth century. It can be of little help in solving this mystery."}
musgrave-ritual	event_7_3	event	\N	Holmes comments on the paper and Brunton's interest in it	6	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_211", "sentence_212", "sentence_213", "sentence_214", "sentence_215", "sentence_216", "sentence_217", "sentence_218", "sentence_219", "sentence_220", "sentence_221", "sentence_222", "sentence_223"], "communicatesContent": "It gives us another mystery, and one which may solve the first. Your butler appears to have been a very clever man. The paper seems to be of practical importance. Brunton probably saw it before that night. He had probably seen it again to refresh his memory. He had some sort of map or chart which he was comparing with the manuscript, and he thrust it into his pocket when you appeared. I don't think we should have much difficulty in determining that."}
musgrave-ritual	event_7_4	event	\N	Holmes and Watson travel to Hurlstone	6	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_224", "sentence_225"], "communicatesContent": null}
