musgrave-ritual	event_11_1	event	\N	A theory is developed about Brunton and the missing girl at the stone slab	10	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_352", "sentence_353", "sentence_354", "sentence_355", "sentence_356", "sentence_357", "sentence_358", "sentence_359"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_11_2	event	\N	Watson describes the girl's later condition as explained by the hidden event	10	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_360"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_11_3	event	\N	Watson sits and thinks the matter out for twenty minutes	10	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_365"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_11_4	event	\N	Musgrave holds out the coins and states they are coins of Charles the First	10	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_11", "speakerId": "musgrave_ritual", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_366", "sentence_367"], "communicatesContent": "These are coins of Charles the First; you see we were right in fixing our date for the Ritual."}
musgrave-ritual	event_11_5	event	\N	Watson asks to see the contents of the bag from the mere	10	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_11", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_368", "sentence_369"], "communicatesContent": "Let me see the contents of the bag which you fished from the mere."}
musgrave-ritual	event_11_6	event	\N	Musgrave and Watson go up to the study and he lays the debris before Watson	10	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_370"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_11_7	event	\N	Watson examines the debris, rubs a stone, and observes it glow	10	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_371", "sentence_372", "sentence_373"], "communicatesContent": null}
musgrave-ritual	event_11_8	event	\N	Watson explains the historical interpretation of the relic	10	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_11", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_374", "sentence_375", "sentence_376", "sentence_377", "sentence_378", "sentence_379", "sentence_380", "sentence_381", "sentence_382"], "communicatesContent": "You must bear in mind that the Royal party made head in England even after the death of the King, and that when they at last fled they probably left many of their most precious possessions buried behind them, with the intention of returning for them in more peaceful times. My ancestor, Sir Ralph Musgrave, was a prominent Cavalier and the right-hand man of Charles the Second in his wanderings. Well now, I think that really should give us the last link that we wanted. I must congratulate you on coming into the possession, though in rather a tragic manner of a relic which is of great intrinsic value, but of even greater importance as an historical curiosity. It is nothing less than the ancient crown of the Kings of England."}
musgrave-ritual	event_12_1	event	\N	Holmes explains the Musgrave Ritual's questions and answers	11	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_383", "sentence_390", "sentence_391"], "communicatesContent": "Consider what the Ritual says: 'Whose was it?' 'His who is gone.' That was after the execution of Charles. Then, 'Who shall have it?' 'He who will come.' That was Charles the Second, whose advent was already foreseen. There can, I think, be no doubt that this battered and shapeless diadem once encircled the brows of the royal Stuarts."}
musgrave-ritual	event_12_2	event	\N	Watson asks how the crown came into the pond	11	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_12", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_392"], "communicatesContent": "And how came it in the pond?"}
musgrave-ritual	event_12_3	event	\N	Holmes narrates his chain of surmise and proof	11	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_393", "sentence_394"], "communicatesContent": "And with that I sketched out to him the whole long chain of surmise and of proof which I had constructed."}
musgrave-ritual	event_12_4	event	\N	Musgrave asks about the relic's return	11	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_12", "speakerId": "reginald_musgrave", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_396", "sentence_397"], "communicatesContent": "And when he returned?"}
musgrave-ritual	event_12_5	event	\N	Holmes explains the secret may have been lost through inheritance	11	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_398", "sentence_399", "sentence_400"], "communicatesContent": "It is likely that the Musgrave who held the secret died in the interval, and by some oversight left this guide to his descendant without explaining the meaning of it. From that day to this it has been handed down from father to son, until at last it came within reach of a man who tore its secret out of it and lost his life in the venture."}
musgrave-ritual	event_12_6	event	\N	Holmes states that the story of the Musgrave Ritual is complete	11	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_401"], "communicatesContent": "And that's the story of the Musgrave Ritual, Watson."}
musgrave-ritual	event_12_7	event	\N	Holmes says the crown is kept at Hurlstone	11	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_402", "sentence_403"], "communicatesContent": "They have the crown down at Hurlstone—though they had some legal bother and a considerable sum to pay before they were allowed to retain it. I am sure that if you mentioned my name they would be happy to show it to you."}
musgrave-ritual	event_12_8	event	\N	Holmes states that the woman escaped from England	11	{"section": "musgrave-ritual-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_404"], "communicatesContent": "Of the woman nothing was ever heard, and the probability is that she got away out of England and carried herself and the memory of her crime to some land beyond the seas."}
reigate-squires	reigate-squires	story	\N	The Reigate Squires	\N	{"sourceFile": "data/raw/memoirs/reigate-squires.txt", "granularity": "sentence"}
