reigate-squires	event_2_1	event	\N	The burglars ransack the library at Acton's house	1	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_2", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_30", "sentence_31"], "communicatesContent": "What an extraordinary assortment!"}
reigate-squires	event_2_2	event	\N	The Colonel's butler rushes in to announce news of a burglary and murder at Cunningham's	1	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_2", "speakerId": "cunningham_family", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_42", "sentence_43", "sentence_44", "sentence_45", "sentence_46", "sentence_47", "sentence_48"], "communicatesContent": "At the Cunningham's sir! Burglary! Murder!"}
reigate-squires	event_2_3	event	\N	The butler reports that William the coachman is shot dead by the burglar	1	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_2", "speakerId": "cunningham_family", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_52", "sentence_53", "sentence_54", "sentence_55", "sentence_56", "sentence_57", "sentence_58", "sentence_59", "sentence_60", "sentence_61", "sentence_62", "sentence_63"], "communicatesContent": "Neither, sir. It was William the coachman. Shot through the heart, sir, and never spoke again. The burglar, sir. He was off like a shot and got clean away. He'd just broke in at the pantry window when William came on him and met his end in saving his master's property. It was last night, sir, somewhere about twelve."}
reigate-squires	event_2_4	event	\N	Holmes comments on the burglary at Acton's	1	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_2", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_68"], "communicatesContent": "And stole that very singular collection."}
reigate-squires	event_2_5	event	\N	The Colonel discusses the local burglary pattern and the lawsuit between Acton and Cunningham	1	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_2", "speakerId": "colonel_hayter", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_64", "sentence_65", "sentence_66", "sentence_67", "sentence_74", "sentence_75", "sentence_76", "sentence_77", "sentence_78"], "communicatesContent": "It's a baddish business; he's our leading man about here, is old Cunningham, and a very decent fellow too. He'll be cut up over this, for the man has been in his service for years and was a good servant. It's evidently the same villains who broke into Acton's. I fancy it's some local practitioner. In that case, of course, Acton's and Cunningham's are just the places he would go for, since they are far the largest about here. Well, they ought to be, but they've had a lawsuit for some years which has sucked the blood out of both of them, I fancy. Old Acton has some claim on half Cunningham's estate, and the lawyers have been at it with both hands."}
reigate-squires	event_3_1	event	\N	Forrester enters the room	2	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_81", "sentence_82"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_3_2	event	\N	Forrester greets the Colonel and says he does not wish to intrude	2	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_3", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_83"], "communicatesContent": "Good-morning, Colonel; I hope I don't intrude, but we hear that Mr. Holmes of Baker Street is here."}
reigate-squires	event_3_3	event	\N	The Colonel indicates Holmes and Forrester bows	2	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_84"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_3_4	event	\N	Forrester invites Holmes to step across	2	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_3", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_85"], "communicatesContent": "We thought that perhaps you would care to step across, Mr. Holmes."}
reigate-squires	event_3_5	event	\N	Holmes laughs and speaks of Watson's bad luck	2	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_3", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_86"], "communicatesContent": "The fates are against you, Watson."}
reigate-squires	event_3_6	event	\N	Holmes says he and Watson were chatting about the matter and asks for details	2	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_3", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_87", "sentence_88"], "communicatesContent": "We were chatting about the matter when you came in, Inspector. Perhaps you can let us have a few details."}
reigate-squires	event_3_7	event	\N	The inspector describes the Acton affair and the killing of William Kirwan	2	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_3", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_90", "sentence_91", "sentence_92", "sentence_94", "sentence_95", "sentence_96", "sentence_97", "sentence_98", "sentence_99", "sentence_100", "sentence_101", "sentence_102", "sentence_103", "sentence_104"], "communicatesContent": "We had no clue in the Acton affair. But here we have plenty to go on, and there's no doubt it is the same party in each case. The man was seen. He was off like a deer after the shot that killed poor William Kirwan was fired. Mr. Cunningham saw him from the bedroom window, and Mr. Alec Cunningham saw him from the back passage. It was quarter to twelve when the alarm broke out. Mr. Cunningham had just got into bed, and Mr. Alec was smoking a pipe in his dressing-gown. They both heard William the coachman calling for help, and Mr. Alec ran down to see what was the matter. The back door was open, and as he came to the foot of the stairs he saw two men wrestling together outside. One of them fired a shot, the other dropped, and the murderer rushed across the garden and over the hedge. Mr. Cunningham, looking out of his bedroom, saw the fellow as he gained the road, but lost sight of him at once. Mr. Alec stopped to see if he could help the dying man, and so the villain got clean away. Beyond the fact that he was a middle-sized man and dressed in some dark stuff, we have no personal clue; but we are making energetic inquiries, and if he is a stranger we shall soon find him out."}
reigate-squires	event_3_8	event	\N	The inspector explains William's movements and his mother's condition	2	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_3", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_105", "sentence_106", "sentence_107", "sentence_108", "sentence_109", "sentence_110", "sentence_111", "sentence_112", "sentence_113", "sentence_114", "sentence_115", "sentence_116"], "communicatesContent": "What was this William doing there? Did he say anything before he died? Not a word. He lives at the lodge with his mother, and as he was a very faithful fellow we imagine that he walked up to the house with the intention of seeing that all was right there. Of course this Acton business has put every one on their guard. The robber must have just burst open the door—the lock has been forced—when William came upon him. Did William say anything to his mother before going out? She is very old and deaf, and we can get no information from her. The shock has made her half-witted, but I understand that she was never very bright. There is one very important circumstance, however. Look at this!"}
reigate-squires	event_3_9	event	\N	Forrester shows Holmes a torn paper fragment found on the dead man	2	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_3", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_116", "sentence_117", "sentence_118", "sentence_119", "sentence_120", "sentence_121"], "communicatesContent": "This was found between the finger and thumb of the dead man. It appears to be a fragment torn from a larger sheet. You will observe that the hour mentioned upon it is the very time at which the poor fellow met his fate. You see that his murderer might have torn the rest of the sheet from him or he might have taken this fragment from the murderer. It reads almost as though it were an appointment."}
reigate-squires	event_4_1	event	\N	Holmes takes up the scrap of paper	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_122"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_4_2	event	\N	The Inspector describes a theory about William Kirwan and the thief	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_124", "sentence_125"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_4_3	event	\N	Holmes examines the writing with intense concentration	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_126"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_4_4	event	\N	Holmes speaks about the case and his observations	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_127", "sentence_129", "sentence_130"], "communicatesContent": "These are much deeper waters than I had thought. Your last remark, as to the possibility of there being an understanding between the burglar and the servant, and this being a note of appointment from one to the other, is an ingenious and not entirely impossible supposition. But this writing opens up—"}
reigate-squires	event_4_5	event	\N	Holmes rises with renewed energy	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_131", "sentence_132"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_4_6	event	\N	Holmes asks permission to inspect the case details with the Inspector	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_133", "sentence_134", "sentence_135", "sentence_136"], "communicatesContent": "I'll tell you what, I should like to have a quiet little glance into the details of this case. There is something in it which fascinates me extremely. If you will permit me, Colonel, I will leave my friend Watson and you, and I will step round with the Inspector to test the truth of one or two little fancies of mine. I will be with you again in half an hour."}
reigate-squires	event_4_7	event	\N	The Inspector returns alone after an hour and a half	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_137"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_4_8	event	\N	The Inspector reports Holmes's request to go to the house together	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_138", "sentence_139", "sentence_140", "sentence_141"], "communicatesContent": "Mr. Holmes is walking up and down in the field outside. He wants us all four to go up to the house together. To Mr. Cunningham's? Yes, sir."}
reigate-squires	event_4_9	event	\N	The Colonel asks what Holmes wants to go to the house for	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": "colonel_hayter", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_142"], "communicatesContent": "What for?"}
reigate-squires	event_4_10	event	\N	The Inspector says he does not know and comments on Holmes's condition	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_143", "sentence_144", "sentence_145", "sentence_146", "sentence_149", "sentence_150"], "communicatesContent": "I don't quite know, sir. Between ourselves, I think Mr. Holmes had not quite got over his illness yet. He's been behaving very queerly, and he is very much excited. But he's all on fire to start, Colonel, so we had best go out if you are ready."}
reigate-squires	event_4_11	event	\N	Watson reassures the Colonel about Holmes	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_147", "sentence_148"], "communicatesContent": "I don't think you need alarm yourself. I have usually found that there was method in his madness."}
reigate-squires	event_4_12	event	\N	The group finds Holmes pacing in the field	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_151"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_4_13	event	\N	Holmes speaks about the morning's progress and the reconnaissance	3	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_4", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_152", "sentence_153", "sentence_154", "sentence_155", "sentence_156", "sentence_157", "sentence_158", "sentence_159", "sentence_160", "sentence_161", "sentence_162", "sentence_163", "sentence_164", "sentence_165", "sentence_166", "sentence_167", "sentence_168", "sentence_169"], "communicatesContent": "The matter grows in interest. Watson, your country-trip has been a distinct success. I have had a charming morning. Yes; the Inspector and I have made quite a little reconnaissance together. Well, we have seen some very interesting things. I'll tell you what we did as we walk. First of all, we saw the body of this unfortunate man. He certainly died from a revolver wound as reported. Oh, it is as well to test everything. Our inspection was not wasted. We then had an interview with Mr. Cunningham and his son, who were able to point out the exact spot where the murderer had broken through the garden-hedge in his flight. That was of great interest. Then we had a look at this poor fellow's mother. We could get no information from her, however, as she is very old and feeble."}
reigate-squires	event_5_1	event	\N	Holmes and the Inspector discuss the fragment of paper as an important clue	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_173", "sentence_174", "sentence_175", "sentence_176", "sentence_177"], "communicatesContent": "I think that we are both agreed, Inspector that the fragment of paper in the dead man's hand, bearing, as it does, the very hour of his death written upon it, is of extreme importance."}
reigate-squires	event_5_2	event	\N	The Inspector says he examined the ground for the missing paper	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_178"], "communicatesContent": "I examined the ground carefully in the hope of finding it."}
reigate-squires	event_5_3	event	\N	Holmes reasons about the torn paper fragment and its concealment	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_179", "sentence_180", "sentence_181", "sentence_182", "sentence_183", "sentence_184", "sentence_185"], "communicatesContent": "It was torn out of the dead man's hand. Why was some one so anxious to get possession of it? Because it incriminated him. And what would he do with it? Thrust it into his pocket, most likely, never noticing that a corner of it had been left in the grip of the corpse. If we could get the rest of that sheet it is obvious that we should have gone a long way towards solving the mystery."}
reigate-squires	event_5_4	event	\N	Holmes reasons that the note was sent to William and asks who delivered it	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_186", "sentence_187", "sentence_188", "sentence_189", "sentence_190", "sentence_191"], "communicatesContent": "Then there is another obvious point. The note was sent to William. The man who wrote it could not have taken it; otherwise, of course, he might have delivered his own message by word of mouth. Who brought the note, then? Or did it come through the post?"}
reigate-squires	event_5_5	event	\N	The Inspector reports William received a letter by the afternoon post and destroyed the envelope	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_192", "sentence_193", "sentence_194"], "communicatesContent": "William received a letter by the afternoon post yesterday. The envelope was destroyed by him."}
reigate-squires	event_5_6	event	\N	Holmes praises the Inspector and says he has seen the postman	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_195", "sentence_196", "sentence_197", "sentence_198"], "communicatesContent": "Excellent! You've seen the postman. It is a pleasure to work with you."}
reigate-squires	event_5_7	event	\N	Holmes leads the party to the lodge and scene of the crime	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_199", "sentence_200", "sentence_201", "sentence_202"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_5_8	event	\N	Holmes orders the constable to throw open the kitchen door	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_203"], "communicatesContent": "Throw the door open, officer."}
reigate-squires	event_5_9	event	\N	Holmes explains the positions of the witnesses during the struggle	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_204", "sentence_205", "sentence_206", "sentence_207"], "communicatesContent": "Now, it was on those stairs that young Mr. Cunningham stood and saw the two men struggling just where we are. Old Mr. Cunningham was at that window—the second on the left—and he saw the fellow get away just to the left of that bush. Then Mr. Alec ran out and knelt beside the wounded man. The ground is very hard, you see, and there are no marks to guide us."}
reigate-squires	event_5_10	event	\N	Two men approach from around the house while Holmes speaks	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_208", "sentence_209"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_5_11	event	\N	Alec Cunningham speaks to Holmes	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "alec_cunningham", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_210", "sentence_211", "sentence_212", "sentence_213", "sentence_214", "sentence_215", "sentence_216"], "communicatesContent": "Still at it, then? I thought you Londoners were never at fault. You don't seem to be so very quick, after all. You'll want it. Why, I don't see that we have any clue at all."}
reigate-squires	event_5_12	event	\N	The Inspector says there is only one clue and Holmes reacts with alarm	4	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_5", "speakerId": "forrester", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_217", "sentence_218", "sentence_219"], "communicatesContent": "There's only one. We thought that if we could only find—Good heavens, Mr. Holmes! What is the matter?"}
reigate-squires	event_6_1	event	\N	William's face suddenly assumes a dreadful expression	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_220"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_6_2	event	\N	William rolls his eyes, writhes in agony, groans, and falls face down	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_221"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_6_3	event	\N	The men carry William into the kitchen and place him in a large chair	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_222"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_6_4	event	\N	William lies back in a large chair and breathes heavily for some minutes	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_222"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	sentence_173	lexical	\N	\N	172	{"text": "Therefore a third person must have entered the room.", "section": "crooked-man-section_5", "entities": []}
reigate-squires	event_6_5	event	\N	William rises again and apologises for his weakness	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_223"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_6_6	event	\N	William explains that he has only just recovered from a severe illness	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "william_fellow", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_224"], "communicatesContent": "I have only just recovered from a severe illness."}
reigate-squires	event_6_7	event	\N	William says that he is liable to sudden nervous attacks	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "william_fellow", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_225"], "communicatesContent": "I am liable to these sudden nervous attacks."}
reigate-squires	event_6_8	event	\N	Old Cunningham asks whether he should send someone home in the trap	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "old_cunningham", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_226", "sentence_227"], "communicatesContent": "Shall I send you home in my trap?"}
reigate-squires	event_6_9	event	\N	Holmes says he wants to verify one point and can easily verify it	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_228", "sentence_229"], "communicatesContent": "Since I am here, there is one point on which I should like to feel sure. We can very easily verify it."}
reigate-squires	event_6_10	event	\N	Holmes suggests that William's arrival may have been after the burglar entered the house	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_230", "sentence_231", "sentence_232"], "communicatesContent": "It is just possible that the arrival of this poor fellow William was not before, but after, the entrance of the burglar into the house. You appear to take it for granted that, although the door was forced, the robber never got in."}
reigate-squires	event_6_11	event	\N	Mr. Cunningham says he thinks the burglar never got in	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "mr_cunningham_older", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_233"], "communicatesContent": "I fancy that is quite obvious."}
reigate-squires	event_6_12	event	\N	Mr. Cunningham explains that Alec had not yet gone to bed and would have heard movement	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "mr_cunningham_older", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_234"], "communicatesContent": "My son Alec had not yet gone to bed, and he would certainly have heard any one moving about."}
reigate-squires	event_6_13	event	\N	Holmes asks where Alec was sitting	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_235"], "communicatesContent": "Where was he sitting?"}
reigate-squires	event_6_14	event	\N	Mr. Cunningham says he was smoking in his dressing-room	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "mr_cunningham_older", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_236"], "communicatesContent": "I was smoking in my dressing-room."}
reigate-squires	event_6_15	event	\N	Holmes asks which window the dressing-room is	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_237"], "communicatesContent": "Which window is that?"}
reigate-squires	event_6_16	event	\N	Mr. Cunningham identifies the dressing-room window as the last on the left next to his father's	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "mr_cunningham_older", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_238"], "communicatesContent": "The last on the left next my father's."}
reigate-squires	event_6_17	event	\N	Holmes asks whether both lamps were lit	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_239"], "communicatesContent": "Both of your lamps were lit, of course?"}
reigate-squires	event_6_18	event	\N	Mr. Cunningham confirms that both lamps were lit	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "mr_cunningham_older", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_240"], "communicatesContent": "Undoubtedly."}
reigate-squires	event_6_19	event	\N	Holmes remarks that there are very singular points in the case	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_241"], "communicatesContent": "There are some very singular points here."}
reigate-squires	event_6_20	event	\N	Holmes says it is extraordinary that a burglar would break in while two family members were still up	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_242", "sentence_243"], "communicatesContent": "It is extraordinary that a burglar should deliberately break into a house at a time when he could see from the lights that two of the family were still afoot. He must have been a cool hand."}
reigate-squires	event_6_21	event	\N	Alec Cunningham says the theory is absurd and asks whether the stolen place would have been disarranged	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "alec_cunningham", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_244", "sentence_245", "sentence_246"], "communicatesContent": "If the case were not an odd one we should not have been driven to ask you for an explanation. But as to your ideas that the man had robbed the house before William tackled him, I think it a most absurd notion. Wouldn't we have found the place disarranged, and missed the things which he had taken?"}
reigate-squires	event_6_22	event	\N	Holmes replies that it depends on what the things were and says the burglar was peculiar	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_247", "sentence_248"], "communicatesContent": "It depends on what the things were. You must remember that we are dealing with a burglar who is a very peculiar fellow, and who appears to work on lines of his own."}
reigate-squires	event_6_23	event	\N	Holmes names some of the missing items	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_249", "sentence_250"], "communicatesContent": "A ball of string, a letter-weight, and I don't know what other odds and ends."}
reigate-squires	event_6_24	event	\N	Old Cunningham agrees to do whatever Holmes or the Inspector suggests	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "old_cunningham", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_251", "sentence_252"], "communicatesContent": "We are quite in your hands, Mr. Holmes. Anything which you or the Inspector may suggest will most certainly be done."}
reigate-squires	event_6_25	event	\N	Holmes instructs the family to offer a reward and hands over a form	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_253", "sentence_254", "sentence_255", "sentence_256"], "communicatesContent": "In the first place, I should like you to offer a reward—coming from yourself, for the officials may take a little time before they would agree upon the sum, and these things cannot be done too promptly. I have jotted down the form here. Fifty pounds was quite enough, I thought."}
reigate-squires	event_6_26	event	\N	Old Cunningham examines the form and says it is not quite correct	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "old_cunningham", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_257", "sentence_258"], "communicatesContent": "This is not quite correct, however."}
reigate-squires	event_6_27	event	\N	Holmes says he wrote the form hurriedly	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_259"], "communicatesContent": "I wrote it rather hurriedly."}
reigate-squires	event_6_28	event	\N	Old Cunningham corrects the time in the form to a quarter to twelve on Tuesday morning	5	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_6", "speakerId": "old_cunningham", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_260", "sentence_261", "sentence_265"], "communicatesContent": "You begin, 'Whereas, at about a quarter to one on Tuesday morning an attempt was made,' and so on. It was at a quarter to twelve, as a matter of fact."}
reigate-squires	event_7_1	event	\N	Holmes puts the slip of paper into his pocket-book	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_267"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_7_2	event	\N	Holmes and the others go over the house together	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_268"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_7_3	event	\N	Holmes examines the forced door before entering	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_269", "sentence_270", "sentence_271"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_7_4	event	\N	Holmes asks whether bars are used	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_272", "sentence_273", "sentence_274"], "communicatesContent": "You don't use bars, then?"}
reigate-squires	event_7_5	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the dog is kept	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_275", "sentence_276"], "communicatesContent": "You don't keep a dog?"}
reigate-squires	event_7_6	event	\N	Holmes asks when the servants go to bed	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_277", "sentence_278"], "communicatesContent": "When do the servants go to bed?"}
reigate-squires	event_7_7	event	\N	Holmes remarks on William being in bed at that hour	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_279", "sentence_280", "sentence_281", "sentence_282"], "communicatesContent": "I understand that William was usually in bed also at that hour."}
reigate-squires	event_7_8	event	\N	Mr. Cunningham shows Holmes and Watson over the house	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_282", "sentence_283", "sentence_284", "sentence_285"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_7_9	event	\N	Holmes walks slowly and studies the architecture of the house	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_286", "sentence_287"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_7_10	event	\N	Mr. Cunningham objects that the inspection is unnecessary	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "cunningham_older_mr", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_288", "sentence_289", "sentence_290"], "communicatesContent": "this is surely very unnecessary. That is my room at the end of the stairs, and my son's is the one beyond it. I leave it to your judgment whether it was possible for the thief to have come up here without disturbing us."}
reigate-squires	event_7_11	event	\N	The son tells Holmes to try another line of inquiry	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "ale c_cunningham", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_291"], "communicatesContent": "You must try round and get on a fresh scent, I fancy."}
reigate-squires	event_7_12	event	\N	Holmes asks to inspect the windows of the bedrooms	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_292", "sentence_293", "sentence_294", "sentence_295"], "communicatesContent": "I should like, for example, to see how far the windows of the bedrooms command the front."}
reigate-squires	event_7_13	event	\N	Holmes opens the son's room and glances into the dressing-room	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_294", "sentence_296"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_7_14	event	\N	Mr. Cunningham asks whether Holmes is satisfied	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "cunningham_older_mr", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_297", "sentence_298"], "communicatesContent": "I hope that you are satisfied now?"}
reigate-squires	event_7_15	event	\N	Holmes says he has seen all that he wished	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_299"], "communicatesContent": "Thank you, I think I have seen all that I wished."}
reigate-squires	event_7_16	event	\N	The J.P. leads the group into his own chamber	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_300", "sentence_301", "sentence_302", "sentence_303"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_7_17	event	\N	Holmes falls back behind the others as they move toward the window	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_304"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_7_18	event	\N	Holmes deliberately knocks over the dish of oranges and the carafe of water	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_305", "sentence_306", "sentence_307"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_7_19	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson that he has done it and made a mess of the carpet	6	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_7", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_308", "sentence_309"], "communicatesContent": "You've done it now, Watson. A pretty mess you've made of the carpet."}
reigate-squires	event_8_1	event	\N	Watson picks up the fruit after the table is upset	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_310", "sentence_311"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_8_2	event	\N	The others set the table on its legs again	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_311"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_8_3	event	\N	Alec Cunningham calls for his father to come with him	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": "alec_cunningham", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_315", "sentence_316", "sentence_317"], "communicatesContent": "Wait here an instant. The fellow is off his head, in my opinion. Come with me, father, and see where he has got to!"}
reigate-squires	event_8_4	event	\N	Alec Cunningham and his father rush out of the room	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_318"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_8_5	event	\N	A scream of 'Help! Help! Murder!' is heard	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_321", "sentence_322", "sentence_323"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_8_6	event	\N	Watson rushes from the room to the landing	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_325"], "communicatesContent": "Help! Help! Murder!"}
reigate-squires	event_8_7	event	\N	Watson goes into the room where the cries came from and then into the dressing-room	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_326", "sentence_327"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_8_8	event	\N	The Cunninghams attack Holmes and are pulled away by the others	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_328", "sentence_329"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_8_9	event	\N	Holmes orders the Inspector to arrest the Cunninghams for murder	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_330", "sentence_331", "sentence_332", "sentence_333"], "communicatesContent": "Arrest these men, Inspector! That of murdering their coachman, William Kirwan!"}
reigate-squires	event_8_10	event	\N	The Inspector whistles and two constables come	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_341", "sentence_342"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_8_11	event	\N	Alec Cunningham draws a revolver and it is knocked down	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_343", "sentence_344", "sentence_345", "sentence_346"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_8_12	event	\N	Holmes displays a crumpled piece of paper and says it is the remainder of the sheet	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_347", "sentence_348", "sentence_349", "sentence_350", "sentence_351", "sentence_352", "sentence_353", "sentence_354", "sentence_355", "sentence_356", "sentence_357"], "communicatesContent": "Keep that; you will find it useful at the trial. But this is what we really wanted. Precisely. I'll make the whole matter clear to you presently. I think, Colonel, that you and Watson might return now, and I will be with you again in an hour at the furthest. The Inspector and I must have a word with the prisoners, but you will certainly see me back at luncheon time."}
reigate-squires	event_8_13	event	\N	Holmes returns in the smoking-room with an elderly gentleman	7	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_8", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_358", "sentence_359"], "communicatesContent": "I wished Mr. Acton to be present while I demonstrated this small matter to you, for it is natural that he should take a keen interest in the details."}
reigate-squires	event_9_1	event	\N	Colonel praises Holmes's methods and admits he has seen no clue	8	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_9", "speakerId": "colonel_hayter", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_362", "sentence_363", "sentence_364"], "communicatesContent": "I consider it the greatest privilege to have been permitted to study your methods of working. I confess that they quite surpass my expectations, and that I am utterly unable to account for your result. I have not yet seen the vestige of a clue."}
reigate-squires	event_9_2	event	\N	Holmes explains his methods and takes brandy from Colonel Hayter	8	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_9", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_365", "sentence_366", "sentence_367", "sentence_368", "sentence_369", "sentence_370", "sentence_371", "sentence_372", "sentence_373", "sentence_374"], "communicatesContent": "I am afraid that my explanation may disillusionize you but it has always been my habit to hide none of my methods, either from my friend Watson or from any one who might take an intelligent interest in them. But, first, as I am rather shaken by the knocking about which I had in the dressing-room, I think that I shall help myself to a dash of your brandy, Colonel. My strength has been rather tried of late. We will come to that in its turn. I will lay an account of the case before you in its due order, showing you the various points which guided me in my decision. Pray interrupt me if there is any inference which is not perfectly clear to you. It is of the highest importance in the art of detection to be able to recognise, out of a number of facts, which are incidental and which vital. Otherwise your energy and attention must be dissipated instead of being concentrated."}
reigate-squires	event_9_3	event	\N	Holmes explains that the key fact lay in the scrap of paper in the dead man's hand	8	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_9", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_375", "sentence_376", "sentence_377", "sentence_378", "sentence_379"], "communicatesContent": "Now, in this case there was not the slightest doubt in my mind from the first that the key of the whole matter must be looked for in the scrap of paper in the dead man's hand. Before going into this, I would draw your attention to the fact that, if Alec Cunningham's narrative was correct, and if the assailant, after shooting William Kirwan, had instantly fled, then it obviously could not be he who tore the paper from the dead man's hand. But if it was not he, it must have been Alec Cunningham himself, for by the time that the old man had descended several servants were upon the scene. The point is a simple one, but the Inspector had overlooked it because he had started with the supposition that these county magnates had had nothing to do with the matter. Now, I make a point of never having any prejudices, and of following docilely wherever fact may lead me, and so, in the very first stage of the investigation, I found myself looking a little askance at the part which had been played by Mr. Alec Cunningham."}
reigate-squires	event_11_3	event	\N	Holmes says he wanted to recover the missing part of the note	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_435", "sentence_436", "sentence_437", "sentence_438", "sentence_439"], "communicatesContent": "What I wanted above all was to get the missing part of that note. I was certain that Alec had torn it out of the dead man's hand, and almost certain that he must have thrust it into the pocket of his dressing-gown. Where else could he have put it? The only question was whether it was still there."}
reigate-squires	event_11_4	event	\N	Holmes and the others go up to the house to search for the paper	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_440"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_11_5	event	\N	The Cunninghams join Holmes and the others outside the kitchen door	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_441"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_9_4	event	\N	Holmes examines the paper fragment and identifies it as a remarkable document written by two people	8	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_9", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_380", "sentence_381", "sentence_382", "sentence_383", "sentence_384", "sentence_385", "sentence_386", "sentence_387", "sentence_388", "sentence_389", "sentence_390", "sentence_391", "sentence_392"], "communicatesContent": "And now I made a very careful examination of the corner of paper which the Inspector had submitted to us. It was at once clear to me that it formed part of a very remarkable document. Here it is. Do you not now observe something very suggestive about it? My dear sir, there cannot be the least doubt in the world that it has been written by two persons doing alternate words. When I draw your attention to the strong t's of 'at' and 'to', and ask you to compare them with the weak ones of 'quarter' and 'twelve,' you will instantly recognise the fact. A very brief analysis of these four words would enable you to say with the utmost confidence that the 'learn' and the 'maybe' are written in the stronger hand, and the 'what' in the weaker. Obviously the business was a bad one, and one of the men who distrusted the other was determined that, whatever was done, each should have an equal hand in it. Now, of the two men, it is clear that the one who wrote the 'at' and 'to' was the ringleader."}
reigate-squires	event_10_1	event	\N	Holmes explains how the two hands can be distinguished and linked	9	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_10", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_393", "sentence_394", "sentence_395", "sentence_396", "sentence_397", "sentence_398", "sentence_399", "sentence_400", "sentence_401"], "communicatesContent": "How do you get at that? We might deduce it from the mere character of the one hand as compared with the other. But we have more assured reasons than that for supposing it. If you examine this scrap with attention you will come to the conclusion that the man with the stronger hand wrote all his words first, leaving blanks for the other to fill up. These blanks were not always sufficient, and you can see that the second man had a squeeze to fit his 'quarter' in between the 'at' and the 'to,' showing that the latter were already written. The man who wrote all his words first is undoubtedly the man who planned the affair. Excellent! But very superficial."}
reigate-squires	event_10_2	event	\N	Holmes describes age differences in the two writers	9	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_10", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_402", "sentence_403", "sentence_404", "sentence_405", "sentence_406", "sentence_407", "sentence_408"], "communicatesContent": "We come now, however, to a point which is of importance. You may not be aware that the deduction of a man's age from his writing is one which has been brought to considerable accuracy by experts. In normal cases one can place a man in his true decade with tolerable confidence. I say normal cases, because ill-health and physical weakness reproduce the signs of old age, even when the invalid is a youth. In this case, looking at the bold, strong hand of the one, and the rather broken-backed appearance of the other, which still retains its legibility although the t's have begun to lose their crossing, we can say that the one was a young man and the other was advanced in years without being positively decrepit. Excellent!"}
reigate-squires	event_10_3	event	\N	Holmes says the writers are blood-relatives and identifies them as the Cunninghams	9	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_10", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_409", "sentence_410", "sentence_411", "sentence_412", "sentence_413", "sentence_414", "sentence_415", "sentence_416"], "communicatesContent": "There is a further point, however, which is subtler and of greater interest. There is something in common between these hands. They belong to men who are blood-relatives. It may be most obvious to you in the Greek e's, but to me there are many small points which indicate the same thing. I have no doubt at all that a family mannerism can be traced in these two specimens of writing. I am only, of course, giving you the leading results now of my examination of the paper. There were twenty-three other deductions which would be of more interest to experts than to you. They all tend to deepen the impression upon my mind that the Cunninghams, father and son, had written this letter."}
reigate-squires	event_10_4	event	\N	Holmes reports that he visits the house with the Inspector and examines the scene	9	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_417", "sentence_418"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_10_5	event	\N	Holmes determines the shooting distance and rejects Alec Cunningham's account	9	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_419", "sentence_420", "sentence_421"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_10_6	event	\N	Holmes notes that father and son agree on the escape point and infers there was no unknown man	9	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_422", "sentence_423", "sentence_424"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_10_7	event	\N	Holmes explains how he investigates the motive through the burglary and lawsuit	9	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_10", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_425", "sentence_426", "sentence_427", "sentence_428"], "communicatesContent": "And now I have to consider the motive of this singular crime. To get at this, I endeavoured first of all to solve the reason of the original burglary at Mr. Acton's. I understood, from something which the Colonel told us, that a lawsuit had been going on between you, Mr. Acton, and the Cunninghams. Of course, it instantly occurred to me that they had broken into your library with the intention of getting at some document which might be of importance in the case."}
reigate-squires	event_11_1	event	\N	Mr. Acton states that the Cunninghams intended to cripple the case by destroying a paper	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": "old_acton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_430", "sentence_431"], "communicatesContent": "There can be no possible doubt as to their intentions. I have the clearest claim upon half of their present estate, and if they could have found a single paper—which, fortunately, was in the strong-box of my solicitors—they would undoubtedly have crippled our case."}
reigate-squires	event_11_2	event	\N	Holmes explains that the burglary attempt was reckless and influenced by young Alec	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_432", "sentence_433", "sentence_434"], "communicatesContent": "It was a dangerous, reckless attempt, in which I seem to trace the influence of young Alec. Having found nothing they tried to divert suspicion by making it appear to be an ordinary burglary, to which end they carried off whatever they could lay their hands upon."}
reigate-squires	event_11_6	event	\N	Holmes tells that the Cunninghams must not be reminded of the paper's existence	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_442", "sentence_443"], "communicatesContent": "It was, of course, of the very first importance that they should not be reminded of the existence of this paper, otherwise they would naturally destroy it without delay."}
reigate-squires	event_11_7	event	\N	Holmes pretends to have a fit and changes the conversation	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_443", "sentence_444", "sentence_445"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_11_8	event	\N	Holmes gets old Cunningham to write the word twelve for comparison	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_448", "sentence_449", "sentence_450", "sentence_451", "sentence_452"], "communicatesContent": "When I recovered I managed, by a device which had perhaps some little merit of ingenuity, to get old Cunningham to write the word 'twelve,' so that I might compare it with the 'twelve' upon the paper."}
reigate-squires	event_11_9	event	\N	Holmes and Watson go upstairs and Holmes slips back to examine the dressing-gown pockets	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_453", "sentence_454", "sentence_455", "sentence_456"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_11_10	event	\N	Holmes recounts that the Cunninghams attacked him when he took the paper	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_454", "sentence_455", "sentence_456"], "communicatesContent": "I had hardly got the paper, however—which was, as I had expected, in one of them—when the two Cunninghams were on me, and would, I verily believe, have murdered me then and there but for your prompt and friendly aid. As it is, I feel that young man's grip on my throat now, and the father has twisted my wrist round in the effort to get the paper out of my hand. They saw that I must know all about it, you see, and the sudden change from absolute security to complete despair made them perfectly desperate."}
reigate-squires	event_11_11	event	\N	Holmes speaks with old Cunningham about the motive of the crime	10	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_11", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_457", "sentence_458", "sentence_459", "sentence_460", "sentence_461", "sentence_462", "sentence_463"], "communicatesContent": "I had a little talk with old Cunningham afterwards as to the motive of the crime. He was tractable enough, though his son was a perfect demon, ready to blow out his own or anybody else's brains if he could have got to his revolver. When Cunningham saw that the case against him was so strong he lost all heart and made a clean breast of everything. It seems that William had secretly followed his two masters on the night when they made their raid upon Mr. Acton's, and having thus got them into his power, proceeded, under threats of exposure, to levy blackmail upon them. Mr. Alec, however, was a dangerous man to play games of that sort with. It was a stroke of positive genius on his part to see in the burglary scare which was convulsing the country side an opportunity of plausibly getting rid of the man whom he feared. William was decoyed up and shot, and had they only got the whole of the note and paid a little more attention to detail in the accessories, it is very possible that suspicion might never have been aroused."}
reigate-squires	event_12_1	event	\N	Holmes places the note before Watson and the others	11	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_12", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_466", "sentence_467"], "communicatesContent": null}
reigate-squires	event_12_2	event	\N	The note states instructions to meet at the east gate and keep silent	11	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_12", "speakerId": "paper_note", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_468", "sentence_469"], "communicatesContent": "If you will only come round at quarter to twelve to the east gate you will learn what will very much surprise you and maybe be of the greatest service to you and also to Annie Morrison. But say nothing to anyone upon the matter"}
reigate-squires	event_12_3	event	\N	Holmes remarks that the note is the sort of thing he expected	11	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_12", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_470"], "communicatesContent": "It is very much the sort of thing that I expected."}
reigate-squires	event_12_4	event	\N	Holmes states that the relations between Alec Cunningham, William Kirwan, and Annie Morrison are not yet known	11	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_12", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_471"], "communicatesContent": "Of course, we do not yet know what the relations may have been between Alec Cunningham, William Kirwan, and Annie Morrison."}
reigate-squires	event_12_5	event	\N	Holmes states that the trap was skillfully baited	11	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_12", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_472"], "communicatesContent": "The results shows that the trap was skillfully baited."}
reigate-squires	event_12_6	event	\N	Holmes states that the handwriting shows traces of heredity and missing i-dots	11	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_12", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_473", "sentence_474"], "communicatesContent": "I am sure that you cannot fail to be delighted with the traces of heredity shown in the p's and in the tails of the g's. The absence of the i-dots in the old man's writing is also most characteristic."}
reigate-squires	event_12_7	event	\N	Holmes says he will return invigorated to Baker Street the next day	11	{"section": "reigate-squires-section_12", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_475"], "communicatesContent": "Watson, I think our quiet rest in the country has been a distinct success, and I shall certainly return much invigorated to Baker Street to-morrow."}
