crooked-man	event_1_7	event	\N	Holmes greets Watson and says he hoped not to be too late	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_12"], "communicatesContent": "Ah, Watson, I hoped that I might not be too late to catch you."}
crooked-man	event_1_8	event	\N	Watson invites Holmes to come in	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_13"], "communicatesContent": "My dear fellow, pray come in."}
crooked-man	event_1_9	event	\N	Holmes comments on Watson's appearance and habits	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_14", "sentence_20"], "communicatesContent": "You look surprised, and no wonder! Relieved, too, I fancy! Hum! You still smoke the Arcadia mixture of your bachelor days then! There's no mistaking that fluffy ash upon your coat. It's easy to tell that you have been accustomed to wear a uniform, Watson. You'll never pass as a pure-bred civilian as long as you keep that habit of carrying your handkerchief in your sleeve."}
crooked-man	event_1_10	event	\N	Holmes asks whether he may stay for the night	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_21"], "communicatesContent": "Could you put me up to-night?"}
crooked-man	event_1_11	event	\N	Watson agrees to host Holmes	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_22", "sentence_25"], "communicatesContent": "With pleasure. I shall be delighted if you will stay."}
crooked-man	event_2_14	event	\N	Holmes states that the facts are two days old	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_73"], "communicatesContent": "The facts are only two days old."}
crooked-man	event_1_12	event	\N	Holmes remarks that Watson has no current visitor and mentions the hat-stand	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_23", "sentence_24"], "communicatesContent": "You told me that you had bachelor quarters for one, and I see that you have no gentleman visitor at present. Your hat-stand proclaims as much."}
crooked-man	event_1_13	event	\N	Holmes comments on the presence of a British workman and asks about the drains	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_28", "sentence_29", "sentence_30"], "communicatesContent": "Sorry to see that you've had the British workman in the house. He's a token of evil. Not the drains, I hope?"}
crooked-man	event_1_14	event	\N	Watson answers that the problem is the gas	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_31"], "communicatesContent": "No, the gas."}
crooked-man	event_1_15	event	\N	Holmes notes the boot marks on the linoleum	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_33"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_1_16	event	\N	Watson hands Holmes his pouch and Holmes smokes in silence	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_35"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_1_17	event	\N	Watson waits for Holmes to begin his business	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_36"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_1_18	event	\N	Holmes observes that Watson is professionally busy and explains his deduction	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_37", "sentence_38", "sentence_39", "sentence_40", "sentence_41", "sentence_42", "sentence_43", "sentence_44", "sentence_45", "sentence_46", "sentence_47", "sentence_48"], "communicatesContent": "I see that you are professionally rather busy just now. I have the advantage of knowing your habits, my dear Watson. When your round is a short one you walk, and when it is a long one you use a hansom. As I perceive that your boots, although used, are by no means dirty, I cannot doubt that you are at present busy enough to justify the hansom. Elementary."}
crooked-man	event_1_19	event	\N	Holmes says he has several threads of a strange case and will obtain the missing ones	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_49", "sentence_50"], "communicatesContent": "Now, at present I am in the position of these same readers, for I hold in this hand several threads of one of the strangest cases which ever perplexed a man's brain, and yet I lack the one or two which are needful to complete my theory. But I'll have them, Watson, I'll have them!"}
crooked-man	event_1_20	event	\N	Holmes's eyes kindle and his face briefly flushes before resuming composure	0	{"section": "crooked-man-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_51", "sentence_52", "sentence_53"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_2_1	event	\N	Holmes states that the problem has exceptional features of interest	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_54", "sentence_55"], "communicatesContent": "The problem presents features of interest, I may even say exceptional features of interest."}
crooked-man	event_2_2	event	\N	Holmes says he has investigated the matter and nearly reached a solution	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_56"], "communicatesContent": "I have already looked into the matter, and have come, as I think, within sight of my solution."}
crooked-man	event_2_3	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson to accompany him in the last step	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_57"], "communicatesContent": "If you could accompany me in that last step you might be of considerable service to me."}
crooked-man	event_2_4	event	\N	Watson agrees to accompany Holmes	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_58"], "communicatesContent": "I should be delighted."}
crooked-man	event_2_5	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Watson can go to Aldershot tomorrow	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_59"], "communicatesContent": "Could you go as far as Aldershot to-morrow?"}
crooked-man	event_2_6	event	\N	Watson says Jackson would take his practice	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_60"], "communicatesContent": "I have no doubt Jackson would take my practice."}
crooked-man	event_2_7	event	\N	Holmes gives Watson the departure time from Waterloo	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_61", "sentence_62", "sentence_63"], "communicatesContent": "Very good. 10 from Waterloo. the 11.10 from Waterloo."}
crooked-man	event_2_8	event	\N	Watson says the plan would give him time	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_64"], "communicatesContent": "That would give me time."}
crooked-man	event_2_9	event	\N	Holmes says he will sketch what has happened and what remains to be done	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_65"], "communicatesContent": "Then, if you are not too sleepy, I will give you a sketch of what has happened, and of what remains to be done."}
crooked-man	event_2_10	event	\N	Watson says he is now wakeful	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_66", "sentence_67"], "communicatesContent": "I was sleepy before you came. I am quite wakeful now."}
crooked-man	event_2_11	event	\N	Holmes says he will compress the story and that Watson may have read about the matter	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_68", "sentence_69"], "communicatesContent": "I will compress the story as far as may be done without omitting anything vital to the case. It is conceivable that you may even have read some account of the matter."}
crooked-man	event_2_12	event	\N	Holmes identifies the case as the supposed murder of Colonel Barclay at Aldershot	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_70"], "communicatesContent": "It is the supposed murder of Colonel Barclay, of the Royal Mallows, at Aldershot, which I am investigating."}
crooked-man	event_2_13	event	\N	Holmes says the matter has attracted little attention except locally	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_72"], "communicatesContent": "It has not excited much attention yet, except locally."}
crooked-man	event_2_15	event	\N	Holmes says the Royal Mallows is a famous Irish regiment	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_75"], "communicatesContent": "The Royal Mallows is, as you know, one of the most famous Irish regiments in the British army."}
naval-treaty	sentence_380	lexical	\N	\N	379	{"text": "\\"He was cold, but not harsh.", "section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "entities": []}
crooked-man	event_2_16	event	\N	Holmes says the Royal Mallows distinguished itself in the Crimea and the Mutiny	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_76"], "communicatesContent": "It did wonders both in the Crimea and the Mutiny, and has since that time distinguished itself upon every possible occasion."}
crooked-man	event_2_17	event	\N	Holmes says James Barclay commanded the regiment up to Monday night	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_77"], "communicatesContent": "It was commanded up to Monday night by James Barclay, a gallant veteran."}
crooked-man	event_2_18	event	\N	Holmes says Colonel Barclay married Nancy Devoy when he was a sergeant	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_78"], "communicatesContent": "Colonel Barclay had married at the time when he was a sergeant, and his wife, whose maiden name was Miss Nancy Devoy, was the daughter of a former colour-sergeant in the same corps."}
crooked-man	event_2_19	event	\N	Holmes says the young couple experienced social friction in their new surroundings	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_79"], "communicatesContent": "There was, therefore, some little social friction when the young couple found themselves in their new surroundings."}
crooked-man	event_2_20	event	\N	Holmes says the Barclays adapted themselves quickly	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_80"], "communicatesContent": "They appear, however, to have quickly adapted themselves."}
crooked-man	event_2_21	event	\N	Holmes says Mrs. Barclay was popular and strikingly beautiful	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_80", "sentence_81"], "communicatesContent": "Mrs. Barclay has always, I understand, been as popular with the ladies of the regiment as her husband was with his brother officers. She was a woman of great beauty, and even now she is still of a striking and queenly appearance."}
crooked-man	event_2_22	event	\N	Holmes says Colonel Barclay's family life was uniformly happy	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_82"], "communicatesContent": "Colonel Barclay's family life appears to have been a uniformly happy one."}
crooked-man	event_2_23	event	\N	Holmes says Major Murphy supplied most of the facts and reported no misunderstanding between the pair	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_83", "sentence_84", "sentence_85", "sentence_86", "sentence_87"], "communicatesContent": "Major Murphy, to whom I owe most of my facts, assures me that he has never heard of any misunderstanding between the pair. On the whole, he thinks that Barclay's devotion to his wife was greater than his wife's to Barclay. He was acutely uneasy if he were absent from her for a day. She, on the other hand, though devoted and faithful, was less obtrusively affectionate. But they were regarded in the regiment as the very model of a middle-aged couple."}
crooked-man	event_2_24	event	\N	Holmes says nothing in the Barclays' relations prepared people for the tragedy	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_88"], "communicatesContent": "There was absolutely nothing in their mutual relations to prepare people for the tragedy which was to follow."}
crooked-man	event_2_25	event	\N	Holmes says Colonel Barclay had violent and vindictive traits and periodic depression	1	{"section": "crooked-man-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_89", "sentence_90", "sentence_91", "sentence_92", "sentence_93", "sentence_94", "sentence_95", "sentence_96", "sentence_97"], "communicatesContent": "Colonel Barclay himself seems to have had some singular traits in his character. He was a dashing, jovial old soldier in his usual mood, but there were occasions on which he seemed to show himself capable of considerable violence and vindictiveness. Another fact was the singular sort of depression which came upon him at times. The smile had often been struck from his mouth, as if by some invisible hand, when he had been joining the gayeties and chaff of the mess-table. For days on end, when the mood was on him, he had been sunk in the deepest gloom. This and a certain tinge of superstition were the only unusual traits in his character which his brother officers had observed. The latter peculiarity took the form of a dislike to being left alone, especially after dark. This feature had often given rise to comment and conjecture."}
