crooked-man	event_8_8	event	\N	Simpson reports that Wood is inside	7	{"section": "crooked-man-section_8", "speakerId": "nancy", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_294", "sentence_295", "sentence_296", "sentence_297"], "communicatesContent": "He's in all right, Mr. Holmes"}
crooked-man	event_8_9	event	\N	Holmes sends in his card with a message that he has come on important business	7	{"section": "crooked-man-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_300"], "communicatesContent": "he had come on important business"}
crooked-man	event_8_10	event	\N	Holmes and Watson meet Henry Wood in his room	7	{"section": "crooked-man-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_300", "sentence_301", "sentence_302", "sentence_303"], "communicatesContent": "Mr. Henry Wood, late of India, I believe"}
crooked-man	event_8_11	event	\N	Holmes states that he has come about Colonel Barclay's death and asks Wood about it	7	{"section": "crooked-man-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_304", "sentence_305", "sentence_306", "sentence_307"], "communicatesContent": "I've come over this little matter of Colonel Barclay's death. That's what I want to ascertain."}
crooked-man	event_8_12	event	\N	Holmes says Mrs. Barclay may be tried for murder unless the matter is cleared up	7	{"section": "crooked-man-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_308", "sentence_309"], "communicatesContent": "unless the matter is cleared up, Mrs. Barclay, who is an old friend of yours, will in all probability be tried for murder"}
crooked-man	event_8_13	event	\N	Wood reacts strongly and asks Holmes whether he is with the police	7	{"section": "crooked-man-section_8", "speakerId": "henry_wood", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_309", "sentence_310", "sentence_311", "sentence_312", "sentence_313", "sentence_314"], "communicatesContent": "I don't know who you are, nor how you come to know what you do know, but will you swear that this is true that you tell me? My God! Are you in the police yourself?"}
crooked-man	event_8_14	event	\N	Holmes says it is every man's business to see justice done	7	{"section": "crooked-man-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_314", "sentence_315", "sentence_316"], "communicatesContent": "No. It's every man's business to see justice done."}
crooked-man	event_8_15	event	\N	Wood says Mrs. Barclay is innocent and Holmes replies that Wood is guilty	7	{"section": "crooked-man-section_8", "speakerId": "henry_wood", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_317", "sentence_318", "sentence_319"], "communicatesContent": "You can take my word that she is innocent."}
crooked-man	event_9_1	event	\N	Henry Wood recounts how Colonel Barclay was killed by providence	8	{"section": "crooked-man-section_9", "speakerId": "henry_wood", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_320", "sentence_321", "sentence_322", "sentence_323"], "communicatesContent": "It was a just providence that killed him. But, mind you this, that if I had knocked his brains out, as it was in my heart to do, he would have had no more than his due from my hands. If his own guilty conscience had not struck him down it is likely enough that I might have had his blood upon my soul."}
crooked-man	event_9_2	event	\N	Henry Wood says he will tell the story	8	{"section": "crooked-man-section_9", "speakerId": "henry_wood", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_324", "sentence_325", "sentence_326"], "communicatesContent": "You want me to tell the story. Well, I don't know why I shouldn't, for there's no cause for me to be ashamed of it. It was in this way, sir."}
crooked-man	event_10_17	event	\N	Harry Wood sees death on Colonel Barclay's face	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_367", "sentence_368"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_18	event	\N	Nancy faints and Harry Wood takes the door key from her hand	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_370"], "communicatesContent": null}
resident-patient	sentence_202	lexical	\N	\N	201	{"text": "We were conscious of a long scrutiny out of the darkness.", "section": "resident-patient-section_6", "entities": []}
crooked-man	event_9_3	event	\N	Henry Wood describes his past, Nancy Devoy, and the regiment in India	8	{"section": "crooked-man-section_9", "speakerId": "henry_wood", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_327", "sentence_328", "sentence_329", "sentence_330", "sentence_331", "sentence_332", "sentence_333"], "communicatesContent": "You see me now with my back like a camel and my ribs all awry, but there was a time when Corporal Henry Wood was the smartest man in the 117th Foot. We were in India then, in cantonments, at a place we'll call Bhurtee. Barclay, who died the other day, was sergeant in the same company as myself, and the belle of the regiment, ay, and the finest girl that ever had the breath of life between her lips, was Nancy Devoy, the daughter of the colour-sergeant. There were two men that loved her, and one that she loved, and it was for my good looks that she loved me. Well, though I had her heart, her father was set upon her marrying Barclay. I was a harum-scarum, reckless lad, and he had had an education, and was already marked for the sword-belt. But the girl held true to me, and it seemed that I would have had her when the Mutiny broke out, and all hell was loose in the country."}
crooked-man	event_9_4	event	\N	The regiment is shut up in Bhurtee during the Mutiny	8	{"section": "crooked-man-section_9", "speakerId": "henry_wood", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_334", "sentence_335", "sentence_336"], "communicatesContent": "We were shut up in Bhurtee, the regiment of us with half a battery of artillery, a company of Sikhs, and a lot of civilians and women-folk. There were ten thousand rebels round us. About the second week of it our water gave out, and it was a question whether we could communicate with General Neill's column, which was moving up country."}
crooked-man	event_9_5	event	\N	Henry Wood volunteers to warn General Neill	8	{"section": "crooked-man-section_9", "speakerId": "henry_wood", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_337", "sentence_338", "sentence_339", "sentence_340"], "communicatesContent": "It was our only chance, for we could not hope to fight our way out with all the women and children, so I volunteered to go out and to warn General Neill of our danger. My offer was accepted, and I talked it over with Sergeant Barclay, who was supposed to know the ground better than any other man, and who drew up a route by which I might get through the rebel lines. At ten o'clock the same night I started off upon my journey. There were a thousand lives to save, but it was of only one that I was thinking when I dropped over the wall that night."}
crooked-man	event_9_6	event	\N	Henry Wood is ambushed, stunned, and bound by enemy sentries	8	{"section": "crooked-man-section_9", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_341", "sentence_342"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_9_7	event	\N	Henry Wood learns that Barclay betrayed him through a native servant	8	{"section": "crooked-man-section_9", "speakerId": "henry_wood", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_343"], "communicatesContent": "But the real blow was to my heart and not to my head, for as I came to and listened to as much as I could understand of their talk, I heard enough to tell me that my comrade, the very man who had arranged the way that I was to take, had betrayed me by means of a native servant into the hands of the enemy."}
crooked-man	event_10_1	event	\N	General Neill relieves Bhurtee	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_346"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_2	event	\N	Rebels take Harry Wood away during their retreat	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_346"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_3	event	\N	Harry Wood is tortured, tries to escape, is captured, and is tortured again	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_347"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_4	event	\N	Harry Wood is left in a crippled state	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_348"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_5	event	\N	Some fugitives take Harry Wood into Nepaul and later he goes past Darjeeling	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_349"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_6	event	\N	Hill-folk kill the rebels and Harry Wood becomes their slave	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_350"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_7	event	\N	Harry Wood escapes and travels north to the Afghans	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_350"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_8	event	\N	Harry Wood wanders among the Afghans for many years	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_350", "sentence_351"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_9	event	\N	Harry Wood returns to the Punjab and lives among the natives	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_351"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_10	event	\N	Harry Wood earns a living by performing conjuring tricks	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_351"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_11	event	\N	Harry Wood comes back to England	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_359", "sentence_360"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_12	event	\N	Harry Wood arrives where the soldiers are and amuses them for money	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_360"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_13	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes recounts the meeting with Mrs. Barclay and the recognition	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_361", "sentence_362"], "communicatesContent": "I have already heard of your meeting with Mrs. Barclay, and your mutual recognition."}
crooked-man	event_10_14	event	\N	Holmes describes Wood following Mrs. Barclay home and seeing an altercation through the window	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_363"], "communicatesContent": "You then, as I understand, followed her home and saw through the window an altercation between her husband and her."}
crooked-man	event_10_15	event	\N	Holmes describes Wood running across the lawn and breaking in upon them	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_364"], "communicatesContent": "Your own feelings overcame you, and you ran across the lawn and broke in upon them."}
crooked-man	event_10_16	event	\N	Harry Wood enters the room and Colonel Barclay collapses dead	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_365", "sentence_366"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	sentence_2245	lexical	\N	\N	2244	{"text": "Chapter II The Bodymaster", "section": "section_51", "entities": []}
crooked-man	event_10_19	event	\N	Harry Wood decides to leave without unlocking the door and flees	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_371", "sentence_373"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_20	event	\N	Harry Wood pockets the key, drops his stick, and chases Teddy	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_372"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_21	event	\N	Harry Wood puts Teddy back into its box	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_373"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_10_22	event	\N	The man reveals Teddy from a hutch in the corner	9	{"section": "crooked-man-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_376", "sentence_377"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_11_1	event	\N	The man identifies the animal as a mongoose and gives alternative names for it	10	{"section": "crooked-man-section_11", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_378", "sentence_379", "sentence_380", "sentence_381"], "communicatesContent": "It's a mongoose. Well, some call them that, and some call them ichneumon. Snake-catcher is what I call them, and Teddy is amazing quick on cobras. I have one here without the fangs, and Teddy catches it every night to please the folk in the canteen."}
crooked-man	event_11_2	event	\N	The speaker offers to come forward again if Mrs. Barclay is in serious trouble	10	{"section": "crooked-man-section_11", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_382", "sentence_383", "sentence_384", "sentence_385", "sentence_386"], "communicatesContent": "Any other point, sir? Well, we may have to apply to you again if Mrs. Barclay should prove to be in serious trouble. In that case, of course, I'd come forward. But if not, there is no object in raking up this scandal against a dead man, foully as he has acted. You have at least the satisfaction of knowing that for thirty years of his life his conscience bitterly reproached him for this wicked deed."}
crooked-man	event_11_3	event	\N	Holmes and Watson overtake Major Murphy on the street	10	{"section": "crooked-man-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_387", "sentence_388", "sentence_389", "sentence_390"], "communicatesContent": null}
crooked-man	event_11_4	event	\N	Major Murphy says the affair has come to nothing and explains the inquest result	10	{"section": "crooked-man-section_11", "speakerId": "major_murphy", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_391", "sentence_392", "sentence_393", "sentence_394", "sentence_395"], "communicatesContent": "I suppose you have heard that all this fuss has come to nothing? The inquest is just over. The medical evidence showed conclusively that death was due to apoplexy. You see it was quite a simple case after all."}
crooked-man	event_11_5	event	\N	Holmes dismisses the case as superficial and says they will not be wanted in Aldershot	10	{"section": "crooked-man-section_11", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_396", "sentence_397"], "communicatesContent": "Oh, remarkably superficial. Come, Watson, I don't think we shall be wanted in Aldershot any more."}
crooked-man	event_11_6	event	\N	Watson asks Holmes about the names David, James, and Henry	10	{"section": "crooked-man-section_11", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_398", "sentence_399"], "communicatesContent": "If the husband's name was James, and the other was Henry, what was this talk about David?"}
crooked-man	event_11_7	event	\N	Holmes explains that David was a term of reproach and compares it to a biblical story	10	{"section": "crooked-man-section_11", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_400", "sentence_401", "sentence_402", "sentence_403", "sentence_404", "sentence_405"], "communicatesContent": "That one word, my dear Watson, should have told me the whole story had I been the ideal reasoner which you are so fond of depicting. It was evidently a term of reproach. Yes; David strayed a little occasionally, you know, and on one occasion in the same direction as Sergeant James Barclay. You remember the small affair of Uriah and Bathsheba? My biblical knowledge is a trifle rusty, I fear, but you will find the story in the first or second of Samuel."}
