greek-interpreter	event_1_1	event	\N	Watson narrates his acquaintance with Holmes and Holmes's reticence about his relations and early life	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3", "sentence_4", "sentence_5"], "communicatesContent": "During my long and intimate acquaintance with Mr. Sherlock Holmes I had never heard him refer to his relations, and hardly ever to his own early life."}
greek-interpreter	event_1_2	event	\N	Holmes begins talking to Watson about his brother	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_6"], "communicatesContent": "he began to talk to me about his brother"}
greek-interpreter	event_1_3	event	\N	Watson and Holmes discuss whether singular gifts come from ancestry or early training	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_7", "sentence_8", "sentence_9", "sentence_10"], "communicatesContent": "from all that you have told me, it seems obvious that your faculty of observation and your peculiar facility for deduction are due to your own systematic training."}
greek-interpreter	event_1_4	event	\N	Holmes states that his ancestors were country squires and that his turn for observation may have come from his grandmother	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_11", "sentence_12", "sentence_13"], "communicatesContent": "My ancestors were country squires, who appear to have led much the same life as is natural to their class. But, none the less, my turn that way is in my veins, and may have come with my grandmother, who was the sister of Vernet, the French artist. Art in the blood is liable to take the strangest forms."}
greek-interpreter	event_1_5	event	\N	Watson asks Holmes how he knows the trait is hereditary	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_14"], "communicatesContent": "But how do you know that it is hereditary?"}
greek-interpreter	event_1_6	event	\N	Holmes states that Mycroft possesses the trait in a larger degree	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_15"], "communicatesContent": "Because my brother Mycroft possesses it in a larger degree than I do."}
greek-interpreter	event_1_7	event	\N	Watson remarks that Holmes's answer is news to him	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_16"], "communicatesContent": null}
greek-interpreter	event_1_8	event	\N	Watson questions why such a man is unknown in England	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_17", "sentence_18"], "communicatesContent": "If there were another man with such singular powers in England, how was it that neither police nor public had heard of him?"}
greek-interpreter	event_1_9	event	\N	Holmes rejects the suggestion that modesty explains his remark	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_19", "sentence_20", "sentence_21", "sentence_22"], "communicatesContent": "My dear Watson, I cannot agree with those who rank modesty among the virtues. To the logician all things should be seen exactly as they are, and to underestimate one's self is as much a departure from truth as to exaggerate one's own powers. When I say, therefore, that Mycroft has better powers of observation than I, you may take it that I am speaking the exact and literal truth."}
greek-interpreter	event_1_10	event	\N	Watson asks whether Mycroft is Holmes's junior	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_23"], "communicatesContent": "Is he your junior?"}
greek-interpreter	event_1_11	event	\N	Holmes states that Mycroft is seven years his senior	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_24"], "communicatesContent": "Seven years my senior."}
greek-interpreter	event_1_12	event	\N	Watson asks how Mycroft comes to be unknown	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_25"], "communicatesContent": "How comes it that he is unknown?"}
greek-interpreter	event_1_13	event	\N	Holmes says that Mycroft is well known in his own circle	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_26", "sentence_27", "sentence_28"], "communicatesContent": "Oh, he is very well known in his own circle. Well, in the Diogenes Club, for example."}
greek-interpreter	event_1_14	event	\N	Holmes checks the time and invites Watson for a stroll to meet Mycroft and the club	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_29", "sentence_30", "sentence_31", "sentence_32"], "communicatesContent": "The Diogenes Club is the queerest club in London, and Mycroft one of the queerest men. He's always there from quarter to five to twenty to eight. It's six now, so if you care for a stroll this beautiful evening I shall be very happy to introduce you to two curiosities."}
greek-interpreter	event_1_15	event	\N	Watson and Holmes walk in the street toward Regent's Circus	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_33"], "communicatesContent": null}
greek-interpreter	event_1_16	event	\N	Holmes states that Mycroft does not use his powers for detective work because he is incapable of it	0	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_34", "sentence_35"], "communicatesContent": "You wonder why it is that Mycroft does not use his powers for detective work. He is incapable of it."}
greek-interpreter	event_2_1	event	\N	Mycroft Holmes explains his superiority in observation and deduction	1	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_2", "speakerId": "mycroft_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_37", "sentence_38", "sentence_39", "sentence_40", "sentence_41", "sentence_42"], "communicatesContent": "I said that he was my superior in observation and deduction. If the art of the detective began and ended in reasoning from an armchair, my brother would be the greatest criminal agent that ever lived. But he has no ambition and no energy. He will not even go out of his way to verify his own solutions, and would rather be considered wrong than take the trouble to prove himself right. Again and again I have taken a problem to him, and have received an explanation which has afterwards proved to be the correct one. And yet he was absolutely incapable of working out the practical points which must be gone into before a case could be laid before a judge or jury."}
greek-interpreter	event_2_2	event	\N	Mycroft Holmes states that detective work is not his profession	1	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_2", "speakerId": "mycroft_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_43", "sentence_44", "sentence_45", "sentence_46"], "communicatesContent": "It is not his profession, then? By no means. What is to me a means of livelihood is to him the merest hobby of a dilettante. He has an extraordinary faculty for figures, and audits the books in some of the government departments."}
greek-interpreter	event_2_3	event	\N	Mycroft Holmes describes his daily routine and the Diogenes Club	1	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_2", "speakerId": "mycroft_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_47", "sentence_48", "sentence_50", "sentence_51", "sentence_52", "sentence_53", "sentence_54", "sentence_55", "sentence_56"], "communicatesContent": "Mycroft lodges in Pall Mall, and he walks round the corner into Whitehall every morning and back every evening. From year's end to year's end he takes no other exercise, and is seen nowhere else, except only in the Diogenes Club, which is just opposite his rooms. Very likely not. There are many men in London, you know, who, some from shyness, some from misanthropy, have no wish for the company of their fellows. Yet they are not averse to comfortable chairs and the latest periodicals. It is for the convenience of these that the Diogenes Club was started, and it now contains the most unsociable and unclubable men in town. No member is permitted to take the least notice of any other one. Save in the Stranger's Room, no talking is, under any circumstances, allowed, and three offences, if brought to the notice of the committee, render the talker liable to expulsion. My brother was one of the founders, and I have myself found it a very soothing atmosphere."}
greek-interpreter	event_2_4	event	\N	Holmes and Watson arrive at Pall Mall and enter the club hall	1	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_57", "sentence_58", "sentence_59"], "communicatesContent": "cautioning me not to speak"}
greek-interpreter	event_2_5	event	\N	Holmes leaves Watson briefly and returns with Mycroft Holmes	1	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_60"], "communicatesContent": null}
greek-interpreter	event_11_21	event	\N	Holmes holds that finding the Grecian girl would reveal how the wrongs of her and her brother were avenged	10	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_447"], "communicatesContent": null}
greek-interpreter	event_2_6	event	\N	Mycroft Holmes greets Watson and Sherlock Holmes	1	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_2", "speakerId": "mycroft_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_64", "sentence_65"], "communicatesContent": "I am glad to meet you, sir. I hear of Sherlock everywhere since you became his chronicler."}
greek-interpreter	event_2_7	event	\N	Mycroft Holmes refers to the Manor House case and says he expected Sherlock Holmes to consult him	1	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_2", "speakerId": "mycroft_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_66", "sentence_67"], "communicatesContent": "By the way, Sherlock, I expected to see you round last week, to consult me over that Manor House case. I thought you might be a little out of your depth."}
greek-interpreter	event_2_8	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes states that he solved the Manor House case	1	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_68", "sentence_69"], "communicatesContent": "No, I solved it. It was Adams, of course."}
greek-interpreter	event_3_1	event	\N	The two men sit down together in the club bow-window	2	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_72"], "communicatesContent": null}
greek-interpreter	event_3_2	event	\N	Mycroft points out the passing men and asks Sherlock Holmes what he makes of the other man	2	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_3", "speakerId": "mycroft_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_73", "sentence_74", "sentence_75", "sentence_76", "sentence_77", "sentence_78"], "communicatesContent": "To any one who wishes to study mankind this is the spot. Look at the magnificent types! Look at these two men who are coming towards us, for example. Precisely. What do you make of the other?"}
greek-interpreter	event_3_3	event	\N	The two men stop opposite the window	2	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_79"], "communicatesContent": null}
greek-interpreter	event_3_4	event	\N	Holmes identifies the first passerby as a billiard-marker and the second as a soldier with other attributes	2	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_80", "sentence_81", "sentence_82", "sentence_83", "sentence_84", "sentence_85", "sentence_86", "sentence_87", "sentence_88", "sentence_89", "sentence_90", "sentence_91", "sentence_92", "sentence_93", "sentence_94", "sentence_95", "sentence_96", "sentence_97", "sentence_98", "sentence_99", "sentence_100", "sentence_101", "sentence_102"], "communicatesContent": "An old soldier, I perceive. Served in India. Royal Artillery, I fancy. And a widower. But with a child."}
greek-interpreter	event_3_5	event	\N	Mycroft receives snuff and brushes grains from his coat	2	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_104"], "communicatesContent": null}
greek-interpreter	event_3_6	event	\N	Mycroft says he has had a singular problem submitted to his judgment and can leave the rest to Mr. Melas	2	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_3", "speakerId": "mycroft_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_105", "sentence_106", "sentence_107", "sentence_108", "sentence_109", "sentence_110", "sentence_111", "sentence_112", "sentence_113", "sentence_114"], "communicatesContent": "I have had something quite after your own heart—a most singular problem—submitted to my judgment. I really had not the energy to follow it up save in a very incomplete fashion, but it gave me a basis for some pleasing speculation. If you would care to hear the facts— My dear Mycroft, I should be delighted. I have asked Mr. Melas to step across. He lodges on the floor above me, and I have some slight acquaintance with him, which led him to come to me in his perplexity. Mr. Melas is a Greek by extraction, as I understand, and he is a remarkable linguist. He earns his living partly as interpreter in the law courts and partly by acting as guide to any wealthy Orientals who may visit the Northumberland Avenue hotels. I think I will leave him to tell his very remarkable experience in his own fashion."}
greek-interpreter	event_3_7	event	\N	Mr. Melas joins the group and greets Sherlock Holmes eagerly	2	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_3", "speakerId": "mr_melas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_115", "sentence_116"], "communicatesContent": "[greeting and eager response to Holmes's hearing his story]"}
greek-interpreter	event_4_1	event	\N	Mr. Melas identifies the time and places the incident on Monday night	3	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_4", "speakerId": "mr_melas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_121", "sentence_122"], "communicatesContent": "This is Wednesday evening. It was Monday night—only two days ago—that all this happened."}
greek-interpreter	event_4_2	event	\N	Mr. Melas explains that he works as an interpreter	3	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_4", "speakerId": "mr_melas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_123", "sentence_124", "sentence_125", "sentence_126"], "communicatesContent": "I am an interpreter. I interpret all languages—or nearly all—but as I am a Greek by birth and with a Grecian name, I am principally associated with Greek. For many years I have been the chief Greek interpreter in London, and my name is very well known in the hotels. It happens not unfrequently that I am sent for at strange hours by foreigners who get into difficulties, or by travelers who arrive late and wish my services."}
greek-interpreter	event_4_3	event	\N	Mr. Latimer comes to Mr. Melas and asks him to accompany him	3	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_4", "speakerId": "latimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_127"], "communicatesContent": "He asks Mr. Melas to accompany him in a cab."}
greek-interpreter	event_4_4	event	\N	Latimer explains that a Greek friend needs an interpreter	3	{"section": "greek-interpreter-section_4", "speakerId": "latimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_128"], "communicatesContent": "A Greek friend has come to see him upon business, and because the friend can speak nothing but his own tongue, the services of an interpreter are indispensable."}
