naval-treaty	event_1_7	event	\N	Percy Phelps writes a letter to Watson asking him to bring Holmes down to see him	0	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_1", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_17", "sentence_18", "sentence_21", "sentence_23", "sentence_24", "sentence_25", "sentence_27", "sentence_30"], "communicatesContent": "I have no doubt that you can remember \\"Tadpole\\" Phelps, who was in the fifth form when you were in the third. It is possible even that you may have heard that through my uncle's influence I obtained a good appointment at the Foreign Office, and that I was in a situation of trust and honour until a horrible misfortune came suddenly to blast my career. In the event of your acceding to my request it is probable that I shall have to narrate them to you. I have only just recovered from nine weeks of brain-fever, and am still exceedingly weak. Do you think that you could bring your friend Mr. Holmes down to see me? I should like to have his opinion of the case, though the authorities assure me that nothing more can be done. Do try to bring him down, and as soon as possible. Assure him that if I have not asked his advice sooner it was not because I did not appreciate his talents, but because I have been off my head ever since the blow fell. Now I am clear again, though I dare not think of it too much for fear of a relapse. I am still so weak that I have to write, as you see, by dictating. Do try to bring him."}
naval-treaty	event_2_1	event	\N	Watson and Holmes meet in Holmes's rooms as Holmes works on a chemical investigation	1	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_34", "sentence_35", "sentence_36", "sentence_37"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_2_2	event	\N	Holmes tests a solution with litmus paper and it turns crimson	1	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_38", "sentence_39", "sentence_40", "sentence_41", "sentence_42", "sentence_43", "sentence_44", "sentence_45", "sentence_46"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_2_3	event	\N	Holmes writes several telegrams and hands them to the page-boy	1	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_47", "sentence_48", "sentence_49"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_2_4	event	\N	Watson hands Holmes the letter and Holmes reads it	1	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_55", "sentence_56", "sentence_57", "sentence_58", "sentence_59", "sentence_60", "sentence_61", "sentence_62", "sentence_63", "sentence_64", "sentence_65", "sentence_66"], "communicatesContent": "It does not tell us very much, does it? Hardly anything. And yet the writing is of interest. But the writing is not his own. Precisely. It is a woman's. No, a woman's, and a woman of rare character. You see, at the commencement of an investigation it is something to know that your client is in close contact with some one who, for good or evil, has an exceptional nature. My interest is already awakened in the case."}
naval-treaty	event_2_5	event	\N	Holmes and Watson depart for Woking by early train	1	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_67", "sentence_68"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_2_6	event	\N	Watson and Holmes arrive at Briarbrae in Woking	1	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_68", "sentence_69"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_2_7	event	\N	Watson and Holmes are shown into the drawing-room and received by Percy Phelps	1	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_70", "sentence_71", "sentence_72"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_2_8	event	\N	Percy Phelps speaks about his family and his difficulty	1	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_2", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_73", "sentence_74", "sentence_75"], "communicatesContent": "I am so glad that you have come. Percy has been inquiring for you all morning. Ah, poor old chap, he clings to any straw! His father and his mother asked me to see you, for the mere mention of the subject is very painful to them."}
naval-treaty	event_3_1	event	\N	Holmes remarks that there are no details yet	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_76"], "communicatesContent": "We have had no details yet."}
naval-treaty	event_3_2	event	\N	Holmes says Watson is not a member of the family	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_77"], "communicatesContent": "I perceive that you are not yourself a member of the family."}
naval-treaty	event_3_3	event	\N	Their acquaintance looks surprised and then laughs	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_78"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_4	event	\N	Joseph Harrison speaks about the monogram on his locket	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "joseph_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_79"], "communicatesContent": "monogram on my locket"}
naval-treaty	event_3_5	event	\N	Joseph Harrison says Holmes had done something clever	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "joseph_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_80"], "communicatesContent": "For a moment I thought you had done something clever."}
naval-treaty	event_3_6	event	\N	Joseph Harrison identifies himself and his relation to Percy and Annie	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "joseph_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_81"], "communicatesContent": "Joseph Harrison is my name, and as Percy is to marry my sister Annie I shall at least be a relation by marriage."}
naval-treaty	event_3_7	event	\N	Joseph Harrison says his sister is in Percy's room and has nursed him	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "joseph_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_82"], "communicatesContent": "You will find my sister in his room, for she has nursed him hand-and-foot this two months back."}
naval-treaty	event_3_8	event	\N	Joseph Harrison urges entering at once because Percy is impatient	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "joseph_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_83", "sentence_84"], "communicatesContent": "Perhaps we'd better go in at once, for I know how impatient he is."}
naval-treaty	event_3_9	event	\N	They are shown into a chamber on the same floor as the drawing-room	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_85"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_10	event	\N	The chamber is furnished as a sitting room and bedroom	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_86"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_11	event	\N	A pale young man lies on a sofa by the open window	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_87"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_12	event	\N	A woman sits beside the invalid and rises as they enter	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_88"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_13	event	\N	Annie Harrison asks whether she should leave Percy	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "annie_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_89", "sentence_90"], "communicatesContent": "Shall I leave, Percy?"}
naval-treaty	event_3_14	event	\N	Percy Phelps clutches Annie's hand to detain her	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_91"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_15	event	\N	Percy Phelps greets Watson and recognizes Holmes	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_92", "sentence_93", "sentence_94", "sentence_95"], "communicatesContent": "How are you, Watson? I should never have known you under that moustache, and I daresay you would not be prepared to swear to me. This I presume is your celebrated friend, Mr. Sherlock Holmes?"}
naval-treaty	event_3_16	event	\N	Watson introduces Holmes	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_96"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_17	event	\N	The stout young man leaves and the sister remains with the invalid	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_97"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_18	event	\N	Annie Harrison is described as striking-looking with dark features	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_98"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_19	event	\N	Percy Phelps appears white-faced and haggard beside Annie	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_99"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_3_20	event	\N	Percy Phelps begins his account	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_100", "sentence_101", "sentence_102"], "communicatesContent": "I won't waste your time. I'll plunge into the matter without further preamble. I was a happy and successful man, Mr. Holmes, and on the eve of being married, when a sudden and dreadful misfortune wrecked all my prospects in life."}
naval-treaty	event_3_21	event	\N	Percy Phelps describes his Foreign Office career and his uncle's trust	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_103", "sentence_104"], "communicatesContent": "I was, as Watson may have told you, in the Foreign Office, and through the influences of my uncle, Lord Holdhurst, I rose rapidly to a responsible position. When my uncle became foreign minister in this administration he gave me several missions of trust, and as I always brought them to a successful conclusion, he came at last to have the utmost confidence in my ability and tact."}
naval-treaty	event_3_22	event	\N	Lord Holdhurst gives Percy a new commission of trust involving the treaty	2	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_3", "speakerId": "lord_holdhurst", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_105", "sentence_106", "sentence_107", "sentence_108", "sentence_109", "sentence_110", "sentence_111", "sentence_112", "sentence_113", "sentence_114"], "communicatesContent": "This is the original of that secret treaty between England and Italy of which some rumours have already got into the public press. It is of enormous importance that nothing further should leak out. The French or the Russian embassy would pay an immense sum to learn the contents of these papers. They should not leave my bureau were it not absolutely necessary to have them copied. You have a desk in your office? Then take the treaty and lock it up there. I shall give directions that you may remain behind when the others go, so that you may copy it at your leisure without fear of being overlooked. When you have finished, relock both the original and the draft in the desk, and hand them over to me personally to-morrow morning."}
naval-treaty	event_4_1	event	\N	Holmes interrupts the account and asks whether the speaker was alone during the conversation.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_116", "sentence_117"], "communicatesContent": "Were you alone during this conversation?"}
naval-treaty	event_4_2	event	\N	The speaker states that he was absolutely alone.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_118"], "communicatesContent": "Absolutely."}
naval-treaty	event_4_3	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the conversation took place in a large room.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_119"], "communicatesContent": "Were you in a large room?"}
naval-treaty	event_4_4	event	\N	The speaker gives the room's dimensions.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_120"], "communicatesContent": "Thirty feet each way."}
naval-treaty	event_4_5	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the speaker was in the centre.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_121"], "communicatesContent": "In the centre?"}
naval-treaty	event_4_6	event	\N	The speaker answers that he was about the centre.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_122"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, about it."}
naval-treaty	event_4_7	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the conversation was spoken in low voices.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_123"], "communicatesContent": "And speaking low?"}
naval-treaty	event_4_8	event	\N	The speaker states that his uncle's voice is always remarkably low and that he hardly spoke at all.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_124", "sentence_125"], "communicatesContent": "My uncle's voice is always remarkably low. I hardly spoke at all."}
naval-treaty	event_4_9	event	\N	Holmes pauses the account and tells the speaker to continue.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_126"], "communicatesContent": "Thank you, pray go on."}
naval-treaty	event_4_10	event	\N	The speaker waits until the other clerks leave, leaves Charles Gorot in his room, and goes out to dine.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_127", "sentence_128"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_11	event	\N	Charles Gorot leaves the room before the speaker returns.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_129"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_12	event	\N	The speaker hurries his work because Joseph Harrison is in town and is going to travel to Woking by the eleven o'clock train.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_130"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_13	event	\N	The speaker examines the treaty and sees that it is highly important.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_131", "sentence_132", "sentence_133", "sentence_134"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_14	event	\N	The speaker glances over the treaty and starts copying it.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_135", "sentence_136"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_15	event	\N	The speaker copies nine articles by nine o'clock and finds it hopeless to catch his train.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_137"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_16	event	\N	The speaker feels drowsy and wants coffee.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_138", "sentence_139"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_17	event	\N	The speaker rings the bell to summon the commissionnaire.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_140", "sentence_141"], "communicatesContent": "A bell summons the commissionnaire."}
naval-treaty	event_4_18	event	\N	The commissionnaire's wife answers the summons and explains that she is the commissionnaire's wife and that she does the charing.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": "commissionnaires_wife", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_142", "sentence_143"], "communicatesContent": "I am the commissionnaire's wife, who does the charing."}
naval-treaty	event_4_19	event	\N	The speaker orders coffee from the commissionnaire's wife.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_143"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_20	event	\N	The speaker writes two more articles, rises, and walks up and down the room.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_144"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_21	event	\N	The speaker opens the door and goes down the corridor to find the cause of the delay.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_145", "sentence_146", "sentence_147", "sentence_148", "sentence_149", "sentence_150"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_22	event	\N	The speaker enters the hall and finds the commissionnaire asleep with the kettle boiling on the spirit-lamp.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_156"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_23	event	\N	The speaker removes the kettle and blows out the lamp because water is spurting over the floor.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_157"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_4_24	event	\N	The speaker reaches out to shake the sleeping commissionnaire, and a bell rings overhead, waking him.	3	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_158"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_1	event	\N	Percy Phelps explains that he came down to see if his coffee was ready	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_161"], "communicatesContent": "I came down to see if my coffee was ready."}
naval-treaty	event_5_2	event	\N	Phelps looks at Watson and then at the bell with astonishment	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_162", "sentence_163"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_3	event	\N	Phelps asks who rang the bell	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_164", "sentence_165"], "communicatesContent": "If you are here, sir, then who rang the bell?"}
naval-treaty	event_5_4	event	\N	Watson cries out and asks what bell it is	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_166", "sentence_167"], "communicatesContent": "What bell is it?"}
naval-treaty	event_5_5	event	\N	Phelps identifies the bell as the bell of the room Watson was working in	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_168"], "communicatesContent": "It's the bell of the room you were working in."}
naval-treaty	event_5_6	event	\N	Watson realizes someone is in the room where the treaty lay	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_169", "sentence_170"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_7	event	\N	Watson runs upstairs and along the passage	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_171"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_8	event	\N	Watson finds no one in the corridors or the room and sees the papers missing from the desk	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_172", "sentence_173", "sentence_174", "sentence_175"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_9	event	\N	Holmes sits up and rubs his hands	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_176"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_10	event	\N	Holmes asks what Watson did next	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_178", "sentence_179"], "communicatesContent": "Pray, what did you do then?"}
naval-treaty	event_5_11	event	\N	Watson states that the thief must have come up the stairs from the side door	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_180", "sentence_181"], "communicatesContent": "I recognised in an instant that the thief must have come up the stairs from the side door."}
naval-treaty	event_5_12	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the thief could have been concealed in the room or corridor	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_182", "sentence_183", "sentence_184", "sentence_185"], "communicatesContent": "You were satisfied that he could not have been concealed in the room all the time, or in the corridor which you have just described as dimly lighted?"}
naval-treaty	event_5_13	event	\N	Holmes thanks Watson and asks him to proceed	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_186", "sentence_187"], "communicatesContent": "Thank you. Pray proceed."}
naval-treaty	event_5_14	event	\N	The commissionnaire follows Watson upstairs after noticing his pale face	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_188"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_15	event	\N	Watson and the commissionnaire rush along the corridor and down the steps to Charles Street	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_189", "sentence_190"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_16	event	\N	Watson and the commissionnaire open the door and rush out into Charles Street	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_191"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_17	event	\N	A nearby clock chimes three times and Watson places the time at quarter to ten	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_192", "sentence_193"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_18	event	\N	Holmes marks the time as important in his note	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_194"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_19	event	\N	Watson and the commissionnaire run along Whitehall in the dark rain	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_195", "sentence_196", "sentence_197"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_5_20	event	\N	Watson reports a robbery and stolen Foreign Office document to the policeman	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_198", "sentence_199", "sentence_200"], "communicatesContent": "A robbery has been committed. A document of immense value has been stolen from the Foreign Office. Has any one passed this way?"}
naval-treaty	event_5_21	event	\N	The policeman says only one person has passed during the last quarter hour, a tall elderly woman with a Paisley shawl	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "police_officer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_201"], "communicatesContent": "I have been standing here for a quarter of an hour, sir, only one person has passed during that time—a woman, tall and elderly, with a Paisley shawl."}
naval-treaty	event_5_22	event	\N	The commissionnaire identifies the woman as his wife and asks whether anyone else passed	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "commissionnaire", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_202", "sentence_203"], "communicatesContent": "Ah, that is only my wife; has no one else passed?"}
naval-treaty	event_5_23	event	\N	The commissionnaire says the thief must have gone the other way and urges Watson to go to the other end of the street	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "commissionnaire", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_204", "sentence_215", "sentence_216"], "communicatesContent": "Then it must be the other way that the thief took. You're only wasting your time, sir, and every minute now is of importance; take my word for it that my old woman has nothing to do with it, and come down to the other end of the street."}
naval-treaty	event_5_24	event	\N	Watson remains unconvinced by the commissionnaire's attempts to draw him away	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_205", "sentence_206", "sentence_207"], "communicatesContent": "How long ago was it? Where did the woman go?"}
naval-treaty	event_5_25	event	\N	The policeman answers Watson's questions about the woman	4	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_5", "speakerId": "police_officer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_208", "sentence_209", "sentence_210", "sentence_211", "sentence_212", "sentence_213", "sentence_214"], "communicatesContent": "I don't know, sir. I noticed her pass, but I had no special reason for watching her. She seemed to be in a hurry. Oh, not very many minutes. Well, it could not be more than five."}
naval-treaty	event_6_1	event	\N	Watson catches a man by the sleeve and asks where he lives.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_218", "sentence_220", "sentence_221"], "communicatesContent": "Where do you live?"}
naval-treaty	event_6_2	event	\N	Phelps gives his address.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_221"], "communicatesContent": "16 Ivy Lane, Brixton"}
naval-treaty	event_6_3	event	\N	Watson advises Phelps not to follow a false scent and to go to the other end of the street.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_222", "sentence_223"], "communicatesContent": "But don't let yourself be drawn away upon a false scent, Mr. Phelps. Come to the other end of the street and let us see if we can hear of anything."}
naval-treaty	event_6_4	event	\N	Watson, Phelps, and a policeman hurry down the street.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_224", "sentence_225"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_6_5	event	\N	They find the street full of traffic and people going about in the wet night.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_225", "sentence_226"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_6_6	event	\N	They return to the office and search the stairs and passage without result.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_227"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_6_7	event	\N	They examine the corridor and find no footmarks.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_228", "sentence_229"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_6_8	event	\N	Phelps states that it has been raining since about seven.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_230", "sentence_231"], "communicatesContent": "Since about seven."}
naval-treaty	event_6_9	event	\N	Watson asks how the woman who entered at about nine left no muddy traces.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_232"], "communicatesContent": "How is it, then, that the woman who came into the room about nine left no traces with her muddy boots?"}
naval-treaty	event_6_10	event	\N	Phelps explains the charwomen's practice of changing boots for list slippers.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_233", "sentence_234", "sentence_235"], "communicatesContent": "The charwomen are in the habit of taking off their boots at the commissionnaire's office, and putting on list slippers."}
naval-treaty	event_6_11	event	\N	Watson asks whether there were no marks despite the wet night.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_236", "sentence_237", "sentence_238"], "communicatesContent": "There were no marks, then, though the night was a wet one?"}
naval-treaty	event_6_12	event	\N	Watson asks what was done next.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_239"], "communicatesContent": "What did you do next?"}
naval-treaty	event_6_13	event	\N	They examine the room and find no possible secret entrance, trap-door, or inner foothold for entry.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_240", "sentence_241", "sentence_242", "sentence_243", "sentence_244"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_6_14	event	\N	Watson asks about the fireplace.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_245"], "communicatesContent": "How about the fireplace?"}
naval-treaty	event_6_15	event	\N	Phelps says they use no fireplace and that there is a stove.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_246", "sentence_247"], "communicatesContent": "They use none. There is a stove."}
naval-treaty	event_6_16	event	\N	Phelps describes the bell-rope and says the bell-ringer must have come up to the desk.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_248", "sentence_249", "sentence_250", "sentence_251"], "communicatesContent": "The bell-rope hangs from the wire just to the right of my desk. Whoever rang it must have come right up to the desk to do it. But why should any criminal wish to ring the bell? It is a most insoluble mystery."}
naval-treaty	event_6_17	event	\N	Watson asks what traces the intruder left in the room.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_252", "sentence_253", "sentence_254"], "communicatesContent": "What were your next steps? You examined the room, I presume, to see if the intruder had left any traces—any cigar-end or dropped glove or hairpin or other trifle?"}
naval-treaty	event_6_18	event	\N	Phelps says there was nothing of the sort and no smell was noticed.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_255", "sentence_256", "sentence_257", "sentence_258", "sentence_259", "sentence_260"], "communicatesContent": "There was nothing of the sort. We never thought of that. I never smoke myself, so I think I should have observed it if there had been any smell of tobacco. There was absolutely no clue of any kind."}
naval-treaty	event_6_19	event	\N	Phelps identifies Mrs. Tangey's hasty departure as the only tangible fact.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_261", "sentence_262"], "communicatesContent": "The only tangible fact was that the commissionnaire's wife—Mrs. Tangey was the name—had hurried out of the place. He could give no explanation save that it was about the time when the woman always went home."}
naval-treaty	event_6_20	event	\N	Watson and the policeman decide to seize Mrs. Tangey before she can dispose of the papers.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_263"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_6_21	event	\N	The alarm reaches Scotland Yard and Mr. Forbes takes up the case.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_264"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_6_22	event	\N	Watson and Phelps hire a hansom and travel to the given address.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_265"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_6_23	event	\N	A young woman opens the door and is identified as Mrs. Tangey's eldest daughter.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_266"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_6_24	event	\N	Watson and Phelps are shown into the front room to wait.	5	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_267"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_1	event	\N	The group opens the kitchen door and enters the kitchen, finding the woman there first	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_271"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_2	event	\N	The woman speaks to the men and identifies Percival Phelps	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": "commissionnaires_wife", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_272", "sentence_273", "sentence_274"], "communicatesContent": "isn't Mr. Phelps, of the office!"}
naval-treaty	event_7_3	event	\N	Forbes accuses the woman of taking an important paper and orders her to return to Scotland Yard	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": "mr_forbes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_278", "sentence_279", "sentence_280"], "communicatesContent": "We have reason to believe that you have taken a paper of importance from the Foreign Office, and that you ran in here to dispose of it. You must come back with us to Scotland Yard to be searched."}
naval-treaty	event_7_4	event	\N	The woman protests and resists arrest	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_281"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_5	event	\N	The group drives back to Scotland Yard in a four-wheeler	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_282", "sentence_285"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_6	event	\N	The kitchen and kitchen fire are examined for the missing papers	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_283", "sentence_284"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_7	event	\N	The woman is handed to the female searcher at Scotland Yard	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_285"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_8	event	\N	A report is brought back that the papers are not found	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_286", "sentence_287"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_8_18	event	\N	Percy Phelps and his nurse watch Holmes with surprise and disappointment	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_354"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_9	event	\N	Percival Phelps collapses into distress over the failed recovery of the treaty	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_288", "sentence_289", "sentence_290", "sentence_291", "sentence_292", "sentence_295", "sentence_298", "sentence_299", "sentence_300", "sentence_301"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_10	event	\N	Officials soothe Percival Phelps and accompany him to Waterloo	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_301", "sentence_302", "sentence_303"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_11	event	\N	Dr. Ferrier takes charge of Percival Phelps on the train and at home he suffers a fit	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_304", "sentence_305"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_12	event	\N	Annie Harrison, his mother, and Dr. Ferrier care for Percival Phelps during a long illness	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_306", "sentence_307", "sentence_308", "sentence_309", "sentence_310", "sentence_311", "sentence_312"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_7_13	event	\N	Percival Phelps wires Mr. Forbes about the case	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_314"], "communicatesContent": "wire to Mr. Forbes"}
naval-treaty	event_7_14	event	\N	Mr. Forbes comes out and reports that no trace of a clue has been found	6	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_7", "speakerId": "mr_forbes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_315", "sentence_316", "sentence_317", "sentence_318", "sentence_319", "sentence_320", "sentence_321"], "communicatesContent": "though everything has been done, no trace of a clue has been discovered"}
naval-treaty	event_8_1	event	\N	Percy Phelps lies back on his cushions while his nurse gives him medicine	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_322"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_8_2	event	\N	Holmes sits silently with his eyes closed in deep concentration	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_323"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_8_3	event	\N	Holmes asks whether anyone was told about the special task	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_324", "sentence_325", "sentence_326"], "communicatesContent": "Did you tell any one that you had this special task to perform?"}
naval-treaty	event_8_4	event	\N	Phelps says that he told no one	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_327"], "communicatesContent": "No one."}
naval-treaty	event_8_5	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Miss Harrison was told	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_328"], "communicatesContent": "Not Miss Harrison here, for example?"}
naval-treaty	event_8_6	event	\N	Phelps says he had not been back to Woking between receiving the order and executing the commission	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_329", "sentence_330"], "communicatesContent": "No. I had not been back to Woking between getting the order and executing the commission."}
naval-treaty	event_8_7	event	\N	Holmes asks whether any of Phelps's people had come to see him	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_331", "sentence_332"], "communicatesContent": "And none of your people had by chance been to see you?"}
naval-treaty	event_8_8	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Phelps's people knew their way around the office	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_333"], "communicatesContent": "Did any of them know their way about in the office?"}
naval-treaty	event_8_9	event	\N	Phelps says that all of his people had been shown over the office	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_334"], "communicatesContent": "Oh, yes, all of them had been shown over it."}
naval-treaty	event_8_10	event	\N	Holmes says the questions are irrelevant if no one was told about the treaty	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_335", "sentence_336"], "communicatesContent": "Still, of course, if you said nothing to any one about the treaty these inquiries are irrelevant."}
naval-treaty	event_8_11	event	\N	Holmes asks about the commissionnaire	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_337"], "communicatesContent": "Do you know anything of the commissionnaire?"}
naval-treaty	event_8_12	event	\N	Phelps says the commissionnaire is an old soldier	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_338"], "communicatesContent": "Nothing except that he is an old soldier."}
naval-treaty	event_8_13	event	\N	Holmes asks what regiment the commissionnaire served in	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_339"], "communicatesContent": "What regiment?"}
naval-treaty	event_8_14	event	\N	Phelps says the commissionnaire was in the Coldstream Guards	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_340"], "communicatesContent": "Oh, I have heard—Coldstream Guards."}
naval-treaty	event_8_15	event	\N	Holmes says he can get details from Forbes and comments on authorities and facts	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_341", "sentence_342", "sentence_343"], "communicatesContent": "Thank you. I have no doubt I can get details from Forbes. The authorities are excellent at amassing facts, though they do not always use them to advantage."}
naval-treaty	event_8_16	event	\N	Holmes admires a rose and walks to the open window	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_344", "sentence_345"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_8_17	event	\N	Holmes speaks at length about religion, deduction, and flowers	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_347", "sentence_348", "sentence_349", "sentence_350", "sentence_351", "sentence_352", "sentence_353"], "communicatesContent": "There is nothing in which deduction is so necessary as in religion. It can be built up as an exact science by the reasoner. Our highest assurance of the goodness of Providence seems to me to rest in the flowers. All other things, our powers our desires, our food, are all really necessary for our existence in the first instance. But this rose is an extra. Its smell and its colour are an embellishment of life, not a condition of it. It is only goodness which gives extras, and so I say again that we have much to hope from the flowers."}
naval-treaty	event_8_19	event	\N	Holmes falls into a reverie holding the moss-rose	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_355", "sentence_356"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_8_20	event	\N	Miss Harrison asks whether Holmes sees any prospect of solving the mystery	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "miss_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_357", "sentence_358"], "communicatesContent": "Do you see any prospect of solving this mystery, Mr. Holmes?"}
naval-treaty	event_8_21	event	\N	Holmes says he will look into the matter and report any points that strike him	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_359", "sentence_360", "sentence_361"], "communicatesContent": "Oh, the mystery! Well, it would be absurd to deny that the case is a very abstruse and complicated one, but I can promise you that I will look into the matter and let you know any points which may strike me."}
naval-treaty	event_8_22	event	\N	Miss Harrison asks whether Holmes sees any clue	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "miss_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_362"], "communicatesContent": "Do you see any clue?"}
naval-treaty	event_8_23	event	\N	Holmes says the clues furnished must be tested	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_363"], "communicatesContent": "You have furnished me with seven, but, of course, I must test them before I can pronounce upon their value."}
naval-treaty	event_8_24	event	\N	Miss Harrison asks whether Holmes suspects someone	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "miss_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_364"], "communicatesContent": "You suspect some one?"}
naval-treaty	event_8_25	event	\N	Holmes says he suspects himself of coming to conclusions too rapidly	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_365", "sentence_366", "sentence_367"], "communicatesContent": "I suspect myself. Of coming to conclusions too rapidly."}
naval-treaty	event_8_26	event	\N	Miss Harrison advises Holmes to go to London and test his conclusions	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "miss_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_368"], "communicatesContent": "Then go to London and test your conclusions."}
naval-treaty	event_8_27	event	\N	Holmes agrees with Watson and advises Phelps not to indulge false hopes	7	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_369", "sentence_370", "sentence_371", "sentence_372"], "communicatesContent": "Your advice is very excellent, Miss Harrison. I think, Watson, we cannot do better. Do not allow yourself to indulge in false hopes, Mr. Phelps. The affair is a very tangled one."}
naval-treaty	event_9_1	event	\N	The diplomatist says he will be in a fever until he sees the others again	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_373"], "communicatesContent": "I shall be in a fever until I see you again."}
naval-treaty	event_9_2	event	\N	The diplomatist says he will come out by the same train tomorrow	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_374"], "communicatesContent": "I'll come out by the same train to-morrow, though it's more than likely that my report will be a negative one."}
naval-treaty	event_9_3	event	\N	Our client thanks the diplomatist for promising to come	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_375"], "communicatesContent": "God bless you for promising to come."}
naval-treaty	event_9_4	event	\N	Our client says that news of action gives him fresh life	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_376"], "communicatesContent": "It gives me fresh life to know that something is being done."}
naval-treaty	event_9_5	event	\N	Our client mentions that he has received a letter from Lord Holdhurst	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_377"], "communicatesContent": "By the way, I have had a letter from Lord Holdhurst."}
naval-treaty	event_9_6	event	\N	Our client reports that Lord Holdhurst was cold but not harsh	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_378", "sentence_379", "sentence_380", "sentence_381", "sentence_382"], "communicatesContent": "He was cold, but not harsh. He repeated that the matter was of the utmost importance, and added that no steps would be taken about my future—by which he means, of course, my dismissal—until my health was restored and I had an opportunity of repairing my misfortune."}
naval-treaty	event_9_7	event	\N	Holmes says the letter was reasonable and considerate	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_383"], "communicatesContent": "Well, that was reasonable and considerate."}
naval-treaty	event_9_8	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson they will go to town for a day's work	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_384"], "communicatesContent": "Come, Watson, for we have a good day's work before us in town."}
naval-treaty	event_9_9	event	\N	Mr. Joseph Harrison drives Holmes and Watson to the station	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_385"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_9_10	event	\N	Holmes and Watson travel by train toward Portsmouth	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_385", "sentence_386"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_9_11	event	\N	Holmes remarks on the London view from the train	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_387", "sentence_388", "sentence_389", "sentence_390", "sentence_391", "sentence_392", "sentence_393"], "communicatesContent": "It's a very cheery thing to come into London by any of these lines which run high, and allow you to look down upon the houses like this. Look at those big, isolated clumps of building rising up above the slates, like brick islands in a lead-coloured sea. The board-schools. Light-houses, my boy! Beacons of the future! Capsules with hundreds of bright little seeds in each, out of which will spring the wise, better England of the future."}
naval-treaty	event_9_12	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Phelps drinks	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_394"], "communicatesContent": "I suppose that man Phelps does not drink?"}
naval-treaty	event_9_13	event	\N	Watson replies that he should not think so	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_395"], "communicatesContent": "I should not think so."}
naval-treaty	event_9_14	event	\N	Holmes says they must consider every possibility and describes Phelps as in deep water	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_396", "sentence_397"], "communicatesContent": "Nor should I, but we are bound to take every possibility into account. The poor devil has certainly got himself into very deep water, and it's a question whether we shall ever be able to get him ashore."}
naval-treaty	event_9_15	event	\N	Holmes asks what Watson thought of Miss Harrison	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_398"], "communicatesContent": "What did you think of Miss Harrison?"}
naval-treaty	event_9_16	event	\N	Watson describes Miss Harrison as a girl of strong character	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_399"], "communicatesContent": "A girl of strong character."}
naval-treaty	event_9_17	event	\N	Holmes says Miss Harrison is a good sort and describes her family	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_400", "sentence_401", "sentence_402", "sentence_403"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, but she is a good sort, or I am mistaken. She and her brother are the only children of an iron-master somewhere up Northumberland way. He got engaged to her when traveling last winter, and she came down to be introduced to his people, with her brother as escort. Then came the smash, and she stayed on to nurse her lover, while brother Joseph, finding himself pretty snug, stayed on too."}
naval-treaty	event_9_18	event	\N	Holmes says he has made independent inquiries and that the day must be one of inquiries	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_404", "sentence_405"], "communicatesContent": "I've been making a few independent inquiries, you see. But to-day must be a day of inquiries."}
naval-treaty	event_9_19	event	\N	Watson begins to mention his practice	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_406"], "communicatesContent": "My practice—"}
naval-treaty	event_9_20	event	\N	Holmes responds with asperity	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_407"], "communicatesContent": "Oh, if you find your own cases more interesting than mine—"}
naval-treaty	event_9_21	event	\N	Watson says his practice can get along for a day or two	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_408"], "communicatesContent": "I was going to say that my practice could get along very well for a day or two, since it is the slackest time in the year."}
naval-treaty	event_9_22	event	\N	Holmes agrees and says they will work on the matter together	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_409", "sentence_410", "sentence_411", "sentence_412"], "communicatesContent": "Excellent. Then we'll look into this matter together. I think that we should begin by seeing Forbes. He can probably tell us all the details we want until we know from what side the case is to be approached."}
naval-treaty	event_9_23	event	\N	Watson asks whether Holmes has a clue	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_413"], "communicatesContent": "You said you had a clue?"}
naval-treaty	event_9_24	event	\N	Holmes says they have several clues and discusses possible profiting parties	8	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_9", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_414", "sentence_415", "sentence_416", "sentence_417", "sentence_418", "sentence_419", "sentence_420"], "communicatesContent": "Well, we have several, but we can only test their value by further inquiry. The most difficult crime to track is the one which is purposeless. Now this is not purposeless. Who is it who profits by it? There is the French ambassador, there is the Russian, there is whoever might sell it to either of these, and there is Lord Holdhurst. Lord Holdhurst! Well, it is just conceivable that a statesman might find himself in a position where he was not sorry to have such a document accidentally destroyed."}
naval-treaty	event_10_1	event	\N	Holmes and Watson prepare to see Lord Holdhurst and Holmes has inquiries started	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_423", "sentence_424"], "communicatesContent": "We shall see the noble lord to-day and find out if he can tell us anything. Meanwhile I have already set inquiries on foot."}
naval-treaty	event_10_2	event	\N	Holmes sends wires from Woking station to every evening paper in London	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_426", "sentence_427"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_10_3	event	\N	Holmes hands Watson a torn note-book sheet containing an advertisement	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_428", "sentence_429", "sentence_430", "sentence_431"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_10_4	event	\N	Watson asks whether the thief came in a cab and Holmes answers	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_433", "sentence_434", "sentence_435", "sentence_436", "sentence_437"], "communicatesContent": "You are confident that the thief came in a cab?"}
naval-treaty	event_10_5	event	\N	Holmes identifies the cab as one of the clues and speculates about the bell	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_439", "sentence_440", "sentence_441", "sentence_442", "sentence_443", "sentence_444", "sentence_445", "sentence_446", "sentence_447"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_10_6	event	\N	Holmes and Watson arrive at Scotland Yard and meet Forbes	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_448", "sentence_449", "sentence_450"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_10_7	event	\N	Forbes speaks frigidly to Holmes and Watson	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": "mr_forbes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_450", "sentence_451", "sentence_452"], "communicatesContent": "I've heard of your methods before now, Mr. Holmes. You are ready enough to use all the information that the police can lay at your disposal, and then you try to finish the case yourself and bring discredit on them."}
naval-treaty	event_10_8	event	\N	Holmes replies about his recent cases and tells Forbes to work with him	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_453", "sentence_454"], "communicatesContent": "On the contrary, out of my last fifty-three cases my name has only appeared in four, and the police have had all the credit in forty-nine. I don't blame you for not knowing this, for you are young and inexperienced, but if you wish to get on in your new duties you will work with me and not against me."}
naval-treaty	event_10_9	event	\N	Forbes asks what steps have been taken	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": "mr_forbes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_455", "sentence_456", "sentence_457"], "communicatesContent": "I'd be very glad of a hint or two. I've certainly had no credit from the case so far. What steps have you taken?"}
naval-treaty	event_10_10	event	\N	Forbes reports that Tangey has been shadowed and comments on Tangey and his wife	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": "mr_forbes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_458", "sentence_459", "sentence_460", "sentence_461"], "communicatesContent": "Tangey, the commissionnaire, has been shadowed. He left the Guards with a good character and we can find nothing against him. His wife is a bad lot, though. I fancy she knows more about this than appears."}
naval-treaty	event_10_11	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Mrs. Tangey has been shadowed	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_462", "sentence_463", "sentence_464"], "communicatesContent": "Have you shadowed her?"}
naval-treaty	event_10_12	event	\N	Forbes says a woman was set on Mrs. Tangey and got nothing from her	9	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_10", "speakerId": "mr_forbes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_463", "sentence_464"], "communicatesContent": "We have set one of our women on to her. Mrs. Tangey drinks, and our woman has been with her twice when she was well on, but she could get nothing out of her."}
naval-treaty	event_11_1	event	\N	Holmes and Watson leave the office and go to interview Lord Holdhurst	10	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_497", "sentence_498", "sentence_499", "sentence_500", "sentence_501"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_11_2	event	\N	Holmes and Watson are shown into Lord Holdhurst's chambers	10	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_501", "sentence_502"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_11_3	event	\N	Lord Holdhurst receives Holmes and Watson courteously and seats them	10	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_502", "sentence_503"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_11_4	event	\N	Lord Holdhurst says Holmes's name is familiar and asks whose interest he represents	10	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_11", "speakerId": "lord_holdhurst", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_504", "sentence_505", "sentence_506", "sentence_507"], "communicatesContent": "Your name is very familiar to me, Mr. Holmes. ... In whose interest are you acting, may I ask?"}
naval-treaty	event_11_5	event	\N	Holmes says he is acting for Mr. Percy Phelps	10	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_11", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_508"], "communicatesContent": "In that of Mr. Percy Phelps."}
naval-treaty	event_11_6	event	\N	Lord Holdhurst identifies Percy Phelps as his unfortunate nephew and says he cannot screen him	10	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_11", "speakerId": "lord_holdhurst", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_509", "sentence_510", "sentence_511"], "communicatesContent": "Ah, my unfortunate nephew! You can understand that our kinship makes it the more impossible for me to screen him in any way. I fear that the incident must have a very prejudicial effect upon his career."}
naval-treaty	event_11_7	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the document will change matters if it is found	10	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_11", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_512", "sentence_513"], "communicatesContent": "But if the document is found?"}
naval-treaty	event_11_8	event	\N	Lord Holdhurst says he will give any information in his power	10	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_11", "speakerId": "lord_holdhurst", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_514", "sentence_515"], "communicatesContent": "I shall be happy to give you any information in my power."}
naval-treaty	event_11_9	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the instructions for copying the document were given in the room	10	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_11", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_516"], "communicatesContent": "Was it in this room that you gave your instructions as to the copying of the document?"}
naval-treaty	event_12_1	event	\N	Holmes and Watson travel to Woking together	11	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_12", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_556", "sentence_557"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_12_2	event	\N	Holmes reports that his inquiry has yielded no result and that he has seen Forbes and Percy Phelps's uncle	11	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_558", "sentence_565", "sentence_566"], "communicatesContent": "My report, as I expected, is a negative one. I have seen Forbes, and I have seen your uncle, and I have set one or two trains of inquiry upon foot which may lead to something."}
naval-treaty	event_12_3	event	\N	Holmes speaks with Lord Holdhurst about the treaty and the thief	11	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_12", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_520", "sentence_524", "sentence_529", "sentence_530", "sentence_533", "sentence_535", "sentence_537", "sentence_539", "sentence_540", "sentence_541", "sentence_542", "sentence_544", "sentence_545", "sentence_547", "sentence_548", "sentence_549"], "communicatesContent": "Did you ever mention to any one that it was your intention to give any one the treaty to be copied? There is another very important point which I wish to discuss with you. You feared, as I understand, that very grave results might follow from the details of this treaty becoming known. If the treaty had reached, let us say, the French or Russian Foreign Office, you would expect to hear of it? Since nearly ten weeks have elapsed, then, and nothing has been heard, it is not unfair to suppose that for some reason the treaty has not reached them. Perhaps he is waiting for a better price. If he waits a little longer he will get no price at all. The treaty will cease to be secret in a few months. Of course, it is a possible supposition that the thief has had a sudden illness. I did not say so."}
naval-treaty	event_12_4	event	\N	Lord Holdhurst answers Holmes's questions and bows the visitors out	11	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_12", "speakerId": "lord_holdhurst", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_521", "sentence_523", "sentence_526", "sentence_531", "sentence_532", "sentence_534", "sentence_536", "sentence_538", "sentence_546", "sentence_549"], "communicatesContent": "Never. Absolutely. Very grave results indeed. Not yet. I should. We can hardly suppose, Mr. Holmes, that the thief took the treaty in order to frame it and hang it up. An attack of brain-fever, for example? Every success to your investigation, be the criminal who it may."}
naval-treaty	event_12_5	event	\N	Holmes and Watson go to see Percy Phelps at Briarbrae, Woking	11	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_12", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_557", "sentence_561", "sentence_562"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_12_6	event	\N	Percy Phelps asks whether there is any news	11	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_12", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_563", "sentence_564"], "communicatesContent": "Any news?"}
naval-treaty	event_13_1	event	\N	Phelps says he has more to tell than his visitors have for him	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_572"], "communicatesContent": "We have more to tell you than you have for us."}
naval-treaty	event_13_2	event	\N	Phelps sits down on the couch	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_572"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_3	event	\N	Phelps reports an adventure during the night	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_574"], "communicatesContent": "We have had an adventure during the night, and one which might have proved to be a serious one."}
naval-treaty	event_13_4	event	\N	Phelps describes a fear of conspiracy and attack on his life and honour	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_576"], "communicatesContent": "I begin to believe that I am the unconscious centre of some monstrous conspiracy, and that my life is aimed at as well as my honour."}
naval-treaty	event_13_5	event	\N	Holmes asks Phelps to hear the account	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_581"], "communicatesContent": "Pray let me hear it."}
naval-treaty	event_13_6	event	\N	Phelps says he slept without a nurse in the room for the first time	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_582", "sentence_583"], "communicatesContent": "Last night was the very first night that I have ever slept without a nurse in the room. I was so much better that I thought I could dispense with one."}
naval-treaty	event_13_7	event	\N	Phelps says he had a night-light burning	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_584"], "communicatesContent": "I had a night-light burning."}
naval-treaty	event_13_8	event	\N	Phelps hears a slight noise and listens	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_585", "sentence_586"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_9	event	\N	Phelps hears a metallic snick from the window	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_587"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_10	event	\N	Phelps sits up in amazement	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_588"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_11	event	\N	An instrument is forced through the slit between the sashes and the catch is pressed back	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_590"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_12	event	\N	A pause lasts about ten minutes while the intruder waits	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_591"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_13	event	\N	The window is slowly opened	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_592"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_14	event	\N	Phelps springs out of bed and opens the shutters	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_594"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_15	event	\N	Phelps sees a man crouching at the window	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_595"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_16	event	\N	The man flees from the window	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_596"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_17	event	\N	The man is wrapped in a cloak covering the lower part of his face	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_597"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_18	event	\N	The man holds a weapon in his hand	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_598", "sentence_599", "sentence_600"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_19	event	\N	Holmes says the account is most interesting and asks what Phelps did next	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_601", "sentence_602"], "communicatesContent": "This is most interesting. Pray what did you do then?"}
naval-treaty	event_13_20	event	\N	Phelps would have followed the intruder through the open window if stronger	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_603"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_21	event	\N	Phelps rings the bell and rouses the house	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_604", "sentence_605"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_22	event	\N	Phelps shouts and Joseph comes down and rouses the others	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_606"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_23	event	\N	Joseph and the groom find marks on the bed outside the window	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_607"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_24	event	\N	Joseph and the groom fail to follow the trail across the grass because the weather has been dry	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_607"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_25	event	\N	A place on the wooden fence shows signs that someone got over it and snapped the top rail	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_608"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_13_26	event	\N	Phelps says he has not yet told the local police	12	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_13", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_609"], "communicatesContent": "I have said nothing to the local police yet, for I thought I had best have your opinion first."}
naval-treaty	event_14_1	event	\N	Holmes rises and paces about the room in excitement	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_611"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_3	event	\N	There is a sudden return	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_868"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_2	event	\N	Phelps smiles and remarks that misfortunes never come single	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_612"], "communicatesContent": "Misfortunes never come single."}
naval-treaty	event_14_3	event	\N	Holmes says Phelps has had his share	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_613"], "communicatesContent": "You have certainly had your share."}
naval-treaty	event_14_4	event	\N	Phelps asks whether the others can walk round the house with him	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_614"], "communicatesContent": "Do you think you could walk round the house with me?"}
naval-treaty	event_14_5	event	\N	Miss Harrison says she would like sunshine and that Joseph will come too	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "miss_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_615", "sentence_616"], "communicatesContent": "Oh, yes, I should like a little sunshine. Joseph will come, too."}
naval-treaty	event_14_6	event	\N	Holmes declines to let Miss Harrison come and instructs her to remain sitting	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_617", "sentence_618", "sentence_619"], "communicatesContent": "I am afraid not. I think I must ask you to remain sitting exactly where you are."}
naval-treaty	event_14_7	event	\N	Miss Harrison resumes her seat displeased	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_620"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_8	event	\N	The group sets off together	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_621"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_9	event	\N	The group passes round the lawn to the outside of the bedroom window	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_622"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_10	event	\N	Holmes examines the blurred marks on the bed	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_623", "sentence_624", "sentence_625"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_11	event	\N	Holmes says no one could make much of the marks	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_625"], "communicatesContent": "I don't think any one could make much of this."}
naval-treaty	event_14_12	event	\N	Holmes suggests going round the house to see why the burglar chose the room	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_626", "sentence_627"], "communicatesContent": "Let us go round the house and see why this particular room was chosen by the burglar. I should have thought those larger windows of the drawing-room and dining-room would have had more attractions for him."}
naval-treaty	event_14_13	event	\N	Joseph Harrison suggests the larger windows are more visible from the road	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "joseph_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_628"], "communicatesContent": "They are more visible from the road."}
naval-treaty	event_14_14	event	\N	Holmes asks about a door and what it is for	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_629", "sentence_630", "sentence_631"], "communicatesContent": "Ah, yes, of course. There is a door here which he might have attempted. What is it for?"}
naval-treaty	event_14_15	event	\N	Joseph Harrison explains that the side entrance is for trades-people and is locked at night	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "joseph_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_632", "sentence_633"], "communicatesContent": "It is the side entrance for trades-people. Of course it is locked at night."}
naval-treaty	event_14_16	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the house has ever had an alarm like this before	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_634", "sentence_635"], "communicatesContent": "Have you ever had an alarm like this before?"}
naval-treaty	event_14_17	event	\N	Phelps answers that there has never been one	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_635"], "communicatesContent": "Never."}
naval-treaty	event_14_18	event	\N	Holmes asks whether plate or anything valuable is kept in the house	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_636", "sentence_637"], "communicatesContent": "Do you keep plate in the house, or anything to attract burglars?"}
naval-treaty	event_14_19	event	\N	Phelps answers that there is nothing of value	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_637"], "communicatesContent": "Nothing of value."}
naval-treaty	event_14_20	event	\N	Holmes strolled round the house in an unusual negligent manner	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_638"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_21	event	\N	Holmes asks Joseph Harrison about the place where the intruder scaled the fence	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_639", "sentence_640"], "communicatesContent": "By the way, you found some place, I understand, where the fellow scaled the fence. Let us have a look at that!"}
naval-treaty	event_14_22	event	\N	Joseph Harrison leads the group to the cracked fence rail	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_641"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_23	event	\N	Holmes removes and examines the hanging wood fragment	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_642", "sentence_643"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_24	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the damage was done last night and says it looks old	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_644", "sentence_645", "sentence_646"], "communicatesContent": "Do you think that was done last night? It looks rather old, does it not?"}
naval-treaty	event_14_25	event	\N	Holmes notes that there are no marks of anyone jumping down on the other side and turns back	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_647", "sentence_648", "sentence_649"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_26	event	\N	Percy Phelps walks slowly while leaning on his future brother-in-law's arm	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_650"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_27	event	\N	Holmes reaches the open bedroom window before the others	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_651"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_28	event	\N	Holmes orders Miss Harrison to stay where she is all day and to lock the bedroom door on the outside at bedtime	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_652", "sentence_653", "sentence_654", "sentence_656", "sentence_657", "sentence_658", "sentence_659", "sentence_660", "sentence_661", "sentence_662", "sentence_663", "sentence_664", "sentence_665"], "communicatesContent": "Miss Harrison, you must stay where you are all day. Let nothing prevent you from staying where you are all day. It is of the utmost importance. When you go to bed lock the door of this room on the outside and keep the key. Promise to do this. He will come to London with us. It is for his sake. You can serve him. Quick! Promise!"}
naval-treaty	event_14_29	event	\N	Miss Harrison assents with a nod	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_665"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_14_30	event	\N	Joseph Harrison asks Annie why she is moping and invites her into the sunshine	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "joseph_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_666", "sentence_667", "sentence_668"], "communicatesContent": "Why do you sit moping there, Annie? Come out into the sunshine!"}
naval-treaty	event_14_31	event	\N	Miss Harrison refuses and says the room is cool and soothing	13	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_14", "speakerId": "miss_harrison", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_669", "sentence_670"], "communicatesContent": "No, thank you, Joseph. I have a slight headache and this room is deliciously cool and soothing."}
naval-treaty	event_15_1	event	\N	Holmes asks Phelps to come up to London with them	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_673", "sentence_674"], "communicatesContent": "It would be a very great help to me if you would come up to London with us."}
naval-treaty	event_15_2	event	\N	Holmes proposes that Phelps stay there to-night	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "mr_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_680", "sentence_681"], "communicatesContent": "Perhaps you would like me to stay there to-night?"}
naval-treaty	event_15_3	event	\N	Holmes arranges for Watson to look after Phelps	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_684", "sentence_685"], "communicatesContent": "My friend Watson is a medical man, you know, and he'll look after you."}
naval-treaty	event_15_4	event	\N	The group has lunch at the house before setting off for town	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_686", "sentence_687", "sentence_688"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_15_5	event	\N	Miss Harrison excuses herself from leaving the bedroom	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_687"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_15_6	event	\N	Holmes escorts Watson and Phelps to the station and into their carriage	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_689"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_15_7	event	\N	Holmes announces that he does not intend to leave Woking	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_689", "sentence_690"], "communicatesContent": "I have no intention of leaving Woking."}
naval-treaty	event_15_8	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson to drive to Baker Street with their friend and remain with him	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_692", "sentence_693", "sentence_694"], "communicatesContent": "When you reach London you would oblige me by driving at once to Baker Street with our friend here, and remaining with him until I see you again."}
naval-treaty	event_15_9	event	\N	Phelps asks about the London investigation	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "mr_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_695", "sentence_696"], "communicatesContent": "But how about our investigation in London?"}
naval-treaty	event_15_10	event	\N	Holmes says the London investigation can be done tomorrow	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_697", "sentence_698"], "communicatesContent": "We can do that to-morrow. I think that just at present I can be of more immediate use here."}
naval-treaty	event_15_11	event	\N	Phelps asks Holmes to tell people at Briarbrae that he hopes to return tomorrow night	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "mr_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_699"], "communicatesContent": "You might tell them at Briarbrae that I hope to be back to-morrow night."}
naval-treaty	event_15_12	event	\N	Holmes says he hardly expects to go back to Briarbrae	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_700"], "communicatesContent": "I hardly expect to go back to Briarbrae."}
naval-treaty	event_15_13	event	\N	Phelps and Watson discuss Holmes's conduct during the journey	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_701"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_15_14	event	\N	Phelps proposes that the burglary involved a burglar and not an ordinary thief	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "mr_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_702", "sentence_703"], "communicatesContent": "I suppose he wants to find out some clue as to the burglary last night, if a burglar it was. For myself, I don't believe it was an ordinary thief."}
naval-treaty	event_15_15	event	\N	Phelps states that he believes a political intrigue is directed against him	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "mr_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_704", "sentence_705", "sentence_706", "sentence_707", "sentence_708"], "communicatesContent": "Upon my word, you may put it down to my weak nerves or not, but I believe there is some deep political intrigue going on around me, and that for some reason that passes my understanding my life is aimed at by the conspirators. It sounds high-flown and absurd, but consider the facts! Why should a thief try to break in at a bedroom window, where there could be no hope of any plunder, and why should he come with a long knife in his hand?"}
naval-treaty	event_15_16	event	\N	Watson asks whether the intruder used a house-breaker's jimmy	14	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_15", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_708"], "communicatesContent": "You are sure it was not a house-breaker's jimmy?"}
naval-treaty	event_18_8	event	\N	Miss Harrison closes her book, fastens the shutters, and retires	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_814"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_16_1	event	\N	Watson says he has seen Holmes do remarkable things	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_725"], "communicatesContent": "I have seen him do some remarkable things."}
naval-treaty	event_16_2	event	\N	Watson says he has known Holmes solve questions with fewer clues	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_727"], "communicatesContent": "I have known him solve questions which presented fewer clues than yours."}
naval-treaty	event_16_3	event	\N	Watson says Holmes has acted on behalf of three reigning houses of Europe	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_730"], "communicatesContent": "To my certain knowledge he has acted on behalf of three of the reigning houses of Europe in very vital matters."}
naval-treaty	event_16_4	event	\N	Phelps asks whether Holmes is hopeful	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_731", "sentence_732", "sentence_733", "sentence_734"], "communicatesContent": "But you know him well, Watson. He is such an inscrutable fellow that I never quite know what to make of him. Do you think he is hopeful? Do you think he expects to make a success of it?"}
naval-treaty	event_16_5	event	\N	Watson says Holmes has said nothing	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_735"], "communicatesContent": "He has said nothing."}
naval-treaty	event_16_6	event	\N	Phelps says Holmes is taciturn when on a scent	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_736", "sentence_737", "sentence_738"], "communicatesContent": "That is a bad sign. On the contrary, I have noticed that when he is off the trail he generally says so. It is when he is on a scent and is not quite absolutely sure yet that it is the right one that he is most taciturn."}
naval-treaty	event_16_7	event	\N	Watson urges Phelps to go to bed and be fresh for tomorrow	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_739"], "communicatesContent": "Now, my dear fellow, we can't help matters by making ourselves nervous about them, so let me implore you to go to bed and so be fresh for whatever may await us to-morrow."}
naval-treaty	event_16_8	event	\N	Watson persuades Phelps to take his advice and go to bed	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_740"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_16_9	event	\N	Watson lies awake brooding over the problem	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_741", "sentence_742", "sentence_743", "sentence_744", "sentence_745"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_16_10	event	\N	Watson wakes and goes to Phelps's room	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_746"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_16_11	event	\N	Phelps asks whether Holmes has arrived	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_747"], "communicatesContent": "whether Holmes had arrived yet."}
naval-treaty	event_16_12	event	\N	Watson says Holmes will arrive when promised	15	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_16", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_748"], "communicatesContent": "He'll be here when he promised, and not an instant sooner or later."}
naval-treaty	event_17_1	event	\N	A hansom arrives at the door and Watson's friend gets out	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_749"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_17_2	event	\N	Watson and Phelps see Holmes's bandaged left hand and pale face	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_750"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_17_3	event	\N	Holmes enters the house and comes upstairs later	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_751"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_17_4	event	\N	Phelps says Holmes looks like a beaten man	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_752"], "communicatesContent": "He looks like a beaten man."}
naval-treaty	event_17_5	event	\N	Watson says the clue probably lies in town	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_754"], "communicatesContent": "After all, the clue of the matter lies probably here in town."}
naval-treaty	event_17_6	event	\N	Phelps groans	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_755"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_17_7	event	\N	Phelps says he had hoped for much from Holmes's return and asks what can be the matter	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_756", "sentence_757", "sentence_758"], "communicatesContent": "I don't know how it is, but I had hoped for so much from his return. But surely his hand was not tied up like that yesterday. What can be the matter?"}
naval-treaty	event_17_8	event	\N	Watson asks Holmes whether he is wounded	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_759", "sentence_760"], "communicatesContent": "You are not wounded, Holmes?"}
naval-treaty	event_17_9	event	\N	Holmes says his injury is only a scratch from his own clumsiness	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_761"], "communicatesContent": "Tut, it is only a scratch through my own clumsiness."}
naval-treaty	event_17_10	event	\N	Holmes describes Phelps's case as one of the darkest he has investigated	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_762"], "communicatesContent": "This case of yours, Mr. Phelps, is certainly one of the darkest which I have ever investigated."}
naval-treaty	event_17_11	event	\N	Holmes says the experience was remarkable	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_764"], "communicatesContent": "It has been a most remarkable experience."}
naval-treaty	event_17_12	event	\N	Watson remarks that the bandage tells of adventures and asks Holmes to explain what happened	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_765", "sentence_766"], "communicatesContent": "That bandage tells of adventures. Won't you tell us what has happened?"}
naval-treaty	event_18_9	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes hears the door shut and infers the key was turned in the lock	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_815"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_17_13	event	\N	Holmes postpones the explanation until after breakfast	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_767", "sentence_768"], "communicatesContent": "After breakfast, my dear Watson. Remember that I have breathed thirty miles of Surrey air this morning."}
naval-treaty	event_17_14	event	\N	Holmes asks whether his cabman advertisement has received no answer and comments on failure	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_769", "sentence_770"], "communicatesContent": "I suppose that there has been no answer from my cabman advertisement? Well, well, we cannot expect to score every time."}
naval-treaty	event_17_15	event	\N	Mrs. Hudson brings tea, coffee, and breakfast covers to the table	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_771", "sentence_772"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_17_16	event	\N	Holmes praises Mrs. Hudson's breakfast and asks Watson what food he has	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_773", "sentence_774", "sentence_775"], "communicatesContent": "Mrs. Hudson has risen to the occasion. Her cuisine is a little limited, but she has as good an idea of breakfast as a Scotch-woman. What have you here, Watson?"}
naval-treaty	event_17_17	event	\N	Watson answers that he has ham and eggs	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_776"], "communicatesContent": "Ham and eggs."}
naval-treaty	event_17_18	event	\N	Holmes invites Phelps to take breakfast food	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_777", "sentence_778"], "communicatesContent": "Good! What are you going to take, Mr. Phelps—curried fowl or eggs, or will you help yourself?"}
naval-treaty	event_17_19	event	\N	Phelps refuses food	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_779", "sentence_780", "sentence_783"], "communicatesContent": "Thank you. I can eat nothing. Thank you, I would really rather not."}
naval-treaty	event_17_20	event	\N	Holmes suggests that Phelps help him instead	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_781", "sentence_782", "sentence_784"], "communicatesContent": "Oh, come! Try the dish before you. Well, then, I suppose that you have no objection to helping me?"}
naval-treaty	event_17_21	event	\N	Phelps raises a cover, screams, stares at the dish, and sees a blue-grey paper cylinder	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_785", "sentence_786"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_17_22	event	\N	Phelps seizes the cylinder, reacts with delight, and collapses into an armchair	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_787", "sentence_788"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_17_23	event	\N	Holmes soothes and pats Phelps on the shoulder	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_789", "sentence_790", "sentence_791", "sentence_792"], "communicatesContent": "There! there! It was too bad to spring it on you like this, but Watson here will tell you that I never can resist a touch of the dramatic."}
naval-treaty	event_17_24	event	\N	Phelps thanks Holmes and says he has saved his honour	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_793", "sentence_794", "sentence_795", "sentence_796"], "communicatesContent": "God bless you! You have saved my honour."}
naval-treaty	event_17_25	event	\N	Holmes says his own honour was at stake and that failing is hateful to him	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_797", "sentence_798"], "communicatesContent": "Well, my own was at stake, you know. I assure you it is just as hateful to me to fail in a case as it can be to you to blunder over a commission."}
naval-treaty	event_17_26	event	\N	Phelps puts the document into his coat pocket	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_799"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_17_27	event	\N	Phelps says he is dying to know how Holmes got the document and where it was	16	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_17", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_800"], "communicatesContent": "I have not the heart to interrupt your breakfast any further, and yet I am dying to know how you got it and where it was."}
naval-treaty	event_18_1	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes eats and drinks breakfast	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_801"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_2	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes rises, lights his pipe, and settles into his chair	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_802"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_3	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes explains his movements before arriving at Briarbrae	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_803", "sentence_804", "sentence_805", "sentence_806"], "communicatesContent": "I'll tell you what I did first, and how I came to do it afterwards. After leaving you at the station I went for a charming walk through some admirable Surrey scenery to a pretty little village called Ripley, where I had my tea at an inn, and took the precaution of filling my flask and of putting a paper of sandwiches in my pocket. There I remained until evening, when I set off for Woking again, and found myself in the high-road outside Briarbrae just after sunset. I waited until the road was clear and then I clambered over the fence into the grounds."}
naval-treaty	event_18_4	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes enters the grounds by climbing over the fence	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_806"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_5	event	\N	Phelps reacts to Holmes's mention of the gate being open	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_807", "sentence_808"], "communicatesContent": "Surely the gate was open!"}
naval-treaty	event_18_6	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes uses the fir-trees as cover and crawls to the rhododendrons	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_809", "sentence_810", "sentence_811", "sentence_812"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_7	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes observes Miss Harrison reading in the room	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_813"], "communicatesContent": null}
final-problem	vere_street	entity	location	Vere Street	\N	{"description": "A street in London.", "firstSection": "final-problem-section_5"}
naval-treaty	event_18_10	event	\N	Phelps responds about the key	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_816", "sentence_817"], "communicatesContent": "The key!"}
naval-treaty	event_18_11	event	\N	Holmes explains that he instructed Miss Harrison to lock the door outside and take the key	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_818", "sentence_819"], "communicatesContent": "I had given Miss Harrison instructions to lock the door on the outside and take the key with her when she went to bed. She carried out every one of my injunctions to the letter."}
naval-treaty	event_18_12	event	\N	Miss Harrison departs and the lights go out	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_820"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_13	event	\N	Holmes waits in the rhododendron bush through the night	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_821", "sentence_822", "sentence_823", "sentence_824", "sentence_825"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_14	event	\N	Mr. Joseph Harrison opens the servants' door and steps into the moonlight	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_826"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_15	event	\N	Phelps reacts to the name Joseph Harrison	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_827", "sentence_828"], "communicatesContent": "Joseph!"}
naval-treaty	event_18_16	event	\N	Joseph Harrison conceals his face with a black coat while moving to the window	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_829", "sentence_830"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_17	event	\N	Joseph Harrison opens the window and the shutters with a knife	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_831"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_18	event	\N	Joseph Harrison lights the candles and turns back the carpet	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_833"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_19	event	\N	Joseph Harrison removes the board and draws out the paper cylinder	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_834", "sentence_835", "sentence_836"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_18_20	event	\N	Joseph Harrison blows out the candles and walks into Holmes's arms	17	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_836"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_19_1	event	\N	Holmes describes Joseph Harrison as attacking with a knife and being subdued	18	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_837", "sentence_838", "sentence_839"], "communicatesContent": "Well, he has rather more viciousness than I gave him credit for, has Master Joseph. He flew at me with his knife, and I had to grasp him twice, and got a cut over the knuckles, before I had the upper hand of him. He looked murder out of the only eye he could see with when we had finished, but he listened to reason and gave up the papers."}
naval-treaty	event_19_2	event	\N	Holmes lets Joseph Harrison go and wires full particulars to Forbes	18	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_19", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_840"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_19_3	event	\N	Holmes says the stolen papers may be recovered before Joseph Harrison reaches the place	18	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_841", "sentence_842"], "communicatesContent": "If he is quick enough to catch his bird, well and good. But if, as I shrewdly suspect, he finds the nest empty before he gets there, why, all the better for the government."}
naval-treaty	event_19_4	event	\N	Holmes says Lord Holdhurst and Percy Phelps would prefer the affair not reach a police-court	18	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_843"], "communicatesContent": "I fancy that Lord Holdhurst for one, and Mr. Percy Phelps for another, would very much rather that the affair never got as far as a police-court."}
naval-treaty	event_19_5	event	\N	Percy Phelps reacts in shock	18	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_19", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_844", "sentence_845", "sentence_846", "sentence_847", "sentence_848", "sentence_849", "sentence_854", "sentence_855", "sentence_856", "sentence_862"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_19_6	event	\N	Holmes explains that Joseph Harrison stole the treaty papers from the Foreign Office room	18	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_850", "sentence_851", "sentence_852", "sentence_853", "sentence_857", "sentence_858", "sentence_859", "sentence_860", "sentence_861", "sentence_863", "sentence_864", "sentence_865", "sentence_866"], "communicatesContent": "Hum! I am afraid Joseph's character is a rather deeper and more dangerous one than one might judge from his appearance. From what I have heard from him this morning, I gather that he has lost heavily in dabbling with stocks, and that he is ready to do anything on earth to better his fortunes. Being an absolutely selfish man, when a chance presented itself he did not allow either his sister's happiness or your reputation to hold his hand. The principal difficulty in your case lay in the fact of there being too much evidence. What was vital was overlaid and hidden by what was irrelevant. Of all the facts which were presented to us we had to pick just those which we deemed to be essential, and then piece them together in their order, so as to reconstruct this very remarkable chain of events. I had already begun to suspect Joseph, from the fact that you had intended to travel home with him that night, and that therefore it was a likely enough thing that he should call for you, knowing the Foreign Office well, upon his way. When I heard that some one had been so anxious to get into the bedroom, in which no one but Joseph could have concealed anything—you told us in your narrative how you had turned Joseph out when you arrived with the doctor—my suspicions all changed to certainties, especially as the attempt was made on the first night upon which the nurse was absent, showing that the intruder was well acquainted with the ways of the house. The facts of the case, as far as I have worked them out, are these: this Joseph Harrison entered the office through the Charles Street door, and knowing his way he walked straight into your room the instant after you left it. Finding no one there he promptly rang the bell, and at the instant that he did so his eyes caught the paper upon the table. A glance showed him that chance had put in his way a State document of immense value, and in an instant he had thrust it into his pocket and was gone. A few minutes elapsed, as you remember, before the sleepy commissionnaire drew your attention to the bell, and those were just enough to give the thief time to make his escape."}
naval-treaty	event_20_1	event	\N	A man travels to Woking by the first train and hides his booty in a safe place	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_867"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_2	event	\N	A man plans to take the booty to the French embassy or another place offering a long price	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_867"], "communicatesContent": null}
final-problem	pall_mall	entity	location	Pall Mall	\N	{"description": "A street in London.", "firstSection": "final-problem-section_5"}
naval-treaty	event_20_4	event	\N	He is bundled out of his room and kept from regaining his treasure	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_869"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_5	event	\N	He thinks he sees his chance	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_871"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_6	event	\N	He tries to steal in but is baffled	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_872"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_7	event	\N	Watson and Holmes discuss the unusual draught	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_873", "sentence_874"], "communicatesContent": "You remember that you did not take your usual draught that night."}
naval-treaty	event_20_8	event	\N	Holmes states that the draught had been made efficacious and relied on unconsciousness	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_875", "sentence_876"], "communicatesContent": "I fancy that he had taken steps to make that draught efficacious, and that he quite relied upon your being unconscious. Of course, I understood that he would repeat the attempt whenever it could be done with safety."}
naval-treaty	event_20_9	event	\N	Watson leaves the room and gives him the chance he wanted	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_877"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_10	event	\N	Holmes keeps Miss Harrison in the room all day	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_878"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_11	event	\N	Holmes gives him the idea that the coast was clear and keeps guard	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_879"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_12	event	\N	Holmes keeps Miss Harrison in the room and guards it to prevent anticipation	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_878", "sentence_879"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_13	event	\N	Holmes lets him take the papers from the hiding-place	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_880", "sentence_881"], "communicatesContent": null}
naval-treaty	event_20_14	event	\N	Phelps asks why he tried the window on the first occasion	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_883"], "communicatesContent": "Why did he try the window on the first occasion, when he might have entered by the door?"}
naval-treaty	event_20_15	event	\N	Holmes explains that reaching the door would require passing seven bedrooms and that the lawn could be reached with ease	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_884", "sentence_885"], "communicatesContent": "In reaching the door he would have to pass seven bedrooms. On the other hand, he could get out on to the lawn with ease."}
naval-treaty	event_20_16	event	\N	Phelps asks whether he had murderous intention and says the knife was only meant as a tool	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": "percival_phelps", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_887", "sentence_888"], "communicatesContent": "You do not think that he had any murderous intention? The knife was only meant as a tool."}
naval-treaty	event_20_17	event	\N	Holmes answers that the matter may be so and refuses to trust Mr. Joseph Harrison's mercy	19	{"section": "naval-treaty-section_20", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_889", "sentence_890"], "communicatesContent": "It may be so. I can only say for certain that Mr. Joseph Harrison is a gentleman to whose mercy I should be extremely unwilling to trust."}
