cardboard-box	event_1_3	event	\N	Holmes reads and re-reads the letter on the sofa	0	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_9"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_1_4	event	\N	Watson throws aside the paper and falls into a brown study	0	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_17"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_9	event	\N	Lestrade sends a note to Holmes	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": "lestrade", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_89", "sentence_90", "sentence_91", "sentence_92", "sentence_93"], "communicatesContent": "I think that this case is very much in your line. We have every hope of clearing the matter up, but we find a little difficulty in getting anything to work upon. We have, of course, wired to the Belfast post-office, but a large number of parcels were handed in upon that day, and they have no means of identifying this particular one, or of remembering the sender. The box is a half-pound box of honeydew tobacco and does not help us in any way. The medical student theory still appears to me to be the most feasible, but if you should have a few hours to spare I should be very happy to see you out here."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	sentence_1	lexical	\N	\N	0	{"text": "THE HOUND OF THE BASKERVILLES", "section": "section_1", "entities": []}
cardboard-box	event_1_5	event	\N	Holmes breaks into Watson's train of thought and comments on it	0	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_1", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_18", "sentence_19", "sentence_20", "sentence_21", "sentence_26", "sentence_27", "sentence_28", "sentence_29", "sentence_30", "sentence_31"], "communicatesContent": "You are right, Watson. It does seem a most preposterous way of settling a dispute. Most preposterous! You remember that some little time ago when I read you the passage in one of Poe's sketches in which a close reasoner follows the unspoken thoughts of his companion, you were inclined to treat the matter as a mere tour-de-force of the author. On my remarking that I was constantly in the habit of doing the same thing you expressed incredulity. Perhaps not with your tongue, my dear Watson, but certainly with your eyebrows. So when I saw you throw down your paper and enter upon a train of thought, I was very happy to have the opportunity of reading it off, and eventually of breaking into it, as a proof that I had been in rapport with you."}
cardboard-box	event_1_6	event	\N	Watson objects that Holmes drew conclusions from observed actions in the example	0	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_1", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_32", "sentence_33", "sentence_34", "sentence_35"], "communicatesContent": "In the example which you read to me, the reasoner drew his conclusions from the actions of the man whom he observed. If I remember right, he stumbled over a heap of stones, looked up at the stars, and so on. But I have been seated quietly in my chair, and what clues can I have given you?"}
cardboard-box	event_2_1	event	\N	Holmes explains a line of facial deduction to Watson	1	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_36", "sentence_37", "sentence_38", "sentence_39", "sentence_40", "sentence_41", "sentence_42", "sentence_43", "sentence_44", "sentence_45", "sentence_46", "sentence_47", "sentence_48", "sentence_49", "sentence_50", "sentence_51", "sentence_52", "sentence_53", "sentence_54", "sentence_55", "sentence_56", "sentence_57", "sentence_58", "sentence_59", "sentence_60", "sentence_61", "sentence_62", "sentence_63", "sentence_64", "sentence_65", "sentence_66", "sentence_67"], "communicatesContent": "You do yourself an injustice. The features are given to man as the means by which he shall express his emotions, and yours are faithful servants. Your features and especially your eyes. Perhaps you cannot yourself recall how your reverie commenced? Then I will tell you. After throwing down your paper, which was the action which drew my attention to you, you sat for half a minute with a vacant expression. Then your eyes fixed themselves upon your newly framed picture of General Gordon, and I saw by the alteration in your face that a train of thought had been started. But it did not lead very far. Your eyes flashed across to the unframed portrait of Henry Ward Beecher which stands upon the top of your books. Then you glanced up at the wall, and of course your meaning was obvious. You were thinking that if the portrait were framed it would just cover that bare space and correspond with Gordon's picture over there. So far I could hardly have gone astray. But now your thoughts went back to Beecher, and you looked hard across as if you were studying the character in his features. Then your eyes ceased to pucker, but you continued to look across, and your face was thoughtful. You were recalling the incidents of Beecher's career. I was well aware that you could not do this without thinking of the mission which he undertook on behalf of the North at the time of the Civil War, for I remember your expressing your passionate indignation at the way in which he was received by the more turbulent of our people. You felt so strongly about it that I knew you could not think of Beecher without thinking of that also. When a moment later I saw your eyes wander away from the picture, I suspected that your mind had now turned to the Civil War, and when I observed that your lips set, your eyes sparkled, and your hands clenched I was positive that you were indeed thinking of the gallantry which was shown by both sides in that desperate struggle. But then, again, your face grew sadder; you shook your head. You were dwelling upon the sadness and horror and useless waste of life. Your hand stole towards your own old wound and a smile quivered on your lips, which showed me that the ridiculous side of this method of settling international questions had forced itself upon your mind. At this point I agreed with you that it was preposterous and was glad to find that all my deductions had been correct."}
cardboard-box	event_2_2	event	\N	Watson expresses amazement	1	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_2", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_62", "sentence_63", "sentence_64"], "communicatesContent": "Absolutely! And now that you have explained it, I confess that I am as amazed as before."}
cardboard-box	event_2_3	event	\N	Holmes says the deduction was superficial and introduces a new problem	1	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_65", "sentence_66", "sentence_67", "sentence_68"], "communicatesContent": "It was very superficial, my dear Watson, I assure you. I should not have intruded it upon your attention had you not shown some incredulity the other day. But I have in my hands here a little problem which may prove to be more difficult of solution than my small essay in thought reading. Have you observed in the paper a short paragraph referring to the remarkable contents of a packet sent through the post to Miss Cushing, of Cross Street, Croydon?"}
cardboard-box	event_2_4	event	\N	Watson says he saw nothing in the paper	1	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_2", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_69"], "communicatesContent": "No, I saw nothing."}
cardboard-box	event_2_5	event	\N	Holmes directs Watson to pass the paper over and read the paragraph aloud	1	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_2", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_70", "sentence_71", "sentence_72", "sentence_73", "sentence_74"], "communicatesContent": "Ah! then you must have overlooked it. Just toss it over to me. Here it is, under the financial column. Perhaps you would be good enough to read it aloud."}
cardboard-box	event_2_6	event	\N	Watson picks up the paper and reads the indicated paragraph	1	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_2", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_75", "sentence_76"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_1	event	\N	A postman hands in a small packet wrapped in brown paper	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_78"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_2	event	\N	The packet contains a cardboard box	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_79"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_3	event	\N	The cardboard box is filled with coarse salt	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_79"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_4	event	\N	Miss Cushing finds two human ears in the box	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_80"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_5	event	\N	The box is sent by parcel post from Belfast	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_81"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_6	event	\N	Miss Cushing let apartments in her house to three young medical students	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_83"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_7	event	\N	Miss Cushing gets rid of the medical students	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_83"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_8	event	\N	The police suspect the medical students of the outrage	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_84"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	sentence_2	lexical	\N	\N	1	{"text": "Another Adventure of Sherlock Holmes", "section": "section_1", "entities": []}
cardboard-box	event_3_10	event	\N	Holmes and Watson travel to Croydon by cab and train	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_95", "sentence_97", "sentence_98", "sentence_99", "sentence_100", "sentence_101", "sentence_102", "sentence_103"], "communicatesContent": "Can you rise superior to the heat and run down to Croydon with me on the off chance of a case for your annals?"}
cardboard-box	event_3_11	event	\N	Lestrade waits at the station for Holmes and Watson	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_102"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_12	event	\N	Holmes and Watson walk to Cross Street, where Miss Cushing resides	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_103"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_13	event	\N	Lestrade taps at the door and a servant girl opens it	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_105"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_3_14	event	\N	Miss Cushing sits in the front room	2	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_106"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_4_1	event	\N	Lestrade enters the outhouse and Miss Cushing speaks about the dreadful things and asks that they be taken away	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_109", "sentence_110", "sentence_111"], "communicatesContent": "They are in the outhouse, those dreadful things. I wish that you would take them away altogether."}
cardboard-box	event_4_2	event	\N	Lestrade says he kept the items for Holmes to see them in Miss Cushing's presence	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "lestrade", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_111", "sentence_112"], "communicatesContent": "So I shall, Miss Cushing. I only kept them here until my friend, Mr. Holmes, should have seen them in your presence."}
cardboard-box	event_4_3	event	\N	Miss Cushing asks why Holmes needed to see them in her presence	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_113"], "communicatesContent": "Why in my presence, sir?"}
cardboard-box	event_4_4	event	\N	Lestrade says Holmes might wish to ask questions	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "lestrade", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_114"], "communicatesContent": "In case he wished to ask any questions."}
cardboard-box	event_4_5	event	\N	Miss Cushing says she knows nothing whatever about it and asks why she should be questioned	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_115"], "communicatesContent": "What is the use of asking me questions when I tell you I know nothing whatever about it?"}
cardboard-box	event_4_6	event	\N	Holmes reassures Miss Cushing and says she has already been annoyed enough	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_116", "sentence_117"], "communicatesContent": "Quite so, madam. I have no doubt that you have been annoyed more than enough already over this business."}
cardboard-box	event_4_7	event	\N	Miss Cushing says she is a quiet woman, lives retired, and dislikes the police in her house and her name in the papers	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_118", "sentence_119", "sentence_120", "sentence_121", "sentence_122"], "communicatesContent": "Indeed, I have, sir. I am a quiet woman and live a retired life. It is something new for me to see my name in the papers and to find the police in my house. I won't have those things in here, Mr. Lestrade. If you wish to see them you must go to the outhouse."}
cardboard-box	event_4_8	event	\N	Lestrade goes into the outhouse and brings out a yellow cardboard box with brown paper and string	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_123", "sentence_124"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_4_9	event	\N	Everyone sits down while Holmes examines the articles from the box	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_125"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_4_10	event	\N	Holmes comments on the tarred twine and asks Lestrade what he makes of it	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_126", "sentence_127"], "communicatesContent": "The string is exceedingly interesting. What do you make of this string, Lestrade?"}
cardboard-box	event_4_11	event	\N	Lestrade says the string has been tarred	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "lestrade", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_128"], "communicatesContent": "It has been tarred."}
cardboard-box	event_4_12	event	\N	Holmes identifies the string as tarred twine and points out the cut cord and intact peculiar knot	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_129", "sentence_130", "sentence_131", "sentence_132", "sentence_133", "sentence_134", "sentence_135", "sentence_136"], "communicatesContent": "Precisely. It is a piece of tarred twine. You have also, no doubt, remarked that Miss Cushing has cut the cord with a scissors, as can be seen by the double fray on each side. This is of importance. The importance lies in the fact that the knot is left intact, and that this knot is of a peculiar character."}
cardboard-box	event_4_13	event	\N	Holmes examines the box wrapper and describes the coffee smell, address, pen, ink, and printed direction	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_137", "sentence_138", "sentence_139", "sentence_140", "sentence_141", "sentence_142", "sentence_143", "sentence_144", "sentence_145", "sentence_146", "sentence_147"], "communicatesContent": "So much for the string, then, now for the box wrapper. Brown paper, with a distinct smell of coffee. What, did you not observe it? I think there can be no doubt of it. Address printed in rather straggling characters: 'Miss S. ' Done with a broad-pointed pen, probably a J, and with very inferior ink. ' The parcel was directed, then, by a man—the printing is distinctly masculine—of limited education and unacquainted with the town of Croydon. So far, so good! The box is a yellow half-pound honeydew box, with nothing distinctive save two thumb marks at the left bottom corner. It is filled with rough salt of the quality used for preserving hides and other of the coarser commercial purposes. And embedded in it are these very singular enclosures."}
cardboard-box	event_4_14	event	\N	Holmes removes the two ears from the box and examines them	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_149", "sentence_150"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_11_3	event	\N	Jim and Mary invite Sarah to stay with them	10	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_406"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_4_15	event	\N	Holmes states that the ears are not a pair and rejects the practical-joke explanation	3	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_151", "sentence_152", "sentence_153", "sentence_154", "sentence_155"], "communicatesContent": "You have observed, of course, that the ears are not a pair."}
cardboard-box	event_6_1	event	\N	Miss Cushing says the parcel was never meant for her and that she has no enemy she knows of	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_200", "sentence_201", "sentence_202"], "communicatesContent": "this matter is a mistake, and the parcel was never meant for me at all. I have said this several times to the gentleman from Scotland Yard, but he simply laughs at me. I have not an enemy in the world, as far as I know, so why should anyone play me such a trick?"}
cardboard-box	event_6_2	event	\N	Holmes sits beside Miss Cushing and begins to agree with her view	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_203"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_6_3	event	\N	Holmes studies Miss Cushing's profile with intentness	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_204", "sentence_205", "sentence_206"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_6_4	event	\N	Holmes asks about Miss Cushing's sisters	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_207", "sentence_208", "sentence_209", "sentence_210"], "communicatesContent": "There were one or two questions. You have two sisters, I believe."}
cardboard-box	event_6_5	event	\N	Holmes explains how he identified Miss Cushing's sisters from the portraits	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_211", "sentence_212"], "communicatesContent": "I observed the very instant that I entered the room that you have a portrait group of three ladies upon the mantelpiece, one of whom is undoubtedly yourself, while the others are so exceedingly like you that there could be no doubt of the relationship."}
cardboard-box	event_6_6	event	\N	Miss Cushing identifies Sarah and Mary as her sisters	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_213", "sentence_214"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, you are quite right. Those are my sisters, Sarah and Mary."}
cardboard-box	event_6_7	event	\N	Holmes remarks on a Liverpool portrait of Miss Cushing's younger sister and infers she was unmarried	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_215", "sentence_216"], "communicatesContent": "And here at my elbow is another portrait, taken at Liverpool, of your younger sister, in the company of a man who appears to be a steward by his uniform. I observe that she was unmarried at the time."}
cardboard-box	event_6_8	event	\N	Miss Cushing praises Holmes's speed of observation	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_217", "sentence_218"], "communicatesContent": "You are very quick at observing. That is my trade."}
cardboard-box	event_6_9	event	\N	Miss Cushing says Mary later married Mr. Browner and that he changed ships to stay near her	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_219", "sentence_220", "sentence_221", "sentence_222", "sentence_223"], "communicatesContent": "Well, you are quite right. But she was married to Mr. Browner a few days afterwards. He was on the South American line when that was taken, but he was so fond of her that he couldn't abide to leave her for so long, and he got into the Liverpool and London boats. Ah, the Conqueror, perhaps? No, the May Day, when last I heard."}
cardboard-box	event_6_10	event	\N	Miss Cushing says Jim visited, broke the pledge, drank ashore, and became violent	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_224", "sentence_225", "sentence_226", "sentence_227", "sentence_228"], "communicatesContent": "Jim came down here to see me once. That was before he broke the pledge; but afterwards he would always take drink when he was ashore, and a little drink would send him stark, staring mad. Ah! it was a bad day that ever he took a glass in his hand again. First he dropped me, then he quarrelled with Sarah, and now that Mary has stopped writing we don't know how things are going with them."}
cardboard-box	event_6_11	event	\N	Miss Cushing becomes communicative and gives details about her steward brother-in-law and medical students	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_229", "sentence_230", "sentence_231"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_6_12	event	\N	Holmes listens and asks further questions about Sarah	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_232", "sentence_233", "sentence_234"], "communicatesContent": "About your second sister, Sarah, I wonder, since you are both maiden ladies, that you do not keep house together."}
cardboard-box	event_6_13	event	\N	Miss Cushing explains that she and Sarah lived together until they parted two months earlier because of Sarah's temper	5	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_6", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_235", "sentence_236", "sentence_237", "sentence_238"], "communicatesContent": "Ah, you don't know Sarah's temper or you would wonder no more. I tried it when I came to Croydon, and we kept on until about two months ago, when we had to part. I don't want to say a word against my own sister, but she was always meddlesome and hard to please, was Sarah."}
cardboard-box	event_7_1	event	\N	Miss Cushing says that Sarah quarreled with the Liverpool relations and that they were once best friends	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_239", "sentence_240", "sentence_241"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, and they were the best of friends at one time. Why, she went up there to live in order to be near them."}
cardboard-box	event_7_2	event	\N	Miss Cushing says Sarah speaks only of Jim Browner's drinking and ways	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_242", "sentence_243"], "communicatesContent": "And now she has no word hard enough for Jim Browner. The last six months that she was here she would speak of nothing but his drinking and his ways."}
cardboard-box	event_7_3	event	\N	Miss Cushing says Jim caught Sarah meddling and reproached her	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "miss_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_244"], "communicatesContent": "He had caught her meddling, I suspect, and given her a bit of his mind, and that was the start of it."}
cardboard-box	event_11_4	event	\N	Jim visits home on weekends and spends time with Sarah	10	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_410", "sentence_411"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_7_4	event	\N	Holmes thanks Miss Cushing, mentions Sarah's address, and takes leave	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_245", "sentence_246", "sentence_247"], "communicatesContent": "Your sister Sarah lives, I think you said, at New Street Wallington? Good-bye, and I am very sorry that you should have been troubled over a case with which, as you say, you have nothing whatever to do."}
cardboard-box	event_7_5	event	\N	Holmes and Watson take a cab toward Wallington	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_248", "sentence_249", "sentence_250", "sentence_251", "sentence_252", "sentence_253", "sentence_254", "sentence_255", "sentence_256"], "communicatesContent": "How far to Wallington? Very good. Jump in, Watson. We must strike while the iron is hot. Simple as the case is, there have been one or two very instructive details in connection with it. Just pull up at a telegraph office as you pass, cabby."}
cardboard-box	event_7_6	event	\N	Holmes sends a short wire	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_257"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_7_7	event	\N	The cab stops at a house in Wallington and Holmes speaks with the doctor at the door	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_258", "sentence_259", "sentence_260", "sentence_261", "sentence_262", "sentence_263", "sentence_264", "sentence_265", "sentence_266"], "communicatesContent": "Is Miss Cushing at home?"}
cardboard-box	event_7_8	event	\N	The young doctor says Sarah Cushing is extremely ill and refuses access	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "grave_young_gentleman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_262", "sentence_263", "sentence_264", "sentence_265", "sentence_266"], "communicatesContent": "Miss Sarah Cushing is extremely ill. She has been suffering since yesterday from brain symptoms of great severity. As her medical adviser, I cannot possibly take the responsibility of allowing anyone to see her. I should recommend you to call again in ten days."}
cardboard-box	event_7_9	event	\N	Holmes and Watson stop for lunch and Holmes talks about violins and Paganini	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_272", "sentence_273", "sentence_274"], "communicatesContent": "He talks about violins, narrating how he had purchased his own Stradivarius, worth at least five hundred guineas, at a Jew broker's in Tottenham Court Road for fifty-five shillings, and then tells anecdotes about Paganini."}
cardboard-box	event_7_10	event	\N	Holmes and Watson arrive at the police-station and Lestrade presents a telegram	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "lestrade", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_275", "sentence_276", "sentence_277"], "communicatesContent": "A telegram for you, Mr. Holmes."}
cardboard-box	event_7_11	event	\N	Holmes opens the telegram and says it is the answer	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_278", "sentence_279", "sentence_280", "sentence_281"], "communicatesContent": "Ha! It is the answer! That's all right."}
cardboard-box	event_7_12	event	\N	Lestrade asks Holmes whether he has found anything and Holmes replies that he has found everything	6	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_7", "speakerId": "lestrade", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_282", "sentence_283", "sentence_284", "sentence_285", "sentence_286", "sentence_287", "sentence_288"], "communicatesContent": "Have you found out anything?"}
cardboard-box	event_8_1	event	\N	Holmes writes on a visiting card and throws it to Lestrade	7	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_290"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_8_2	event	\N	Holmes tells Lestrade he cannot arrest until tomorrow night and asks him not to mention Holmes's name	7	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_292", "sentence_293"], "communicatesContent": "You cannot effect an arrest until to-morrow night at the earliest. I should prefer that you do not mention my name at all in connection with the case, as I choose to be only associated with those crimes which present some difficulty in their solution."}
cardboard-box	event_8_3	event	\N	Holmes and Watson walk to the station	7	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_294", "sentence_295"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_8_4	event	\N	Holmes explains the case to Watson	7	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_296", "sentence_298"], "communicatesContent": "The case is one where we have been compelled to reason backward from effects to causes. I have written to Lestrade asking him to supply us with the details which are now wanting, and which he will only get after he has secured his man. That he may be safely trusted to do, for although he is absolutely devoid of reason, he is as tenacious as a bulldog when he once understands what he has to do, and, indeed, it is just this tenacity which has brought him to the top at Scotland Yard."}
cardboard-box	event_8_5	event	\N	Watson asks whether the case is complete	7	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_8", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_299", "sentence_300"], "communicatesContent": "Your case is not complete, then?"}
cardboard-box	event_8_6	event	\N	Holmes says the case is fairly complete and identifies Jim Browner as the suspected author	7	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_301", "sentence_306"], "communicatesContent": "It is fairly complete in essentials. We know who the author of the revolting business is, although one of the victims still escapes us."}
cardboard-box	event_8_7	event	\N	Watson names Jim Browner as the suspected man	7	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_8", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_303", "sentence_304"], "communicatesContent": "I presume that this Jim Browner, the steward of a Liverpool boat, is the man whom you suspect?"}
cardboard-box	event_8_8	event	\N	Holmes says the suspicion is more than a suspicion	7	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_305", "sentence_306"], "communicatesContent": "Oh! it is more than a suspicion."}
cardboard-box	event_11_5	event	\N	Jim returns home, finds Mary absent, and finds Sarah at home	10	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_415"], "communicatesContent": null}
cardboard-box	event_11_6	event	\N	Sarah takes Jim's hand and then leaves the room after speaking mockingly	10	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_11", "speakerId": "sarah_cushing", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_419", "sentence_423", "sentence_424", "sentence_425"], "communicatesContent": "Steady old Jim!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	sentence_3	lexical	\N	\N	2	{"text": "by A.", "section": "section_1", "entities": []}
cardboard-box	event_8_9	event	\N	Holmes recounts his reasoning steps from the case	7	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_8", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_307", "sentence_326"], "communicatesContent": "On the contrary, to my mind nothing could be more clear. Let me run over the principal steps. We approached the case with an absolutely blank mind. We had formed no theories. We were simply there to observe and to draw inferences from our observations. We saw a placid and respectable lady and a portrait which showed me that she had two younger sisters. It flashed across my mind that the box might have been meant for one of these. Then we went to the garden and saw the contents of the little yellow box. The string was of the quality used by sailmakers aboard ship, the knot was popular with sailors, the parcel had been posted at a port, and the male ear was pierced for an earring, so I was certain that the actors were among our seafaring classes. The packet was addressed to Miss S. Cushing. The oldest sister would be Miss Cushing, but the initial might belong to one of the others as well. I went into the house intending to clear up this point. I was about to assure Miss Cushing that a mistake had been made when I came suddenly to a stop, because I had just seen something which filled me with surprise and narrowed the field of our inquiry immensely."}
cardboard-box	event_9_1	event	\N	Holmes identifies Miss Cushing's ear as matching the ear from the box	8	{"section": "cardboard-box-section_9", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_330", "sentence_331", "sentence_332", "sentence_333", "sentence_334"], "communicatesContent": null}
