hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_1_1	event	\N	A. Conan Doyle addresses Robinson in a dedication letter	0	{"section": "section_1", "speakerId": "a_conan_doyle", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_5", "sentence_6", "sentence_7", "sentence_8", "sentence_9", "sentence_10"], "communicatesContent": "My dear Robinson, It was to your account of a West-Country legend that this tale owes its inception. For this and for your help in the details all thanks. Yours most truly, A. Conan Doyle."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_1_2	event	\N	The title page names The Hound of the Baskervilles	0	{"section": "section_1", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1", "sentence_2", "sentence_3", "sentence_4"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_1	event	\N	Holmes is seated at the breakfast table	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_17"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_2	event	\N	Watson picks up the visitor's stick from the hearth-rug	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_18"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_3	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson what he makes of the stick	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_24", "sentence_29"], "communicatesContent": "Well, Watson, what do you make of it? But, tell me, Watson, what do you make of our visitor’s stick?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_4	event	\N	Watson says Holmes has eyes in the back of his head	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_26", "sentence_27"], "communicatesContent": "How did you know what I was doing? I believe you have eyes in the back of your head."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_5	event	\N	Holmes says he has a coffee-pot in front of him	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_28"], "communicatesContent": "I have, at least, a well-polished, silver-plated coffee-pot in front of me."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_6	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson to reconstruct the visitor from the stick	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_30", "sentence_31"], "communicatesContent": "Since we have been so unfortunate as to miss him and have no notion of his errand, this accidental souvenir becomes of importance. Let me hear you reconstruct the man by an examination of it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_7	event	\N	Watson describes Dr. Mortimer as a successful elderly medical man	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_32"], "communicatesContent": "Dr. Mortimer is a successful, elderly medical man, well-esteemed since those who know him give him this mark of their appreciation."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_8	event	\N	Watson describes the visitor as a country practitioner who walks a great deal	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_36", "sentence_37", "sentence_38", "sentence_39"], "communicatesContent": "The probability is in favour of his being a country practitioner who does a great deal of his visiting on foot, because this stick has been so knocked about and the thick-iron ferrule is worn down."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_16	event	\N	The manuscript narrates Hugo Baskerville falling in love with a yeoman's daughter	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_218"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_9	event	\N	Watson identifies the inscription as 'friends of the C.C.H.' and guesses its meaning	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_42", "sentence_43"], "communicatesContent": "There is the 'friends of the C.C.H.' I should guess that to be the Something Hunt, the local hunt to whose members he has possibly given some surgical assistance, and which has made him a small presentation in return."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_10	event	\N	Holmes praises Watson's deductions	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_44", "sentence_45", "sentence_46", "sentence_47", "sentence_48"], "communicatesContent": "Really, Watson, you excel yourself. I am bound to say that in all the accounts which you have been so good as to give of my own small achievements you have habitually underrated your own abilities. It may be that you are not yourself luminous, but you are a conductor of light. Some people without possessing genius have a remarkable power of stimulating it. I confess, my dear fellow, that I am very much in your debt."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_11	event	\N	Holmes takes the stick from Watson and examines it	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_51"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_12	event	\N	Holmes examines the stick with a convex lens	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_52"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_3_13	event	\N	Holmes says the stick is interesting and elementary	2	{"section": "section_3", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_53", "sentence_54", "sentence_55"], "communicatesContent": "Interesting, though elementary. There are certainly one or two indications upon the stick. It gives us the basis for several deductions."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_1	event	\N	Watson asks whether anything escaped him	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_56", "sentence_57"], "communicatesContent": "Has anything escaped me?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_2	event	\N	Watson asks whether anything of consequence was overlooked	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_58"], "communicatesContent": "I trust that there is nothing of consequence which I have overlooked?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_3	event	\N	Holmes states that Watson's conclusions were mostly erroneous	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_59", "sentence_60", "sentence_61"], "communicatesContent": "most of your conclusions were erroneous. When I said that you stimulated me I meant, to be frank, that in noting your fallacies I was occasionally guided towards the truth. Not that you are entirely wrong in this instance."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_4	event	\N	Holmes identifies the man as a country practitioner and says he walks a good deal	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_62", "sentence_63"], "communicatesContent": "The man is certainly a country practitioner. And he walks a good deal."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_5	event	\N	Watson claims he was right	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_64", "sentence_65", "sentence_66"], "communicatesContent": "Then I was right. But that was all."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_6	event	\N	Holmes explains his inference about C.C.H. and Charing Cross Hospital	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_67", "sentence_68", "sentence_69"], "communicatesContent": "not all—by no means all. I would suggest, for example, that a presentation to a doctor is more likely to come from a hospital than from a hunt, and that when the initials 'C.C.' are placed before that hospital the words 'Charing Cross' very naturally suggest themselves."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_7	event	\N	Watson accepts Holmes may be right	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_70", "sentence_71", "sentence_72"], "communicatesContent": "You may be right."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_8	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson to infer further from the hypothesis	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_73", "sentence_74", "sentence_75", "sentence_76", "sentence_77"], "communicatesContent": "supposing that 'C.C.H.' does stand for 'Charing Cross Hospital,' what further inferences may we draw? Do none suggest themselves? You know my methods. Apply them!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_9	event	\N	Watson proposes the man practised in town before going to the country	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_78"], "communicatesContent": "I can only think of the obvious conclusion that the man has practised in town before going to the country."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_10	event	\N	Holmes suggests the presentation likely marked Dr. Mortimer's withdrawal from hospital service	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_79", "sentence_80", "sentence_81", "sentence_82", "sentence_83", "sentence_84", "sentence_85", "sentence_86", "sentence_87"], "communicatesContent": "I think that we might venture a little farther than this. Look at it in this light. On what occasion would it be most probable that such a presentation would be made? When would his friends unite to give him a pledge of their good will? Obviously at the moment when Dr. Mortimer withdrew from the service of the hospital in order to start a practice for himself. We know there has been a presentation. We believe there has been a change from a town hospital to a country practice. Is it, then, stretching our inference too far to say that the presentation was on the occasion of the change?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_11	event	\N	Holmes states the man could not have been on the hospital staff and must have been a house-surgeon or house-physician	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_88", "sentence_89", "sentence_90", "sentence_91", "sentence_92"], "communicatesContent": "Now, you will observe that he could not have been on the staff of the hospital, since only a man well-established in a London practice could hold such a position, and such a one would not drift into the country. What was he, then? If he was in the hospital and yet not on the staff he could only have been a house-surgeon or a house-physician—little more than a senior student. And he left five years ago—the date is on the stick. So your grave, middle-aged family practitioner vanishes into thin air, my dear Watson, and there emerges a young fellow under thirty, amiable, unambitious, absent-minded, and the possessor of a favourite dog, which I should describe roughly as being larger than a terrier and smaller than a mastiff."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_17	event	\N	The manuscript narrates the maiden avoiding Hugo Baskerville	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_219"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_12	event	\N	Watson laughs and says Holmes's claims about age and career are hard to check directly	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_93", "sentence_94"], "communicatesContent": "As to the latter part, I have no means of checking you, but at least it is not difficult to find out a few particulars about the man’s age and professional career."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_4_13	event	\N	Watson consults the Medical Directory and reads Mortimer's record aloud	3	{"section": "section_4", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_95", "sentence_96", "sentence_97", "sentence_98", "sentence_99", "sentence_100", "sentence_101", "sentence_102", "sentence_103", "sentence_104", "sentence_105", "sentence_106"], "communicatesContent": "There were several Mortimers, but only one who could be our visitor. I read his record aloud. 1882, Grimpen, Dartmoor, Devon. House-surgeon, from 1882 to 1884, at Charing Cross Hospital. Winner of the Jackson prize for Comparative Pathology, with essay entitled 'Is Disease a Reversion?' Corresponding member of the Swedish Pathological Society. Author of 'Some Freaks of Atavism' (Lancet 1882). 'Do We Progress?' (Journal of Psychology, March, 1883). Medical Officer for the parishes of Grimpen, Thorsley, and High Barrow."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_5_1	event	\N	Holmes states that a dog has been carrying the stick behind its master	4	{"section": "section_5", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_111", "sentence_112", "sentence_113"], "communicatesContent": "Has been in the habit of carrying this stick behind his master. Being a heavy stick the dog has held it tightly by the middle, and the marks of his teeth are very plainly visible."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_5_2	event	\N	Holmes identifies the dog as a curly-haired spaniel	4	{"section": "section_5", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_114", "sentence_115"], "communicatesContent": "It may have been—yes, by Jove, it is a curly-haired spaniel."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_5_3	event	\N	Holmes announces the arrival of Dr. James Mortimer and calls him in	4	{"section": "section_5", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_123", "sentence_124", "sentence_125"], "communicatesContent": "What does Dr. James Mortimer, the man of science, ask of Sherlock Holmes, the specialist in crime? Come in!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_5_4	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer enters Holmes's room and rushes to the stick	4	{"section": "section_5", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_126", "sentence_127", "sentence_128", "sentence_129", "sentence_130"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_5_5	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer expresses gladness about recovering his stick	4	{"section": "section_5", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_131", "sentence_132", "sentence_133"], "communicatesContent": "I am so very glad. I was not sure whether I had left it here or in the Shipping Office. I would not lose that stick for the world."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_5_6	event	\N	Holmes asks about the stick's origin and Mortimer answers	4	{"section": "section_5", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_134", "sentence_135", "sentence_136", "sentence_137"], "communicatesContent": "A presentation, I see. From Charing Cross Hospital?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_5_7	event	\N	Holmes remarks that Mortimer's marriage has upset the deductions and Mortimer explains his marriage and resignation	4	{"section": "section_5", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_138", "sentence_139", "sentence_140", "sentence_141", "sentence_142", "sentence_143", "sentence_144", "sentence_145", "sentence_146", "sentence_147"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, sir. I married, and so left the hospital, and with it all hopes of a consulting practice. It was necessary to make a home of my own."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_5_8	event	\N	Holmes addresses Mortimer by name and Mortimer corrects the title to Mister	4	{"section": "section_5", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_148", "sentence_149", "sentence_150", "sentence_151", "sentence_152"], "communicatesContent": "Mister, sir, Mister—a humble M.R.C.S."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_6_1	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer greets Holmes and speaks about his interest in Holmes	5	{"section": "section_6", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_154", "sentence_155", "sentence_156", "sentence_157", "sentence_158", "sentence_159", "sentence_160"], "communicatesContent": "Glad to meet you, sir. I have heard your name mentioned in connection with that of your friend. You interest me very much, Mr. Holmes. I had hardly expected so dolichocephalic a skull or such well-marked supra-orbital development. Would you have any objection to my running my finger along your parietal fissure? A cast of your skull, sir, until the original is available, would be an ornament to any anthropological museum. It is not my intention to be fulsome, but I confess that I covet your skull."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_6_2	event	\N	Holmes waves the visitor into a chair	5	{"section": "section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_161"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_6_3	event	\N	Holmes comments on the visitor's expertise and invites him to smoke	5	{"section": "section_6", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_162", "sentence_163", "sentence_164"], "communicatesContent": "You are an enthusiast in your line of thought, I perceive, sir, as I am in mine. I observe from your forefinger that you make your own cigarettes. Have no hesitation in lighting one."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_6_4	event	\N	The visitor makes and lights a cigarette	5	{"section": "section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_165", "sentence_166"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_6_5	event	\N	Holmes silently watches the visitor with interest	5	{"section": "section_6", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_167"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_6_6	event	\N	Holmes asks why the visitor has come	5	{"section": "section_6", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_168"], "communicatesContent": "I presume, sir, that it was not merely for the purpose of examining my skull that you have done me the honour to call here last night and again today?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_18	event	\N	The manuscript narrates Hugo Baskerville and his companions abducting the maiden	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_220"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_19	event	\N	The manuscript narrates the maiden being placed in an upper chamber	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_221"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_20	event	\N	The manuscript narrates Hugo Baskerville and his friends carousing	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_221", "sentence_222"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_6_7	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer denies that skull examination was the only purpose of the visit and explains why he came	5	{"section": "section_6", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_169", "sentence_170", "sentence_171", "sentence_175", "sentence_177", "sentence_178", "sentence_179"], "communicatesContent": "No, sir, no; though I am happy to have had the opportunity of doing that as well. I came to you, Mr. Holmes, because I recognized that I am myself an unpractical man and because I am suddenly confronted with a most serious and extraordinary problem. Recognizing, as I do, that you are the second highest expert in Europe— To the man of precisely scientific mind the work of Monsieur Bertillon must always appeal strongly. I said, sir, to the precisely scientific mind. But as a practical man of affairs it is acknowledged that you stand alone. I trust, sir, that I have not inadvertently—"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_6_8	event	\N	Holmes asks Dr. Mortimer to state the problem plainly	5	{"section": "section_6", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_172", "sentence_173", "sentence_174", "sentence_176", "sentence_180", "sentence_181"], "communicatesContent": "Indeed, sir! May I inquire who has the honour to be the first? Then had you not better consult him? Just a little. I think, Dr. Mortimer, you would do wisely if without more ado you would kindly tell me plainly what the exact nature of the problem is in which you demand my assistance."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_1	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says he has a manuscript in his pocket	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_184"], "communicatesContent": "I have in my pocket a manuscript."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_2	event	\N	Holmes says he observed the manuscript as Mortimer entered	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_185"], "communicatesContent": "I observed it as you entered the room."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_3	event	\N	Mortimer says the manuscript is old	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_186"], "communicatesContent": "It is an old manuscript."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_4	event	\N	Holmes says the manuscript dates to the early eighteenth century unless it is a forgery	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_187"], "communicatesContent": "Early eighteenth century, unless it is a forgery."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_5	event	\N	Holmes says Mortimer has shown only part of the manuscript during his talk	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_189", "sentence_190", "sentence_191", "sentence_192", "sentence_193"], "communicatesContent": "You have presented an inch or two of it to my examination all the time that you have been talking. It would be a poor expert who could not give the date of a document within a decade or so. You may possibly have read my little monograph upon the subject. I put that at 1730. The exact date is 1742."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_6	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer removes the manuscript from his breast-pocket	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_194"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_7	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says Sir Charles Baskerville entrusted the family paper to him	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_195", "sentence_196", "sentence_197", "sentence_198"], "communicatesContent": "This family paper was committed to my care by Sir Charles Baskerville, whose sudden and tragic death some three months ago created so much excitement in Devonshire. I may say that I was his personal friend as well as his medical attendant. He was a strong-minded man, sir, shrewd, practical, and as unimaginative as I am myself. Yet he took this document very seriously, and his mind was prepared for just such an end as did eventually overtake him."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_8	event	\N	Holmes takes the manuscript and flattens it on his knee	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_199"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_9	event	\N	Holmes explains the manuscript dating clues	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_200", "sentence_201"], "communicatesContent": "You will observe, Watson, the alternative use of the long _s_ and the short. It is one of several indications which enabled me to fix the date."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_10	event	\N	Watson looks over Holmes's shoulder at the manuscript	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_202", "sentence_203"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_11	event	\N	Holmes says the manuscript appears to be a statement about a Baskerville family legend	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_204", "sentence_205"], "communicatesContent": "It appears to be a statement of some sort. Yes, it is a statement of a certain legend which runs in the Baskerville family."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_12	event	\N	Holmes says Mortimer wants help with a modern and practical matter	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_206", "sentence_207", "sentence_208", "sentence_209", "sentence_210"], "communicatesContent": "But I understand that it is something more modern and practical upon which you wish to consult me? Most modern. A most practical, pressing matter, which must be decided within twenty-four hours. But the manuscript is short and is intimately connected with the affair. With your permission I will read it to you."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_13	event	\N	Mortimer turns the manuscript to the light and reads it aloud	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_212"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_14	event	\N	The manuscript states the origin story of the Hound of the Baskervilles	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": "the_manuscript", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_213", "sentence_214", "sentence_215"], "communicatesContent": "Of the origin of the Hound of the Baskervilles there have been many statements, yet as I come in a direct line from Hugo Baskerville, and as I had the story from my father, who also had it from his, I have set it down with all belief that it occurred even as is here set forth. And I would have you believe, my sons, that the same Justice which punishes sin may also most graciously forgive it, and that no ban is so heavy but that by prayer and repentance it may be removed. Learn then from this story not to fear the fruits of the past, but rather to be circumspect in the future, that those foul passions whereby our family has suffered so grievously may not again be loosed to our undoing."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_15	event	\N	The manuscript narrates Hugo Baskerville holding the Baskerville manor during the Great Rebellion and being a wild, profane, godless man	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_216", "sentence_217"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_7_21	event	\N	The manuscript narrates the maiden escaping by ivy and going homeward across the moor	6	{"section": "section_7", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_223"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_1	event	\N	Hugo finds his captive's cage empty	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_224"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_2	event	\N	Hugo announces he will yield himself to evil powers if he overtakes the maiden	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": "hugo_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_225"], "communicatesContent": "he would that very night render his body and soul to the Powers of Evil if he might but overtake the wench"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_3	event	\N	One of the company calls for the hounds to be put on the maiden	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": "hugo_companions", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_226"], "communicatesContent": "they should put the hounds upon her"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_4	event	\N	Hugo orders the mare saddled and the pack unkenneled, then sets the hounds on the maid's kerchief and rides out	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_227"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_5	event	\N	The revellers take horse and begin pursuit	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_231"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_6	event	\N	The revellers question the shepherd about the hunt	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": "the_revellers", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_233"], "communicatesContent": "if he had seen the hunt"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_7	event	\N	The shepherd says he saw the maiden with the hounds on her track and saw Hugo Baskerville pass him on a black mare	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": "the_shepherd", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_234", "sentence_235"], "communicatesContent": "he had indeed seen the unhappy maiden, with the hounds upon her track; Hugo Baskerville passed me upon his black mare, and there ran mute behind him such a hound of hell as God forbid should ever be at my heels"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_8	event	\N	The revellers ride onward	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_236"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_9	event	\N	A black mare passes with an empty saddle	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_237"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_10	event	\N	The revellers follow the mare across the moor in fear	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_238"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_11	event	\N	The company halts at the head of the goyal beside the hounds	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_239", "sentence_240", "sentence_241"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_12	event	\N	Three bold revellers ride down the goyal	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_242"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_13	event	\N	The three find the maiden dead in the clearing with Hugo Baskerville nearby and the black beast over him	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_243", "sentence_244", "sentence_245"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_14	event	\N	The black beast tears out Hugo Baskerville's throat and the three flee screaming	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_246"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_8_15	event	\N	One reveller dies that night and two others are broken for life	7	{"section": "section_8", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_247"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_9_1	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer finishes reading the family narrative and looks at Holmes	8	{"section": "section_9", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_254"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_9_2	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer draws a newspaper from his pocket	8	{"section": "section_9", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_260"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_9_3	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer presents the Devon County Chronicle to Holmes	8	{"section": "section_9", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_261", "sentence_262", "sentence_263"], "communicatesContent": "Now, Mr. Holmes, we will give you something a little more recent. This is the Devon County Chronicle of May 14th of this year. It is a short account of the facts elicited at the death of Sir Charles Baskerville which occurred a few days before that date."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_9_4	event	\N	Holmes leans forward and becomes intent	8	{"section": "section_9", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_264"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_9_5	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer begins reading the newspaper account of Sir Charles Baskerville's death	8	{"section": "section_9", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_265", "sentence_266", "sentence_267", "sentence_268", "sentence_269", "sentence_270", "sentence_271", "sentence_272", "sentence_273", "sentence_274", "sentence_275", "sentence_276", "sentence_277", "sentence_278", "sentence_279"], "communicatesContent": "The recent sudden death of Sir Charles Baskerville, whose name has been mentioned as the probable Liberal candidate for Mid-Devon at the next election, has cast a gloom over the county. Though Sir Charles had resided at Baskerville Hall for a comparatively short period his amiability of character and extreme generosity had won the affection and respect of all who had been brought into contact with him. In these days of nouveaux riches it is refreshing to find a case where the scion of an old county family which has fallen upon evil days is able to make his own fortune and to bring it back with him to restore the fallen grandeur of his line. Sir Charles made large sums of money in South African speculation. He realised his gains and returned to England with them. It is only two years since he took up his residence at Baskerville Hall, and large schemes of reconstruction and improvement were interrupted by his death. Being childless, it was his desire that the whole countryside should profit by his good fortune. His generous donations to local and county charities have been frequently chronicled. The circumstances connected with the death of Sir Charles cannot be said to have been entirely cleared up by the inquest, but enough has been done to dispose of rumours. There is no reason whatever to suspect foul play, or to imagine that death could be from any but natural causes. Sir Charles was a widower and in some ways of an eccentric habit of mind. His indoor servants at Baskerville Hall consisted of a married couple named Barrymore, the husband acting as butler and the wife as housekeeper. Their evidence, corroborated by that of several friends, tends to show that Sir Charles's health has for some time been impaired, and points especially to some affection of the heart, manifesting itself in changes of colour, breathlessness, and acute attacks of nervous depression. Dr. James Mortimer, the friend and medical attendant of the deceased, has given evidence to the same effect."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_1	event	\N	Sir Charles Baskerville walks the yew alley before bed each night	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_281", "sentence_282"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_2	event	\N	Sir Charles declares he will start for London the next day and orders Barrymore to prepare his luggage	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": "sir_charles_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_283"], "communicatesContent": "I intend to start next day for London, and Barrymore is to prepare my luggage."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_3	event	\N	Sir Charles goes out for his nocturnal walk and smokes a cigar	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_284"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_4	event	\N	Sir Charles never returns	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_285"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_5	event	\N	Barrymore finds the hall door open and goes in search of his master with a lantern	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_286"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_6	event	\N	Sir Charles's footmarks are traced down the alley after the wet day	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_287"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_7	event	\N	Sir Charles stands for a time by the moor-gate	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_288", "sentence_289"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_8	event	\N	Sir Charles proceeds down the alley and his body is discovered at the far end	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_290"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_9	event	\N	Barrymore reports that Sir Charles's footprints altered after the moor-gate	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_291"], "communicatesContent": "My master's footprints altered their character from the time that he passed the moor-gate, and he appeared from thence onward to have been walking upon his toes."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_10	event	\N	Murphy is present on the moor and hears cries	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": "murphy", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_292", "sentence_293"], "communicatesContent": "I heard cries."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_11	event	\N	The post-mortem examination shows long-standing organic disease and the coroner's jury returns a verdict in accordance with the medical evidence	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_295"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_12	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer refolds his paper and replaces it in his pocket	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_300"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_13	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer tells Holmes that these are the public facts about Sir Charles Baskerville's death	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_301"], "communicatesContent": "Those are the public facts, Mr. Holmes, in connection with the death of Sir Charles Baskerville."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_14	event	\N	Holmes asks for the private facts	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_302", "sentence_304", "sentence_306"], "communicatesContent": "Then let me have the private ones."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_10_15	event	\N	Holmes assumes an impassive and judicial expression	9	{"section": "section_10", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_307"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_1	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says he has not confided this information to anyone	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_308"], "communicatesContent": "I am telling that which I have not confided to anyone."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_2	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer explains his motive for withholding the information from the coroner's inquiry	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_309"], "communicatesContent": "My motive for withholding it from the coroner’s inquiry is that a man of science shrinks from placing himself in the public position of seeming to indorse a popular superstition."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_3	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer explains a further motive for withholding the information	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_310"], "communicatesContent": "I had the further motive that Baskerville Hall, as the paper says, would certainly remain untenanted if anything were done to increase its already rather grim reputation."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_4	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says he thought he was justified in telling less than he knew	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_311"], "communicatesContent": "For both these reasons I thought that I was justified in telling rather less than I knew, since no practical good could result from it, but with you there is no reason why I should not be perfectly frank."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_5	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer describes the moor as sparsely inhabited	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_312"], "communicatesContent": "The moor is very sparsely inhabited, and those who live near each other are thrown very much together."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_6	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says he saw a good deal of Sir Charles Baskerville	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_313"], "communicatesContent": "For this reason I saw a good deal of Sir Charles Baskerville."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_7	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer names the other educated men nearby	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_314"], "communicatesContent": "With the exception of Mr. Frankland, of Lafter Hall, and Mr. Stapleton, the naturalist, there are no other men of education within many miles."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_8	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer describes Sir Charles as retiring and says illness brought them together	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_315"], "communicatesContent": "Sir Charles was a retiring man, but the chance of his illness brought us together, and a community of interests in science kept us so."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_9	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says Sir Charles brought back scientific information from South Africa and they discussed anatomy together	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_316"], "communicatesContent": "He had brought back much scientific information from South Africa, and many a charming evening we have spent together discussing the comparative anatomy of the Bushman and the Hottentot."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_10	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says Sir Charles's nervous system becomes strained	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_317"], "communicatesContent": "Within the last few months it became increasingly plain to me that Sir Charles’s nervous system was strained to the breaking point."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_11	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says Sir Charles takes the legend to heart	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_318"], "communicatesContent": "He had taken this legend which I have read you exceedingly to heart—so much so that, although he would walk in his own grounds, nothing would induce him to go out upon the moor at night."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_12	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says Sir Charles was convinced a dreadful fate overhung his family	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_319"], "communicatesContent": "Incredible as it may appear to you, Mr. Holmes, he was honestly convinced that a dreadful fate overhung his family, and certainly the records which he was able to give of his ancestors were not encouraging."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_13	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says Sir Charles is haunted by a ghastly presence and asks about strange creatures and hounds	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_320", "sentence_321"], "communicatesContent": "The idea of some ghastly presence constantly haunted him, and on more than one occasion he has asked me whether I had on my medical journeys at night ever seen any strange creature or heard the baying of a hound. The latter question he put to me several times, and always with a voice which vibrated with excitement."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_14	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer drives up to Sir Charles's house in the evening	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_322"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_15	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer sees Sir Charles at the hall door	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_323"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_16	event	\N	Sir Charles stares past Dr. Mortimer with dreadful horror	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_324"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_17	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer turns and sees something like a large black calf at the head of the drive	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_325"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_18	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer goes to the spot where the animal was and looks around for it	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_326"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_19	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer finds that the animal is gone	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_327"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_20	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer stays with Sir Charles for the evening and Sir Charles entrusts him with a narrative	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "sir_charles_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_328"], "communicatesContent": "the narrative"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_21	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says he considered the episode trivial at the time	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_329"], "communicatesContent": "I mention this small episode because it assumes some importance in view of the tragedy which followed, but I was convinced at the time that the matter was entirely trivial and that his excitement had no justification."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_22	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says he advised Sir Charles to go to London	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_330"], "communicatesContent": "It was at my advice that Sir Charles was about to go to London."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_23	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says Sir Charles's heart was affected and anxiety was harming his health	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_331", "sentence_332"], "communicatesContent": "His heart was, I knew, affected, and the constant anxiety in which he lived, however chimerical the cause of it might be, was evidently having a serious effect upon his health. I thought that a few months among the distractions of town would send him back a new man."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_24	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says Mr. Stapleton agreed	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_333"], "communicatesContent": "Mr. Stapleton, a mutual friend who was much concerned at his state of health, was of the same opinion."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_11_25	event	\N	A terrible catastrophe occurs at the last instant	10	{"section": "section_11", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_334"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_12_1	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer recounts the discovery and examination of Sir Charles Baskerville's body	11	{"section": "section_12", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_335", "sentence_336", "sentence_337", "sentence_338", "sentence_339", "sentence_340", "sentence_341", "sentence_342", "sentence_343", "sentence_344", "sentence_345", "sentence_346", "sentence_347", "sentence_348"], "communicatesContent": "On the night of Sir Charles’s death Barrymore the butler, who made the discovery, sent Perkins the groom on horseback to me, and as I was sitting up late I was able to reach Baskerville Hall within an hour of the event. I checked and corroborated all the facts which were mentioned at the inquest. I followed the footsteps down the yew alley, I saw the spot at the moor-gate where he seemed to have waited, I remarked the change in the shape of the prints after that point, I noted that there were no other footsteps save those of Barrymore on the soft gravel, and finally I carefully examined the body, which had not been touched until my arrival. Sir Charles lay on his face, his arms out, his fingers dug into the ground, and his features convulsed with some strong emotion to such an extent that I could hardly have sworn to his identity. There was certainly no physical injury of any kind. But one false statement was made by Barrymore at the inquest. He said that there were no traces upon the ground round the body. He did not observe any. But I did—some little distance off, but fresh and clear. Footprints. They were the footprints of a gigantic hound."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_14_7	event	\N	Holmes suggests that Sir Henry should go to the home of his fathers	13	{"section": "section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_461"], "communicatesContent": "Why should he not go to the home of his fathers?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_22_8	event	\N	The cab driver is signaled and the cab speeds away down Regent Street	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_814"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_13_1	event	\N	Holmes questions Dr. Mortimer about the marks, the alley, and Sir Charles's final movements	12	{"section": "section_13", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_354", "sentence_355", "sentence_356", "sentence_357", "sentence_358", "sentence_359", "sentence_360", "sentence_361", "sentence_362", "sentence_363", "sentence_364", "sentence_365", "sentence_366", "sentence_367", "sentence_368", "sentence_369", "sentence_370", "sentence_371", "sentence_372", "sentence_373", "sentence_374", "sentence_375", "sentence_376", "sentence_377", "sentence_378", "sentence_379", "sentence_380", "sentence_381", "sentence_382", "sentence_383", "sentence_384", "sentence_385", "sentence_386", "sentence_387", "sentence_388", "sentence_389", "sentence_390", "sentence_391", "sentence_392", "sentence_393", "sentence_394", "sentence_395", "sentence_396", "sentence_397", "sentence_398", "sentence_399", "sentence_400", "sentence_401", "sentence_402", "sentence_403", "sentence_404", "sentence_405", "sentence_406", "sentence_407", "sentence_408", "sentence_409", "sentence_410"], "communicatesContent": "You saw this? And you said nothing? How was it that no one else saw it? There are many sheep-dogs on the moor? You say it was large? But it had not approached the body? What sort of night was it? But not actually raining? What is the alley like? Is there anything between the hedges and the walk? I understand that the yew hedge is penetrated at one point by a gate? Is there any other opening? So that to reach the yew alley one either has to come down it from the house or else to enter it by the moor-gate? Had Sir Charles reached this? Now, tell me, Dr. Mortimer—and this is important—the marks which you saw were on the path and not on the grass? Were they on the same side of the path as the moor-gate? Was the wicket-gate closed? How high was it? Then anyone could have got over it? And what marks did you see by the wicket-gate? Did no one examine? And found nothing? How do you know that? But the marks?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_13_2	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer answers Holmes's questions about the marks, gate, and Sir Charles's pause	12	{"section": "section_13", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_355", "sentence_357", "sentence_359", "sentence_360", "sentence_362", "sentence_364", "sentence_366", "sentence_368", "sentence_370", "sentence_372", "sentence_373", "sentence_375", "sentence_377", "sentence_379", "sentence_381", "sentence_383", "sentence_385", "sentence_387", "sentence_391", "sentence_393", "sentence_395", "sentence_397", "sentence_400", "sentence_401", "sentence_402", "sentence_403", "sentence_405", "sentence_409", "sentence_410"], "communicatesContent": "As clearly as I see you. What was the use? The marks were some twenty yards from the body and no one gave them a thought. I don’t suppose I should have done so had I not known this legend. No doubt, but this was no sheep-dog. Enormous. No. Damp and raw. No. There are two lines of old yew hedge, twelve feet high and impenetrable. The walk in the centre is about eight feet across. Yes, there is a strip of grass about six feet broad on either side. Yes, the wicket-gate which leads on to the moor. None. There is an exit through a summer-house at the far end. No; he lay about fifty yards from it. No marks could show on the grass. Yes; they were on the edge of the path on the same side as the moor-gate. Closed and padlocked. About four feet high. Yes. None in particular. Yes, I examined, myself. It was all very confused. Sir Charles had evidently stood there for five or ten minutes. Because the ash had twice dropped from his cigar. He had left his own marks all over that small patch of gravel. I could discern no others."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_13_3	event	\N	Holmes reacts with excitement and laments not being called in	12	{"section": "section_13", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_353", "sentence_411", "sentence_412", "sentence_413", "sentence_414", "sentence_415", "sentence_416", "sentence_417"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_13_4	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer explains why he did not call Holmes in	12	{"section": "section_13", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_418", "sentence_419", "sentence_420", "sentence_421", "sentence_422"], "communicatesContent": "I could not call you in, Mr. Holmes, without disclosing these facts to the world, and I have already given my reasons for not wishing to do so. Besides, besides— There is a realm in which the most acute and most experienced of detectives is helpless. You mean that the thing is supernatural?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_14_1	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer explains that several people had seen a creature on the moor before the tragedy	13	{"section": "section_14", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_425", "sentence_427", "sentence_428", "sentence_429", "sentence_430"], "communicatesContent": "Since the tragedy, several incidents have come to my ears; before the terrible event occurred, several people had seen a creature upon the moor that corresponds with the Baskerville demon, could not possibly be any animal known to science, was huge, luminous, ghastly, and spectral, and there is a reign of terror in the district."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_14_2	event	\N	Holmes says he has confined his investigations to this world	13	{"section": "section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_433", "sentence_434", "sentence_435", "sentence_436"], "communicatesContent": "I have hitherto confined my investigations to this world. In a modest way I have combated evil, but to take on the Father of Evil himself would, perhaps, be too ambitious a task. Yet you must admit that the footmark is material."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_14_3	event	\N	Mortimer says the original hound was material and diabolical	13	{"section": "section_14", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_437"], "communicatesContent": "The original hound was material enough to tug a man’s throat out, and yet he was diabolical as well."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_14_4	event	\N	Holmes questions why Mortimer has come to consult him	13	{"section": "section_14", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_438", "sentence_439", "sentence_440", "sentence_441", "sentence_442", "sentence_443"], "communicatesContent": "I see that you have quite gone over to the supernaturalists. But now, Dr. Mortimer, tell me this. If you hold these views, why have you come to consult me at all? You tell me in the same breath that it is useless to investigate Sir Charles’s death, and that you desire me to do it. Then, how can I assist you?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_14_5	event	\N	Mortimer says Sir Henry Baskerville is arriving at Waterloo Station and asks what to do with him	13	{"section": "section_14", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_444"], "communicatesContent": "By advising me as to what I should do with Sir Henry Baskerville, who arrives at Waterloo Station in exactly one hour and a quarter."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_14_6	event	\N	Mortimer identifies Sir Henry Baskerville as the heir and describes the family succession	13	{"section": "section_14", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_445", "sentence_446", "sentence_447", "sentence_448", "sentence_449", "sentence_450", "sentence_451", "sentence_452", "sentence_453", "sentence_454", "sentence_455", "sentence_456", "sentence_457", "sentence_458", "sentence_459", "sentence_460"], "communicatesContent": "Yes. On the death of Sir Charles we inquired for this young gentleman and found that he had been farming in Canada. From the accounts which have reached us he is an excellent fellow in every way. I speak now not as a medical man but as a trustee and executor of Sir Charles’s will. None. The only other kinsman whom we have been able to trace was Rodger Baskerville, the youngest of three brothers of whom poor Sir Charles was the elder. The second brother, who died young, is the father of this lad Henry. The third, Rodger, was the black sheep of the family. He made England too hot to hold him, fled to Central America, and died there in 1876 of yellow fever. Henry is the last of the Baskervilles. In one hour and five minutes I meet him at Waterloo Station. I have had a wire that he arrived at Southampton this morning. Now, Mr. Holmes, what would you advise me to do with him?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_14_8	event	\N	Mortimer says every Baskerville who goes to the home meets with an evil fate and asks Holmes for advice	13	{"section": "section_14", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_462", "sentence_463", "sentence_464", "sentence_465", "sentence_466", "sentence_467"], "communicatesContent": "It seems natural, does it not? And yet, consider that every Baskerville who goes there meets with an evil fate. I feel sure that if Sir Charles could have spoken with me before his death he would have warned me against bringing this, the last of the old race, and the heir to great wealth, to that deadly place. And yet it cannot be denied that the prosperity of the whole poor, bleak countryside depends upon his presence. All the good work which has been done by Sir Charles will crash to the ground if there is no tenant of the Hall. I fear lest I should be swayed too much by my own obvious interest in the matter, and that is why I bring the case before you and ask for your advice."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_1	event	\N	Holmes recommends that Mortimer take a cab and meet Sir Henry Baskerville at Waterloo	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_479"], "communicatesContent": "I recommend, sir, that you take a cab, call off your spaniel who is scratching at my front door, and proceed to Waterloo to meet Sir Henry Baskerville."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_2	event	\N	Holmes instructs Mortimer to say nothing to Sir Henry until Holmes decides the matter	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_480", "sentence_481"], "communicatesContent": "And then you will say nothing to him at all until I have made up my mind about the matter."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_3	event	\N	Mortimer agrees to Holmes's request to meet the next day with Sir Henry	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_484", "sentence_485"], "communicatesContent": "I will do so, Mr. Holmes."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_4	event	\N	Mortimer leaves after scribbling the appointment on his shirt-cuff	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_486"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_5	event	\N	Holmes stops Mortimer at the head of the stair and asks about the apparition on the moor	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_487", "sentence_488", "sentence_489"], "communicatesContent": "Only one more question, Dr. Mortimer. You say that before Sir Charles Baskerville’s death several people saw this apparition upon the moor?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_6	event	\N	Mortimer answers that three people saw the apparition before Sir Charles Baskerville's death and that he has not heard of any after	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_490", "sentence_491", "sentence_492"], "communicatesContent": "Three people did. I have not heard of any."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_7	event	\N	Holmes returns to his seat and resumes work on the case	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_495"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_8	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson to go out, request tobacco from Bradley's, and not return before evening	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_496", "sentence_497", "sentence_498", "sentence_499", "sentence_500", "sentence_501", "sentence_502", "sentence_503"], "communicatesContent": "Going out, Watson? Unless I can help you. No, my dear fellow, it is at the hour of action that I turn to you for aid. But this is splendid, really unique from some points of view. When you pass Bradley’s, would you ask him to send up a pound of the strongest shag tobacco? Thank you. It would be as well if you could make it convenient not to return before evening. Then I should be very glad to compare impressions as to this most interesting problem which has been submitted to us this morning."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_9	event	\N	Watson spends the day at his club and does not return to Baker Street until evening	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_504", "sentence_505", "sentence_506"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_15_10	event	\N	Watson enters the sitting-room and finds it filled with tobacco smoke while Holmes smokes in an armchair	14	{"section": "section_15", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_507", "sentence_508", "sentence_509", "sentence_510"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_16_1	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes comments on Watson's cold and the thick atmosphere	15	{"section": "section_16", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_511", "sentence_512", "sentence_513", "sentence_514", "sentence_515", "sentence_516", "sentence_517", "sentence_518", "sentence_519", "sentence_520", "sentence_521"], "communicatesContent": "Caught cold, Watson? ... Open the window, then! You have been at your club all day, I perceive. Am I right?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_16_2	event	\N	Holmes laughs at Watson's bewildered expression	15	{"section": "section_16", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_522"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_16_3	event	\N	Holmes explains how he inferred Watson's movements and speaks about his own absence	15	{"section": "section_16", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_523", "sentence_524", "sentence_525", "sentence_526", "sentence_527", "sentence_528", "sentence_529", "sentence_530", "sentence_531", "sentence_532", "sentence_533", "sentence_534", "sentence_535", "sentence_536", "sentence_537", "sentence_538", "sentence_539", "sentence_540", "sentence_541"], "communicatesContent": "There is a delightful freshness about you, Watson, which makes it a pleasure to exercise any small powers which I possess at your expense. A gentleman goes forth on a showery and miry day. He returns immaculate in the evening with the gloss still on his hat and his boots. He has been a fixture therefore all day. He is not a man with intimate friends. Where, then, could he have been? Is it not obvious? The world is full of obvious things which nobody by any chance ever observes. Where do you think that I have been? A fixture also. On the contrary, I have been to Devonshire. In spirit? Exactly. My body has remained in this armchair and has, I regret to observe, consumed in my absence two large pots of coffee and an incredible amount of tobacco. After you left I sent down to Stamford's for the Ordnance map of this portion of the moor, and my spirit has hovered over it all day. I flatter myself that I could find my way about. Very large."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_16_4	event	\N	Holmes unrolls a map section and indicates the district under discussion	15	{"section": "section_16", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_542", "sentence_543", "sentence_544", "sentence_545", "sentence_546", "sentence_547", "sentence_548", "sentence_549", "sentence_550", "sentence_551", "sentence_552", "sentence_553", "sentence_554", "sentence_555"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_16_5	event	\N	Holmes discusses the possibility of supernatural forces and frames the questions of the case	15	{"section": "section_16", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_556", "sentence_557", "sentence_558", "sentence_559", "sentence_560", "sentence_561", "sentence_562", "sentence_563", "sentence_564", "sentence_565", "sentence_566", "sentence_567", "sentence_568"], "communicatesContent": "It must be a wild place. The setting is a worthy one. If the devil did desire to have a hand in the affairs of men— The devil's agents may be of flesh and blood, may they not? There are two questions waiting for us at the outset. The one is whether any crime has been committed at all; the second is, what is the crime and how was it committed? Of course, if Dr. Mortimer's surmise should be correct, and we are dealing with forces outside the ordinary laws of Nature, there is an end of our investigation. But we are bound to exhaust all other hypotheses before falling back upon this one. I think we'll shut that window again, if you don't mind. It is a singular thing, but I find that a concentrated atmosphere helps a concentration of thought. I have not pushed it to the length of getting into a box to think, but that is the logical outcome of my convictions. Have you turned the case over in your mind?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_17_1	event	\N	Holmes discusses the footprints and the man's movement	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_569", "sentence_570", "sentence_571", "sentence_572", "sentence_573", "sentence_574", "sentence_575", "sentence_576", "sentence_577", "sentence_578", "sentence_579", "sentence_580", "sentence_581", "sentence_582", "sentence_583", "sentence_584", "sentence_585", "sentence_586", "sentence_587", "sentence_588", "sentence_589", "sentence_590", "sentence_591", "sentence_592", "sentence_593", "sentence_594", "sentence_595", "sentence_596", "sentence_597", "sentence_598", "sentence_599", "sentence_600"], "communicatesContent": "It is very bewildering. It has certainly a character of its own. There are points of distinction about it. That change in the footprints, for example. Mortimer said that the man had walked on tiptoe down that portion of the alley. He only repeated what some fool had said at the inquest. Why should a man walk on tiptoe down the alley? What then? He was running, Watson—running desperately, running for his life, running until he burst his heart—and fell dead upon his face. Running from what? There lies our problem. There are indications that the man was crazed with fear before ever he began to run. I am presuming that the cause of his fears came to him across the moor. If that were so, and it seems most probable, only a man who had lost his wits would have run from the house instead of towards it. If the gipsy’s evidence may be taken as true, he ran with cries for help in the direction where help was least likely to be. Then, again, whom was he waiting for that night, and why was he waiting for him in the yew alley rather than in his own house? The man was elderly and infirm. We can understand his taking an evening stroll, but the ground was damp and the night inclement. Is it natural that he should stand for five or ten minutes, as Dr. Mortimer, with more practical sense than I should have given him credit for, deduced from the cigar ash? But he went out every evening. I think it unlikely that he waited at the moor-gate every evening. On the contrary, the evidence is that he avoided the moor. That night he waited there. It was the night before he made his departure for London. The thing takes shape, Watson. It becomes coherent. Might I ask you to hand me my violin, and we will postpone all further thought upon this business until we have had the advantage of meeting Dr. Mortimer and Sir Henry Baskerville in the morning."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_17_2	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson to hand him his violin	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_600"], "communicatesContent": "Might I ask you to hand me my violin"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_1	event	\N	Holmes waits in his dressing-gown for the promised interview	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_603"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_2	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer and Sir Henry Baskerville arrive for the appointment	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_604"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_3	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer identifies the visitor as Sir Henry Baskerville	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_607"], "communicatesContent": "This is Sir Henry Baskerville."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_4	event	\N	Sir Henry says he would have come to Holmes on his own account	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_608", "sentence_609"], "communicatesContent": "The strange thing is, if my friend here had not proposed coming round to you this morning I should have come on my own account. I understand that you think out little puzzles, and I’ve had one this morning which wants more thinking out than I am able to give it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_5	event	\N	Holmes invites Sir Henry to take a seat and asks about a remarkable experience in London	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_610", "sentence_611"], "communicatesContent": "Pray take a seat, Sir Henry. Do I understand you to say that you have yourself had some remarkable experience since you arrived in London?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_6	event	\N	Sir Henry says the letter was the only matter of importance that morning	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_612", "sentence_613", "sentence_614"], "communicatesContent": "Nothing of much importance, Mr. Holmes. Only a joke, as like as not. It was this letter, if you can call it a letter, which reached me this morning."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_7	event	\N	Sir Henry places the envelope on the table and the group examines it	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_615", "sentence_616", "sentence_617"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_8	event	\N	Holmes asks who knew Sir Henry was going to the Northumberland Hotel	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_618", "sentence_619"], "communicatesContent": "Who knew that you were going to the Northumberland Hotel?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_9	event	\N	Sir Henry says no one could have known and that they decided after he met Dr. Mortimer	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_620", "sentence_621"], "communicatesContent": "No one could have known. We only decided after I met Dr. Mortimer."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_10	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says he had been staying with a friend	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_622", "sentence_623"], "communicatesContent": "No, I had been staying with a friend."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_11	event	\N	Sir Henry says there was no possible indication that they intended to go to the hotel	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_624"], "communicatesContent": "There was no possible indication that we intended to go to this hotel."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_12	event	\N	Holmes remarks that someone is deeply interested in Sir Henry's movements	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_625", "sentence_626"], "communicatesContent": "Someone seems to be very deeply interested in your movements."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_13	event	\N	Holmes opens the folded paper from the envelope and spreads it on the table	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_627", "sentence_628", "sentence_629", "sentence_630", "sentence_631", "sentence_632"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_14	event	\N	Sir Henry asks Holmes for the meaning of the note	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_633"], "communicatesContent": "Perhaps you will tell me, Mr. Holmes, what in thunder is the meaning of that, and who it is that takes so much interest in my affairs?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_15	event	\N	Holmes asks Dr. Mortimer what he makes of the note	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_634", "sentence_635"], "communicatesContent": "What do you make of it, Dr. Mortimer? You must allow that there is nothing supernatural about this, at any rate?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_16	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer replies that the note might come from someone convinced the business is supernatural	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_636"], "communicatesContent": "No, sir, but it might very well come from someone who was convinced that the business is supernatural."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_17	event	\N	Sir Henry sharply asks what business Holmes and Mortimer mean	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_637", "sentence_638", "sentence_639"], "communicatesContent": "What business?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_18	event	\N	Holmes promises to share their knowledge before Sir Henry leaves the room	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_640", "sentence_641"], "communicatesContent": "You shall share our knowledge before you leave this room, Sir Henry. I promise you that."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_18_19	event	\N	Holmes says he will confine discussion to the document and asks Watson for yesterday's Times	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_642", "sentence_643", "sentence_644", "sentence_645", "sentence_646", "sentence_647", "sentence_648"], "communicatesContent": "We will confine ourselves for the present with your permission to this very interesting document, which must have been put together and posted yesterday evening. Have you yesterday’s Times, Watson? Might I trouble you for it—the inside page, please, with the leading articles? Capital article this on free trade. Permit me to give you an extract from it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_1	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson for his reaction	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_650"], "communicatesContent": "What do you think of that, Watson?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_2	event	\N	Holmes laughs and rubs his hands together	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_651"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_3	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the sentiment is admirable	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_652"], "communicatesContent": "Don't you think that is an admirable sentiment?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_4	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer looks at Holmes with professional interest and Sir Henry looks at Watson puzzled	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_653"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_5	event	\N	Sir Henry says they have strayed from the trail	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_654"], "communicatesContent": "I don't know much about the tariff and things of that kind, but it seems to me we've got a bit off the trail so far as that note is concerned."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_6	event	\N	Holmes says they are on the trail and that Watson has not grasped the sentence's significance	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_655", "sentence_656"], "communicatesContent": "On the contrary, I think we are particularly hot upon the trail, Sir Henry. Watson here knows more about my methods than you do, but I fear that even he has not quite grasped the significance of this sentence."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_7	event	\N	Watson says he sees no connection	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_657"], "communicatesContent": "No, I confess that I see no connection."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_8	event	\N	Holmes explains that the sentence fragments were extracted from the quotation	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_658", "sentence_659", "sentence_660"], "communicatesContent": "And yet, my dear Watson, there is so very close a connection that the one is extracted out of the other. 'You,' 'your,' 'your,' 'life,' 'reason,' 'value,' 'keep away,' 'from the.' Don't you see now whence these words have been taken?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_9	event	\N	Sir Henry reacts to Holmes's deduction	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_661", "sentence_662", "sentence_663"], "communicatesContent": "By thunder, you're right! Well, if that isn't smart!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_10	event	\N	Holmes says the cutting was done in one piece	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_664"], "communicatesContent": "If any possible doubt remained it is settled by the fact that 'keep away' and 'from the' are cut out in one piece."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_11	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer praises Holmes's deduction	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_666", "sentence_667", "sentence_668"], "communicatesContent": "Really, Mr. Holmes, this exceeds anything which I could have imagined. I could understand anyone saying that the words were from a newspaper; but that you should name which, and add that it came from the leading article, is really one of the most remarkable things which I have ever known. How did you do it?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_8	event	\N	Sir Henry says the boots were tan and unvarnished and that is why he put them out	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_744", "sentence_745"], "communicatesContent": "They were tan boots and had never been varnished. That was why I put them out."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_9	event	\N	Holmes asks whether on arriving in London yesterday Sir Henry went out and bought a pair of boots	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_746"], "communicatesContent": "Then I understand that on your arrival in London yesterday you went out at once and bought a pair of boots?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_1	event	\N	Holmes questions Watson about the man in the cab	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_821", "sentence_822", "sentence_823"], "communicatesContent": "Who was the man? A spy?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_12	event	\N	Holmes compares identifying typefaces to identifying skulls	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_669", "sentence_670", "sentence_671", "sentence_672", "sentence_673", "sentence_674", "sentence_675", "sentence_676", "sentence_677", "sentence_678", "sentence_679"], "communicatesContent": "I presume, Doctor, that you could tell the skull of a negro from that of an Esquimau? Because that is my special hobby. The differences are obvious. The supra-orbital crest, the facial angle, the maxillary curve, the— But this is my special hobby, and the differences are equally obvious. There is as much difference to my eyes between the leaded bourgeois type of a Times article and the slovenly print of an evening half-penny paper as there could be between your negro and your Esquimau. The detection of types is one of the most elementary branches of knowledge to the special expert in crime, though I confess that once when I was very young I confused the Leeds Mercury with the Western Morning News. But a Times leader is entirely distinctive, and these words could have been taken from nothing else. As it was done yesterday the strong probability was that we should find the words in yesterday’s issue."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_19_13	event	\N	Sir Henry summarizes the cutting of the message	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_680", "sentence_681", "sentence_682", "sentence_683", "sentence_684", "sentence_685", "sentence_686", "sentence_687", "sentence_688", "sentence_689"], "communicatesContent": "Someone cut out this message with a scissors—"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_20_1	event	\N	Holmes explains the letter's clues and composition	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_692", "sentence_693", "sentence_694", "sentence_695", "sentence_696", "sentence_697", "sentence_698", "sentence_699", "sentence_700", "sentence_701"], "communicatesContent": "There are indications, but the clues have been carefully removed; the address is printed in rough characters; the Times is usually found only in highly educated hands; the letter was composed by an educated man posing as uneducated; the writing may be known or become known by you; the pasted words are uneven; 'Life' is out of place; the cutter may have been careless or agitated and in a hurry; the address may have been written in a hotel; the pen and ink suggest hotel writing; the message may be traced by searching hotel waste-paper baskets around Charing Cross for the remains of the mutilated Times leader."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_20_2	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer comments on Holmes's reasoning	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_702", "sentence_703", "sentence_704", "sentence_705"], "communicatesContent": "We are coming now rather into the region of guesswork."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_20_3	event	\N	Holmes inspects the foolscap and finds nothing on it	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_715", "sentence_716", "sentence_717", "sentence_718", "sentence_719"], "communicatesContent": "Nothing. It is a blank half-sheet of paper, without even a water-mark upon it. I think we have drawn as much as we can from this curious letter; and now, Sir Henry, has anything else of interest happened to you since you have been in London?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_20_4	event	\N	Holmes asks whether anyone has followed or watched Sir Henry	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_722", "sentence_725", "sentence_726", "sentence_728"], "communicatesContent": "You have not observed anyone follow or watch you? You have nothing else to report to us before we go into this matter? I think anything out of the ordinary routine of life well worth reporting."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_20_5	event	\N	Sir Henry replies about the inquiry	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_720", "sentence_721", "sentence_723", "sentence_724", "sentence_727"], "communicatesContent": "Why, no, Mr. Holmes. I think not. I seem to have walked right into the thick of a dime novel. Why in thunder should anyone follow or watch me? Well, it depends upon what you think worth reporting."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_1	event	\N	Sir Henry smiles	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_729"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_2	event	\N	Sir Henry says he has spent nearly all his time in the States and Canada and hopes losing a boot is not ordinary life in Britain	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_730", "sentence_731"], "communicatesContent": "I don’t know much of British life yet, for I have spent nearly all my time in the States and in Canada. But I hope that to lose one of your boots is not part of the ordinary routine of life over here."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_3	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Sir Henry has lost one of his boots	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_732"], "communicatesContent": "You have lost one of your boots?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_4	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says the boot is only mislaid and will be found when Sir Henry returns to the hotel	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_733", "sentence_734", "sentence_735"], "communicatesContent": "My dear sir, it is only mislaid. You will find it when you return to the hotel. What is the use of troubling Mr. Holmes with trifles of this kind?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_5	event	\N	Holmes says he asked for anything outside the ordinary routine	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_736", "sentence_737", "sentence_738"], "communicatesContent": "Well, he asked me for anything outside the ordinary routine. Exactly, however foolish the incident may seem. You have lost one of your boots, you say?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_6	event	\N	Sir Henry explains that he put both boots outside his door, found only one in the morning, could not get sense from the boot-cleaner, and bought the pair in the Strand last night without wearing them	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_739", "sentence_740", "sentence_741", "sentence_742"], "communicatesContent": "Well, mislaid it, anyhow. I put them both outside my door last night, and there was only one in the morning. I could get no sense out of the chap who cleans them. The worst of it is that I only bought the pair last night in the Strand, and I have never had them on."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_7	event	\N	Holmes asks why Sir Henry put unworn boots out to be cleaned	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_743"], "communicatesContent": "If you have never worn them, why did you put them out to be cleaned?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_46_6	event	\N	Sir Henry and the others stay for lunch at Merripit House	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1797"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_10	event	\N	Sir Henry says he did shopping, went round with Dr. Mortimer, bought brown boots, paid six dollars, and had one stolen before wearing them	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_747", "sentence_748", "sentence_749", "sentence_750"], "communicatesContent": "I did a good deal of shopping. Dr. Mortimer here went round with me. You see, if I am to be squire down there I must dress the part, and it may be that I have got a little careless in my ways out West. Among other things I bought these brown boots—gave six dollars for them—and had one stolen before ever I had them on my feet."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_11	event	\N	Holmes remarks that stealing the boot seems useless and says he shares the belief that the missing boot will soon be found	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_751", "sentence_752"], "communicatesContent": "It seems a singularly useless thing to steal. I confess that I share Dr. Mortimer’s belief that it will not be long before the missing boot is found."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_12	event	\N	Sir Henry says he has spoken enough and asks for a full account of what they are driving at	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_753", "sentence_754"], "communicatesContent": "And, now, gentlemen, it seems to me that I have spoken quite enough about the little that I know. It is time that you kept your promise and gave me a full account of what we are all driving at."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_13	event	\N	Holmes agrees the request is reasonable and asks Dr. Mortimer to tell his story	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_755", "sentence_756"], "communicatesContent": "Your request is a very reasonable one. Dr. Mortimer, I think you could not do better than to tell your story as you told it to us."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_14	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer presents the whole case from his papers	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_757"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_15	event	\N	Sir Henry listens to the narrative and exclaims in surprise	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_758"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_16	event	\N	Sir Henry says he has come into an inheritance, has heard of the hound since childhood, did not take it seriously before, and cannot yet make clear his uncle's death	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_759", "sentence_760", "sentence_761", "sentence_762", "sentence_763"], "communicatesContent": "Well, I seem to have come into an inheritance with a vengeance. Of course, I’ve heard of the hound ever since I was in the nursery. It’s the pet story of the family, though I never thought of taking it seriously before. But as to my uncle’s death—well, it all seems boiling up in my head, and I can’t get it clear yet. You don’t seem quite to have made up your mind whether it’s a case for a policeman or a clergyman."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_17	event	\N	Sir Henry says the hotel letter fits into the matter	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_765", "sentence_766"], "communicatesContent": "And now there’s this affair of the letter to me at the hotel. I suppose that fits into its place."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_18	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says someone knows more about what goes on upon the moor than they do	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_767"], "communicatesContent": "It seems to show that someone knows more than we do about what goes on upon the moor."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_19	event	\N	Holmes says someone is not ill-disposed toward Sir Henry because they warn him of danger	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_768"], "communicatesContent": "that someone is not ill-disposed towards you, since they warn you of danger."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_20	event	\N	Sir Henry says the sender may have wanted to scare him away for their own purposes	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_769"], "communicatesContent": "Or it may be that they wish, for their own purposes, to scare me away."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_21	event	\N	Holmes says that possibility is also possible and thanks Dr. Mortimer for introducing an interesting problem	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_770", "sentence_771"], "communicatesContent": "Well, of course, that is possible also. I am very much indebted to you, Dr. Mortimer, for introducing me to a problem which presents several interesting alternatives."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_21_22	event	\N	Holmes says the practical question is whether Sir Henry should go to Baskerville Hall	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_772"], "communicatesContent": "But the practical point which we now have to decide, Sir Henry, is whether it is or is not advisable for you to go to Baskerville Hall."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_22_1	event	\N	Mortimer and Baskerville leave by the front door	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_795"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_22_2	event	\N	Holmes changes into action and quickly gets ready to go out	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_796", "sentence_797", "sentence_798", "sentence_799", "sentence_800"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_22_3	event	\N	Holmes and Watson go into the street	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_800"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_22_4	event	\N	Holmes and Watson follow Mortimer and Baskerville along Oxford Street and Regent Street	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_801", "sentence_806", "sentence_807"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_22_5	event	\N	Mortimer and Baskerville stop and look in a shop window	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_808"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_22_6	event	\N	Holmes also stops and looks into the shop window	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_808"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_22_7	event	\N	Holmes notices a hansom cab and starts after it	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_809", "sentence_810", "sentence_811", "sentence_812", "sentence_813", "sentence_814", "sentence_815", "sentence_816"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_2	event	\N	Holmes explains that Baskerville has been shadowed in town	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_824", "sentence_825", "sentence_826", "sentence_827", "sentence_828", "sentence_829", "sentence_830", "sentence_831"], "communicatesContent": "It was evident from what we have heard that Baskerville has been very closely shadowed by someone since he has been in town. How else could it be known so quickly that it was the Northumberland Hotel which he had chosen? If they had followed him the first day I argued that they would follow him also the second. You may have observed that I twice strolled over to the window while Dr. Mortimer was reading his legend. I was looking out for loiterers in the street, but I saw none. We are dealing with a clever man, Watson. This matter cuts very deep, and though I have not finally made up my mind whether it is a benevolent or a malevolent agency which is in touch with us, I am conscious always of power and design."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_3	event	\N	Holmes follows Dr. Mortimer and his companion	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_832", "sentence_833", "sentence_834", "sentence_835", "sentence_836", "sentence_837"], "communicatesContent": "When our friends left I at once followed them in the hopes of marking down their invisible attendant. So wily was he that he had not trusted himself upon foot, but he had availed himself of a cab so that he could loiter behind or dash past them and so escape their notice. His method had the additional advantage that if they were to take a cab he was all ready to follow them. It puts him in the power of the cabman."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_4	event	\N	Watson regrets not getting the cab number	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_838"], "communicatesContent": "What a pity we did not get the number!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_5	event	\N	Holmes states that cab 2704 is the man	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_839", "sentence_840", "sentence_841", "sentence_842"], "communicatesContent": "My dear Watson, clumsy as I have been, you surely do not seriously imagine that I neglected to get the number? No. 2704 is our man. But that is no use to us for the moment."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_6	event	\N	Holmes explains how he should have followed the cab	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_843", "sentence_844", "sentence_845", "sentence_846", "sentence_847"], "communicatesContent": "I fail to see how you could have done more. On observing the cab I should have instantly turned and walked in the other direction. I should then at my leisure have hired a second cab and followed the first at a respectful distance, or, better still, have driven to the Northumberland Hotel and waited there. When our unknown had followed Baskerville home we should have had the opportunity of playing his own game upon himself and seeing where he made for. As it is, by an indiscreet eagerness, which was taken advantage of with extraordinary quickness and energy by our opponent, we have betrayed ourselves and lost our man."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_7	event	\N	Holmes observes that Dr. Mortimer and his companion have vanished	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_848", "sentence_849", "sentence_850", "sentence_851", "sentence_852", "sentence_853", "sentence_854", "sentence_855", "sentence_856"], "communicatesContent": "We had been sauntering slowly down Regent Street during this conversation, and Dr. Mortimer, with his companion, had long vanished in front of us. There is no object in our following them. The shadow has departed and will not return. We must see what further cards we have in our hands and play them with decision. Could you swear to that man’s face within the cab? And so could I—from which I gather that in all probability it was a false one. A clever man upon so delicate an errand has no use for a beard save to conceal his features. Come in here, Watson!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_8	event	\N	Holmes enters the district messenger office and is greeted by the manager	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_857", "sentence_858", "sentence_859", "sentence_860", "sentence_861", "sentence_862", "sentence_863", "sentence_864", "sentence_865", "sentence_866"], "communicatesContent": "Ah, Wilson, I see you have not forgotten the little case in which I had the good fortune to help you? My dear fellow, you exaggerate. I have some recollection, Wilson, that you had among your boys a lad named Cartwright, who showed some ability during the investigation. thank you! And I should be glad to have change of this five-pound note."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_9	event	\N	Wilson responds to Holmes and confirms Cartwright is still with them	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "wilson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_859", "sentence_860", "sentence_861", "sentence_862", "sentence_863"], "communicatesContent": "No, sir, indeed I have not. You saved my good name, and perhaps my life. My dear fellow, you exaggerate. I have some recollection, Wilson, that you had among your boys a lad named Cartwright, who showed some ability during the investigation. Yes, sir, he is still with us."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_23_10	event	\N	Holmes requests change for a five-pound note	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_864", "sentence_865", "sentence_866"], "communicatesContent": "thank you! And I should be glad to have change of this five-pound note."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_24_1	event	\N	Cartwright arrives and stands before Holmes	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_867", "sentence_868"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_24_2	event	\N	Holmes asks for the Hotel Directory	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_869"], "communicatesContent": "Let me have the Hotel Directory."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_6	event	\N	Holmes says the matter is well worth troubling about.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_953"], "communicatesContent": "I think it’s well worth troubling about."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_7	event	\N	A man says he does not attempt to explain the matter and calls it the maddest, queerest thing that ever happened to him.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "unknown", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_956", "sentence_957"], "communicatesContent": "I just don’t attempt to explain it. It seems the very maddest, queerest thing that ever happened to me."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_5	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer states that he received a thousand pounds under Sir Charles's will	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1009"], "communicatesContent": "I also had a thousand pounds left to me."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_49_1	event	\N	Barrymore crouches at the window holding a candle against the glass	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1874"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_24_3	event	\N	Holmes instructs Cartwright to visit the hotels and search for the page of the Times	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_871", "sentence_872", "sentence_874", "sentence_876", "sentence_877", "sentence_879", "sentence_880", "sentence_881", "sentence_883", "sentence_884", "sentence_885", "sentence_886", "sentence_888", "sentence_889", "sentence_890", "sentence_891", "sentence_892", "sentence_893", "sentence_894"], "communicatesContent": "There are the names of twenty-three hotels here, all in the immediate neighbourhood of Charing Cross. You will visit each of these in turn. You will begin in each case by giving the outside porter one shilling. Here are twenty-three shillings. You will tell him that you want to see the waste-paper of yesterday. You will say that an important telegram has miscarried and that you are looking for it. But what you are really looking for is the centre page of the Times with some holes cut in it with scissors. Here is a copy of the Times. It is this page. You could easily recognize it, could you not? In each case the outside porter will send for the hall porter, to whom also you will give a shilling. Here are twenty-three shillings. You will then learn in possibly twenty cases out of the twenty-three that the waste of the day before has been burned or removed. In the three other cases you will be shown a heap of paper and you will look for this page of the Times among it. The odds are enormously against your finding it. There are ten shillings over in case of emergencies. Let me have a report by wire at Baker Street before evening."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_24_4	event	\N	Holmes and Watson plan to find the cabman by wire and later go to picture galleries and the hotel	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_895", "sentence_896"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_25_1	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes inspects the hotel register	24	{"section": "section_25", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_904", "sentence_905", "sentence_906", "sentence_907", "sentence_908"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_25_2	event	\N	Holmes questions the porter about Theophilus Johnson and Mrs. Oldmore	24	{"section": "section_25", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_909", "sentence_910", "sentence_911", "sentence_912", "sentence_913", "sentence_914", "sentence_915", "sentence_916", "sentence_917", "sentence_918", "sentence_919", "sentence_920", "sentence_921"], "communicatesContent": "Surely that must be the same Johnson whom I used to know; a lawyer, is he not, grey-headed, and walks with a limp? Surely you are mistaken about his trade? Mrs. Oldmore, too; I seem to remember the name. Excuse my curiosity, but often in calling upon one friend one finds another. Thank you; I am afraid I cannot claim her acquaintance."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_25_3	event	\N	Holmes and Watson go upstairs together while Holmes speaks in a low voice	24	{"section": "section_25", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_922", "sentence_923", "sentence_924", "sentence_925"], "communicatesContent": "We have established a most important fact by these questions, Watson. We know now that the people who are so interested in our friend have not settled down in his own hotel. That means that while they are very anxious to watch him, they are equally anxious that he should not see them. Now, this is a most suggestive fact."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_25_4	event	\N	Sir Henry Baskerville confronts Holmes and Watson with an old dusty boot	24	{"section": "section_25", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_928", "sentence_929", "sentence_930"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_25_5	event	\N	Sir Henry Baskerville complains about the missing boot	24	{"section": "section_25", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_931", "sentence_932", "sentence_933", "sentence_934"], "communicatesContent": "Seems to me they are playing me for a sucker in this hotel. They’ll find they’ve started in to monkey with the wrong man unless they are careful. By thunder, if that chap can’t find my missing boot there will be trouble. I can take a joke with the best, Mr. Holmes, but they’ve got a bit over the mark this time."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_25_6	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Sir Henry is still looking for the boot	24	{"section": "section_25", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_935", "sentence_936"], "communicatesContent": "Still looking for your boot?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_25_7	event	\N	Sir Henry explains that his boot has changed from new brown to old black	24	{"section": "section_25", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_937", "sentence_938", "sentence_939"], "communicatesContent": "So it was, sir. And now it’s an old black one."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_25_8	event	\N	Holmes reacts in surprise to the boot account	24	{"section": "section_25", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_940", "sentence_941", "sentence_942", "sentence_943", "sentence_944", "sentence_945", "sentence_946"], "communicatesContent": "What! you don’t mean to say—?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_25_9	event	\N	An agitated German waiter appears	24	{"section": "section_25", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_947"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_1	event	\N	A man reports that he has made inquiry all over the hotel but can hear no word of the missing boot.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "unknown", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_948"], "communicatesContent": "No, sir; I have made inquiry all over the hotel, but I can hear no word of it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_2	event	\N	A man threatens to leave the hotel unless the boot is returned before sundown and he can speak to the manager.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "unknown", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_949"], "communicatesContent": "Well, either that boot comes back before sundown or I’ll see the manager and tell him that I go right straight out of this hotel."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_3	event	\N	A man promises that the boot will be found if given patience.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "unknown", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_950"], "communicatesContent": "It shall be found, sir—I promise you that if you will have a little patience it will be found."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_4	event	\N	A man says he will lose no more property in the hotel.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "unknown", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_951"], "communicatesContent": "Mind it is, for it’s the last thing of mine that I’ll lose in this den of thieves."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_5	event	\N	A man excuses himself to Holmes for troubling him about a trifle.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "unknown", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_952", "sentence_953"], "communicatesContent": "Well, well, Mr. Holmes, you’ll excuse my troubling you about such a trifle—"}
valley-of-fear	sentence_42	lexical	\N	\N	41	{"text": "“I was about to say, as he is unknown to the public.”", "section": "section_4", "entities": []}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_8	event	\N	Holmes remarks that the case is very complex and that several threads may guide them to the truth.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_960", "sentence_961", "sentence_962", "sentence_963", "sentence_964"], "communicatesContent": "I don’t profess to understand it yet. This case of yours is very complex, Sir Henry. When taken in conjunction with your uncle’s death I am not sure that of all the five hundred cases of capital importance which I have handled there is one which cuts so deep. But we hold several threads in our hands, and the odds are that one or other of them guides us to the truth. We may waste time in following the wrong one, but sooner or later we must come upon the right."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_9	event	\N	Holmes asks Baskerville about his intentions.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_966"], "communicatesContent": "What were your intentions?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_10	event	\N	Sir Henry says he will go to Baskerville Hall at the end of the week.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_967", "sentence_968", "sentence_969"], "communicatesContent": "To go to Baskerville Hall. At the end of the week."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_11	event	\N	Holmes says he thinks Sir Henry's decision is wise and that unknown people are dogging him in London.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_970", "sentence_971", "sentence_972"], "communicatesContent": "On the whole, I think that your decision is a wise one. I have ample evidence that you are being dogged in London, and amid the millions of this great city it is difficult to discover who these people are or what their object can be. If their intentions are evil they might do you a mischief, and we should be powerless to prevent it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_12	event	\N	Holmes asks Dr. Mortimer whether he knew he was followed that morning from Holmes's house.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_973"], "communicatesContent": "You did not know, Dr. Mortimer, that you were followed this morning from my house?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_13	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer starts violently.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_974"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_14	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer asks who followed him.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_975", "sentence_976"], "communicatesContent": "Followed! By whom?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_15	event	\N	Holmes says he cannot tell who followed Dr. Mortimer.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_977"], "communicatesContent": "That, unfortunately, is what I cannot tell you."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_16	event	\N	Holmes asks whether any neighbour or acquaintance on Dartmoor has a black full beard.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_978"], "communicatesContent": "Have you among your neighbours or acquaintances on Dartmoor any man with a black, full beard?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_17	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer names Barrymore as a man with a full black beard and says he is in charge of the Hall.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_979", "sentence_980", "sentence_983"], "communicatesContent": "No—or, let me see—why, yes. Barrymore, Sir Charles’s butler, is a man with a full, black beard. He is in charge of the Hall."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_18	event	\N	Holmes says they had best ascertain whether Barrymore is at the Hall or possibly in London.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_981", "sentence_982", "sentence_984"], "communicatesContent": "Ha! Where is Barrymore? We had best ascertain if he is really there, or if by any possibility he might be in London."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_19	event	\N	Holmes asks for a telegraph form and composes telegrams to Barrymore and the Grimpen postmaster.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_985", "sentence_986", "sentence_987", "sentence_988", "sentence_989", "sentence_990", "sentence_991", "sentence_992", "sentence_993", "sentence_994"], "communicatesContent": "Is all ready for Sir Henry? Address to Mr. Barrymore, Baskerville Hall. Send a second wire to the postmaster, Grimpen: 'Telegram to Mr. Barrymore to be delivered into his own hand. If absent, please return wire to Sir Henry Baskerville, Northumberland Hotel.'"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_20	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer says Barrymore is the son of the old caretaker, who is dead, and that the Barrymores have looked after the Hall for four generations.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_996", "sentence_997", "sentence_998", "sentence_999"], "communicatesContent": "He is the son of the old caretaker, who is dead. They have looked after the Hall for four generations now. So far as I know, he and his wife are as respectable a couple as any in the county."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_26_21	event	\N	Baskerville says the Barrymores have a fine home and nothing to do while no family member is at the Hall.	25	{"section": "section_26", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1000"], "communicatesContent": "At the same time, it’s clear enough that so long as there are none of the family at the Hall these people have a mighty fine home and nothing to do."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_1	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Barrymore profited from Sir Charles's will	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1002", "sentence_1003"], "communicatesContent": "Did Barrymore profit at all by Sir Charles’s will?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_2	event	\N	It is stated that Barrymore and his wife receive five hundred pounds each under Sir Charles's will	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1004"], "communicatesContent": "He and his wife had five hundred pounds each."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_3	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Barrymore and his wife knew they would receive the money	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1005", "sentence_1006"], "communicatesContent": "Did they know that they would receive this?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_4	event	\N	It is stated that Sir Charles often talked about the provisions of his will	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1007"], "communicatesContent": "Yes; Sir Charles was very fond of talking about the provisions of his will."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_49_2	event	\N	Barrymore stands watching out into the moor	48	{"section": "section_49", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1875", "sentence_1876"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_6	event	\N	Holmes asks whether anyone else received a legacy	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1010", "sentence_1011"], "communicatesContent": "And anyone else?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_7	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer states that many small sums and public charities were included in the will	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1012", "sentence_1013"], "communicatesContent": "There were many insignificant sums to individuals, and a large number of public charities. The residue all went to Sir Henry."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_8	event	\N	Holmes asks how much the residue was	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1014"], "communicatesContent": "And how much was the residue?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_9	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer states that the residue was seven hundred and forty thousand pounds	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1015"], "communicatesContent": "Seven hundred and forty thousand pounds."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_10	event	\N	Holmes reacts with surprise at the size of the sum	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1016", "sentence_1017"], "communicatesContent": "I had no idea that so gigantic a sum was involved."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_11	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer explains the estate's value and Sir Charles's wealth	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1018", "sentence_1019"], "communicatesContent": "Sir Charles had the reputation of being rich, but we did not know how very rich he was until we came to examine his securities. The total value of the estate was close on to a million."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_12	event	\N	Holmes comments that the sum is enough to tempt desperate action	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1020", "sentence_1021"], "communicatesContent": "It is a stake for which a man might well play a desperate game."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_13	event	\N	Holmes asks who would inherit the estate	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1022", "sentence_1023", "sentence_1024"], "communicatesContent": "who would inherit the estate?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_14	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer states that after Rodger Baskerville's death the estate would descend to the Desmonds	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1025", "sentence_1026"], "communicatesContent": "Since Rodger Baskerville, Sir Charles’s younger brother died unmarried, the estate would descend to the Desmonds, who are distant cousins. James Desmond is an elderly clergyman in Westmoreland."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_15	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Dr. Mortimer has met Mr. James Desmond	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1029"], "communicatesContent": "Have you met Mr. James Desmond?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_16	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer states that James Desmond once visited Sir Charles and refused a settlement	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1030", "sentence_1031", "sentence_1032"], "communicatesContent": "Yes; he once came down to visit Sir Charles. He is a man of venerable appearance and of saintly life. I remember that he refused to accept any settlement from Sir Charles, though he pressed it upon him."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_17	event	\N	Holmes remarks that the heir would be entitled to Sir Charles's wealth	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1033"], "communicatesContent": "And this man of simple tastes would be the heir to Sir Charles’s thousands."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_18	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer explains that James Desmond would inherit the entailed estate and possibly the money	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1034", "sentence_1035"], "communicatesContent": "He would be the heir to the estate because that is entailed. He would also be the heir to the money unless it were willed otherwise by the present owner, who can, of course, do what he likes with it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_19	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Sir Henry has made his will	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1036"], "communicatesContent": "And have you made your will, Sir Henry?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_20	event	\N	Sir Henry states that he has not made his will	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1037", "sentence_1038", "sentence_1039", "sentence_1040", "sentence_1041", "sentence_1042"], "communicatesContent": "No, Mr. Holmes, I have not. I’ve had no time, for it was only yesterday that I learned how matters stood. But in any case I feel that the money should go with the title and estate. That was my poor uncle’s idea. How is the owner going to restore the glories of the Baskervilles if he has not money enough to keep up the property? House, land, and dollars must go together."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_21	event	\N	Holmes advises Sir Henry to go to Devonshire without delay	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1043", "sentence_1044"], "communicatesContent": "I am of one mind with you as to the advisability of your going down to Devonshire without delay."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_22	event	\N	Holmes states that Sir Henry must not go alone and should take a trusty man	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1045", "sentence_1046", "sentence_1047", "sentence_1048", "sentence_1049", "sentence_1050"], "communicatesContent": "There is only one provision which I must make. You certainly must not go alone. Dr. Mortimer returns with me. But Dr. Mortimer has his practice to attend to, and his house is miles away from yours. With all the goodwill in the world he may be unable to help you. No, Sir Henry, you must take with you someone, a trusty man, who will be always by your side."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_23	event	\N	Sir Henry asks whether Holmes could come himself	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1051"], "communicatesContent": "Is it possible that you could come yourself, Mr. Holmes?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_7	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer replies that it depends on the breed of men as much as on the county	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1239"], "communicatesContent": "It depends upon the breed of men quite as much as on the county."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_87_1	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons hesitates and looks down	86	{"section": "section_87", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3481"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_27_24	event	\N	Holmes states that he cannot go to Dartmoor for an indefinite time	26	{"section": "section_27", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1052", "sentence_1053", "sentence_1054"], "communicatesContent": "If matters came to a crisis I should endeavour to be present in person; but you can understand that, with my extensive consulting practice and with the constant appeals which reach me from many quarters, it is impossible for me to be absent from London for an indefinite time. At the present instant one of the most revered names in England is being besmirched by a blackmailer, and only I can stop a disastrous scandal. You will see how impossible it is for me to go to Dartmoor."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_1	event	\N	Holmes recommends Watson as a companion for the trip	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1056", "sentence_1057", "sentence_1058"], "communicatesContent": "If my friend would undertake it there is no man who is better worth having at your side when you are in a tight place. No one can say so more confidently than I."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_2	event	\N	Baskerville grips Watson's hand and asks him to come to Baskerville Hall	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1059", "sentence_1060", "sentence_1061", "sentence_1062"], "communicatesContent": "Well, now, that is real kind of you, Dr. Watson. You see how it is with me, and you know just as much about the matter as I do. If you will come down to Baskerville Hall and see me through I’ll never forget it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_3	event	\N	Watson agrees to come to Baskerville Hall	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1063", "sentence_1064", "sentence_1065"], "communicatesContent": "I will come, with pleasure. I do not know how I could employ my time better."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_4	event	\N	Holmes instructs Watson to report carefully and to be ready by Saturday	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1066", "sentence_1067", "sentence_1068", "sentence_1069", "sentence_1070", "sentence_1071"], "communicatesContent": "And you will report very carefully to me. When a crisis comes, as it will do, I will direct how you shall act. I suppose that by Saturday all might be ready?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_5	event	\N	The group agrees to meet at the ten-thirty train from Paddington on Saturday	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1069", "sentence_1070", "sentence_1071"], "communicatesContent": "Then on Saturday, unless you hear to the contrary, we shall meet at the ten-thirty train from Paddington."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_6	event	\N	Baskerville finds his missing brown boot under a cabinet	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1072", "sentence_1073", "sentence_1074"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_7	event	\N	Holmes remarks that the boot has returned easily	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1075", "sentence_1076"], "communicatesContent": "May all our difficulties vanish as easily!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_8	event	\N	Mortimer comments on the boot's appearance and says he searched the room before lunch	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1077", "sentence_1078"], "communicatesContent": "But it is a very singular thing. I searched this room carefully before lunch."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_9	event	\N	Baskerville says he also searched the room and that the boot was not there then	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1079", "sentence_1080", "sentence_1081"], "communicatesContent": "And so did I. Every inch of it. There was certainly no boot in it then."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_10	event	\N	Holmes suggests the waiter placed the boot there while they were lunching	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1082"], "communicatesContent": "In that case the waiter must have placed it there while we were lunching."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_11	event	\N	The German is sent for and questioned about the boot	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "the_german", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1083"], "communicatesContent": "I know nothing of the matter."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_12	event	\N	Holmes and Watson drive back to Baker Street in a cab	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1086"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_13	event	\N	Holmes sits in thought through the afternoon and evening	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1087"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_14	event	\N	Two telegrams are handed in before dinner	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1088", "sentence_1089", "sentence_1090", "sentence_1091", "sentence_1092", "sentence_1093", "sentence_1094"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_15	event	\N	Holmes states that Barrymore is at the Hall and that Cartwright failed to trace the cut sheet of The Times	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "cartwright", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1090", "sentence_1091", "sentence_1093", "sentence_1094"], "communicatesContent": "Have just heard that Barrymore is at the Hall. Visited twenty-three hotels as directed, but sorry to report unable to trace cut sheet of The Times."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_16	event	\N	Holmes says he must seek another lead	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1095", "sentence_1096", "sentence_1097"], "communicatesContent": "There go two of my threads, Watson. There is nothing more stimulating than a case where everything goes against you. We must cast round for another scent."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_17	event	\N	Holmes says he has wired to learn the cabman's name and address	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1098", "sentence_1099", "sentence_1100", "sentence_1101"], "communicatesContent": "Exactly. I have wired to get his name and address from the Official Registry. I should not be surprised if this were an answer to my question."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_28_18	event	\N	The cabman's arrival at the door follows the bell ring	27	{"section": "section_28", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1102"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_36_3	event	\N	The narrator lies awake and waits in the night	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1385", "sentence_1386", "sentence_1387"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	sentence_43	lexical	\N	\N	42	{"text": "“A touch!", "section": "section_4", "entities": []}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_1	event	\N	A cabman arrives and questions Holmes about what he has against him	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "the_cabman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1103", "sentence_1104", "sentence_1105", "sentence_1106", "sentence_1107", "sentence_1108", "sentence_1109", "sentence_1110"], "communicatesContent": "I got a message from the head office that a gent at this address had been inquiring for No. 2704. I’ve driven my cab this seven years and never a word of complaint. I came here straight from the Yard to ask you to your face what you had against me."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_2	event	\N	Holmes offers the cabman money for answers and asks for his name and address	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1107", "sentence_1108", "sentence_1111", "sentence_1112", "sentence_1113", "sentence_1114"], "communicatesContent": "I have nothing in the world against you, my good man. On the contrary, I have half a sovereign for you if you will give me a clear answer to my questions. First of all your name and address, in case I want you again."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_3	event	\N	The cabman gives his name, address, and cab yard	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "the_cabman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1112", "sentence_1113"], "communicatesContent": "John Clayton, 3 Turpey Street, the Borough. My cab is out of Shipley's Yard, near Waterloo Station."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_4	event	\N	Holmes makes a note of the cabman's details	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1114"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_5	event	\N	Holmes asks the cabman about the fare who watched the house and followed the gentlemen	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1115"], "communicatesContent": "Tell me all about the fare who came and watched this house at ten o’clock this morning and afterwards followed the two gentlemen down Regent Street."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_6	event	\N	The cabman says the fare told him he was a detective and ordered silence	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "the_cabman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1117", "sentence_1118", "sentence_1119", "sentence_1120", "sentence_1121", "sentence_1122", "sentence_1123", "sentence_1124", "sentence_1125"], "communicatesContent": "The gentleman told me that he was a detective and that I was to say nothing about him to anyone. Yes, he did. When he left me. He mentioned his name."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_7	event	\N	Holmes reacts with triumph and asks for the name mentioned	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1126", "sentence_1127", "sentence_1128", "sentence_1129"], "communicatesContent": "That was imprudent. What was the name that he mentioned?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_8	event	\N	The cabman identifies the fare as Mr. Sherlock Holmes	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "the_cabman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1130"], "communicatesContent": "His name was Mr. Sherlock Holmes."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_9	event	\N	Holmes laughs and remarks on the cabman's touch	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1131", "sentence_1132", "sentence_1133", "sentence_1134", "sentence_1135", "sentence_1136", "sentence_1137", "sentence_1138"], "communicatesContent": "A touch, Watson—an undeniable touch! I feel a foil as quick and supple as my own. He got home upon me very prettily that time. So his name was Sherlock Holmes, was it?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_10	event	\N	The cabman confirms the name	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "the_cabman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1139"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, sir, that was the gentleman’s name."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_11	event	\N	Holmes asks where the cabman picked up the fare and what occurred	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1140", "sentence_1141"], "communicatesContent": "Tell me where you picked him up and all that occurred."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_29_12	event	\N	The cabman describes picking up the fare in Trafalgar Square and following two gentlemen by cab	28	{"section": "section_29", "speakerId": "the_cabman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1142", "sentence_1143", "sentence_1144", "sentence_1145", "sentence_1146", "sentence_1147", "sentence_1148", "sentence_1149", "sentence_1150", "sentence_1151", "sentence_1152", "sentence_1153", "sentence_1154", "sentence_1155", "sentence_1156", "sentence_1157"], "communicatesContent": "He hailed me at half-past nine in Trafalgar Square. He said that he was a detective, and he offered me two guineas if I would do exactly what he wanted all day and ask no questions. I was glad enough to agree. First we drove down to the Northumberland Hotel and waited there until two gentlemen came out and took a cab from the rank. We followed their cab until it pulled up somewhere near here. This very door. We pulled up halfway down the street and waited an hour and a half. Then the two gentlemen passed us, walking, and we followed down Baker Street and along— Until we got three-quarters down Regent Street. Then my gentleman threw up the trap, and he cried that I should drive right away to Waterloo Station as hard as I could go. I whipped up the mare and we were there under the ten minutes. Then he paid up his two guineas, like a good one, and away he went into the station. Only just as he was leaving he turned round and he said: ‘It might interest you to know that you have been driving Mr. Sherlock Holmes.’ That’s how I come to know the name."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_30_1	event	\N	The cabman answers that he saw no more of Holmes after Holmes went into the station	29	{"section": "section_30", "speakerId": "the_cabman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1158", "sentence_1159", "sentence_1160"], "communicatesContent": "Not after he went into the station."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_30_2	event	\N	The cabman describes Holmes’s appearance	29	{"section": "section_30", "speakerId": "the_cabman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1161", "sentence_1162", "sentence_1163", "sentence_1164", "sentence_1165", "sentence_1166", "sentence_1167", "sentence_1168", "sentence_1169", "sentence_1170"], "communicatesContent": "Well, he wasn’t altogether such an easy gentleman to describe. I’d put him at forty years of age, and he was of a middle height, two or three inches shorter than you, sir. He was dressed like a toff, and he had a black beard, cut square at the end, and a pale face. I don’t know as I could say more than that. No, I can’t say that. No, sir; nothing."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_30_3	event	\N	Holmes gives the cabman half-sovereigns and dismisses him	29	{"section": "section_30", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1171", "sentence_1172", "sentence_1173", "sentence_1174"], "communicatesContent": "Well, then, here is your half-sovereign. There’s another one waiting for you if you can bring any more information. Good-night!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_30_4	event	\N	John Clayton departs chuckling	29	{"section": "section_30", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1175"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_30_5	event	\N	Holmes turns to Watson and comments on the investigation	29	{"section": "section_30", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1175", "sentence_1176", "sentence_1177", "sentence_1178", "sentence_1179", "sentence_1180", "sentence_1181", "sentence_1182", "sentence_1183", "sentence_1184", "sentence_1185", "sentence_1186"], "communicatesContent": "Snap goes our third thread, and we end where we began. The cunning rascal! He knew our number, knew that Sir Henry Baskerville had consulted me, spotted who I was in Regent Street, conjectured that I had got the number of the cab and would lay my hands on the driver, and so sent back this audacious message. I tell you, Watson, this time we have got a foeman who is worthy of our steel. I’ve been checkmated in London. I can only wish you better luck in Devonshire. But I’m not easy in my mind about it. About sending you. It’s an ugly business, Watson, an ugly dangerous business, and the more I see of it the less I like it. Yes, my dear fellow, you may laugh, but I give you my word that I shall be very glad to have you back safe and sound in Baker Street once more."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_31_1	event	\N	Sir Henry Baskerville and Dr. Mortimer depart for Devonshire	30	{"section": "section_31", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1189"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_31_2	event	\N	Holmes drives Watson to the station and gives parting instructions	30	{"section": "section_31", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1190", "sentence_1191", "sentence_1194", "sentence_1195", "sentence_1196", "sentence_1197", "sentence_1198", "sentence_1199", "sentence_1200", "sentence_1201", "sentence_1202", "sentence_1203", "sentence_1204", "sentence_1205", "sentence_1206", "sentence_1207", "sentence_1208", "sentence_1209", "sentence_1211", "sentence_1213", "sentence_1214"], "communicatesContent": "I will not bias your mind by suggesting theories or suspicions, Watson; I wish you simply to report facts in the fullest possible manner to me, and you can leave me to do the theorizing. Anything which may seem to have a bearing however indirect upon the case, and especially the relations between young Baskerville and his neighbours or any fresh particulars concerning the death of Sir Charles. I have made some inquiries myself in the last few days, but the results have been negative. One thing only appears to be certain, and that is that Mr. James Desmond, who is the next heir, is an elderly gentleman of a very amiable disposition, so that this persecution does not arise from him. I really think that we may eliminate him entirely from our calculations. There remain the people who will actually surround Sir Henry Baskerville upon the moor. By no means. You could not make a greater mistake. If they are innocent it would be a cruel injustice, and if they are guilty we should be giving up all chance of bringing it home to them. No, no, we will preserve them upon our list of suspects. Then there is a groom at the Hall, if I remember right. There are two moorland farmers. There is our friend Dr. Mortimer, whom I believe to be entirely honest, and there is his wife, of whom we know nothing. There is this naturalist, Stapleton, and there is his sister, who is said to be a young lady of attractions. There is Mr. Frankland, of Lafter Hall, who is also an unknown factor, and there are one or two other neighbours. These are the folk who must be your very special study. You have arms, I suppose? Most certainly. Keep your revolver near you night and day, and never relax your precautions."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_31_3	event	\N	Watson says he has taken his arms	30	{"section": "section_31", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1211", "sentence_1212"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, I thought it as well to take them."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_31_4	event	\N	Sir Henry and Dr. Mortimer wait on the platform with a first-class carriage	30	{"section": "section_31", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1215"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_31_5	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer reports that they have no news and have not been shadowed	30	{"section": "section_31", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1216", "sentence_1217", "sentence_1218"], "communicatesContent": "We have no news of any kind. We have not been shadowed during the last two days. We have never gone out without keeping a sharp watch, and no one could have escaped our notice."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_31_6	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Sir Henry and Dr. Mortimer have always kept together	30	{"section": "section_31", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1219"], "communicatesContent": "You have always kept together, I presume?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_31_7	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer explains that he spent yesterday afternoon at the Museum of the College of Surgeons	30	{"section": "section_31", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1220", "sentence_1221"], "communicatesContent": "Except yesterday afternoon. I usually give up one day to pure amusement when I come to town, so I spent it at the Museum of the College of Surgeons."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_31_8	event	\N	Sir Henry says he went to look at the folk in the park	30	{"section": "section_31", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1222"], "communicatesContent": "I went to look at the folk in the park."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_31_9	event	\N	Holmes warns Sir Henry not to go about alone and to avoid the moor at night	30	{"section": "section_31", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1224", "sentence_1225", "sentence_1226", "sentence_1227", "sentence_1228", "sentence_1229", "sentence_1230", "sentence_1231", "sentence_1232"], "communicatesContent": "It was imprudent, all the same. I beg, Sir Henry, that you will not go about alone. Some great misfortune will befall you if you do. Did you get your other boot? Indeed. That is very interesting. Well, good-bye. Bear in mind, Sir Henry, one of the phrases in that queer old legend which Dr. Mortimer has read to us, and avoid the moor in those hours of darkness when the powers of evil are exalted."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_1	event	\N	Holmes stands motionless on the platform as the train leaves	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1233"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_2	event	\N	The speaker plays with Dr. Mortimer’s spaniel during the journey	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1234"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_3	event	\N	The landscape changes as the train travels through Devon	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1235"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_4	event	\N	Sir Henry Baskerville stares out of the window and cries aloud with delight	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1236"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_5	event	\N	Sir Henry Baskerville says he has never seen a place to compare with Devon	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1237"], "communicatesContent": "I’ve been over a good part of the world since I left it, Dr. Watson, but I have never seen a place to compare with it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_6	event	\N	Watson remarks that Devonshire men swear by their county	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1238"], "communicatesContent": "I never saw a Devonshire man who did not swear by his county."}
valley-of-fear	sentence_44	lexical	\N	\N	43	{"text": "A distinct touch!”", "section": "section_4", "entities": []}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_8	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer comments on a friend’s Celtic head type and Sir Charles’s mixed Gaelic and Ivernian type	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1240", "sentence_1241"], "communicatesContent": "A glance at our friend here reveals the rounded head of the Celt, which carries inside it the Celtic enthusiasm and power of attachment. Poor Sir Charles’s head was of a very rare type, half Gaelic, half Ivernian in its characteristics."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_9	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer asks whether Sir Henry was very young when he last saw Baskerville Hall	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1242"], "communicatesContent": "But you were very young when you last saw Baskerville Hall, were you not?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_10	event	\N	Sir Henry says he was a boy when his father died, never saw the Hall, left for America, and is eager to see the moor	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1243", "sentence_1244", "sentence_1245"], "communicatesContent": "I was a boy in my teens at the time of my father’s death and had never seen the Hall, for he lived in a little cottage on the South Coast. Thence I went straight to a friend in America. I tell you it is all as new to me as it is to Dr. Watson, and I’m as keen as possible to see the moor."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_11	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer points out the first sight of the moor	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1246", "sentence_1247"], "communicatesContent": "Then your wish is easily granted, for there is your first sight of the moor."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_12	event	\N	The train reveals the moor in the distance	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1248"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_13	event	\N	Baskerville sits for a long time looking at the moor	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1249"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_14	event	\N	The narrator observes Sir Henry in the carriage and reflects on his descent	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1250", "sentence_1251", "sentence_1252"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_15	event	\N	The train stops at a small wayside station and everyone descends	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1253"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_16	event	\N	A wagonette with a pair of cobs waits outside the station	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1254"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_17	event	\N	Station-master and porters gather to carry luggage	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1255"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_18	event	\N	Two soldierly men in dark uniforms stand by the gate and watch the travellers pass	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1256"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_19	event	\N	The coachman salutes Sir Henry Baskerville and the party drives away swiftly	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1257"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_32_20	event	\N	The countryside and the moor come into view as the carriage speeds along the road	31	{"section": "section_32", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1258"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_33_1	event	\N	The wagonette travels up a side road through the lanes and stream valley	32	{"section": "section_33", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1259", "sentence_1260", "sentence_1261", "sentence_1262"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_33_2	event	\N	Baskerville exclaims with delight while asking questions during the drive	32	{"section": "section_33", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1263"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_33_3	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer notices a mounted soldier on the moor and asks Perkins what it is	32	{"section": "section_33", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1267", "sentence_1268", "sentence_1272", "sentence_1273"], "communicatesContent": "what is this?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_33_4	event	\N	Perkins explains that an escaped convict from Princetown has been out for three days and is being watched for by warders	32	{"section": "section_33", "speakerId": "perkins", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1274", "sentence_1275", "sentence_1276", "sentence_1277", "sentence_1278", "sentence_1279", "sentence_1280", "sentence_1281", "sentence_1282", "sentence_1283"], "communicatesContent": "There’s a convict escaped from Princetown, sir. He’s been out three days now, and the warders watch every road and every station, but they’ve had no sight of him yet. The farmers about here don’t like it, sir, and that’s a fact. It is Selden, the Notting Hill murderer."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_33_5	event	\N	The wagonette reaches the moor and the party looks back toward the fertile country	32	{"section": "section_33", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1286", "sentence_1287", "sentence_1291", "sentence_1292", "sentence_1293", "sentence_1294", "sentence_1295", "sentence_1296", "sentence_1297"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_1	event	\N	Sir Henry arrives at Baskerville Hall	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1298", "sentence_1330", "sentence_1331"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_2	event	\N	The party reaches the lodge-gates	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1300"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_3	event	\N	The party passes into the avenue	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1302"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_4	event	\N	Sir Henry shudders while looking up the drive to the house	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1303"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_5	event	\N	Sir Henry speaks about the yew alley being on the other side	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1304", "sentence_1305", "sentence_1306"], "communicatesContent": "Was it here? No, no, the yew alley is on the other side."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_36_4	event	\N	A woman sobs somewhere in the house	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1388", "sentence_1389"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_6	event	\N	Sir Henry comments on the place frightening any man	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1308", "sentence_1309", "sentence_1310"], "communicatesContent": "It’s no wonder my uncle felt as if trouble were coming on him in such a place as this. It’s enough to scare any man. I’ll have a row of electric lamps up here inside of six months, and you won’t know it again, with a thousand candle-power Swan and Edison right here in front of the hall door."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_7	event	\N	The house comes into view before the party	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1311", "sentence_1312", "sentence_1313", "sentence_1314", "sentence_1315", "sentence_1316"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_8	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer welcomes Sir Henry to Baskerville Hall	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1317", "sentence_1318"], "communicatesContent": "Welcome, Sir Henry! Welcome to Baskerville Hall!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_9	event	\N	Barrymore opens the wagonette door	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1319"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_10	event	\N	A woman helps hand down the bags	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1320", "sentence_1321"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_11	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer declines dinner and leaves for home	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "dr_mortimer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1322", "sentence_1323", "sentence_1324", "sentence_1325", "sentence_1326", "sentence_1327", "sentence_1328", "sentence_1329", "sentence_1330"], "communicatesContent": "You don’t mind my driving straight home, Sir Henry? My wife is expecting me. No, I must go. I shall probably find some work awaiting me. I would stay to show you over the house, but Barrymore will be a better guide than I. Good-bye, and never hesitate night or day to send for me if I can be of service."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_12	event	\N	Sir Henry and Watson enter the hall	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1330", "sentence_1331"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_13	event	\N	Sir Henry and Watson warm themselves by the fire	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1332", "sentence_1333"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_34_14	event	\N	Sir Henry speaks about the hall as his family home	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1335", "sentence_1336", "sentence_1337", "sentence_1338"], "communicatesContent": "It’s just as I imagined it. Is it not the very picture of an old family home? To think that this should be the same hall in which for five hundred years my people have lived. It strikes me solemn to think of it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_35_1	event	\N	Barrymore returns from taking luggage to the rooms and asks about serving dinner	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1341", "sentence_1342", "sentence_1344", "sentence_1345", "sentence_1346", "sentence_1347"], "communicatesContent": "Would you wish dinner to be served at once, sir?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_35_2	event	\N	Barrymore explains that hot water will be in the rooms and that he and his wife can remain until fresh arrangements are made	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1346", "sentence_1347", "sentence_1348", "sentence_1349", "sentence_1350", "sentence_1351", "sentence_1352", "sentence_1353"], "communicatesContent": "In a very few minutes, sir. You will find hot water in your rooms. My wife and I will be happy, Sir Henry, to stay with you until you have made your fresh arrangements, but you will understand that under the new conditions this house will require a considerable staff. I only meant, sir, that Sir Charles led a very retired life, and we were able to look after his wants. You would, naturally, wish to have more company, and so you will need changes in your household. Only when it is quite convenient to you, sir."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_35_3	event	\N	Sir Henry questions whether Barrymore and his wife wish to leave and says he would regret breaking the old family connection	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1352", "sentence_1353", "sentence_1354", "sentence_1355"], "communicatesContent": "Do you mean that your wife and you wish to leave? But your family have been with us for several generations, have they not? I should be sorry to begin my life here by breaking an old family connection."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_35_4	event	\N	Barrymore says that he and his wife were attached to Sir Charles, that his death shocked them, and that they expect never to be easy at Baskerville Hall again	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1356", "sentence_1357", "sentence_1358", "sentence_1359", "sentence_1360", "sentence_1361", "sentence_1362", "sentence_1363"], "communicatesContent": "I feel that also, sir, and so does my wife. But to tell the truth, sir, we were both very much attached to Sir Charles, and his death gave us a shock and made these surroundings very painful to us. I fear that we shall never again be easy in our minds at Baskerville Hall. I have no doubt, sir, that we shall succeed in establishing ourselves in some business. Sir Charles’s generosity has given us the means to do so. And now, sir, perhaps I had best show you to your rooms."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_35_5	event	\N	Barrymore shows Sir Henry to the rooms	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1363", "sentence_1364", "sentence_1365", "sentence_1366", "sentence_1367"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_35_6	event	\N	The party eats dinner in the dining-room and then retires to the billiard-room	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1368", "sentence_1369", "sentence_1370", "sentence_1371", "sentence_1372", "sentence_1373", "sentence_1374"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_36_1	event	\N	Sir Henry comments on the house as uncheerful	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1375", "sentence_1376", "sentence_1377", "sentence_1378"], "communicatesContent": "it isn’t a very cheerful place; one can tone down to it; my uncle got a little jumpy if he lived all alone in such a house as this; we will retire early tonight and perhaps things may seem more cheerful in the morning."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_36_2	event	\N	The narrator looks out from the window before bed	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1379", "sentence_1380", "sentence_1381", "sentence_1382", "sentence_1383", "sentence_1384"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	sentence_45	lexical	\N	\N	44	{"text": "cried Holmes.", "section": "section_4", "entities": []}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_36_5	event	\N	Watson sits up and listens intently	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1390", "sentence_1391", "sentence_1392"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_1	event	\N	The morning at Baskerville Hall appears cheerful after the previous evening	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1395", "sentence_1396", "sentence_1397"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_2	event	\N	Sir Henry speaks about blaming themselves rather than the house	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1398", "sentence_1399", "sentence_1400", "sentence_1401"], "communicatesContent": "I guess it is ourselves and not the house that we have to blame! We were tired with our journey and chilled by our drive, so we took a grey view of the place. Now we are fresh and well, so it is all cheerful once more."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_3	event	\N	Watson asks whether Sir Henry heard sobbing in the night	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1402", "sentence_1403"], "communicatesContent": "Did you, for example, happen to hear someone, a woman I think, sobbing in the night?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_4	event	\N	Sir Henry says he half-asleep fancied he heard something and then thought it was a dream	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1404", "sentence_1405"], "communicatesContent": "That is curious, for I did when I was half asleep fancy that I heard something of the sort. I waited quite a time, but there was no more of it, so I concluded that it was all a dream."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_5	event	\N	Watson says he heard the sobbing distinctly and believes it was a woman	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1406"], "communicatesContent": "I heard it distinctly, and I am sure that it was really the sob of a woman."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_6	event	\N	Sir Henry rings the bell and Watson asks Barrymore about the experience	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1407", "sentence_1408"], "communicatesContent": "whether he could account for our experience"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_7	event	\N	Barrymore answers that only two women are in the house and the sound could not have come from his wife	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1410", "sentence_1411", "sentence_1412"], "communicatesContent": "There are only two women in the house, Sir Henry. One is the scullery-maid, who sleeps in the other wing. The other is my wife, and I can answer for it that the sound could not have come from her."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_8	event	\N	Watson meets Mrs. Barrymore in the corridor after breakfast	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1413", "sentence_1414", "sentence_1415"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_9	event	\N	Watson infers that Mrs. Barrymore wept in the night and that Barrymore must know it	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1416", "sentence_1417", "sentence_1418", "sentence_1419"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_10	event	\N	Watson reflects that Barrymore is associated with mystery and gloom and may have been in the cab in Regent Street	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1420", "sentence_1421", "sentence_1422", "sentence_1423", "sentence_1424", "sentence_1425"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_11	event	\N	Watson decides to go to Grimpen postmaster to check the telegram and report to Holmes	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1426", "sentence_1427", "sentence_1428"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_12	event	\N	Watson walks from Baskerville Hall toward Grimpen past the moor and reaches the hamlet near the inn and Dr. Mortimer's house	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1429"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_37_13	event	\N	The postmaster recalls the telegram	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "the_postmaster", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1430"], "communicatesContent": "the telegram"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_38_1	event	\N	James says he delivered the telegram to Mr. Barrymore exactly as directed	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "james", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1431", "sentence_1433", "sentence_1434", "sentence_1435"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, father, I delivered it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_38_2	event	\N	James explains that he gave the telegram to Mrs. Barrymore because Mr. Barrymore was in the loft	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "james", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1436", "sentence_1437", "sentence_1438", "sentence_1439", "sentence_1440"], "communicatesContent": "Well, he was up in the loft at the time, so that I could not put it into his own hands, but I gave it into Mrs. Barrymore’s hands, and she promised to deliver it at once."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_38_3	event	\N	The postmaster argues that Mrs. Barrymore ought to know where Mr. Barrymore is	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "section_38_postmaster", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1441", "sentence_1442", "sentence_1443", "sentence_1444"], "communicatesContent": "Well, surely his own wife ought to know where he is."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_38_4	event	\N	Watson considers that Barrymore's innocence remains unproved and reflects on the case	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1445", "sentence_1446", "sentence_1447", "sentence_1448", "sentence_1449", "sentence_1450", "sentence_1451", "sentence_1452", "sentence_1453", "sentence_1454", "sentence_1455"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_38_5	event	\N	A stranger calls out to Watson and overtakes him on the road	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "strapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1456", "sentence_1457", "sentence_1458", "sentence_1459", "sentence_1460"], "communicatesContent": "You will, I am sure, excuse my presumption, Dr. Watson."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_38_6	event	\N	Stapleton introduces himself to Watson as Stapleton of Merripit House	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "strapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1461", "sentence_1462", "sentence_1463"], "communicatesContent": "Here on the moor we are homely folk and do not wait for formal introductions. You may possibly have heard my name from our mutual friend, Mortimer. I am Stapleton, of Merripit House."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_46_7	event	\N	Sir Henry dines with the Stapletons and plans to visit them the next week	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1800"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_38_7	event	\N	Watson remarks that Stapleton's net and box identify him as a naturalist	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1464", "sentence_1465"], "communicatesContent": "Your net and box would have told me as much, for I knew that Mr. Stapleton was a naturalist."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_38_8	event	\N	Stapleton explains that Mortimer pointed Watson out from the surgery window and that he came to introduce himself	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "strapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1466", "sentence_1467", "sentence_1468"], "communicatesContent": "I have been calling on Mortimer, and he pointed you out to me from the window of his surgery as you passed. As our road lay the same way I thought that I would overtake you and introduce myself. I trust that Sir Henry is none the worse for his journey?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_39_1	event	\N	Stapleton speaks with Watson about Sir Henry, the legend, and Sir Charles's death	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1469", "sentence_1470", "sentence_1471", "sentence_1472", "sentence_1473", "sentence_1474", "sentence_1475", "sentence_1476", "sentence_1477", "sentence_1478", "sentence_1479", "sentence_1480", "sentence_1481", "sentence_1482", "sentence_1483", "sentence_1484", "sentence_1485", "sentence_1486", "sentence_1487", "sentence_1488", "sentence_1489", "sentence_1490", "sentence_1491", "sentence_1492", "sentence_1493", "sentence_1494", "sentence_1495", "sentence_1496", "sentence_1497", "sentence_1498", "sentence_1499", "sentence_1500", "sentence_1501", "sentence_1502", "sentence_1503", "sentence_1504", "sentence_1505", "sentence_1506", "sentence_1507"], "communicatesContent": "He is very well, thank you. We were all rather afraid that after the sad death of Sir Charles the new baronet might refuse to live here. It is asking much of a wealthy man to come down and bury himself in a place of this kind, but I need not tell you that it means a very great deal to the countryside. Sir Henry has, I suppose, no superstitious fears in the matter? Of course you know the legend of the fiend dog which haunts the family? It is extraordinary how credulous the peasants are about here! Any number of them are ready to swear that they have seen such a creature upon the moor. The story took a great hold upon the imagination of Sir Charles, and I have no doubt that it led to his tragic end. His nerves were so worked up that the appearance of any dog might have had a fatal effect upon his diseased heart. I fancy that he really did see something of the kind upon that last night in the yew alley. I feared that some disaster might occur, for I was very fond of the old man, and I knew that his heart was weak. My friend Mortimer told me. You think, then, that some dog pursued Sir Charles, and that he died of fright in consequence? Have you any better explanation? Has Mr. Sherlock Holmes? It is useless for us to pretend that we do not know you, Dr. Watson. The records of your detective have reached us here, and you could not celebrate him without being known yourself. When Mortimer told me your name he could not deny your identity. If you are here, then it follows that Mr. Sherlock Holmes is interesting himself in the matter, and I am naturally curious to know what view he may take. I am afraid that I cannot answer that question. May I ask if he is going to honour us with a visit himself? He cannot leave town at present. He has other cases which engage his attention. What a pity! He might throw some light on that which is so dark to us. But as to your own researches, if there is any possible way in which I can be of service to you I trust that you will command me. If I had any indication of the nature of your suspicions or how you propose to investigate the case, I might perhaps even now give you some aid or advice. I assure you that I am simply here upon a visit to my friend, Sir Henry, and that I need no help of any kind. Excellent! You are perfectly right to be wary and discreet. I am justly reproved for what I feel was an unjustifiable intrusion, and I promise you that I will not mention the matter again."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_39_2	event	\N	Watson and Stapleton continue along the moor road	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1508", "sentence_1509", "sentence_1510", "sentence_1511"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_40_1	event	\N	Stapleton invites Watson to Merripit House and to meet his sister	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1512", "sentence_1513", "sentence_1518"], "communicatesContent": "A moderate walk along this moor-path brings us to Merripit House. Perhaps you will spare an hour that I may have the pleasure of introducing you to my sister."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_40_2	event	\N	Watson accepts Stapleton's invitation and they walk down the path together	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1518"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_40_3	event	\N	Stapleton describes the moor and its difficulties	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1519", "sentence_1520", "sentence_1521", "sentence_1522", "sentence_1523", "sentence_1524", "sentence_1525", "sentence_1526", "sentence_1527", "sentence_1528", "sentence_1529", "sentence_1530", "sentence_1531", "sentence_1532", "sentence_1533", "sentence_1534", "sentence_1535", "sentence_1536", "sentence_1537", "sentence_1538", "sentence_1539", "sentence_1540", "sentence_1541", "sentence_1542", "sentence_1543", "sentence_1544", "sentence_1545", "sentence_1546", "sentence_1547", "sentence_1548", "sentence_1549", "sentence_1550", "sentence_1551", "sentence_1552", "sentence_1553", "sentence_1554", "sentence_1555", "sentence_1556", "sentence_1557", "sentence_1558", "sentence_1559", "sentence_1560", "sentence_1561", "sentence_1562", "sentence_1563", "sentence_1564", "sentence_1565"], "communicatesContent": "It is a wonderful place, the moor. You never tire of the moor. You cannot think the wonderful secrets which it contains. It is so vast, and so barren, and so mysterious. I have only been here two years. The residents would call me a newcomer. We came shortly after Sir Charles settled. But my tastes led me to explore every part of the country round, and I should think that there are few men who know it better than I do. Very hard. That is the great Grimpen Mire. A false step yonder means death to man or beast. Only yesterday I saw one of the moor ponies wander into it. He never came out. I saw his head for quite a long time craning out of the bog-hole, but it sucked him down at last. Even in dry seasons it is a danger to cross it, but after these autumn rains it is an awful place. And yet I can find my way to the very heart of it and return alive. Two in two days, and many more, perhaps, for they get in the way of going there in the dry weather and never know the difference until the mire has them in its clutches. It’s a bad place, the great Grimpen Mire. Yes, there are one or two paths which a very active man can take. I have found them out. Well, you see the hills beyond? They are really islands cut off on all sides by the impassable mire, which has crawled round them in the course of years. That is where the rare plants and the butterflies are, if you have the wit to reach them. For God’s sake put such an idea out of your mind. Your blood would be upon my head. I assure you that there would not be the least chance of your coming back alive. It is only by remembering certain complex landmarks that I am able to do it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_40_4	event	\N	Stapleton points out the mire as a deadly trap and describes a pony sinking into it	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1537", "sentence_1538", "sentence_1539", "sentence_1540", "sentence_1541", "sentence_1542", "sentence_1544", "sentence_1545", "sentence_1546", "sentence_1548", "sentence_1549", "sentence_1550", "sentence_1551", "sentence_1552"], "communicatesContent": "That is the great Grimpen Mire. A false step yonder means death to man or beast. Only yesterday I saw one of the moor ponies wander into it. He never came out. I saw his head for quite a long time craning out of the bog-hole, but it sucked him down at last. It’s gone! The mire has him. Two in two days, and many more, perhaps, for they get in the way of going there in the dry weather and never know the difference until the mire has them in its clutches. It’s a bad place, the great Grimpen Mire."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_40_5	event	\N	Stapleton says he can penetrate the mire by using paths and landmarks	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1553", "sentence_1554", "sentence_1555", "sentence_1557", "sentence_1558", "sentence_1559", "sentence_1562", "sentence_1563", "sentence_1564", "sentence_1565"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, there are one or two paths which a very active man can take. I have found them out. They are really islands cut off on all sides by the impassable mire, which has crawled round them in the course of years. That is where the rare plants and the butterflies are, if you have the wit to reach them. For God’s sake put such an idea out of your mind. Your blood would be upon my head. I assure you that there would not be the least chance of your coming back alive. It is only by remembering certain complex landmarks that I am able to do it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_40_6	event	\N	Watson voices an intention to try his luck in the mire	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1560"], "communicatesContent": "I shall try my luck some day."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_40_7	event	\N	Watson and Stapleton observe a moor pony being swallowed by the mire	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1544", "sentence_1545", "sentence_1546"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_41_1	event	\N	A long, low moan sweeps over the moor	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1569", "sentence_1570", "sentence_1571"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_43_4	event	\N	Watson presses Miss Stapleton for an explanation	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1710", "sentence_1711", "sentence_1712", "sentence_1715", "sentence_1716", "sentence_1717", "sentence_1718", "sentence_1719", "sentence_1720"], "communicatesContent": "I can't forget them, Miss Stapleton. I am Sir Henry’s friend, and his welfare is a very close concern of mine. Tell me why it was that you were so eager that Sir Henry should return to London. Please, please, be frank with me, Miss Stapleton, for ever since I have been here I have been conscious of shadows all round me. Life has become like that great Grimpen Mire, with little green patches everywhere into which one may sink and with no guide to point the track. Tell me then what it was that you meant, and I will promise to convey your warning to Sir Henry."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_47_1	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer lunches with the household	46	{"section": "section_47", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1806"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	sentence_82	lexical	\N	\N	81	{"text": "Should it miscarry, you are undone.", "section": "section_5", "entities": []}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_41_2	event	\N	Stapleton explains the moor’s strange sound and the moor’s features	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1573", "sentence_1574", "sentence_1575", "sentence_1576", "sentence_1577", "sentence_1583", "sentence_1584", "sentence_1585", "sentence_1586", "sentence_1587", "sentence_1588", "sentence_1589", "sentence_1590", "sentence_1591", "sentence_1592", "sentence_1593", "sentence_1594", "sentence_1595", "sentence_1596", "sentence_1597", "sentence_1598", "sentence_1599", "sentence_1600", "sentence_1601", "sentence_1602", "sentence_1603", "sentence_1604", "sentence_1605", "sentence_1606", "sentence_1607", "sentence_1608", "sentence_1609", "sentence_1610"], "communicatesContent": "Queer place, the moor! The peasants say it is the Hound of the Baskervilles calling for its prey. I’ve heard it once or twice before, but never quite so loud. Bogs make queer noises sometimes. It’s the mud settling, or the water rising, or something. Well, perhaps it was. Did you ever hear a bittern booming? It’s a very rare bird—practically extinct—in England now, but all things are possible upon the moor. Yes, I should not be surprised to learn that what we have heard is the cry of the last of the bitterns. Yes, it’s rather an uncanny place altogether. Look at the hillside yonder. What do you make of those? No, they are the homes of our worthy ancestors. Prehistoric man lived thickly on the moor, and as no one in particular has lived there since, we find all his little arrangements exactly as he left them. These are his wigwams with the roofs off. You can even see his hearth and his couch if you have the curiosity to go inside. Neolithic man—no date. He grazed his cattle on these slopes, and he learned to dig for tin when the bronze sword began to supersede the stone axe. Look at the great trench in the opposite hill. That is his mark. Yes, you will find some very singular points about the moor, Dr. Watson."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_41_3	event	\N	Stapleton chases a moth toward the mire	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1611", "sentence_1612", "sentence_1613", "sentence_1614", "sentence_1615"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_41_4	event	\N	Watson sees Stapleton’s sister approach on the path	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1616", "sentence_1617", "sentence_1618", "sentence_1619", "sentence_1620", "sentence_1621", "sentence_1622", "sentence_1623", "sentence_1624"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_42_1	event	\N	Beryl Stapleton orders Watson to go back to London at once and not return to the moor.	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "beryl_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1625", "sentence_1626", "sentence_1627", "sentence_1628", "sentence_1629", "sentence_1630", "sentence_1631", "sentence_1632", "sentence_1633", "sentence_1634", "sentence_1635", "sentence_1636", "sentence_1637", "sentence_1638", "sentence_1639", "sentence_1640", "sentence_1641", "sentence_1642", "sentence_1643", "sentence_1644", "sentence_1645", "sentence_1646"], "communicatesContent": "Go back! Go straight back to London, instantly. I cannot explain. But for God’s sake do what I ask you. Go back and never set foot upon the moor again. Can you not tell when a warning is for your own good? Go back to London! Start tonight! Get away from this place at all costs! Hush, my brother is coming! Not a word of what I have said. Would you mind getting that orchid for me among the mare’s-tails yonder? We are very rich in orchids on the moor, though, of course, you are rather late to see the beauties of the place."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_42_2	event	\N	Stapleton returns to Beryl and Watson after abandoning the chase.	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1647"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_42_3	event	\N	Stapleton greets Beryl and Watson and says he has been chasing a rare Cyclopides.	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1648", "sentence_1649", "sentence_1650", "sentence_1651", "sentence_1652", "sentence_1653", "sentence_1654"], "communicatesContent": "Halloa, Beryl! Well, Jack, you are very hot. Yes, I was chasing a Cyclopides. He is very rare and seldom found in the late autumn. What a pity that I should have missed him!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_42_4	event	\N	Beryl and Stapleton exchange remarks about Watson's identity and the timing of their talk.	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "beryl_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1655", "sentence_1656", "sentence_1657", "sentence_1658", "sentence_1659", "sentence_1660", "sentence_1661", "sentence_1662", "sentence_1663", "sentence_1664", "sentence_1665", "sentence_1666", "sentence_1667", "sentence_1668"], "communicatesContent": "You have introduced yourselves, I can see. I was telling Sir Henry that it was rather late for him to see the true beauties of the moor. I talked as if Dr. Watson were a resident instead of being merely a visitor. It cannot much matter to him whether it is early or late for the orchids. But you will come on, will you not, and see Merripit House?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_42_5	event	\N	Watson, Beryl, and Stapleton walk to Merripit House and enter it.	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1669", "sentence_1670", "sentence_1671", "sentence_1672"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_42_6	event	\N	Stapleton explains that he once had a school in the north country, that an epidemic ruined it, and that he now has botanical and zoological work on the moor.	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1678", "sentence_1679", "sentence_1680", "sentence_1681", "sentence_1682", "sentence_1683", "sentence_1684", "sentence_1685"], "communicatesContent": "I had a school. It was in the north country. The work to a man of my temperament was mechanical and uninteresting, but the privilege of living with youth, of helping to mould those young minds, and of impressing them with one’s own character and ideals was very dear to me. However, the fates were against us. A serious epidemic broke out in the school and three of the boys died. It never recovered from the blow, and much of my capital was irretrievably swallowed up. And yet, if it were not for the loss of the charming companionship of the boys, I could rejoice over my own misfortune, for, with my strong tastes for botany and zoology, I find an unlimited field of work here, and my sister is as devoted to Nature as I am. All this, Dr. Watson, has been brought upon your head by your expression as you surveyed the moor out of our window."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_43_1	event	\N	Miss Stapleton offers to show Dr. Watson her Lepidoptera collection	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "miss_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1696", "sentence_1697", "sentence_1698"], "communicatesContent": "Will you come upstairs, Dr. Watson, and inspect my collection of Lepidoptera? I think it is the most complete one in the south-west of England. By the time that you have looked through them lunch will be almost ready."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_43_2	event	\N	Watson leaves at once for his return journey	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1699", "sentence_1702"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_43_3	event	\N	Miss Stapleton cuts off Watson on the track	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "miss_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1703", "sentence_1704", "sentence_1705", "sentence_1706", "sentence_1707", "sentence_1708", "sentence_1709"], "communicatesContent": "I have run all the way in order to cut you off, Dr. Watson. I had not even time to put on my hat. I must not stop, or my brother may miss me. I wanted to say to you how sorry I am about the stupid mistake I made in thinking that you were Sir Henry. Please forget the words I said, which have no application whatever to you."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_43_5	event	\N	Miss Stapleton explains her concern about Sir Henry and the Baskerville family	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "miss_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1721", "sentence_1722", "sentence_1723", "sentence_1724", "sentence_1725", "sentence_1726", "sentence_1727"], "communicatesContent": "You make too much of it, Dr. Watson. My brother and I were very much shocked by the death of Sir Charles. We knew him very intimately, for his favourite walk was over the moor to our house. He was deeply impressed with the curse which hung over the family, and when this tragedy came I naturally felt that there must be some grounds for the fears which he had expressed. I was distressed therefore when another member of the family came down to live here, and I felt that he should be warned of the danger which he will run. That was all which I intended to convey."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_43_6	event	\N	Watson inspects Miss Stapleton’s Lepidoptera collection	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1696", "sentence_1697", "sentence_1698"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_44_1	event	\N	Miss Stapleton warns that Sir Henry should be taken away from the dangerous place	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "miss_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1731", "sentence_1732", "sentence_1733", "sentence_1734", "sentence_1735", "sentence_1736"], "communicatesContent": "If you have any influence with Sir Henry, take him away from a place which has always been fatal to his family. The world is wide. Why should he wish to live at the place of danger? Because it is the place of danger. That is Sir Henry’s nature."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_44_2	event	\N	Miss Stapleton says she cannot give definite information	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "miss_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1737", "sentence_1738"], "communicatesContent": "I cannot say anything definite, for I do not know anything definite."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_44_3	event	\N	Miss Stapleton explains that her brother wants the Hall inhabited and would be angry if he knew she had spoken	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "miss_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1742", "sentence_1743", "sentence_1744", "sentence_1745", "sentence_1746"], "communicatesContent": "My brother is very anxious to have the Hall inhabited, for he thinks it is for the good of the poor folk upon the moor. He would be very angry if he knew that I have said anything which might induce Sir Henry to go away. But I have done my duty now and I will say no more. I must go back, or he will miss me and suspect that I have seen you. Good-bye!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_44_4	event	\N	Miss Stapleton turns away and disappears among the boulders	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1747"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_44_5	event	\N	Watson continues toward Baskerville Hall	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1747"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_45_1	event	\N	Watson transcribes his own letters to Holmes	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1750", "sentence_1751"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_45_2	event	\N	Watson dates a letter from Baskerville Hall	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1752", "sentence_1753"], "communicatesContent": "MY DEAR HOLMES,"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_45_3	event	\N	Watson says his earlier letters and telegrams have kept Holmes informed	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1754"], "communicatesContent": "My previous letters and telegrams have kept you pretty well up to date as to all that has occurred in this most God-forsaken corner of the world."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_45_4	event	\N	Watson describes the moor's effect and ancient remains	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1755", "sentence_1756", "sentence_1757", "sentence_1758", "sentence_1759", "sentence_1760"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_45_5	event	\N	Watson returns to the facts concerning Sir Henry Baskerville	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1761", "sentence_1762", "sentence_1763"], "communicatesContent": "Let me, therefore, return to the facts concerning Sir Henry Baskerville."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_45_6	event	\N	Watson reports that there had been nothing important to relate before that day	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1764", "sentence_1765", "sentence_1766"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_45_7	event	\N	Watson identifies the escaped convict as a factor in the situation	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1767", "sentence_1768", "sentence_1769", "sentence_1770", "sentence_1771", "sentence_1772", "sentence_1773", "sentence_1774"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_45_8	event	\N	Watson says the Stapletons may need protection and suggests Perkins should sleep there	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1775", "sentence_1776", "sentence_1777", "sentence_1778", "sentence_1779"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_46_1	event	\N	Sir Henry Baskerville shows interest in Miss Stapleton	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1780", "sentence_1781", "sentence_1798", "sentence_1799", "sentence_1801", "sentence_1803"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_46_2	event	\N	Sir Henry visits Merripit House and makes the acquaintance of Miss Stapleton	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1797"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_46_3	event	\N	Sir Henry and Watson are taken to the origin site of the legend of wicked Hugo	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1788", "sentence_1789", "sentence_1790", "sentence_1791", "sentence_1792"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_46_4	event	\N	Sir Henry questions Stapleton about supernatural interference	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1793", "sentence_1794", "sentence_1795", "sentence_1796"], "communicatesContent": "whether he did really believe in the possibility of the interference of the supernatural in the affairs of men"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_46_5	event	\N	Stapleton replies guardedly about supernatural cases	45	{"section": "section_46", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1795", "sentence_1796"], "communicatesContent": "He told us of similar cases, where families had suffered from some evil influence."}
