hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_52_5	event	\N	Stapleton runs toward Sir Henry and Miss Stapleton and gesticulates excitedly in front of them.	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1964", "sentence_1965", "sentence_1966"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_52_6	event	\N	Stapleton abuses Sir Henry while Sir Henry offers explanations and Miss Stapleton stands by in silence.	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1967", "sentence_1968"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_52_7	event	\N	Stapleton turns to his sister and beckons her; she walks off beside him after a glance at Sir Henry.	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1969", "sentence_1970", "sentence_1971"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_52_8	event	\N	Sir Henry stands looking after Stapleton and Miss Stapleton, then walks back the way he came with his head hanging.	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1971"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_52_9	event	\N	Watson runs down the hill and meets Sir Henry at the bottom.	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1973"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_52_10	event	\N	Watson explains that he followed Sir Henry and witnessed what occurred.	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1979"], "communicatesContent": "how I had found it impossible to remain behind, how I had followed you, and how I had witnessed all that had occurred"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_52_11	event	\N	Sir Henry questions Watson about his presence and about whether Watson saw Stapleton come out onto them.	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1975", "sentence_1976", "sentence_1977", "sentence_1978", "sentence_1982", "sentence_1984", "sentence_1985", "sentence_1986", "sentence_1987", "sentence_1988", "sentence_1989", "sentence_1990", "sentence_1991", "sentence_1992", "sentence_1993", "sentence_1994", "sentence_1995", "sentence_1996"], "communicatesContent": "Halloa, Watson! Where have you dropped from? You don’t mean to say that you came after me in spite of all? Where had you engaged a seat? Quite in the back row, eh? But her brother was well up to the front. Did you see him come out on us? Did he ever strike you as being crazy—this brother of hers? I dare say not. I always thought him sane enough until today, but you can take it from me that either he or I ought to be in a straitjacket. What’s the matter with me, anyhow? You’ve lived near me for some weeks, Watson. Tell me straight, now! Is there anything that would prevent me from making a good husband to a woman that I loved?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_52_12	event	\N	Watson answers Sir Henry that he would not be prevented from making a good husband.	51	{"section": "section_52", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1996"], "communicatesContent": "I should say not."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_1	event	\N	Sir Henry offers to marry Stapleton's sister	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2008", "sentence_2009"], "communicatesContent": "I offered to marry her."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_2	event	\N	Stapleton runs at Sir Henry and his sister in anger	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2009", "sentence_2010"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_3	event	\N	Stapleton questions Sir Henry about his conduct toward the lady	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "mr_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2011", "sentence_2013"], "communicatesContent": "What was I doing with the lady? How dared I offer her attentions which were distasteful to her? Did I think that because I was a baronet I could do what I liked?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_4	event	\N	Sir Henry states his feelings for Stapleton's sister and repeats his proposal of marriage	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2015"], "communicatesContent": "My feelings towards his sister were such as I was not ashamed of, and I hoped that she might honour me by becoming my wife."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_5	event	\N	Sir Henry and Stapleton exchange heated words	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2016"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_6	event	\N	Stapleton leaves with his sister	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2017"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_7	event	\N	Holmes and Watson remain puzzled after Sir Henry's account	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2018", "sentence_2019", "sentence_2020", "sentence_2021"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_8	event	\N	Stapleton visits Sir Henry to offer apologies	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "mr_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2022", "sentence_2023", "sentence_2024"], "communicatesContent": "He came to offer apologies for his rudeness of the morning."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_9	event	\N	Stapleton and Sir Henry have a private interview and reconcile	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2023"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_10	event	\N	Sir Henry says the breach is healed and he is invited to dine at Merripit House	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2023"], "communicatesContent": "The breach is quite healed, and we are to dine at Merripit House next Friday as a sign of it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_53_11	event	\N	Sir Henry describes Stapleton's apology as handsome	52	{"section": "section_53", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2024"], "communicatesContent": "No man could make a more handsome apology than he has done."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_1	event	\N	Stapleton's sister says that she is everything in his life	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "mr_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2026"], "communicatesContent": "His sister is everything in his life."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_2	event	\N	Watson states that Stapleton and his sister have always been together and that he was a lonely man with her as his only companion	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2028"], "communicatesContent": "They have always been together, and according to his account he has been a very lonely man with only her as a companion."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_3	event	\N	Watson states that Stapleton was shocked when he saw Watson's attachment to his sister	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2029"], "communicatesContent": "He had not understood that I was becoming attached to her, but when he saw with his own eyes that it was really so, and that she might be taken away from him, it gave him such a shock that for a time he was not responsible for what he said or did."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_4	event	\N	Watson states that Stapleton was sorry for what had passed and recognized his conduct as foolish and selfish	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2030"], "communicatesContent": "He was very sorry for all that had passed, and he recognized how foolish and how selfish it was that he should imagine that he could hold a beautiful woman like his sister to himself for her whole life."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_5	event	\N	Watson states that Stapleton preferred his sister leaving him to a neighbour rather than to anyone else	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2031"], "communicatesContent": "If she had to leave him he had rather it was to a neighbour like myself than to anyone else."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_6	event	\N	Watson states that Stapleton would withdraw opposition if Watson promised to wait three months and seek only friendship	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2033", "sentence_2034"], "communicatesContent": "He would withdraw all opposition upon his part if I would promise for three months to let the matter rest and to be content with cultivating the lady’s friendship during that time without claiming her love."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_7	event	\N	Watson promises to let the matter rest for three months and to seek only friendship	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2034"], "communicatesContent": "I promised."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_8	event	\N	Watson reports that a mystery is cleared up and that Stapleton looked with disfavour upon his sister's suitor	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2035", "sentence_2037"], "communicatesContent": "So there is one of our small mysteries cleared up. We know now why Stapleton looked with disfavour upon his sister’s suitor—even when that suitor was so eligible a one as Sir Henry."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_9	event	\N	Watson and Sir Henry keep watch in Sir Henry's rooms until nearly three in the morning without hearing anything	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2042", "sentence_2043"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_10	event	\N	Watson and Sir Henry lower the lamp and sit smoking cigarettes in silence the next night	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2045", "sentence_2046"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_11	event	\N	Watson and Sir Henry hear a creak in the passage and follow it	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2047", "sentence_2048", "sentence_2049", "sentence_2050"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_12	event	\N	Watson and Sir Henry pursue the man through the corridor and other wing	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2051", "sentence_2052", "sentence_2053", "sentence_2054", "sentence_2055", "sentence_2056"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_54_13	event	\N	Watson and Sir Henry find the man crouching at the window with a candle	53	{"section": "section_54", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2058", "sentence_2059"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_1	event	\N	Sir Henry enters the room and Barrymore springs up from the window	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2061", "sentence_2062"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_2	event	\N	Sir Henry questions Barrymore about his presence at the window	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2063", "sentence_2064", "sentence_2068", "sentence_2069"], "communicatesContent": "What are you doing here, Barrymore?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_3	event	\N	Barrymore says he was checking the windows and holding a candle to the window	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2064", "sentence_2066", "sentence_2067", "sentence_2075", "sentence_2076"], "communicatesContent": "Nothing, sir. It was the window, sir. I go round at night to see that they are fastened. I was doing no harm, sir. I was holding a candle to the window."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_4	event	\N	Sir Henry demands the truth from Barrymore and asks what he was doing at the window	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2070", "sentence_2071", "sentence_2072", "sentence_2073"], "communicatesContent": "We have made up our minds to have the truth out of you, so it will save you trouble to tell it sooner rather than later. Come, now! No lies! What were you doing at that window?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_5	event	\N	Watson takes Barrymore's candle and holds it up as a possible signal	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2081", "sentence_2082", "sentence_2083", "sentence_2084"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_6	event	\N	A yellow light appears outside the window and Watson cries out	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2085", "sentence_2086", "sentence_2087", "sentence_2088"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_7	event	\N	Sir Henry moves the light across the window and confronts Barrymore about the signal	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2091", "sentence_2092", "sentence_2093", "sentence_2094", "sentence_2095", "sentence_2096"], "communicatesContent": "Move your light across the window, Watson! See, the other moves also! Now, you rascal, do you deny that it is a signal? Come, speak up! Who is your confederate out yonder, and what is this conspiracy that is going on?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_8	event	\N	Barrymore refuses to explain and says the matter is his business	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2097", "sentence_2098", "sentence_2099"], "communicatesContent": "It is my business, and not yours. I will not tell."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_9	event	\N	Sir Henry dismisses Barrymore from his employment and tells him to leave in disgrace	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2100", "sentence_2101", "sentence_2102", "sentence_2103", "sentence_2104", "sentence_2105"], "communicatesContent": "Then you leave my employment right away. And you go in disgrace. By thunder, you may well be ashamed of yourself. Your family has lived with mine for over a hundred years under this roof, and here I find you deep in some dark plot against me."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_55_10	event	\N	Mrs. Barrymore enters the doorway and speaks to Sir Henry	54	{"section": "section_55", "speakerId": "mrs_barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2107", "sentence_2108", "sentence_2109", "sentence_2110", "sentence_2111", "sentence_2112", "sentence_2113", "sentence_2114"], "communicatesContent": "No, no, sir; no, not against you! We have to go, Eliza. This is the end of it. You can pack our things. Oh, John, John, have I brought you to this? It is my doing, Sir Henry—all mine. He has done nothing except for my sake and because I asked him."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_56_1	event	\N	Mrs. Barrymore explains the night light signals and Selden's hiding place	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": "the_barrymore_woman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2117", "sentence_2118", "sentence_2119", "sentence_2120", "sentence_2121", "sentence_2122", "sentence_2123", "sentence_2124", "sentence_2125"], "communicatesContent": "My unhappy brother is starving on the moor. We cannot let him perish at our very gates. The light is a signal to him that food is ready for him, and his light out yonder is to show the spot to which to bring it. The escaped convict, sir—Selden, the criminal. I said that it was not my secret and that I could not tell it to you. But now you have heard it, and you will see that if there was a plot it was not against you."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_56_2	event	\N	Mrs. Barrymore identifies Selden as her younger brother	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": "the_barrymore_woman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2128", "sentence_2129", "sentence_2130", "sentence_2131"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, sir, my name was Selden, and he is my younger brother. We humoured him too much when he was a lad and gave him his own way in everything until he came to think that the world was made for his pleasure, and that he could do what he liked in it. Then as he grew older he met wicked companions, and the devil entered into him until he broke my mother’s heart and dragged our name in the dirt. From crime to crime he sank lower and lower until it is only the mercy of God which has snatched him from the scaffold; but to me, sir, he was always the little curly-headed boy that I had nursed and played with as an elder sister would."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_56_3	event	\N	Mrs. Barrymore explains why Selden broke prison and was sheltered	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": "the_barrymore_woman", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2132", "sentence_2133", "sentence_2134", "sentence_2135", "sentence_2136", "sentence_2137", "sentence_2138", "sentence_2139"], "communicatesContent": "That was why he broke prison, sir. He knew that I was here and that we could not refuse to help him. When he dragged himself here one night, weary and starving, with the warders hard at his heels, what could we do? We took him in and fed him and cared for him. Then you returned, sir, and my brother thought he would be safer on the moor than anywhere else until the hue and cry was over, so he lay in hiding there. But every second night we made sure if he was still there by putting a light in the window, and if there was an answer my husband took out some bread and meat to him. Every day we hoped that he was gone, but as long as he was there we could not desert him. That is the whole truth, as I am an honest Christian woman and you will see that if there is blame in the matter it does not lie with my husband but with me, for whose sake he has done all that he has."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_56_4	event	\N	Barrymore confirms Mrs. Barrymore's account	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2141", "sentence_2142", "sentence_2143"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, Sir Henry. Every word of it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_56_5	event	\N	Sir Henry dismisses the matter and sends Barrymore and his wife to their room	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2144", "sentence_2145", "sentence_2146"], "communicatesContent": "Well, I cannot blame you for standing by your own wife. Forget what I have said. Go to your room, you two, and we shall talk further about this matter in the morning."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_56_6	event	\N	Sir Henry opens the window and the group looks out at the distant yellow light	55	{"section": "section_56", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2147", "sentence_2148", "sentence_2149", "sentence_2150", "sentence_2151", "sentence_2152", "sentence_2153", "sentence_2154"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_1	event	\N	Watson agrees to come with Sir Henry	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2167"], "communicatesContent": "I will come."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_2	event	\N	Sir Henry tells Watson to get his revolver and boots and to start at once	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2168", "sentence_2169"], "communicatesContent": "Then get your revolver and put on your boots. The sooner we start the better, as the fellow may put out his light and be off."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_3	event	\N	Watson and Sir Henry leave the door and start their expedition	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2170"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_4	event	\N	Watson and Sir Henry hurry through the shrubbery and out onto the moor in rain and darkness	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2171", "sentence_2172", "sentence_2173"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_5	event	\N	Watson asks whether Sir Henry is armed	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2175", "sentence_2176"], "communicatesContent": "Are you armed?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_6	event	\N	Sir Henry says he has a hunting-crop	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2177"], "communicatesContent": "I have a hunting-crop."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_7	event	\N	Watson warns that they must close in rapidly and surprise the man	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2178", "sentence_2179"], "communicatesContent": "We must close in on him rapidly, for he is said to be a desperate fellow. We shall take him by surprise and have him at our mercy before he can resist."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_8	event	\N	Sir Henry asks what Holmes would say and quotes the hour of darkness	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2180", "sentence_2181"], "communicatesContent": "what would Holmes say to this? How about that hour of darkness in which the power of evil is exalted?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_9	event	\N	A strange cry sounds out across the moor	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2182", "sentence_2183", "sentence_2184"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_10	event	\N	Sir Henry grabs Watson's sleeve and reacts in fear	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2185", "sentence_2186"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_11	event	\N	Watson says the sound is a moor sound he heard once before	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2187", "sentence_2188", "sentence_2189"], "communicatesContent": "I don't know. It's a sound they have on the moor. I heard it once before."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_12	event	\N	The cry dies away and silence falls around them	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2190", "sentence_2191"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_13	event	\N	Sir Henry identifies the sound as the cry of a hound	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2192"], "communicatesContent": "it was the cry of a hound."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_14	event	\N	Watson says the countryside folk call it the cry of the Hound of the Baskervilles	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2194", "sentence_2195", "sentence_2196", "sentence_2197", "sentence_2198", "sentence_2199", "sentence_2200", "sentence_2201", "sentence_2202", "sentence_2203"], "communicatesContent": "They say it is the cry of the Hound of the Baskervilles."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_57_15	event	\N	Sir Henry states that a hound made the sound and that it seemed to come from far away	56	{"section": "section_57", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2204", "sentence_2205", "sentence_2206"], "communicatesContent": "A hound it was, but it seemed to come from miles away, over yonder, I think."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_58_1	event	\N	The travelers continue toward the yellow light on the moor	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2236", "sentence_2237", "sentence_2238"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_58_2	event	\N	They crouch behind a boulder and watch the candle	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2239", "sentence_2240", "sentence_2241"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_58_3	event	\N	Sir Henry asks what they should do and Watson tells him to wait and look for the man	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2242", "sentence_2243", "sentence_2244", "sentence_2245", "sentence_2246"], "communicatesContent": "What shall we do now?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_58_4	event	\N	They see the man by the candle	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2247", "sentence_2248", "sentence_2249", "sentence_2250", "sentence_2251", "sentence_2252", "sentence_2253"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_58_5	event	\N	Watson and Sir Henry rush forward as the convict curses and throws a rock	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2254", "sentence_2255", "sentence_2256"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_58_6	event	\N	The moon breaks through the clouds and the convict runs down the hill	57	{"section": "section_58", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2257", "sentence_2258", "sentence_2259"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_59_1	event	\N	The pursuers run after the fugitive but fail to catch him	58	{"section": "section_59", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2260", "sentence_2262"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_59_2	event	\N	The pursuers stop and watch the fugitive disappear into the distance	58	{"section": "section_59", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2263"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_5	event	\N	Watson leaves Sir Henry behind and drives off on his quest	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2565"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_59_3	event	\N	A strange man appears on the tor and is seen by Watson	58	{"section": "section_59", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2264", "sentence_2267", "sentence_2270", "sentence_2271"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_59_4	event	\N	Watson points out the man to the baronet	58	{"section": "section_59", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2276"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_59_5	event	\N	The man disappears from the tor	58	{"section": "section_59", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2276", "sentence_2277"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_59_6	event	\N	The speaker and the baronet discuss the sighting and the explanation	58	{"section": "section_59", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2279", "sentence_2280", "sentence_2281", "sentence_2282", "sentence_2283"], "communicatesContent": "A warder, no doubt; the moor has been thick with them since this fellow escaped."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_59_7	event	\N	The speaker says they will communicate to the Princetown people where they should look for the missing man	58	{"section": "section_59", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2284"], "communicatesContent": "Today we mean to communicate to the Princetown people where they should look for their missing man, but it is hard lines that we have not actually had the triumph of bringing him back as our own prisoner."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_59_8	event	\N	The speaker reports that they are making progress and that the Barrymores' motive has been found	58	{"section": "section_59", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2285", "sentence_2286", "sentence_2287", "sentence_2288", "sentence_2289", "sentence_2290", "sentence_2291", "sentence_2292"], "communicatesContent": "Such are the adventures of last night; much of what I tell you is irrelevant, but I feel it is best to let you have all the facts; we are certainly making some progress; so far as the Barrymores go we have found the motive of their actions; the moor with its mysteries and its strange inhabitants remains as inscrutable as ever; perhaps in my next I may be able to throw some light upon this also; best of all would it be if you could come down to us; in any case you will hear from me again in the course of the next few days."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_1	event	\N	Watson describes a dull, foggy day with drizzle over the moor house	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2299", "sentence_2300", "sentence_2301"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_2	event	\N	Watson notes that the baronet is in a black reaction after the night's excitements	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2302"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_3	event	\N	Watson records a feeling of impending danger	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2303"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_4	event	\N	Watson recalls the death of the last occupant of the Hall matching the family legend and reports from peasants of a strange creature on the moor	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2306", "sentence_2307"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_5	event	\N	Watson hears a sound resembling the distant baying of a hound twice	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2308", "sentence_2314"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_6	event	\N	Watson rejects a spectral hound explanation	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2309", "sentence_2310", "sentence_2311", "sentence_2312"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_7	event	\N	Watson states that Holmes would not listen to such fancies and that he is Holmes's agent	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2313"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_8	event	\N	Watson considers a huge hound as a possible explanation for the cries and the moor disturbance	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2315", "sentence_2316", "sentence_2317"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_9	event	\N	Watson identifies the human agency in London, the man in the cab, and the warning letter as real	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2318", "sentence_2319"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_10	event	\N	Watson asks where the protecting friend or enemy is now and whether he remained in London or followed them down	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2320", "sentence_2321"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_11	event	\N	Watson wonders whether the stranger he saw upon the tor is the same person	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2322", "sentence_2323"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_12	event	\N	Watson says he has now met all the neighbours and has not seen the figure down there	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2324", "sentence_2325", "sentence_2326"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_13	event	\N	Watson concludes that a stranger is still dogging them, as in London	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2327", "sentence_2328"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_60_14	event	\N	Watson vows to devote all his energies to finding the man	59	{"section": "section_60", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2329", "sentence_2330"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_1	event	\N	The narrator decides to say little and take his own steps	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2331", "sentence_2332", "sentence_2335"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_2	event	\N	Sir Henry is described as silent and distrait	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2333", "sentence_2334"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_3	event	\N	Barrymore asks leave to speak with Sir Henry and they confer in the study	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2336", "sentence_2337"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_4	event	\N	Watson hears raised voices during the discussion	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2338"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_5	event	\N	Sir Henry calls for Watson	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2339"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_6	event	\N	Sir Henry states that Barrymore considers himself wronged	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2340", "sentence_2341"], "communicatesContent": "Barrymore considers that he has a grievance. He thinks that it was unfair on our part to hunt his brother-in-law down when he, of his own free will, had told us the secret."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_7	event	\N	Barrymore apologizes for speaking too warmly and explains his surprise at the chase	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2342", "sentence_2343", "sentence_2344", "sentence_2345"], "communicatesContent": "I may have spoken too warmly, sir, and if I have, I am sure that I beg your pardon. At the same time, I was very much surprised when I heard you two gentlemen come back this morning and learned that you had been chasing Selden. The poor fellow has enough to fight against without my putting more upon his track."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_8	event	\N	Sir Henry argues that the secrecy was not voluntary and says Selden is a public danger	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2346", "sentence_2348", "sentence_2349", "sentence_2350", "sentence_2351", "sentence_2352"], "communicatesContent": "If you had told us of your own free will it would have been a different thing; you only told us, or rather your wife only told us, when it was forced from you and you could not help yourself. The man is a public danger. There are lonely houses scattered over the moor, and he is a fellow who would stick at nothing. You only want to get a glimpse of his face to see that. Look at Mr. Stapleton's house, for example, with no one but himself to defend it. There's no safety for anyone until he is under lock and key."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_9	event	\N	Barrymore insists Selden will not break into houses and will leave the country soon	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2353", "sentence_2354", "sentence_2355", "sentence_2356", "sentence_2357", "sentence_2358", "sentence_2359", "sentence_2360"], "communicatesContent": "He'll break into no house, sir. I give you my solemn word upon that. But he will never trouble anyone in this country again. I assure you, Sir Henry, that in a very few days the necessary arrangements will have been made and he will be on his way to South America. For God's sake, sir, I beg of you not to let the police know that he is still on the moor. They have given up the chase there, and he can lie quiet until the ship is ready for him. You can't tell on him without getting my wife and me into trouble. I beg you, sir, to say nothing to the police."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_10	event	\N	Watson gives his opinion that Selden's removal would relieve the taxpayer of a burden	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2361", "sentence_2362", "sentence_2363"], "communicatesContent": "If he were safely out of the country it would relieve the tax-payer of a burden."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_11	event	\N	Sir Henry raises the concern that Selden might hold someone up before leaving	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2364"], "communicatesContent": "But how about the chance of his holding someone up before he goes?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_12	event	\N	Barrymore says they have provided Selden with supplies and that committing a crime would reveal his hiding place	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2365", "sentence_2366", "sentence_2367"], "communicatesContent": "He would not do anything so mad, sir. We have provided him with all that he can want. To commit a crime would be to show where he was hiding."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_61_13	event	\N	Sir Henry agrees and calls Barrymore to account, then relents	60	{"section": "section_61", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2368", "sentence_2369", "sentence_2370", "sentence_2371", "sentence_2372", "sentence_2373", "sentence_2374"], "communicatesContent": "That is true. Well, Barrymore—"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_1	event	\N	Barrymore returns and reveals information about Sir Charles's death	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2375", "sentence_2376", "sentence_2377", "sentence_2378", "sentence_2379"], "communicatesContent": "I know something, Sir Henry, and perhaps I should have said it before, but it was long after the inquest that I found it out. I’ve never breathed a word about it yet to mortal man. It’s about poor Sir Charles’s death."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_2	event	\N	Sir Henry asks how Sir Charles died	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2380", "sentence_2381", "sentence_2382", "sentence_2383"], "communicatesContent": "Do you know how he died?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_3	event	\N	Barrymore says Sir Charles was at the gate to meet a woman	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2384", "sentence_2385", "sentence_2386", "sentence_2387", "sentence_2388"], "communicatesContent": "I know why he was at the gate at that hour. It was to meet a woman. Yes, sir."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_4	event	\N	Sir Henry asks for the woman's name	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2389", "sentence_2390", "sentence_2391", "sentence_2392"], "communicatesContent": "And the woman’s name?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_5	event	\N	Barrymore says the woman's initials were L. L.	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2390", "sentence_2391", "sentence_2392"], "communicatesContent": "I can’t give you the name, sir, but I can give you the initials. Her initials were L. L."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_6	event	\N	Sir Henry asks how Barrymore knows this	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2393"], "communicatesContent": "How do you know this, Barrymore?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_7	event	\N	Barrymore explains about the letter Sir Charles received that morning	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2394", "sentence_2395", "sentence_2396", "sentence_2397", "sentence_2398"], "communicatesContent": "Your uncle had a letter that morning. He had usually a great many letters, for he was a public man and well known for his kind heart, so that everyone who was in trouble was glad to turn to him. But that morning, as it chanced, there was only this one letter, so I took the more notice of it. It was from Coombe Tracey, and it was addressed in a woman’s hand."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_4	event	\N	Sir Henry first agrees to come and then decides that Watson should go alone	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2563", "sentence_2564"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_8	event	\N	Barrymore explains that his wife found ashes of a burned letter in Sir Charles's study	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2399", "sentence_2400", "sentence_2401", "sentence_2402", "sentence_2403", "sentence_2404"], "communicatesContent": "Well, sir, I thought no more of the matter, and never would have done had it not been for my wife. Only a few weeks ago she was cleaning out Sir Charles’s study and she found the ashes of a burned letter in the back of the grate. The greater part of it was charred to pieces, but one little slip, the end of a page, hung together, and the writing could still be read. It seemed to us to be a postscript at the end of the letter and it said: ‘Please, please, as you are a gentleman, burn this letter, and be at the gate by ten o clock. Beneath it were signed the initials L. L.’"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_9	event	\N	Sir Henry asks whether Barrymore has the slip	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2405", "sentence_2406"], "communicatesContent": "Have you got that slip?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_10	event	\N	Barrymore says the slip crumbled after it was moved	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2406"], "communicatesContent": "No, sir, it crumbled all to bits after we moved it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_11	event	\N	Sir Henry asks whether Sir Charles received other letters in the same writing	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2407", "sentence_2408", "sentence_2409"], "communicatesContent": "Had Sir Charles received any other letters in the same writing?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_12	event	\N	Barrymore says he has no idea who L. L. is and that finding the lady would help explain Sir Charles's death	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2410", "sentence_2411", "sentence_2412", "sentence_2413", "sentence_2414", "sentence_2415"], "communicatesContent": "No, sir. No more than you have. But I expect if we could lay our hands upon that lady we should know more about Sir Charles’s death."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_13	event	\N	Sir Henry says Barrymore concealed the information	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2416"], "communicatesContent": "I cannot understand, Barrymore, how you came to conceal this important information."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_14	event	\N	Barrymore explains why he concealed the information	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2417", "sentence_2418", "sentence_2419", "sentence_2420", "sentence_2421", "sentence_2422", "sentence_2423"], "communicatesContent": "It was immediately after that our own trouble came to us. We were both of us very fond of Sir Charles, as we well might be considering all that he has done for us. To rake this up couldn’t help our poor master, and it’s well to go carefully when there’s a lady in the case. I thought no good could come of it. But now you have been kind to us, and I feel as if it would be treating you unfairly not to tell you all that I know about the matter."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_62_15	event	\N	Sir Henry dismisses Barrymore	61	{"section": "section_62", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2424", "sentence_2425", "sentence_2426"], "communicatesContent": "Very good, Barrymore; you can go."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_63_1	event	\N	Watson writes a report for Holmes	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2438"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_63_2	event	\N	Watson walks on the moor in the evening	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2449"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_63_3	event	\N	Watson finds the black tor and looks out across the downs	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2451", "sentence_2452", "sentence_2453", "sentence_2454"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_63_4	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer overtakes Watson and gives him a lift homeward	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2457", "sentence_2459"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_63_5	event	\N	Watson and Dr. Mortimer travel in the dog-cart	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2459"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_63_6	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer becomes troubled over his spaniel's disappearance	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2460", "sentence_2461"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_63_7	event	\N	Watson asks Mortimer for the name of a woman with initials L. L.	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2463", "sentence_2464", "sentence_2465", "sentence_2466"], "communicatesContent": "I suppose there are few people living within driving distance of this whom you do not know? Can you, then, tell me the name of any woman whose initials are L. L.?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_63_8	event	\N	Dr. Mortimer thinks for a few minutes	62	{"section": "section_63", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2467"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_1	event	\N	He says there is no one among the farmers or gentry whose initials are those	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2468", "sentence_2469"], "communicatesContent": "No. There are a few gipsies and labouring folk for whom I can’t answer, but among the farmers or gentry there is no one whose initials are those."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_2	event	\N	He mentions Laura Lyons and her initials	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2470", "sentence_2471", "sentence_2472"], "communicatesContent": "Wait a bit though. There is Laura Lyons—her initials are L.—but she lives in Coombe Tracey."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_3	event	\N	Watson asks who she is	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2474", "sentence_2475"], "communicatesContent": "Who is she?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_4	event	\N	He identifies Laura Lyons as Frankland’s daughter	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2476"], "communicatesContent": "She is Frankland’s daughter."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_5	event	\N	Watson reacts and names old Frankland as the crank	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2477", "sentence_2478"], "communicatesContent": "What! Old Frankland the crank?"}
valley-of-fear	sentence_125	lexical	\N	\N	124	{"text": "said he, leaning back and staring at the ceiling.", "section": "section_6", "entities": []}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_6	event	\N	He explains Laura Lyons married Lyons, who deserted her	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2479", "sentence_2480", "sentence_2481", "sentence_2482", "sentence_2483", "sentence_2484"], "communicatesContent": "Exactly. She married an artist named Lyons, who came sketching on the moor. He proved to be a blackguard and deserted her. The fault from what I hear may not have been entirely on one side. Her father refused to have anything to do with her because she had married without his consent and perhaps for one or two other reasons as well. So, between the old sinner and the young one the girl has had a pretty bad time."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_7	event	\N	He explains how Laura Lyons is supported and helped	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2485", "sentence_2486", "sentence_2487", "sentence_2488", "sentence_2489", "sentence_2490", "sentence_2491"], "communicatesContent": "How does she live? I fancy old Frankland allows her a pittance, but it cannot be more, for his own affairs are considerably involved. Whatever she may have deserved one could not allow her to go hopelessly to the bad. Her story got about, and several of the people here did something to enable her to earn an honest living. Stapleton did for one, and Sir Charles for another. I gave a trifle myself. It was to set her up in a typewriting business."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_8	event	\N	Watson says he will go to Coombe Tracey to see Mrs. Laura Lyons	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2492", "sentence_2493"], "communicatesContent": "Tomorrow morning I shall find my way to Coombe Tracey, and if I can see this Mrs. Laura Lyons, of equivocal reputation, a long step will have been made towards clearing one incident in this chain of mysteries."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_9	event	\N	Watson questions Barrymore about a relation and another man on the moor	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2497", "sentence_2498", "sentence_2499", "sentence_2500", "sentence_2501", "sentence_2502", "sentence_2503", "sentence_2504", "sentence_2505", "sentence_2506", "sentence_2507", "sentence_2508", "sentence_2509", "sentence_2510", "sentence_2511", "sentence_2512", "sentence_2513", "sentence_2514", "sentence_2515", "sentence_2516", "sentence_2517"], "communicatesContent": "Well, has this precious relation of yours departed, or is he still lurking out yonder?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_10	event	\N	Barrymore says he has not heard of the relation for three days and that food disappeared	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2501", "sentence_2502", "sentence_2503", "sentence_2504", "sentence_2505", "sentence_2506", "sentence_2507"], "communicatesContent": "I don’t know, sir. I hope to heaven that he has gone, for he has brought nothing but trouble here! I’ve not heard of him since I left out food for him last, and that was three days ago. No, sir, but the food was gone when next I went that way. So you would think, sir, unless it was the other man who took it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_11	event	\N	Watson asks Barrymore about another man on the moor	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2509", "sentence_2510"], "communicatesContent": "You know that there is another man then?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_64_12	event	\N	Barrymore says there is another man upon the moor	63	{"section": "section_64", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2511", "sentence_2512", "sentence_2513", "sentence_2514", "sentence_2515", "sentence_2516", "sentence_2517"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, sir; there is another man upon the moor. No, sir. Selden told me of him, sir, a week ago or more. He’s in hiding, too, but he’s not a convict as far as I can make out. I don’t like it, Dr. Watson—I tell you straight, sir, that I don’t like it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_65_1	event	\N	Barrymore objects to the continuing disturbances and wants Sir Henry to leave for London	64	{"section": "section_65", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2518", "sentence_2519", "sentence_2520", "sentence_2521", "sentence_2522", "sentence_2523", "sentence_2524", "sentence_2525"], "communicatesContent": "It's all these goings-on, sir. There's foul play somewhere, and there's black villainy brewing, to that I’ll swear! Very glad I should be, sir, to see Sir Henry on his way back to London again!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_65_2	event	\N	Watson asks Barrymore about the stranger hiding on the moor	64	{"section": "section_65", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2526", "sentence_2531", "sentence_2535", "sentence_2536", "sentence_2537", "sentence_2538"], "communicatesContent": "But what is it that alarms you? But about this stranger. Can you tell me anything about him? What did Selden say? Did he find out where he hid, or what he was doing?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_65_3	event	\N	Barrymore reports what Selden saw and what he found about the stranger	64	{"section": "section_65", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2539", "sentence_2540", "sentence_2541", "sentence_2542", "sentence_2543", "sentence_2544", "sentence_2545", "sentence_2546"], "communicatesContent": "He saw him once or twice, but he is a deep one and gives nothing away. At first he thought that he was the police, but soon he found that he had some lay of his own. A kind of gentleman he was, as far as he could see, but what he was doing he could not make out. Among the old houses on the hillside—the stone huts where the old folk used to live. Selden found out that he has got a lad who works for him and brings all he needs. I dare say he goes to Coombe Tracey for what he wants."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_65_4	event	\N	Watson ends the conversation and Barrymore leaves	64	{"section": "section_65", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2547", "sentence_2548", "sentence_2549"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_65_5	event	\N	Watson looks out through the black window at the stormy moor	64	{"section": "section_65", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2549", "sentence_2550", "sentence_2551", "sentence_2552", "sentence_2553", "sentence_2554"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_1	event	\N	The narrator recounts prior deductions and begins the next phase of the inquiry	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2557", "sentence_2558", "sentence_2559", "sentence_2560"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_2	event	\N	Watson cannot tell Sir Henry what he has learned because Dr. Mortimer stays with him at cards until late	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2561"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_3	event	\N	Watson tells Sir Henry about his discovery and asks to accompany him to Coombe Tracey	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2562"], "communicatesContent": "I informed him about my discovery and asked him whether he would care to accompany me to Coombe Tracey."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_6	event	\N	Watson arrives at Coombe Tracey, puts up the horses, and makes inquiries for Mrs. Lyons	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2566", "sentence_2567"], "communicatesContent": "put up the horses"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_7	event	\N	A maid shows Watson into Mrs. Lyons's sitting-room	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2568"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_8	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons greets Watson, sees he is a stranger, sits down again, and asks the object of his visit	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": "laura_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2568", "sentence_2569"], "communicatesContent": "the object of your visit"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_9	event	\N	Watson observes Mrs. Lyons's appearance	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2570", "sentence_2571", "sentence_2572", "sentence_2573", "sentence_2574", "sentence_2575", "sentence_2576"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_10	event	\N	Watson introduces himself by saying he knows Mrs. Lyons's father	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2578"], "communicatesContent": "I have the pleasure of knowing your father."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_11	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons rejects the connection with her father and says she owes him nothing	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": "laura_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2580", "sentence_2581", "sentence_2582"], "communicatesContent": "There is nothing in common between my father and me. I owe him nothing, and his friends are not mine. If it were not for the late Sir Charles Baskerville and some other kind hearts I might have starved for all that my father cared."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_66_12	event	\N	Watson states that he has come to see Mrs. Lyons about Sir Charles Baskerville	65	{"section": "section_66", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2583"], "communicatesContent": "It was about the late Sir Charles Baskerville that I have come here to see you."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_67_1	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons answers questions about Sir Charles Baskerville and a letter she wrote to him	66	{"section": "section_67", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2584", "sentence_2585", "sentence_2586", "sentence_2587", "sentence_2588", "sentence_2589", "sentence_2590", "sentence_2591", "sentence_2592", "sentence_2593", "sentence_2594", "sentence_2595", "sentence_2596", "sentence_2597", "sentence_2598", "sentence_2599", "sentence_2600", "sentence_2601", "sentence_2602", "sentence_2603", "sentence_2604", "sentence_2605", "sentence_2606", "sentence_2607", "sentence_2608", "sentence_2609", "sentence_2610", "sentence_2611", "sentence_2612", "sentence_2613", "sentence_2614", "sentence_2615", "sentence_2616", "sentence_2617", "sentence_2618", "sentence_2619", "sentence_2620", "sentence_2621", "sentence_2622", "sentence_2623", "sentence_2624", "sentence_2625", "sentence_2626", "sentence_2627", "sentence_2628", "sentence_2629", "sentence_2630", "sentence_2631", "sentence_2632", "sentence_2633", "sentence_2634", "sentence_2635", "sentence_2636", "sentence_2637", "sentence_2638", "sentence_2639", "sentence_2640", "sentence_2641", "sentence_2642"], "communicatesContent": "What can I tell you about him? You knew him, did you not? I have already said that I owe a great deal to his kindness. If I am able to support myself it is largely due to the interest which he took in my unhappy situation. I certainly wrote to him once or twice to acknowledge his delicacy and his generosity. No. Yes, once or twice, when he came into Coombe Tracey. There were several gentlemen who knew my sad history and united to help me. One was Mr. Stapleton, a neighbour and intimate friend of Sir Charles’s. He was exceedingly kind, and it was through him that Sir Charles learned about my affairs. Then I answer, certainly not. Yes, I did write it. I did write it. Why should I deny it? I have no reason to be ashamed of it. I wished him to help me. I believed that if I had an interview I could gain his help, so I asked him to meet me. Because I had only just learned that he was going to London next day and might be away for months. There were reasons why I could not get there earlier. Do you think a woman could go alone at that hour to a bachelor’s house?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_67_2	event	\N	Holmes questions Mrs. Lyons about her correspondence with Sir Charles	66	{"section": "section_67", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2590", "sentence_2591", "sentence_2592", "sentence_2593", "sentence_2594", "sentence_2595", "sentence_2600", "sentence_2602", "sentence_2604", "sentence_2607", "sentence_2613", "sentence_2614", "sentence_2616", "sentence_2617", "sentence_2619", "sentence_2622", "sentence_2623", "sentence_2624", "sentence_2626", "sentence_2628", "sentence_2631", "sentence_2632", "sentence_2633", "sentence_2634", "sentence_2635", "sentence_2636", "sentence_2637", "sentence_2638", "sentence_2639", "sentence_2641", "sentence_2642"], "communicatesContent": "Did you correspond with him? Did you correspond with Sir Charles? Have you the dates of those letters? Have you ever met him? But if you saw him so seldom and wrote so seldom, how did he know enough about your affairs to be able to help you, as you say that he has done? Did you ever write to Sir Charles asking him to meet you? I am sorry, madam, but I must repeat it. Not on the very day of Sir Charles’s death? Surely your memory deceives you. I could even quote a passage of your letter. It ran 'Please, please, as you are a gentleman, burn this letter, and be at the gate by ten o’clock.' You do Sir Charles an injustice. He did burn the letter. But sometimes a letter may be legible even when burned. You acknowledge now that you wrote it? But why at such an hour? But why a rendezvous in the garden instead of a visit to the house?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_68_1	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons denies going to the appointment	67	{"section": "section_68", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2644", "sentence_2647", "sentence_2648"], "communicatesContent": "I never went. Something intervened to prevent my going."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_68_2	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons refuses to disclose the reason she did not go	67	{"section": "section_68", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2649", "sentence_2650", "sentence_2651"], "communicatesContent": "That is a private matter. I cannot tell it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_68_3	event	\N	Watson states that Mrs. Lyons admits making an appointment with Sir Charles and denies keeping it	67	{"section": "section_68", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2652", "sentence_2653"], "communicatesContent": "You acknowledge then that you made an appointment with Sir Charles at the very hour and place at which he met his death, but you deny that you kept the appointment."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_68_4	event	\N	Watson questions Mrs. Lyons about her letter and her reasons	67	{"section": "section_68", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2655", "sentence_2656", "sentence_2657", "sentence_2659", "sentence_2660", "sentence_2661", "sentence_2662", "sentence_2663", "sentence_2664", "sentence_2665"], "communicatesContent": "You are taking a very great responsibility and putting yourself in a very false position by not making an absolutely clean breast of all that you know. If I have to call in the aid of the police you will find how seriously you are compromised. If your position is innocent, why did you in the first instance deny having written to Sir Charles upon that date? And why were you so pressing that Sir Charles should destroy your letter? I did not say that I had read all the letter. I quoted the postscript. The letter had, as I said, been burned and it was not all legible. I ask you once again why it was that you were so pressing that Sir Charles should destroy this letter which he received on the day of his death."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_20	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says both cases were decided in his favour	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2718"], "communicatesContent": "Both cases decided, Dr. Watson, and both in my favour."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_68_5	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons explains her marriage and the reason for the letter	67	{"section": "section_68", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2668", "sentence_2669", "sentence_2671", "sentence_2672", "sentence_2673", "sentence_2674", "sentence_2675"], "communicatesContent": "I will tell you, then. If you have heard anything of my unhappy history you will know that I made a rash marriage and had reason to regret it. My life has been one incessant persecution from a husband whom I abhor. The law is upon his side, and every day I am faced by the possibility that he may force me to live with him. At the time that I wrote this letter to Sir Charles I had learned that there was a prospect of my regaining my freedom if certain expenses could be met. It meant everything to me—peace of mind, happiness, self-respect—everything. I knew Sir Charles’s generosity, and I thought that if he heard the story from my own lips he would help me."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_68_6	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons explains why she did not go and why she did not write again	67	{"section": "section_68", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2676", "sentence_2677", "sentence_2678", "sentence_2679"], "communicatesContent": "Because I received help in the interval from another source. So I should have done had I not seen his death in the paper next morning."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_68_7	event	\N	Watson leaves the interview baffled and considers the next clue	67	{"section": "section_68", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2680", "sentence_2681", "sentence_2682", "sentence_2683", "sentence_2684", "sentence_2685", "sentence_2686", "sentence_2687", "sentence_2688", "sentence_2689", "sentence_2690", "sentence_2691", "sentence_2692"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_1	event	\N	Dr. Watson drives back across the moor	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2694"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_2	event	\N	Dr. Watson notices traces on the hills	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2694"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_3	event	\N	Barrymore gives a vague indication about the stranger's lodging	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2695"], "communicatesContent": "the stranger lived in one of these abandoned huts"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_4	event	\N	Dr. Watson recalls seeing the man on the Black Tor	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2696"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_5	event	\N	Dr. Watson sets the Black Tor as the centre of the search	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2697"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_6	event	\N	Dr. Watson plans to search every hut on the moor	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2698"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_7	event	\N	Dr. Watson plans to confront the man if he is found inside the hut	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2699"], "communicatesContent": "who he was and why he had dogged us so long"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_8	event	\N	Dr. Watson notes that the man might slip away in Regent Street but not on the lonely moor	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2700"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_9	event	\N	Dr. Watson plans to remain at the hut if the tenant is absent	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2701"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_10	event	\N	Holmes misses the man in London	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2702"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_11	event	\N	Dr. Watson considers finding the man where Holmes failed a triumph	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2703"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_12	event	\N	Dr. Watson meets Mr. Frankland outside his garden gate	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2705", "sentence_2706"], "communicatesContent": "Good-day, Dr. Watson, you must really give your horses a rest and come in to have a glass of wine and to congratulate me."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_13	event	\N	Dr. Watson alights from the wagonette and sends a message to Sir Henry	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2708"], "communicatesContent": "that I should walk over in time for dinner"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_14	event	\N	Dr. Watson follows Frankland into the dining-room	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2709"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_15	event	\N	Mr. Frankland announces a great day and a double event	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2710", "sentence_2711"], "communicatesContent": "It is a great day for me, sir—one of the red-letter days of my life. I have brought off a double event."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_16	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says he will teach locals that law is law	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2712"], "communicatesContent": "I mean to teach them in these parts that law is law, and that there is a man here who does not fear to invoke it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_17	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says he established a right of way through Middleton's park	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2713", "sentence_2714"], "communicatesContent": "I have established a right of way through the centre of old Middleton’s park, slap across it, sir, within a hundred yards of his own front door."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_18	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says he will teach magnates that they cannot ride roughshod over commoners' rights	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2715"], "communicatesContent": "We’ll teach these magnates that they cannot ride roughshod over the rights of the commoners, confound them!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_19	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says he has closed the wood used by the Fernworthy folk	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2716", "sentence_2717"], "communicatesContent": "And I’ve closed the wood where the Fernworthy folk used to picnic. These infernal people seem to think that there are no rights of property, and that they can swarm where they like with their papers and their bottles."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_21	event	\N	Mr. Frankland mentions his earlier trespass case against Sir John Morland	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2719", "sentence_2720", "sentence_2721", "sentence_2722", "sentence_2723", "sentence_2724"], "communicatesContent": "I haven’t had such a day since I had Sir John Morland for trespass because he shot in his own warren. Look it up in the books, sir. It will repay reading—Frankland v. Morland, Court of Queen’s Bench. It cost me £200, but I got my verdict."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_22	event	\N	Dr. Watson asks whether the lawsuit did Mr. Frankland any good	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2725"], "communicatesContent": "Did it do you any good?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_23	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says the lawsuit did him no good and that he acted from public duty	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2726", "sentence_2727", "sentence_2728"], "communicatesContent": "None, sir, none. I am proud to say that I had no interest in the matter. I act entirely from a sense of public duty."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_24	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says the Fernworthy people may burn him in effigy	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2729", "sentence_2730"], "communicatesContent": "I have no doubt, for example, that the Fernworthy people will burn me in effigy tonight. I told the police last time they did it that they should stop these disgraceful exhibitions."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_25	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says the County Constabulary is in a scandalous state and has not protected him	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2731"], "communicatesContent": "The County Constabulary is in a scandalous state, sir, and it has not afforded me the protection to which I am entitled."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_26	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says the Frankland v. Regina case will draw public attention	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2732", "sentence_2733"], "communicatesContent": "The case of Frankland v. Regina will bring the matter before the attention of the public."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_69_27	event	\N	Mr. Frankland says his warning words have already come true	68	{"section": "section_69", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2734"], "communicatesContent": "I told them that they would have occasion to regret their treatment of me, and already my words have come true."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_1	event	\N	Watson asks how Frankland means this	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2735", "sentence_2736"], "communicatesContent": "How so?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_2	event	\N	Frankland says he could inform on the men and refuse to help them	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2737", "sentence_2738"], "communicatesContent": "Because I could tell them what they are dying to know; but nothing would induce me to help the rascals in any way."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_3	event	\N	Watson asks whether Frankland means the convict on the moor	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2741", "sentence_2742"], "communicatesContent": "Some poaching case, no doubt?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_4	event	\N	Frankland identifies the matter as the convict on the moor	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2743", "sentence_2744"], "communicatesContent": "Ha, ha, my boy, a very much more important matter than that! What about the convict on the moor?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_5	event	\N	Watson asks whether Frankland knows where the convict is	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2745", "sentence_2746", "sentence_2747"], "communicatesContent": "You don’t mean that you know where he is?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_6	event	\N	Frankland claims he could help police catch the convict by tracing his food supply	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2748", "sentence_2749"], "communicatesContent": "I may not know exactly where he is, but I am quite sure that I could help the police to lay their hands on him. Has it never struck you that the way to catch that man was to find out where he got his food and so trace it to him?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_7	event	\N	Frankland says he has seen the messenger who takes food to the convict	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2751", "sentence_2752"], "communicatesContent": "I know it because I have seen with my own eyes the messenger who takes him his food."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_8	event	\N	Frankland says the convict's food is taken by a child who passes daily through his telescope view	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2755", "sentence_2756", "sentence_2757", "sentence_2758"], "communicatesContent": "You’ll be surprised to hear that his food is taken to him by a child. I see him every day through my telescope upon the roof. He passes along the same path at the same hour, and to whom should he be going except to the convict?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_9	event	\N	Watson suggests the boy may be a shepherd's son carrying dinner to his father	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2766"], "communicatesContent": "I should say that it was much more likely that it was the son of one of the moorland shepherds taking out his father’s dinner."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_10	event	\N	Frankland rejects Watson's suggestion and points out the stony ground near Black Tor	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2769", "sentence_2770", "sentence_2771", "sentence_2772", "sentence_2773", "sentence_2774", "sentence_2775"], "communicatesContent": "Indeed, sir! Do you see that Black Tor over yonder? Well, do you see the low hill beyond with the thornbush upon it? It is the stoniest part of the whole moor. Is that a place where a shepherd would be likely to take his station? Your suggestion, sir, is a most absurd one."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_11	event	\N	Watson admits he spoke without knowing all the facts	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2776"], "communicatesContent": "I had spoken without knowing all the facts."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_1	event	\N	Watson sits breathless for a moment after hearing Holmes's voice	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2865", "sentence_2866"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_2	event	\N	Watson cries out Holmes's name	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2868", "sentence_2869"], "communicatesContent": "Holmes!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_70_12	event	\N	Frankland says he has seen the boy repeatedly with a bundle and notices movement on the hillside	69	{"section": "section_70", "speakerId": "mr_frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2778", "sentence_2779", "sentence_2780", "sentence_2781", "sentence_2782", "sentence_2783", "sentence_2784", "sentence_2785"], "communicatesContent": "You may be sure, sir, that I have very good grounds before I come to an opinion. I have seen the boy again and again with his bundle. Every day, and sometimes twice a day, I have been able—but wait a moment, Dr. Watson. Do my eyes deceive me, or is there at the present moment something moving upon that hillside? Come, sir, come! You will see with your own eyes and judge for yourself."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_1	event	\N	A telescope stands on the flat leads of the house	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2786"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_2	event	\N	Frankland looks through the telescope and cries out in satisfaction	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2787"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_3	event	\N	Frankland speaks urgently to Watson about the boy before he passes over the hill	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": "frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2788", "sentence_2789"], "communicatesContent": "Quick, Dr. Watson, quick, before he passes over the hill!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_4	event	\N	The boy climbs slowly up the hill with a bundle on his shoulder	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2789"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_5	event	\N	Watson sees the boy outlined against the sky at the crest of the hill	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2790"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_6	event	\N	The boy looks round furtively as if dreading pursuit	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2791"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_7	event	\N	The boy vanishes over the hill	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2792"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_8	event	\N	Frankland says the boy has a secret errand and demands secrecy from Watson	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": "frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2793", "sentence_2794", "sentence_2795", "sentence_2796", "sentence_2797", "sentence_2798", "sentence_2799", "sentence_2800"], "communicatesContent": "Am I right? Certainly, there is a boy who seems to have some secret errand. And what the errand is even a county constable could guess. But not one word shall they have from me, and I bind you to secrecy also, Dr. Watson. Not a word! You understand!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_9	event	\N	Frankland complains that the police treated him shamefully and refuses to help them	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2801", "sentence_2802", "sentence_2803", "sentence_2804", "sentence_2805"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_10	event	\N	Frankland invites Watson to drink and celebrate	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": "frankland", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2806", "sentence_2807"], "communicatesContent": "Surely you are not going! You will help me to empty the decanter in honour of this great occasion!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_11	event	\N	Watson resists Frankland's solicitations and declines to walk home with him	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2808"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_12	event	\N	Watson leaves the road, crosses the moor, and heads for the hill	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2809"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_13	event	\N	Watson climbs to the summit of the hill at sunset	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2811"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_14	event	\N	Watson sees the stone huts below the hill	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2818"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_15	event	\N	Watson approaches the hut and confirms that the place had been used as a habitation	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2822", "sentence_2823"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_71_16	event	\N	Watson enters the hut and finds it empty	70	{"section": "section_71", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2824", "sentence_2825", "sentence_2826", "sentence_2827", "sentence_2828"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_72_1	event	\N	The narrator finds signs of occupation in the hut	71	{"section": "section_72", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2831", "sentence_2832", "sentence_2833", "sentence_2834", "sentence_2835", "sentence_2836"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_72_2	event	\N	The narrator examines the cloth bundle and finds a paper note beneath it	71	{"section": "section_72", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2837", "sentence_2838"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_72_3	event	\N	The paper note states that Dr. Watson has gone to Coombe Tracey	71	{"section": "section_72", "speakerId": "paper_note", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2838"], "communicatesContent": "Dr. Watson has gone to Coombe Tracey."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_72_4	event	\N	The narrator searches the hut for other reports	71	{"section": "section_72", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2844", "sentence_2845"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_72_5	event	\N	The narrator waits in the hut for the tenant to return	71	{"section": "section_72", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2854", "sentence_2855"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_72_6	event	\N	Footsteps approach the hut and a shadow falls across the opening	71	{"section": "section_72", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2856", "sentence_2857", "sentence_2858", "sentence_2859", "sentence_2860"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_73_1	event	\N	A well-known voice speaks to Watson and says it is a lovely evening and that he will be more comfortable outside than in	72	{"section": "section_73", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2861", "sentence_2862"], "communicatesContent": "It is a lovely evening, my dear Watson. I really think that you will be more comfortable outside than in."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_87_2	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons says she knew him	86	{"section": "section_87", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3482"], "communicatesContent": "I knew him."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_3	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson to come out and be careful with the revolver	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2870"], "communicatesContent": "Come out, and please be careful with the revolver."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_4	event	\N	Watson stoops under the lintel and sees Holmes seated outside	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2871", "sentence_2872", "sentence_2873"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_5	event	\N	Watson expresses gladness at seeing Holmes and shakes his hand	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2874"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_6	event	\N	Holmes comments on Watson's surprise and explains how he found Watson's retreat	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2875", "sentence_2876", "sentence_2877", "sentence_2878", "sentence_2879", "sentence_2880", "sentence_2881", "sentence_2882", "sentence_2883", "sentence_2884"], "communicatesContent": "Or more astonished, eh? The surprise was not all on one side, I assure you. I had no idea that you had found my occasional retreat, still less that you were inside it, until I was within twenty paces of the door. My footprint, I presume? No, Watson, I fear that I could not undertake to recognize your footprint amid all the footprints of the world. If you seriously desire to deceive me you must change your tobacconist; for when I see the stub of a cigarette marked Bradley, Oxford Street, I know that my friend Watson is in the neighbourhood. You will see it there beside the path. You threw it down, no doubt, at that supreme moment when you charged into the empty hut. Exactly."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_7	event	\N	Holmes explains that he thought Watson was waiting in ambush	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2885", "sentence_2886", "sentence_2887", "sentence_2888"], "communicatesContent": "I thought as much—and knowing your admirable tenacity I was convinced that you were sitting in ambush, a weapon within reach, waiting for the tenant to return. So you actually thought that I was the criminal? I did not know who you were, but I was determined to find out. Excellent, Watson!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_8	event	\N	Holmes asks how Watson localized him and mentions the moonlit sighting	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2889", "sentence_2890", "sentence_2891", "sentence_2892", "sentence_2893", "sentence_2894", "sentence_2895"], "communicatesContent": "And how did you localise me? You saw me, perhaps, on the night of the convict hunt, when I was so imprudent as to allow the moon to rise behind me? Yes, I saw you then. And have no doubt searched all the huts until you came to this one? No, your boy had been observed, and that gave me a guide where to look. The old gentleman with the telescope, no doubt. I could not make it out when first I saw the light flashing upon the lens."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_9	event	\N	Holmes looks into the hut and notices Cartwright's supplies and Watson's paper	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2896", "sentence_2897", "sentence_2898", "sentence_2899"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_10	event	\N	Watson confirms that he went to Coombe Tracey to see Mrs. Laura Lyons	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2900", "sentence_2901", "sentence_2902"], "communicatesContent": "Yes. Exactly."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_11	event	\N	Holmes praises Watson and says their researches have been running on parallel lines	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2903", "sentence_2904"], "communicatesContent": "Well done! Our researches have evidently been running on parallel lines, and when we unite our results I expect we shall have a fairly full knowledge of the case."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_12	event	\N	Watson says the responsibility and mystery were becoming too much for his nerves and asks how Holmes came there	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2905", "sentence_2906", "sentence_2907"], "communicatesContent": "Well, I am glad from my heart that you are here, for indeed the responsibility and the mystery were both becoming too much for my nerves. But how in the name of wonder did you come here, and what have you been doing? I thought that you were in Baker Street working out that case of blackmailing."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_13	event	\N	Holmes says he wanted Watson to think he was in Baker Street	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2908"], "communicatesContent": "That was what I wished you to think."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_74_14	event	\N	Watson accuses Holmes of using him and not trusting him	73	{"section": "section_74", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2909", "sentence_2910", "sentence_2911"], "communicatesContent": "Then you use me, and yet do not trust me! I think that I have deserved better at your hands, Holmes."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_75_1	event	\N	Holmes explains why he concealed himself and why he kept Watson uninformed	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2912", "sentence_2913", "sentence_2914", "sentence_2915", "sentence_2916", "sentence_2917", "sentence_2918", "sentence_2919", "sentence_2920", "sentence_2921"], "communicatesContent": "My dear fellow, you have been invaluable to me in this as in many other cases, and I beg that you will forgive me if I have seemed to play a trick upon you. In truth, it was partly for your own sake that I did it, and it was my appreciation of the danger which you ran which led me to come down and examine the matter for myself. Had I been with Sir Henry and you it is confident that my point of view would have been the same as yours, and my presence would have warned our very formidable opponents to be on their guard. As it is, I have been able to get about as I could not possibly have done had I been living in the Hall, and I remain an unknown factor in the business, ready to throw in all my weight at a critical moment. For you to know could not have helped us and might possibly have led to my discovery. You would have wished to tell me something, or in your kindness you would have brought me out some comfort or other, and so an unnecessary risk would be run. I brought Cartwright down with me—you remember the little chap at the express office—and he has seen after my simple wants: a loaf of bread and a clean collar. What does man want more? He has given me an extra pair of eyes upon a very active pair of feet, and both have been invaluable."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_75_2	event	\N	Holmes returns Watson's reports and praises him	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2924", "sentence_2925", "sentence_2926", "sentence_2927"], "communicatesContent": "Here are your reports, my dear fellow, and very well thumbed, I assure you. I made excellent arrangements, and they are only delayed one day upon their way. I must compliment you exceedingly upon the zeal and the intelligence which you have shown over an extraordinarily difficult case."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_75_3	event	\N	Watson remains upset and then calms after Holmes's praise	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2928", "sentence_2929", "sentence_2930"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_75_4	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson about the result of his visit to Mrs. Laura Lyons	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2931", "sentence_2932"], "communicatesContent": "And now tell me the result of your visit to Mrs. Laura Lyons—it was not difficult for me to guess that it was to see her that you had gone, for I am already aware that she is the one person in Coombe Tracey who might be of service to us in the matter. In fact, if you had not gone today it is exceedingly probable that I should have gone tomorrow."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_75_5	event	\N	Watson and Holmes withdraw into the hut at dusk as the air turns chill	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2933", "sentence_2934"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_75_6	event	\N	Watson tells Holmes about his conversation with Mrs. Laura Lyons	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2935", "sentence_2936"], "communicatesContent": "I told Holmes of my conversation with the lady."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_75_7	event	\N	Holmes says the information is important and that it fills a gap	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2937", "sentence_2938"], "communicatesContent": "This is most important. It fills up a gap which I had been unable to bridge in this most complex affair."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_75_8	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson about an intimacy between Mrs. Laura Lyons and Stapleton	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2939", "sentence_2940"], "communicatesContent": "You are aware, perhaps, that a close intimacy exists between this lady and the man Stapleton?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_75_9	event	\N	Holmes states that Mrs. Laura Lyons and Stapleton meet, write, and understand each other	74	{"section": "section_75", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2941", "sentence_2942", "sentence_2943", "sentence_2944"], "communicatesContent": "There can be no doubt about the matter. They meet, they write, there is a complete understanding between them. Now, this puts a very powerful weapon into our hands. If I could only use it to detach his wife—"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_1	event	\N	Holmes reveals that Miss Stapleton is Stapleton's wife	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2946", "sentence_2947", "sentence_2951", "sentence_2953"], "communicatesContent": "The lady who has passed here as Miss Stapleton is in reality his wife."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_2	event	\N	Watson asks why the deception was used	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2954", "sentence_2955"], "communicatesContent": "But why this elaborate deception?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_3	event	\N	Holmes explains why Stapleton kept his wife disguised as a free woman	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2955"], "communicatesContent": "Because he foresaw that she would be very much more useful to him in the character of a free woman."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_4	event	\N	Watson identifies the naturalist as the enemy	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2956", "sentence_2957", "sentence_2958", "sentence_2959", "sentence_2960", "sentence_2961", "sentence_2962"], "communicatesContent": "It is he, then, who is our enemy—it is he who dogged us in London?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_5	event	\N	Watson asks how Holmes knows the woman is Stapleton's wife	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2963", "sentence_2964"], "communicatesContent": "But are you sure of this, Holmes? How do you know that the woman is his wife?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_6	event	\N	Holmes explains the identification of Stapleton as a former schoolmaster	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2965", "sentence_2966", "sentence_2967", "sentence_2968", "sentence_2969", "sentence_2970", "sentence_2971"], "communicatesContent": "Because he so far forgot himself as to tell you a true piece of autobiography upon the occasion when he first met you. He was once a schoolmaster in the north of England. A school had come to grief under atrocious circumstances, and the man who had owned it had disappeared with his wife. The descriptions agreed. The missing man was devoted to entomology."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_7	event	\N	Watson asks where Mrs. Laura Lyons fits in	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2973", "sentence_2974"], "communicatesContent": "If this woman is in truth his wife, where does Mrs. Laura Lyons come in?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_8	event	\N	Holmes explains Mrs. Laura Lyons's projected divorce plan	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2975", "sentence_2976", "sentence_2977", "sentence_2978"], "communicatesContent": "That is one of the points upon which your own researches have shed a light. Your interview with the lady has cleared the situation very much. I did not know about a projected divorce between herself and her husband. In that case, regarding Stapleton as an unmarried man, she counted no doubt upon becoming his wife."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_9	event	\N	Watson asks what happens when Mrs. Laura Lyons learns the truth	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2979"], "communicatesContent": "And when she is undeceived?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_10	event	\N	Holmes orders Watson to see Mrs. Laura Lyons with him tomorrow	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2980", "sentence_2981"], "communicatesContent": "Why, then we may find the lady of service. It must be our first duty to see her—both of us—tomorrow."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_11	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson to return to Baskerville Hall	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2982", "sentence_2983"], "communicatesContent": "Don’t you think, Watson, that you are away from your charge rather long? Your place should be at Baskerville Hall."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_12	event	\N	Watson asks Holmes what the plan means and what Stapleton is after	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2986", "sentence_2987", "sentence_2988", "sentence_2989"], "communicatesContent": "Surely there is no need of secrecy between you and me. What is the meaning of it all? What is he after?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_76_13	event	\N	Holmes says the plot is murder and warns that Stapleton may strike early	75	{"section": "section_76", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2990", "sentence_2991", "sentence_2992", "sentence_2993", "sentence_2994", "sentence_2995", "sentence_2996", "sentence_2997", "sentence_2998"], "communicatesContent": "It is murder, Watson—refined, cold-blooded, deliberate murder. Do not ask me for particulars. My nets are closing upon him, even as his are upon Sir Henry, and with your help he is already almost at my mercy. There is but one danger which can threaten us. It is that he should strike before we are ready to do so. Another day—two at the most—and I have my case complete, but until then guard your charge as closely as ever a fond mother watched her ailing child. Your mission today has justified itself, and yet I could almost wish that you had not left his side."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_1	event	\N	A terrible scream bursts out of the silence of the moor	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_2999"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_2	event	\N	Holmes rises and looks out from the hut door	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3005"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_3	event	\N	Holmes whispers for silence	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3006", "sentence_3007", "sentence_3008"], "communicatesContent": "Hush!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_4	event	\N	The cry sounds nearer and louder	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3009", "sentence_3010"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_5	event	\N	Holmes asks where the sound is	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3011", "sentence_3012", "sentence_3013"], "communicatesContent": "Where is it, Watson?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_6	event	\N	Watson points into the darkness	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3014", "sentence_3015"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_7	event	\N	The cry comes again and a rumbling sound joins it	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3016", "sentence_3017", "sentence_3018"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_8	event	\N	Holmes identifies the sound as the hound and urges Watson to run	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3019", "sentence_3020", "sentence_3021", "sentence_3022"], "communicatesContent": "The hound! Come, Watson, come! Great heavens, if we are too late!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_9	event	\N	Holmes and Watson run over the moor	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3023"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_10	event	\N	A last yell and a heavy thud come from the broken ground ahead	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3024"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_11	event	\N	Holmes and Watson stop and listen	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3025", "sentence_3026"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_12	event	\N	Holmes shows distress and stamps his feet	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3027", "sentence_3028"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_13	event	\N	Holmes says they are too late and blames himself and Watson	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3029", "sentence_3030", "sentence_3031", "sentence_3032", "sentence_3033", "sentence_3034"], "communicatesContent": "He has beaten us, Watson. We are too late. Fool that I was to hold my hand. And you, Watson, see what comes of abandoning your charge! But, by Heaven, if the worst has happened we’ll avenge him!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_14	event	\N	Holmes and Watson run blindly across the moor toward the sounds	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3035", "sentence_3036"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_15	event	\N	Holmes asks whether anything can be seen	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3037", "sentence_3038", "sentence_3039"], "communicatesContent": "Can you see anything?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_16	event	\N	A low moan is heard from the left	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3040", "sentence_3041"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_17	event	\N	Holmes and Watson reach a cliff with a figure on its face	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3042", "sentence_3043", "sentence_3044", "sentence_3045"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_18	event	\N	Holmes and Watson stoop over a dead man	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3046", "sentence_3047", "sentence_3048"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_77_19	event	\N	Holmes strikes a match and reveals the victim and Sir Henry Baskerville	76	{"section": "section_77", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3049", "sentence_3050", "sentence_3051", "sentence_3052", "sentence_3053"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_78_1	event	\N	Watson cries out against the brute and blames himself for leaving Sir Henry to his fate	77	{"section": "section_78", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3054", "sentence_3055", "sentence_3056", "sentence_3057"], "communicatesContent": "Oh Holmes, I shall never forgive myself for having left him to his fate."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_78_2	event	\N	Holmes admits more blame and says he ruined the case by sacrificing his client	77	{"section": "section_78", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3058", "sentence_3059", "sentence_3060", "sentence_3061", "sentence_3062"], "communicatesContent": "I am more to blame than you, Watson. In order to have my case well rounded and complete, I have thrown away the life of my client. It is the greatest blow which has befallen me in my career. But how could I know—how could I know—that he would risk his life alone upon the moor in the face of all my warnings? —and yet have been unable to save him!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_11	event	\N	Stapleton approaches over the moor with a cigar	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3128", "sentence_3129", "sentence_3131", "sentence_3132", "sentence_3133"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	sentence_126	lexical	\N	\N	125	{"text": "“Perhaps there are points which have escaped your Machiavellian intellect.", "section": "section_6", "entities": []}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_78_3	event	\N	Holmes asks where the hound and Stapleton are and vows that Stapleton will answer for the deed	77	{"section": "section_78", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3063", "sentence_3064", "sentence_3065", "sentence_3066", "sentence_3067", "sentence_3068", "sentence_3069"], "communicatesContent": "Where is this brute of a hound which drove him to his death? It may be lurking among these rocks at this instant. And Stapleton, where is he? He shall answer for this deed."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_78_4	event	\N	Holmes states that the uncle and nephew were murdered and that the connection between the man and the beast must be proved	77	{"section": "section_78", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3070", "sentence_3071", "sentence_3072", "sentence_3073"], "communicatesContent": "Uncle and nephew have been murdered—the one frightened to death by the very sight of a beast which he thought to be supernatural, the other driven to his end in his wild flight to escape from it. But now we have to prove the connection between the man and the beast. Save from what we heard, we cannot even swear to the existence of the latter, since Sir Henry has evidently died from the fall. But, by heavens, cunning as he is, the fellow shall be in my power before another day is past!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_78_5	event	\N	Watson and Holmes stand by the body and grieve over the disaster	77	{"section": "section_78", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3074", "sentence_3075", "sentence_3076", "sentence_3077", "sentence_3078"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_78_6	event	\N	Watson suggests seizing Stapleton at once and Holmes rejects it as premature	77	{"section": "section_78", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3079", "sentence_3080", "sentence_3081", "sentence_3082", "sentence_3083", "sentence_3084", "sentence_3085", "sentence_3086"], "communicatesContent": "Why should we not seize him at once?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_78_7	event	\N	Watson and Holmes go down the slope to the body to perform the last offices	77	{"section": "section_78", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3087", "sentence_3088", "sentence_3089", "sentence_3090", "sentence_3091"], "communicatesContent": "We must send for help, Holmes! We cannot carry him all the way to the Hall."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_78_8	event	\N	Holmes inspects the body, cries out, laughs, and identifies a beard	77	{"section": "section_78", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3092", "sentence_3093", "sentence_3094", "sentence_3095", "sentence_3096", "sentence_3097", "sentence_3098", "sentence_3099", "sentence_3100", "sentence_3101", "sentence_3102", "sentence_3103"], "communicatesContent": "A beard! A beard! The man has a beard! A beard? It is not the baronet—it is—why, it is my neighbour, the convict!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_78_9	event	\N	Watson recognizes the body as Selden and realizes the clothing is Sir Henry's	77	{"section": "section_78", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3104", "sentence_3105", "sentence_3106", "sentence_3107", "sentence_3108", "sentence_3109"], "communicatesContent": "I told Holmes how the matter stood, my heart bubbling over with thankfulness and joy."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_1	event	\N	A speaker states that the clothes have been the poor devil's death	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3110"], "communicatesContent": "Then the clothes have been the poor devil’s death."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_2	event	\N	A speaker states that the hound was laid on from Sir Henry's boot and ran Selden down	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3111"], "communicatesContent": "It is clear enough that the hound has been laid on from some article of Sir Henry’s—the boot which was abstracted in the hotel, in all probability—and so ran this man down."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_3	event	\N	A speaker asks how Selden knew the hound was on his trail in the darkness	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3112"], "communicatesContent": "How came Selden, in the darkness, to know that the hound was on his trail?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_4	event	\N	A speaker replies that Selden heard the hound	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3113"], "communicatesContent": "He heard him."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_5	event	\N	A speaker argues that hearing a hound would not usually terrify Selden into screaming for help	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3114", "sentence_3115", "sentence_3116"], "communicatesContent": "To hear a hound upon the moor would not work a hard man like this convict into such a paroxysm of terror that he would risk recapture by screaming wildly for help. By his cries he must have run a long way after he knew the animal was on his track. How did he know?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_6	event	\N	A speaker says the hound should be loose tonight and suggests Stapleton let it go for Sir Henry's presence	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3117", "sentence_3119", "sentence_3120", "sentence_3121"], "communicatesContent": "A greater mystery to me is why this hound, presuming that all our conjectures are correct—Well, then, why this hound should be loose tonight. I suppose that it does not always run loose upon the moor. Stapleton would not let it go unless he had reason to think that Sir Henry would be there."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_7	event	\N	A speaker says that they will likely soon get an explanation of one mystery while another may remain a mystery	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3122"], "communicatesContent": "My difficulty is the more formidable of the two, for I think that we shall very shortly get an explanation of yours, while mine may remain forever a mystery."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_8	event	\N	A speaker asks what to do with Selden's body	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3123", "sentence_3124"], "communicatesContent": "The question now is, what shall we do with this poor wretch’s body? We cannot leave it here to the foxes and the ravens."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_9	event	\N	A speaker suggests putting the body in a hut until the police can be contacted	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3125"], "communicatesContent": "I suggest that we put it in one of the huts until we can communicate with the police."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_10	event	\N	A speaker agrees and says they could carry the body that far	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3126", "sentence_3127"], "communicatesContent": "Exactly. I have no doubt that you and I could carry it so far."}
valley-of-fear	sentence_331	lexical	\N	\N	330	{"text": "“That’s so.”", "section": "section_9", "entities": []}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_12	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson not to reveal suspicions	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3130"], "communicatesContent": "Not a word to show your suspicions—not a word, or my plans crumble to the ground."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_13	event	\N	Stapleton greets Watson and asks about the body	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3134", "sentence_3135", "sentence_3136", "sentence_3137", "sentence_3138"], "communicatesContent": "Why, Dr. Watson, that’s not you, is it? You are the last man that I should have expected to see out on the moor at this time of night. But, dear me, what’s this? Somebody hurt? Not—don’t tell me that it is our friend Sir Henry!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_14	event	\N	Stapleton stoops over the dead man and drops his cigar	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3139", "sentence_3140"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_15	event	\N	Stapleton asks who the dead man is	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3141", "sentence_3142"], "communicatesContent": "Who—who’s this?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_16	event	\N	Holmes identifies the dead man as Selden, the escaped convict	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3143"], "communicatesContent": "It is Selden, the man who escaped from Princetown."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_17	event	\N	Stapleton reacts and asks how Selden died	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3144", "sentence_3145", "sentence_3146", "sentence_3147", "sentence_3148"], "communicatesContent": "Dear me! What a very shocking affair! How did he die?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_18	event	\N	Holmes says Selden appears to have broken his neck by falling over the rocks and explains hearing a cry while strolling on the moor	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3149", "sentence_3150"], "communicatesContent": "He appears to have broken his neck by falling over these rocks. My friend and I were strolling on the moor when we heard a cry."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_19	event	\N	Stapleton says he also heard a cry and came out because he was uneasy about Sir Henry	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3151", "sentence_3152", "sentence_3153"], "communicatesContent": "I heard a cry also. That was what brought me out. I was uneasy about Sir Henry."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_20	event	\N	Stapleton explains that he had suggested Sir Henry come over and that he became alarmed when he heard cries upon the moor	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3154", "sentence_3155", "sentence_3156", "sentence_3157"], "communicatesContent": "Because I had suggested that he should come over. When he did not come I was surprised, and I naturally became alarmed for his safety when I heard cries upon the moor."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_79_21	event	\N	Stapleton asks whether anything else was heard besides a cry and says he wondered about evidence of a phantom hound	78	{"section": "section_79", "speakerId": "stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3158", "sentence_3159", "sentence_3160", "sentence_3161", "sentence_3162", "sentence_3163", "sentence_3164"], "communicatesContent": "By the way—did you hear anything else besides a cry? No. What do you mean, then? Oh, you know the stories that the peasants tell about a phantom hound, and so on. It is said to be heard at night upon the moor. I was wondering if there were any evidence of such a sound tonight."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_1	event	\N	I says nothing of the kind	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3165"], "communicatesContent": "We heard nothing of the kind."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_2	event	\N	A speaker asks Holmes for a theory of the man's death	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "mr_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3166"], "communicatesContent": "And what is your theory of this poor fellow's death?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_3	event	\N	A speaker explains a theory of the death	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3167", "sentence_3168"], "communicatesContent": "I have no doubt that anxiety and exposure have driven him off his head. He has rushed about the moor in a crazy state and eventually fallen over here and broken his neck."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_4	event	\N	Stapleton agrees that the theory seems reasonable	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "mr_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3169"], "communicatesContent": "That seems the most reasonable theory."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_5	event	\N	Holmes is asked for his view	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "mr_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3170"], "communicatesContent": "What do you think about it, Mr. Sherlock Holmes?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_6	event	\N	Holmes bows his compliments	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3171"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_7	event	\N	Holmes identifies the speaker quickly	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3172"], "communicatesContent": "You are quick at identification."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_8	event	\N	Holmes says he and Watson had been expected in the area	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3173", "sentence_3174"], "communicatesContent": "We have been expecting you in these parts since Dr. Watson came down. You are in time to see a tragedy."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_9	event	\N	Holmes says he will return to London tomorrow	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3175", "sentence_3176", "sentence_3177"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, indeed. I have no doubt that my friend's explanation will cover the facts. I will take an unpleasant remembrance back to London with me tomorrow."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_10	event	\N	Stapleton asks whether Holmes returns tomorrow	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "mr_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3178", "sentence_3179"], "communicatesContent": "Oh, you return tomorrow?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_11	event	\N	Holmes says that returning tomorrow is his intention	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3179"], "communicatesContent": "That is my intention."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_91_11	event	\N	Sir Henry says Holmes has saved his life	90	{"section": "section_91", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3650", "sentence_3651"], "communicatesContent": "You have saved my life."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_12	event	\N	Stapleton asks whether the visit has shed light on the occurrences	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "mr_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3180"], "communicatesContent": "I hope your visit has cast some light upon those occurrences which have puzzled us?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_13	event	\N	Holmes answers that he cannot always succeed and that the case has not been satisfactory	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3181", "sentence_3182", "sentence_3183", "sentence_3184"], "communicatesContent": "One cannot always have the success for which one hopes. An investigator needs facts and not legends or rumours. It has not been a satisfactory case."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_14	event	\N	Stapleton turns to Watson	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3186", "sentence_3187"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_15	event	\N	Stapleton proposes carrying the body to his house and covering the face until morning	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "mr_stapleton", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3188", "sentence_3189", "sentence_3190"], "communicatesContent": "I would suggest carrying this poor fellow to my house, but it would give my sister such a fright that I do not feel justified in doing it. I think that if we put something over his face he will be safe until morning."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_16	event	\N	Holmes and Watson set off for Baskerville Hall	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3191"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_17	event	\N	Holmes and Watson see Stapleton return alone over the moor	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3191", "sentence_3192"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_18	event	\N	Holmes says they are at close grips at last	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3193"], "communicatesContent": "We're at close grips at last."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_19	event	\N	Holmes says the fellow showed nerve and the wrong man fell victim to his plot	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3194", "sentence_3195", "sentence_3196"], "communicatesContent": "What a nerve the fellow has! How he pulled himself together in the face of what must have been a paralyzing shock when he found that the wrong man had fallen a victim to his plot. I told you in London, Watson, and I tell you now again, that we have never had a foeman more worthy of our steel."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_20	event	\N	Watson says he is sorry that Holmes has been seen	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3197"], "communicatesContent": "I am sorry that he has seen you."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_21	event	\N	Holmes says he was sorry at first but could not avoid being seen	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3198", "sentence_3199"], "communicatesContent": "And so was I at first. But there was no getting out of it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_22	event	\N	Watson asks what effect Holmes's presence will have on the man's plans	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3200"], "communicatesContent": "What effect do you think it will have upon his plans now that he knows you are here?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_23	event	\N	Holmes says the man's response may be caution or desperate action	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3201", "sentence_3202"], "communicatesContent": "It may cause him to be more cautious, or it may drive him to desperate measures at once. Like most clever criminals, he may be too confident in his own cleverness and imagine that he has completely deceived us."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_24	event	\N	Watson asks why they should not arrest the man at once	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3203"], "communicatesContent": "Why should we not arrest him at once?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_25	event	\N	Holmes says Watson is a man of action and that arresting the man tonight would not help	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3204", "sentence_3205", "sentence_3206", "sentence_3207", "sentence_3208", "sentence_3209"], "communicatesContent": "My dear Watson, you were born to be a man of action. Your instinct is always to do something energetic. But supposing, for argument's sake, that we had him arrested tonight, what on earth the better off should we be for that? We could prove nothing against him. There’s the devilish cunning of it! If he were acting through a human agent we could get some evidence, but if we were to drag this great dog to the light of day it would not help us in putting a rope round the neck of its master."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_26	event	\N	Watson says they have a case	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3210"], "communicatesContent": "Surely we have a case."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_80_27	event	\N	Holmes says they have only surmise and conjecture and would be laughed out of court	79	{"section": "section_80", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3211", "sentence_3212"], "communicatesContent": "Not a shadow of one—only surmise and conjecture. We should be laughed out of court if we came with such a story and such evidence."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_81_1	event	\N	Holmes and Watson discuss Sir Charles's death and the lack of proof	80	{"section": "section_81", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3213", "sentence_3219"], "communicatesContent": "There is Sir Charles’s death. Found dead without a mark upon him. But we have to prove all this, and we are not in a position to do it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_81_2	event	\N	Holmes says they cannot prove the hound caused Sir Charles's death	80	{"section": "section_81", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3214", "sentence_3218"], "communicatesContent": "Found dead without a mark upon him. You and I know that he died of sheer fright, and we know also what frightened him, but how are we to get twelve stolid jurymen to know it? Of course we know that a hound does not bite a dead body and that Sir Charles was dead before ever the brute overtook him."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_9	event	\N	Holmes says Watson has informed him of what Mrs. Laura Lyons communicated and withheld about Sir Charles's death	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3420", "sentence_3421"], "communicatesContent": "My friend here, Dr. Watson, has informed me of what you have communicated, and also of what you have withheld in connection with that matter."}
valley-of-fear	sentence_332	lexical	\N	\N	331	{"text": "“A fine room, is it not?”", "section": "section_9", "entities": []}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_81_3	event	\N	Holmes says there is no direct proof connecting the hound to the death	80	{"section": "section_81", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3221", "sentence_3226"], "communicatesContent": "We are not much better off tonight. Again, there was no direct connection between the hound and the man’s death. We never saw the hound. We heard it, but we could not prove that it was running upon this man’s trail. There is a complete absence of motive. No, my dear fellow; we must reconcile ourselves to the fact that we have no case at present, and that it is worth our while to run any risk in order to establish one."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_81_4	event	\N	Holmes says Mrs. Laura Lyons may help them and that he has another plan	80	{"section": "section_81", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3227", "sentence_3230"], "communicatesContent": "I have great hopes of what Mrs. Laura Lyons may do for us when the position of affairs is made clear to her. And I have my own plan as well. Sufficient for tomorrow is the evil thereof; but I hope before the day is past to have the upper hand at last."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_81_5	event	\N	Holmes and Watson walk to the Baskerville gates	80	{"section": "section_81", "speakerId": "narrator", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3231"], "communicatesContent": "I could draw nothing further from him, and he walked, lost in thought, as far as the Baskerville gates."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_81_6	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson not to mention the hound to Sir Henry	80	{"section": "section_81", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3233", "sentence_3237"], "communicatesContent": "Yes; I see no reason for further concealment. But one last word, Watson. Say nothing of the hound to Sir Henry. Let him think that Selden’s death was as Stapleton would have us believe. He will have a better nerve for the ordeal which he will have to undergo tomorrow, when he is engaged, if I remember your report aright, to dine with these people."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_81_7	event	\N	Watson says he is also engaged to dine with those people	80	{"section": "section_81", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3238"], "communicatesContent": "And so am I."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_81_8	event	\N	Holmes says Watson must excuse himself and Sir Henry must go alone to dinner	80	{"section": "section_81", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3239", "sentence_3241"], "communicatesContent": "Then you must excuse yourself and he must go alone. That will be easily arranged. And now, if we are too late for dinner, I think that we are both ready for our suppers."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_1	event	\N	Sir Henry sees Sherlock Holmes arrive	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3244"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_2	event	\N	Sir Henry notices Holmes has no luggage and no explanation for it	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3245"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_3	event	\N	Watson and Holmes explain their experience to Sir Henry over a belated supper	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3246"], "communicatesContent": "as much of our experience as it seemed desirable that he should know"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_4	event	\N	Watson breaks the news to Barrymore and his wife	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "dr_watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3247"], "communicatesContent": "the news"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_5	event	\N	Mrs. Barrymore weeps	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3248"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_6	event	\N	Sir Henry says he has been moping in the house since Watson left	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3251"], "communicatesContent": "I’ve been moping in the house all day since Watson went off in the morning"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_7	event	\N	Sir Henry says he kept his promise	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3252"], "communicatesContent": "I guess I should have some credit, for I have kept my promise"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_8	event	\N	Sir Henry says Stapleton sent him a message asking him to come over	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3253"], "communicatesContent": "If I hadn’t sworn not to go about alone I might have had a more lively evening, for I had a message from Stapleton asking me over there"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_9	event	\N	Holmes says they had been mourning Sir Henry as having broken his neck	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3254", "sentence_3255"], "communicatesContent": "I don’t suppose you appreciate that we have been mourning over you as having broken your neck?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_10	event	\N	Holmes identifies the dead man as dressed in Sir Henry's clothes	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3258", "sentence_3259"], "communicatesContent": "This poor wretch was dressed in your clothes. I fear your servant who gave them to him may get into trouble with the police."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_11	event	\N	Holmes says the household is on the wrong side of the law and Watson's reports are incriminating	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3262", "sentence_3264"], "communicatesContent": "That’s lucky for him—in fact, it’s lucky for all of you, since you are all on the wrong side of the law in this matter. I am not sure that as a conscientious detective my first duty is not to arrest the whole household. Watson’s reports are most incriminating documents."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_12	event	\N	Sir Henry asks Holmes about the case	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3265", "sentence_3267", "sentence_3268"], "communicatesContent": "But how about the case? Have you made anything out of the tangle? I don’t know that Watson and I are much the wiser since we came down."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_13	event	\N	Holmes says he will soon make the situation clearer	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3269", "sentence_3271"], "communicatesContent": "I think that I shall be in a position to make the situation rather more clear to you before long. It has been an exceedingly difficult and most complicated business. There are several points upon which we still want light—but it is coming all the same."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_10	event	\N	Mrs. Laura Lyons asks what she has withheld	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "mrs_laura_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3422", "sentence_3423"], "communicatesContent": "What have I withheld?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_14	event	\N	Sir Henry says they heard the hound on the moor	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3272", "sentence_3273", "sentence_3274", "sentence_3275"], "communicatesContent": "We’ve had one experience, as Watson has no doubt told you. We heard the hound on the moor, so I can swear that it is not all empty superstition. I had something to do with dogs when I was out West, and I know one when I hear one. If you can muzzle that one and put him on a chain I’ll be ready to swear you are the greatest detective of all time."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_15	event	\N	Holmes says he will muzzle and chain the hound if Sir Henry helps	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3276"], "communicatesContent": "I think I will muzzle him and chain him all right if you will give me your help."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_16	event	\N	Sir Henry agrees to do whatever Holmes tells him	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3277", "sentence_3279"], "communicatesContent": "Whatever you tell me to do I will do. Just as you like."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_17	event	\N	Holmes asks Sir Henry to obey blindly without asking the reason	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3278"], "communicatesContent": "Very good; and I will ask you also to do it blindly, without always asking the reason."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_82_18	event	\N	Holmes abruptly looks and stares upward	81	{"section": "section_82", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3282", "sentence_3283"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_83_1	event	\N	Holmes admires the portraits on the opposite wall	82	{"section": "section_83", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3288", "sentence_3290"], "communicatesContent": "Excuse the admiration of a connoisseur."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_83_2	event	\N	Holmes identifies the family portraits by name and ancestry	82	{"section": "section_83", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3294", "sentence_3306"], "communicatesContent": "That’s a Kneller, I’ll swear, that lady in the blue silk over yonder, and the stout gentleman with the wig ought to be a Reynolds. They are all family portraits, I presume? That is Rear-Admiral Baskerville ... The man with the blue coat and the roll of paper is Sir William Baskerville ... That is the cause of all the mischief, the wicked Hugo, who started the Hound of the Baskervilles."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_83_3	event	\N	Barrymore coaches Sir Henry in the names of the portraits	82	{"section": "section_83", "speakerId": "barrymore", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3299"], "communicatesContent": "Barrymore has been coaching me in them."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_83_4	event	\N	Holmes examines the portrait during supper	82	{"section": "section_83", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3312"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_83_5	event	\N	Holmes returns to the banqueting-hall with a candle and inspects the portrait	82	{"section": "section_83", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3314", "sentence_3322"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_83_6	event	\N	Holmes reveals Stapleton's face in the portrait	82	{"section": "section_83", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3322", "sentence_3326", "sentence_3330"], "communicatesContent": "Do you see anything there? Is it like anyone you know? Just a suggestion, perhaps. But wait an instant! ... My eyes have been trained to examine faces and not their trimmings. It is the first quality of a criminal investigator that he should see through a disguise."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_1	event	\N	Holmes identifies the Baskerville man as having designs on the succession and says he will be trapped soon	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3331", "sentence_3332", "sentence_3333", "sentence_3334", "sentence_3335", "sentence_3336", "sentence_3337", "sentence_3338", "sentence_3339"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, it is an interesting instance of a throwback, which appears to be both physical and spiritual. A study of family portraits is enough to convert a man to the doctrine of reincarnation. The fellow is a Baskerville—that is evident. With designs upon the succession. Exactly. This chance of the picture has supplied us with one of our most obvious missing links. We have him, Watson, we have him, and I dare swear that before tomorrow night he will be fluttering in our net as helpless as one of his own butterflies. A pin, a cork, and a card, and we add him to the Baker Street collection!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_2	event	\N	Holmes laughs and turns away from the portrait	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3339", "sentence_3340"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_3	event	\N	Holmes arrives earlier in the morning and speaks with Watson	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3341", "sentence_3342", "sentence_3343", "sentence_3344"], "communicatesContent": "Yes, we should have a full day today. The nets are all in place, and the drag is about to begin. We’ll know before the day is out whether we have caught our big, lean-jawed pike, or whether he has got through the meshes."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_4	event	\N	Holmes reports Selden's death to Princetown and reassures Cartwright	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3345", "sentence_3346", "sentence_3347", "sentence_3348"], "communicatesContent": "I have sent a report from Grimpen to Princetown as to the death of Selden. I think I can promise that none of you will be troubled in the matter. And I have also communicated with my faithful Cartwright, who would certainly have pined away at the door of my hut, as a dog does at his master’s grave, if I had not set his mind at rest about my safety."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_5	event	\N	Holmes says the next move is to see Sir Henry	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3349", "sentence_3350", "sentence_3351"], "communicatesContent": "To see Sir Henry. Ah, here he is!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_11	event	\N	Holmes states that Mrs. Laura Lyons asked Sir Charles to be at the gate at ten o'clock and that this was the place and hour of his death	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3424", "sentence_3425", "sentence_3426"], "communicatesContent": "You have confessed that you asked Sir Charles to be at the gate at ten o'clock. We know that that was the place and hour of his death. You have withheld what the connection is between these events."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_92_16	event	\N	Sir Henry and Dr. Mortimer travel together round the world	91	{"section": "section_92", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3738"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_6	event	\N	Sir Henry greets Holmes and discusses the day's plan	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3352", "sentence_3353", "sentence_3354", "sentence_3355", "sentence_3356", "sentence_3357", "sentence_3358", "sentence_3359", "sentence_3360", "sentence_3361", "sentence_3362", "sentence_3363"], "communicatesContent": "Good-morning, Holmes. You look like a general who is planning a battle with his chief of the staff. And so do I. I hoped that you were going to see me through this business. The Hall and the moor are not very pleasant places when one is alone. I hope that you will come also. They are very hospitable people, and I am sure that they would be very glad to see you. I fear that Watson and I must go to London. To London? Yes, I think that we should be more useful there at the present juncture."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_7	event	\N	Sir Henry feels hurt by the planned departure	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3364", "sentence_3365", "sentence_3366", "sentence_3373"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_8	event	\N	Holmes instructs Sir Henry to trust him, relay an excuse to the Stapletons, and remember the message	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3367", "sentence_3368", "sentence_3369", "sentence_3370", "sentence_3371", "sentence_3372", "sentence_3374", "sentence_3375"], "communicatesContent": "My dear fellow, you must trust me implicitly and do exactly what I tell you. You can tell your friends that we should have been happy to have come with you, but that urgent business required us to be in town. We hope very soon to return to Devonshire. Will you remember to give them that message? If you insist upon it. There is no alternative, I assure you."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_9	event	\N	Holmes and Watson plan to leave immediately after breakfast and travel to Coombe Tracey, while Watson leaves his things as a pledge	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3376", "sentence_3377", "sentence_3378", "sentence_3379", "sentence_3380"], "communicatesContent": "Immediately after breakfast. We will drive in to Coombe Tracey, but Watson will leave his things as a pledge that he will come back to you. Watson, you will send a note to Stapleton to tell him that you regret that you cannot come."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_10	event	\N	Sir Henry says he wants to go to London and not stay alone at the Hall	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": "sir_henry_baskerville", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3379", "sentence_3380"], "communicatesContent": "I have a good mind to go to London with you. Why should I stay here alone?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_11	event	\N	Holmes sends a report from Grimpen to Princetown about Selden's death	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3346"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_84_12	event	\N	Holmes communicates with Cartwright about his safety	83	{"section": "section_84", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3348"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_1	event	\N	Holmes orders Watson to stay and gives further travel instructions	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3381", "sentence_3382", "sentence_3383", "sentence_3384", "sentence_3385", "sentence_3386", "sentence_3387", "sentence_3388", "sentence_3389", "sentence_3390", "sentence_3391", "sentence_3392", "sentence_3393", "sentence_3394", "sentence_3395", "sentence_3396"], "communicatesContent": "Because it is your post of duty. Because you gave me your word that you would do as you were told, and I tell you to stay. One more direction! I wish you to drive to Merripit House. Send back your trap, however, and let them know that you intend to walk home. Yes. This time you may do it with safety. If I had not every confidence in your nerve and courage I would not suggest it, but it is essential that you should do it. And as you value your life do not go across the moor in any direction save along the straight path which leads from Merripit House to the Grimpen Road, and is your natural way home. Very good. I should be glad to get away as soon after breakfast as possible, so as to reach London in the afternoon."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_2	event	\N	Holmes, Watson, and their companion travel to Coombe Tracey and send the trap back	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3397", "sentence_3398", "sentence_3399"], "communicatesContent": "There was nothing for it, however, but implicit obedience; so we bade good-bye to our rueful friend, and a couple of hours afterwards we were at the station of Coombe Tracey and had dispatched the trap upon its return journey."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_3	event	\N	Holmes instructs Cartwright to wire Sir Henry and check for a station message	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3400", "sentence_3401", "sentence_3402", "sentence_3403", "sentence_3404", "sentence_3405"], "communicatesContent": "You will take this train to town, Cartwright. The moment you arrive you will send a wire to Sir Henry Baskerville, in my name, to say that if he finds the pocketbook which I have dropped he is to send it by registered post to Baker Street. And ask at the station office if there is a message for me."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_4	event	\N	Cartwright returns with a telegram and Holmes reads it to Watson	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "the_telegraph_answer", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3406", "sentence_3407", "sentence_3408", "sentence_3409", "sentence_3410", "sentence_3411"], "communicatesContent": "Wire received. Coming down with unsigned warrant. Arrive five-forty. Lestrade."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_5	event	\N	Holmes states that Lestrade is the best professional and that they may need his help	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3412", "sentence_3413"], "communicatesContent": "That is in answer to mine of this morning. He is the best of the professionals, I think, and we may need his assistance."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_6	event	\N	Holmes proposes calling on Mrs. Laura Lyons	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3414"], "communicatesContent": "Now, Watson, I think that we cannot employ our time better than by calling upon your acquaintance, Mrs. Laura Lyons."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_7	event	\N	Holmes explains his campaign and the use of Sir Henry and the telegram	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3415", "sentence_3416", "sentence_3417", "sentence_3418"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_85_8	event	\N	Holmes interviews Mrs. Laura Lyons in her office	84	{"section": "section_85", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3419"], "communicatesContent": "I am investigating the circumstances which attended the death of the late Sir Charles Baskerville."}
valley-of-fear	section_30	section	\N	Section 30	29	{"wordCount": 658}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_1	event	\N	Holmes denies that there is any connection	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3427", "sentence_3428", "sentence_3429"], "communicatesContent": "There is no connection. In that case the coincidence must indeed be an extraordinary one. But I think that we shall succeed in establishing a connection, after all."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_2	event	\N	Holmes states that the case is treated as murder and that evidence may implicate Mr. Stapleton and his wife	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3430", "sentence_3431"], "communicatesContent": "I wish to be perfectly frank with you, Mrs. Lyons. We regard this case as one of murder, and the evidence may implicate not only your friend Mr. Stapleton but his wife as well."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_3	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons reacts by springing from her chair and crying out	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3432", "sentence_3433", "sentence_3434"], "communicatesContent": "His wife!"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_4	event	\N	Holmes states that the supposed sister is really the man's wife	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3435", "sentence_3436"], "communicatesContent": "The fact is no longer a secret. The person who has passed for his sister is really his wife."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_5	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons resumes her seat and grips the chair arms	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3437", "sentence_3438"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_6	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons demands proof that Mr. Stapleton is married	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3439", "sentence_3440", "sentence_3441", "sentence_3442"], "communicatesContent": "His wife! His wife! He is not a married man."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_7	event	\N	Holmes produces papers as proof	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3443", "sentence_3444", "sentence_3445", "sentence_3446", "sentence_3447", "sentence_3448", "sentence_3449", "sentence_3450", "sentence_3451", "sentence_3452"], "communicatesContent": "I have come prepared to do so. Here is a photograph of the couple taken in York four years ago. It is indorsed ‘Mr. and Mrs. Vandeleur,’ but you will have no difficulty in recognizing him, and her also, if you know her by sight. Here are three written descriptions by trustworthy witnesses of Mr. and Mrs. Vandeleur, who at that time kept St. Oliver’s private school. Read them and see if you can doubt the identity of these people."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_8	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons accuses the man of lying and of using her as a tool	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3453", "sentence_3454", "sentence_3455", "sentence_3456", "sentence_3457", "sentence_3458", "sentence_3459", "sentence_3460", "sentence_3461", "sentence_3462", "sentence_3463"], "communicatesContent": "Mr. Holmes, this man had offered me marriage on condition that I could get a divorce from my husband. He has lied to me, the villain, in every conceivable way. Not one word of truth has he ever told me. And why—why? I imagined that all was for my own sake. But now I see that I was never anything but a tool in his hands. Why should I preserve faith with him who never kept any with me? Why should I try to shield him from the consequences of his own wicked acts? Ask me what you like, and there is nothing which I shall hold back. One thing I swear to you, and that is that when I wrote the letter I never dreamed of any harm to the old gentleman, who had been my kindest friend."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_9	event	\N	Holmes says he believes Mrs. Lyons and asks about the letter and the appointment	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3464", "sentence_3465", "sentence_3466"], "communicatesContent": "I entirely believe you, madam. The recital of these events must be very painful to you, and perhaps it will make it easier if I tell you what occurred, and you can check me if I make any material mistake. The sending of this letter was suggested to you by Stapleton?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_10	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons says Stapleton dictated the letter	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3467"], "communicatesContent": "He dictated it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_11	event	\N	Holmes asks about the reason given for the letter	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3468"], "communicatesContent": "I presume that the reason he gave was that you would receive help from Sir Charles for the legal expenses connected with your divorce?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_12	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons confirms the reason for the letter	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3469"], "communicatesContent": "Exactly."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_13	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Stapleton dissuaded her from keeping the appointment	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3470"], "communicatesContent": "And then after you had sent the letter he dissuaded you from keeping the appointment?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_14	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons reports Stapleton's explanation and promise	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3471"], "communicatesContent": "He told me that it would hurt his self-respect that any other man should find the money for such an object, and that though he was a poor man himself he would devote his last penny to removing the obstacles which divided us."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_15	event	\N	Holmes asks whether she heard nothing until the newspaper report of the death	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3472", "sentence_3473"], "communicatesContent": "He appears to be a very consistent character. And then you heard nothing until you read the reports of the death in the paper?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_16	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons says she heard nothing	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3474"], "communicatesContent": "No."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_17	event	\N	Holmes asks whether Stapleton made her swear to keep silent	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3475"], "communicatesContent": "And he made you swear to say nothing about your appointment with Sir Charles?"}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_86_18	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons says Stapleton did and frightened her into silence	85	{"section": "section_86", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3476", "sentence_3477", "sentence_3478"], "communicatesContent": "He did. He said that the death was a very mysterious one, and that I should certainly be suspected if the facts came out. He frightened me into remaining silent."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_87_3	event	\N	Mrs. Lyons says she would have kept faith with him if he had kept faith with her	86	{"section": "section_87", "speakerId": "mrs_lyons", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3483"], "communicatesContent": "But if he had kept faith with me I should always have done so with him."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_87_4	event	\N	Holmes tells Mrs. Lyons she has had a fortunate escape	86	{"section": "section_87", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3484", "sentence_3485", "sentence_3486", "sentence_3487"], "communicatesContent": "I think that on the whole you have had a fortunate escape. You have had him in your power and he knew it, and yet you are alive. You have been walking for some months very near to the edge of a precipice. We must wish you good-morning now, Mrs. Lyons, and it is probable that you will very shortly hear from us again."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_87_5	event	\N	Holmes speaks about the case becoming rounded off while waiting for the express	86	{"section": "section_87", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3488", "sentence_3489", "sentence_3490", "sentence_3491", "sentence_3492"], "communicatesContent": "Our case becomes rounded off, and difficulty after difficulty thins away in front of us. I shall soon be in the position of being able to put into a single connected narrative one of the most singular and sensational crimes of modern times. Students of criminology will remember the analogous incidents in Godno, in Little Russia, in the year ’66, and of course there are the Anderson murders in North Carolina, but this case possesses some features which are entirely its own. Even now we have no clear case against this very wily man. But I shall be very much surprised if it is not clear enough before we go to bed this night."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_87_6	event	\N	The London express arrives and Lestrade springs from a first-class carriage	86	{"section": "section_87", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3493"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_87_7	event	\N	We all three shake hands with Lestrade	86	{"section": "section_87", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3494"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_87_8	event	\N	Holmes says the biggest thing has happened for years	86	{"section": "section_87", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3496", "sentence_3497", "sentence_3498"], "communicatesContent": "The biggest thing for years."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_87_9	event	\N	Holmes says they have two hours before starting and proposes dinner and a night journey to Dartmoor	86	{"section": "section_87", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3499", "sentence_3500", "sentence_3501", "sentence_3502"], "communicatesContent": "We have two hours before we need think of starting. I think we might employ it in getting some dinner and then, Lestrade, we will take the London fog out of your throat by giving you a breath of the pure night air of Dartmoor. Never been there? Ah, well, I don’t suppose you will forget your first visit."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_88_1	event	\N	Holmes, Watson, and Lestrade travel by wagonette toward Merripit House	87	{"section": "section_88", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3509", "sentence_3510", "sentence_3511", "sentence_3512", "sentence_3513", "sentence_3514", "sentence_3515", "sentence_3516"], "communicatesContent": "A waiting game."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_88_2	event	\N	Holmes and the others leave the wagonette and walk toward Merripit House	87	{"section": "section_88", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3515", "sentence_3516"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_88_3	event	\N	Lestrade says he is armed	87	{"section": "section_88", "speakerId": "lestrade", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3517", "sentence_3518", "sentence_3519"], "communicatesContent": "As long as I have my trousers I have a hip-pocket, and as long as I have my hip-pocket I have something in it."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_88_4	event	\N	Holmes says he and Watson are ready for emergencies	87	{"section": "section_88", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3520", "sentence_3521"], "communicatesContent": "My friend and I are also ready for emergencies."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_88_5	event	\N	Holmes and Lestrade discuss the plan and move into an ambush position near Merripit House	87	{"section": "section_88", "speakerId": "lestrade", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3522", "sentence_3523", "sentence_3524", "sentence_3525", "sentence_3526", "sentence_3527", "sentence_3528", "sentence_3529", "sentence_3530", "sentence_3531", "sentence_3532", "sentence_3533", "sentence_3534", "sentence_3535", "sentence_3536", "sentence_3537", "sentence_3538", "sentence_3539", "sentence_3540", "sentence_3541", "sentence_3542", "sentence_3543"], "communicatesContent": "What’s the game now?"}
