hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_95_6	event	\N	Sir Henry and Dr. Mortimer call upon Holmes and Watson	94	{"section": "section_95", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3790"], "communicatesContent": null}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_95_7	event	\N	Holmes explains the course of events from Stapleton's point of view	94	{"section": "section_95", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3791"], "communicatesContent": "The whole course of events, from the point of view of the man who called himself Stapleton, was simple and direct."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_95_8	event	\N	Holmes tells Watson that Mrs. Stapleton's conversations cleared up the case	94	{"section": "section_95", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3792", "sentence_3793"], "communicatesContent": "I have had the advantage of two conversations with Mrs. Stapleton, and the case has now been so entirely cleared up that I am not aware that there is anything which has remained a secret to us."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_95_9	event	\N	Holmes gives Watson a sketch of the course of events from memory	94	{"section": "section_95", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3794", "sentence_3795", "sentence_3801"], "communicatesContent": "Certainly, though I cannot guarantee that I carry all the facts in my mind. So far as the case of the hound goes, I will give you the course of events as nearly as I can, and you will suggest anything which I may have forgotten."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_95_10	event	\N	Holmes says his current work displaces earlier cases from his recollection	94	{"section": "section_95", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3796", "sentence_3797", "sentence_3798", "sentence_3799", "sentence_3800"], "communicatesContent": "Intense mental concentration has a curious way of blotting out what has passed. So each of my cases displaces the last, and Mlle. Carére has blurred my recollection of Baskerville Hall. Tomorrow some other little problem may be submitted to my notice which will in turn dispossess the fair French lady and the infamous Upwood."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_95_11	event	\N	Holmes reports that the family portrait identified the man as a Baskerville	94	{"section": "section_95", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3802"], "communicatesContent": "My inquiries show beyond all question that the family portrait did not lie, and that this fellow was indeed a Baskerville."}
hound-of-the-baskervilles	event_95_12	event	\N	Holmes explains Rodger Baskerville's family history and flight to South America	94	{"section": "section_95", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_3803", "sentence_3804"], "communicatesContent": "He was a son of Rodger Baskerville, the younger brother of Sir Charles, who fled with a sinister reputation to South America, where he was said to have died unmarried. He did, as a matter of fact, marry, and had one child, this fellow, whose real name is the same as his father's."}
valley-of-fear	section_33	section	\N	Section 33	32	{"wordCount": 668}
