valley-of-fear	event_17_17	event	\N	The butler says the curtains were drawn when the lamps were lit, shortly after four	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "cecil_james_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_662", "sentence_663"], "communicatesContent": "When the lamps were lit. It would be shortly after four."}
valley-of-fear	event_17_18	event	\N	The sergeant states that someone had been hiding there	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_664", "sentence_665"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_17_19	event	\N	The sergeant states the intruder entered after four and before six and hid behind the curtain	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "sergeant_wilson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_666", "sentence_667", "sentence_668", "sentence_669", "sentence_670", "sentence_671", "sentence_672"], "communicatesContent": "I’m bound to say this bears out your theory, Mr. Barker. It looks as if the man got into the house after four when the curtains were drawn, and before six when the bridge was raised. He slipped into this room, because it was the first that he saw. There was no other place where he could hide, so he popped in behind this curtain. That all seems clear enough. It is likely that his main idea was to burgle the house; but Mr. Douglas chanced to come upon him, so he murdered him and escaped."}
valley-of-fear	event_17_20	event	\N	Barker agrees with the sergeant's reading	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "cecil_james_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_672"], "communicatesContent": "That's how I read it."}
valley-of-fear	event_17_21	event	\N	Barker asks to scout the country before the fellow gets away	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "cecil_james_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_673", "sentence_674"], "communicatesContent": "But, I say, aren't we wasting precious time? Couldn't we start out and scout the country before the fellow gets away?"}
valley-of-fear	event_17_22	event	\N	The sergeant says the suspect cannot get away by rail and should not be left yet	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "sergeant_wilson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_675", "sentence_676", "sentence_677", "sentence_678", "sentence_679"], "communicatesContent": "There are no trains before six in the morning; so he can't get away by rail. If he goes by road with his legs all dripping, it's odds that someone will notice him. Anyhow, I can't leave here myself until I am relieved. But I think none of you should go until we see more clearly how we all stand."}
valley-of-fear	event_17_24	event	\N	The doctor says the mark is not tattooed and suggests the man was branded	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "dr_wood", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_686", "sentence_687", "sentence_688", "sentence_689"], "communicatesContent": "It's not tattooed. I never saw anything like it. The man has been branded at some time as they brand cattle. What is the meaning of this?"}
valley-of-fear	event_17_25	event	\N	Barker says he has seen the mark on Douglas many times for ten years	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "cecil_james_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_690"], "communicatesContent": "I have seen the mark on Douglas many times this last ten years."}
valley-of-fear	event_17_26	event	\N	The butler says he has often noticed the same mark on Douglas	16	{"section": "section_17", "speakerId": "cecil_james_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_691", "sentence_692", "sentence_693"], "communicatesContent": "And so have I. Many a time when the master has rolled up his sleeves I have noticed that very mark. I’ve often wondered what it could be."}
valley-of-fear	event_18_1	event	\N	The sergeant says the matter has nothing to do with the crime	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sergeant_wilson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_694"], "communicatesContent": "Then it has nothing to do with the crime, anyhow"}
valley-of-fear	event_18_2	event	\N	The butler exclaims and points at the dead man's outstretched hand	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_698"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_18_3	event	\N	The butler reports that the dead man's wedding ring has been taken	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "mrs_allen", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_699", "sentence_700"], "communicatesContent": "They’ve taken his wedding ring!"}
valley-of-fear	event_18_4	event	\N	The butler explains the dead man's rings and identifies the missing wedding ring	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "mrs_allen", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_702", "sentence_705"], "communicatesContent": "Master always wore his plain gold wedding ring on the little finger of his left hand. That ring with the rough nugget on it was above it, and the twisted snake ring on the third finger. There’s the nugget and there’s the snake, but the wedding ring is gone."}
valley-of-fear	event_18_5	event	\N	Barker confirms the butler's statement	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "cecil_james_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_706"], "communicatesContent": "He’s right"}
valley-of-fear	event_18_6	event	\N	The sergeant asks whether the wedding ring was below the other rings	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sergeant_wilson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_707"], "communicatesContent": "Do you tell me that the wedding ring was below the other?"}
valley-of-fear	event_18_7	event	\N	The butler answers that it was always below the other rings	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "mrs_allen", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_708"], "communicatesContent": "Always!"}
valley-of-fear	event_18_8	event	\N	The sergeant describes how the murderer removed and replaced the rings	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sergeant_wilson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_709", "sentence_710"], "communicatesContent": "Then the murderer, or whoever it was, first took off this ring you call the nugget ring, then the wedding ring, and afterwards put the nugget ring back again."}
valley-of-fear	event_18_9	event	\N	The country policeman says London should be consulted on the case	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sergeant_wilson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_711", "sentence_717"], "communicatesContent": "Seems to me the sooner we get London on to this case the better. It won’t be long now before he is here to help us. But I expect we’ll have to look to London before we are through. Anyhow, I’m not ashamed to say that it is a deal too thick for the likes of me."}
valley-of-fear	event_18_10	event	\N	White Mason arrives from headquarters behind a trotter	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_719"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_18_11	event	\N	White Mason sends a message to Scotland Yard	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_720"], "communicatesContent": "his message"}
valley-of-fear	event_18_12	event	\N	White Mason welcomes Holmes and Watson at Birlstone station	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_720"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_18_13	event	\N	White Mason identifies the Westville Arms as the party's rooming place	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_729", "sentence_732"], "communicatesContent": "Your room is at the Westville Arms. There’s no other place; but I hear that it is clean and good. The man will carry your bags. This way, gentlemen, if you please."}
valley-of-fear	event_18_14	event	\N	The group takes quarters at the inn	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_734"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_18_15	event	\N	White Mason gives a rapid sketch of the previous events	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_735"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_18_16	event	\N	MacDonald makes notes while Holmes listens in admiration	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_736"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_18_17	event	\N	White Mason says the case is remarkable and peculiar	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_737", "sentence_739"], "communicatesContent": "Remarkable! most remarkable! I can hardly recall any case where the features have been more peculiar."}
valley-of-fear	event_18_18	event	\N	White Mason says he has told Holmes how matters stood up to his takeover	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_740", "sentence_747"], "communicatesContent": "I thought you would say so, Mr. Holmes. We’re well up with the times in Sussex. I’ve told you now how matters were, up to the time when I took over from Sergeant Wilson between three and four this morning. My word! I made the old mare go! But I need not have been in such a hurry, as it turned out; for there was nothing immediate that I could do. Sergeant Wilson had all the facts. I checked them and considered them and maybe added a few of my own."}
valley-of-fear	event_19_1	event	\N	Holmes asks what the examined items were	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_748", "sentence_749"], "communicatesContent": "What were they?"}
valley-of-fear	event_19_2	event	\N	A hammer is examined with Dr. Wood's help	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_750", "sentence_751", "sentence_752", "sentence_754", "sentence_760"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_19_3	event	\N	White Mason explains his hope about the hammer	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_753"], "communicatesContent": "I was hoping that if Mr. Douglas defended himself with the hammer, he might have left his mark upon the murderer before he dropped it on the mat."}
valley-of-fear	event_19_4	event	\N	MacDonald remarks that the hammer proves nothing	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_755", "sentence_756"], "communicatesContent": "That, of course, proves nothing at all. There has been many a hammer murder and no trace on the hammer."}
valley-of-fear	event_19_5	event	\N	White Mason examines the gun	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_761", "sentence_762", "sentence_764", "sentence_765"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_19_6	event	\N	Sergeant Wilson points out the wired triggers	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sergeant_wilson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_762"], "communicatesContent": "They were buckshot cartridges, and the triggers were wired together so that, if you pulled on the hinder one, both barrels were discharged."}
valley-of-fear	event_19_7	event	\N	Holmes identifies the gun maker from the barrel markings	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_766", "sentence_767", "sentence_768", "sentence_769"], "communicatesContent": "Pennsylvania Small Arms Company—well-known American firm."}
valley-of-fear	event_19_8	event	\N	White Mason suggests the gun is American and the killer was American	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_777", "sentence_778", "sentence_779", "sentence_780"], "communicatesContent": "No doubt it is an American shotgun. I seem to have read that a sawed-off shotgun is a weapon used in some parts of America. Apart from the name upon the barrel, the idea had occurred to me. There is some evidence then, that this man who entered the house and killed its master was an American."}
valley-of-fear	event_19_9	event	\N	MacDonald rejects the claim that a stranger was in the house	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_781", "sentence_782", "sentence_783", "sentence_784", "sentence_785", "sentence_786", "sentence_787", "sentence_788"], "communicatesContent": "Man, you are surely travelling overfast. I have heard no evidence yet that any stranger was ever in the house at all. The open window, the blood on the sill, the queer card, the marks of boots in the corner, the gun! Nothing there that could not have been arranged. Mr. Douglas was an American, or had lived long in America. So had Mr. Barker. You don’t need to import an American from outside in order to account for American doings."}
valley-of-fear	event_19_10	event	\N	MacDonald asks about Ames and his reliability	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_789", "sentence_790", "sentence_791"], "communicatesContent": "Ames—what about him? Is he reliable?"}
valley-of-fear	event_19_11	event	\N	White Mason describes Ames's service	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_792", "sentence_793", "sentence_794"], "communicatesContent": "Ten years with Sir Charles Chandos—as solid as a rock. He has been with Douglas ever since he took the Manor House five years ago. He has never seen a gun of this sort in the house."}
valley-of-fear	event_19_12	event	\N	MacDonald objects that the gun could have been concealed	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_795", "sentence_796", "sentence_797", "sentence_798", "sentence_799"], "communicatesContent": "The gun was made to conceal. That’s why the barrels were sawed. It would fit into any box. How could he swear there was no such gun in the house?"}
valley-of-fear	event_19_13	event	\N	MacDonald says Ames had never seen such a gun	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_799"], "communicatesContent": "Well, anyhow, he had never seen one."}
valley-of-fear	event_19_14	event	\N	MacDonald argues that no one entered the house	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_800", "sentence_801", "sentence_802", "sentence_803", "sentence_804", "sentence_805"], "communicatesContent": "I’m not convinced yet that there was ever anyone in the house. I’m asking you to conseedar what it involves if you suppose that this gun was ever brought into the house, and that all these strange things were done by a person from outside. Oh, man, it’s just inconceivable! It’s clean against common sense! I put it to you, Mr. Holmes, judging it by what we have heard."}
valley-of-fear	event_20_1	event	\N	Holmes asks White Mason to state his case	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_806"], "communicatesContent": "state your case, Mr. Mac"}
valley-of-fear	event_20_2	event	\N	White Mason argues that the man was not a burglar and that the ring business and the card pointed to premeditated murder for a private reason	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_807", "sentence_808"], "communicatesContent": "The man is not a burglar, supposing that he ever existed. The ring business and the card point to premeditated murder for some private reason."}
valley-of-fear	event_20_3	event	\N	Holmes accepts the argument	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_809"], "communicatesContent": "Very good."}
valley-of-fear	event_20_4	event	\N	White Mason presents the murder scenario and the weapon-choice argument	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_810", "sentence_811", "sentence_812", "sentence_813", "sentence_814", "sentence_815", "sentence_816", "sentence_817"], "communicatesContent": "Here is a man who slips into a house with the deliberate intention of committing murder. He knows, if he knows anything, that he will have a difficulty in making his escape, as the house is surrounded with water. What weapon would he choose? You would say the most silent in the world. Then he could hope when the deed was done to slip quickly from the window, to wade the moat, and to get away at his leisure. But is it understandable that he should go out of his way to bring with him the most noisy weapon he could select, knowing well that it will fetch every human being in the house to the spot as quick as they can run, and that it is all odds that he will be seen before he can get across the moat? Is that credible, Mr. Holmes?"}
valley-of-fear	event_20_5	event	\N	Holmes replies that the case is strongly put and needs justification	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_818", "sentence_819"], "communicatesContent": "You put the case strongly. It certainly needs a good deal of justification."}
valley-of-fear	event_21_12	event	\N	Holmes listens intently to White Mason.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_881"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_20_6	event	\N	Holmes asks whether the farther side of the moat was examined for signs of escape	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_820"], "communicatesContent": "May I ask, Mr. White Mason, whether you examined the farther side of the moat at once to see if there were any signs of the man having climbed out from the water?"}
valley-of-fear	event_20_7	event	\N	White Mason reports that there were no signs, tracks, or marks on the farther side of the moat	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_821", "sentence_822", "sentence_823", "sentence_824"], "communicatesContent": "There were no signs, Mr. Holmes. But it is a stone ledge, and one could hardly expect them. None."}
valley-of-fear	event_20_8	event	\N	Holmes asks to go down to the house at once	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_825", "sentence_826", "sentence_827"], "communicatesContent": "Would there be any objection, Mr. White Mason, to our going down to the house at once? There may possibly be some small point which might be suggestive."}
valley-of-fear	event_20_9	event	\N	White Mason agrees and says he wanted to put Holmes in touch with all the facts first	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_828", "sentence_829"], "communicatesContent": "I was going to propose it, Mr. Holmes; but I thought it well to put you in touch with all the facts before we go. I suppose if anything should strike you—"}
valley-of-fear	event_20_10	event	\N	Inspector MacDonald says he has worked with Holmes before	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_830", "sentence_831"], "communicatesContent": "I have worked with Mr. Holmes before. He plays the game."}
valley-of-fear	event_20_11	event	\N	Holmes states his view of the game and his working method	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_832", "sentence_833", "sentence_834", "sentence_835", "sentence_836"], "communicatesContent": "My own idea of the game, at any rate, is that I go into a case to help the ends of justice and the work of the police. If I have ever separated myself from the official force, it is because they have first separated themselves from me. I have no wish ever to score at their expense. At the same time, Mr. White Mason, I claim the right to work in my own way and give my results at my own time—complete rather than in stages."}
valley-of-fear	event_20_12	event	\N	White Mason welcomes Holmes and invites Watson along	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_837", "sentence_838"], "communicatesContent": "I am sure we are honoured by your presence and to show you all we know. Come along, Dr. Watson, and when the time comes we'll all hope for a place in your book."}
valley-of-fear	event_20_13	event	\N	The group walks down the village street toward the manor house	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_839", "sentence_840", "sentence_841", "sentence_842"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_20_14	event	\N	Watson describes the manor house and its moat, garden, and drawbridge	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_841", "sentence_842", "sentence_843", "sentence_844", "sentence_845", "sentence_846"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_21_1	event	\N	White Mason identifies the window by the drawbridge and says it is open as it was found the previous night.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_847", "sentence_848"], "communicatesContent": "That’s the window, that one on the immediate right of the drawbridge. It’s open just as it was found last night."}
valley-of-fear	event_21_2	event	\N	Holmes inspects the moat edge, stone ledge, and grass border.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_853", "sentence_854"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_21_3	event	\N	White Mason says there is no sign that anyone landed and asks why a sign would be left.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_855", "sentence_856"], "communicatesContent": "I’ve had a good look, Mr. Holmes. There is nothing there, no sign that anyone has landed—but why should he leave any sign?"}
valley-of-fear	event_21_4	event	\N	Holmes asks about the water and White Mason answers that the stream brings down clay and makes it generally turbid.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_857", "sentence_858", "sentence_859", "sentence_860", "sentence_861"], "communicatesContent": "Is the water always turbid?"}
valley-of-fear	event_21_5	event	\N	Holmes asks the depth of the water and White Mason states its depth at the sides and middle.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_862", "sentence_863"], "communicatesContent": "How deep is it?"}
valley-of-fear	event_21_6	event	\N	Holmes concludes that the man was not drowned while crossing and White Mason agrees a child could not be drowned in it.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_864", "sentence_865"], "communicatesContent": "So we can put aside all idea of the man having been drowned in crossing."}
valley-of-fear	event_21_7	event	\N	Holmes and the others cross the drawbridge and are admitted by the butler Ames.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_866"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_21_8	event	\N	The village sergeant remains in the room of Fate while the doctor departs.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_868", "sentence_869"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_21_9	event	\N	White Mason asks Sergeant Wilson if there is anything fresh, then orders him home, tells Ames to wait outside, and instructs that Cecil Barker, Mrs. Douglas, and the housekeeper be warned they may be wanted.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_870", "sentence_871", "sentence_872", "sentence_873", "sentence_874", "sentence_875", "sentence_876", "sentence_877"], "communicatesContent": "Anything fresh, Sergeant Wilson? No, sir. Then you can go home. You’ve had enough. We can send for you if we want you. The butler had better wait outside. Tell him to warn Mr. Cecil Barker, Mrs. Douglas, and the housekeeper that we may want a word with them presently."}
valley-of-fear	event_21_10	event	\N	White Mason asks to give the assembled men the views he has formed first.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_878"], "communicatesContent": "Now, gentlemen, perhaps you will allow me to give you the views I have formed first, and then you will be able to arrive at your own."}
valley-of-fear	event_21_11	event	\N	MacDonald speaks of White Mason’s professional abilities.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_879", "sentence_880"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_21_13	event	\N	White Mason argues that the death was not suicide and must therefore have been murder.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_882", "sentence_883", "sentence_884", "sentence_885", "sentence_886", "sentence_887", "sentence_888", "sentence_889", "sentence_890"], "communicatesContent": "Is it suicide, or is it murder—that’s our first question, gentlemen, is it not? If it were suicide, then we have to believe that this man began by taking off his wedding ring and concealing it; that he then came down here in his dressing gown, trampled mud into a corner behind the curtain in order to give the idea someone had waited for him, opened the window, put blood on the—"}
valley-of-fear	event_21_14	event	\N	White Mason supposes that the crime was done by someone inside the house and describes the sequence required.	20	{"section": "section_21", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_891", "sentence_892", "sentence_893", "sentence_894", "sentence_895", "sentence_896", "sentence_897", "sentence_898"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_22_1	event	\N	Holmes agrees with the preceding theory discussion	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_899", "sentence_900"], "communicatesContent": "I am inclined to agree with you."}
valley-of-fear	event_22_2	event	\N	The discussion reconstructs the intruder's entry and attack on Douglas	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_901", "sentence_902", "sentence_903", "sentence_904", "sentence_905", "sentence_906", "sentence_907", "sentence_908", "sentence_909", "sentence_910", "sentence_911", "sentence_912", "sentence_913", "sentence_914", "sentence_915", "sentence_916", "sentence_917", "sentence_918", "sentence_919", "sentence_920", "sentence_921", "sentence_922", "sentence_923", "sentence_924", "sentence_925", "sentence_926", "sentence_927", "sentence_928"], "communicatesContent": "Well, now, we can reconstruct things on those lines. Mr. Douglas enters the room. He puts down the candle. A man appears from behind the curtain. He is armed with this gun. He demands the wedding ring—Heaven only knows why, but so it must have been. Mr. Douglas gave it up. Then either in cold blood or in the course of a struggle—Douglas may have gripped the hammer that was found upon the mat—he shot Douglas in this horrible way. He dropped his gun and also it would seem this queer card—V.V. 341, whatever that may mean—and he made his escape through the window and across the moat at the very moment when Cecil Barker was discovering the crime."}
valley-of-fear	event_22_3	event	\N	MacDonald objects to the proposed theory and demands a lead from Holmes	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_929", "sentence_930", "sentence_931", "sentence_932", "sentence_933", "sentence_934", "sentence_935"], "communicatesContent": "Man, it would be absolute nonsense if it wasn’t that anything else is even worse! Somebody killed the man, and whoever it was I could clearly prove to you that he should have done it some other way. What does he mean by allowing his retreat to be cut off like that? What does he mean by using a shotgun when silence was his one chance of escape? Come, Mr. Holmes, it’s up to you to give us a lead, since you say Mr. White Mason’s theory is unconvincing."}
valley-of-fear	event_22_4	event	\N	Holmes kneels by the body and asks for the butler	21	{"section": "section_22", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_936", "sentence_937", "sentence_938", "sentence_939", "sentence_940", "sentence_941", "sentence_942", "sentence_943"], "communicatesContent": "I should like a few more facts before I get so far as a theory, Mr. Mac. Dear me! these injuries are really appalling. Can we have the butler in for a moment?... Ames, I understand that you have often seen this very unusual mark—a branded triangle inside a circle—upon Mr. Douglas’s forearm? You never heard any speculation as to what it meant?"}
valley-of-fear	event_23_1	event	\N	Ames says that Douglas cut himself while shaving yesterday morning	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "ames", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_949"], "communicatesContent": "he cut himself in shaving yesterday morning"}
valley-of-fear	event_23_2	event	\N	Ames says that Douglas had not cut himself in shaving for a very long time	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "ames", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_951"], "communicatesContent": "Not for a very long time"}
valley-of-fear	event_23_3	event	\N	Ames says that Douglas was a little restless and excited yesterday	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "ames", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_956"], "communicatesContent": "he was a little restless and excited"}
valley-of-fear	event_23_4	event	\N	White Mason says that he thinks the card and forearm badge suggest a secret society	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_973", "sentence_974"], "communicatesContent": "It gives me the impression of a secret society of some sort; the same with his badge upon the forearm."}
valley-of-fear	event_23_5	event	\N	Holmes proposes a working hypothesis about the murder	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_976", "sentence_977", "sentence_978", "sentence_979", "sentence_981", "sentence_983", "sentence_985", "sentence_987", "sentence_988"], "communicatesContent": "We can adopt it as a working hypothesis and then see how far our difficulties disappear. An agent from such a society makes his way into the house, waits for Mr. Douglas, blows his head nearly off with this weapon, and escapes by wading the moat, after leaving a card beside the dead man, which will, when mentioned in the papers, tell other members of the society that vengeance has been done. But why this gun, of all weapons? And why the missing ring? And why no arrest?"}
valley-of-fear	event_23_6	event	\N	Holmes examines the blood mark on the sill	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_989", "sentence_990", "sentence_991", "sentence_992", "sentence_993"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_23_7	event	\N	Holmes notices Mr. Douglas's dumb-bells under the side table and asks about the missing second dumb-bell	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_994", "sentence_995", "sentence_996", "sentence_997", "sentence_998", "sentence_999", "sentence_1000", "sentence_1001"], "communicatesContent": "What’s this under the side table? Dumb-bell—there’s only one. Where’s the other?"}
valley-of-fear	event_23_8	event	\N	Cecil Barker enters the room	22	{"section": "section_23", "speakerId": "cecil_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1002", "sentence_1003", "sentence_1004", "sentence_1005"], "communicatesContent": "Sorry to interrupt your consultation, but you should hear the latest news."}
valley-of-fear	event_24_1	event	\N	People inspect the found bicycle in the drive	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1012", "sentence_1013", "sentence_1014"], "communicatesContent": "Come and have a look. It is within a hundred yards of the hall door."}
valley-of-fear	event_24_2	event	\N	The inspector remarks on the evidential value of numbered bicycles	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1015", "sentence_1016", "sentence_1017", "sentence_1018", "sentence_1019", "sentence_1020", "sentence_1021", "sentence_1022", "sentence_1023"], "communicatesContent": "It would be a grand help to the police if these things were numbered and registered. But we must be thankful for what we’ve got. If we can’t find where he went to, at least we are likely to get where he came from. But what in the name of all that is wonderful made the fellow leave it behind? And how in the world has he got away without it? We don’t seem to get a gleam of light in the case, Mr. Holmes."}
valley-of-fear	event_24_3	event	\N	White Mason invites the house evidence to be heard in the dining room	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": "white_mason", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1025", "sentence_1026", "sentence_1027", "sentence_1028", "sentence_1029", "sentence_1030"], "communicatesContent": "Have you seen all you want of the study? Then perhaps you would now like to hear the evidence of some of the people in the house. We could use the dining room, Ames. Please come yourself first and tell us what you know."}
valley-of-fear	event_24_4	event	\N	Ames gives an account of Douglas's movements and the events after the bell rings	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": "ames", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1031", "sentence_1032", "sentence_1033", "sentence_1034", "sentence_1035", "sentence_1036", "sentence_1037", "sentence_1038", "sentence_1039", "sentence_1040", "sentence_1041", "sentence_1042", "sentence_1043", "sentence_1044", "sentence_1045", "sentence_1046", "sentence_1047", "sentence_1048", "sentence_1049", "sentence_1050", "sentence_1051", "sentence_1052", "sentence_1053", "sentence_1054", "sentence_1055", "sentence_1056", "sentence_1057", "sentence_1058", "sentence_1059", "sentence_1060"], "communicatesContent": "He had been engaged five years before, when Douglas first came to Birlstone. He understood that Mr. Douglas was a rich gentleman who had made his money in America. He had been a kind and considerate employer. He never saw any signs of apprehension in Mr. Douglas. He ordered the drawbridge to be pulled up every night. Mr. Douglas seldom went to London or left the village; but on the day before the crime he had been shopping at Tunbridge Wells. He had observed restlessness and excitement in Mr. Douglas that day. He had not gone to bed that night; but was in the pantry putting away the silver when he heard the bell ring violently. He heard no shot. The housekeeper had come out of her room. They had gone to the front of the house together. He had seen Mrs. Douglas coming down the stairs. Mr. Barker had rushed out of the study and begged Mrs. Douglas to go back. Mrs. Douglas had gone back after persuasion. She did not scream. She made no outcry. Mrs. Allen had taken her upstairs and stayed with her in the bedroom. Ames and Mr. Barker had then returned to the study, where they had found everything exactly as the police had seen it. The candle was not lit; but the lamp was burning. They had looked out of the window, but nothing could be seen or heard. They had then rushed out into the hall, where Ames had turned the windlass which lowered the drawbridge. Mr. Barker had then hurried off to get the police."}
valley-of-fear	event_24_5	event	\N	Ames puts away the silver in the pantry	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1039"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_24_6	event	\N	The bell rings violently	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1039", "sentence_1041"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_24_7	event	\N	Ames and Mrs. Allen go to the front of the house	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1041", "sentence_1042"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_24_8	event	\N	Mrs. Douglas comes down the stairs	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1043", "sentence_1044"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_24_9	event	\N	Barker rushes from the study and orders Mrs. Douglas to go back	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": "cecil_james_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1045", "sentence_1046", "sentence_1047", "sentence_1048", "sentence_1049", "sentence_1050", "sentence_1051"], "communicatesContent": "For God’s sake, go back to your room! Poor Jack is dead! You can do nothing. For God’s sake, go back!"}
valley-of-fear	event_24_10	event	\N	Mrs. Douglas goes back upstairs and is taken to the bedroom	23	{"section": "section_24", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1052", "sentence_1053", "sentence_1054", "sentence_1055"], "communicatesContent": null}
