valley-of-fear	event_33_7	event	\N	Mrs. Douglas and Barker ring the bell and summon the servants at quarter past eleven	32	{"section": "section_33", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1470"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_33_8	event	\N	Watson states that Mrs. Douglas and Barker have an understanding	32	{"section": "section_33", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1473", "sentence_1474"], "communicatesContent": "that there is an understanding between those two people"}
valley-of-fear	event_33_9	event	\N	Watson says Mrs. Douglas sits laughing at a jest within hours of her husband's murder	32	{"section": "section_33", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1474"], "communicatesContent": "She must be a heartless creature to sit laughing at some jest within a few hours of her husband's murder."}
valley-of-fear	event_33_10	event	\N	Holmes replies about Barker and Mrs. Douglas concealing the truth	32	{"section": "section_33", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1482", "sentence_1483", "sentence_1484", "sentence_1485"], "communicatesContent": "If you put it that Mrs. Douglas and Barker know the truth about the murder, and are conspiring to conceal it, then I can give you a whole-souled answer. I am sure they do."}
a-case-of-identity	sentence_172	lexical	\N	\N	171	{"text": "“But you have no notion as to what it could have been?”", "section": "section_16", "entities": []}
valley-of-fear	event_34_1	event	\N	Watson says the woman and man could not be a couple who conspire to murder the husband	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1491", "sentence_1492", "sentence_1493", "sentence_1494", "sentence_1495"], "communicatesContent": "That, I am sure, cannot be true. Well, at least they gave that impression. However, we will suppose that they are an extraordinarily astute couple, who deceive everyone upon this point, and conspire to murder the husband. He happens to be a man over whose head some danger hangs— We have only their word for that."}
valley-of-fear	event_34_2	event	\N	Holmes responds thoughtfully and outlines Watson's theory	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1496", "sentence_1497", "sentence_1498", "sentence_1499", "sentence_1500", "sentence_1501"], "communicatesContent": "I see, Watson. You are sketching out a theory by which everything they say from the beginning is false. According to your idea, there was never any hidden menace, or secret society, or Valley of Fear, or Boss MacSomebody, or anything else. Well, that is a good sweeping generalization. Let us see what that brings us to."}
valley-of-fear	event_34_3	event	\N	Holmes describes the couple inventing an explanation for the crime and staging evidence	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1502", "sentence_1503", "sentence_1504", "sentence_1505", "sentence_1506"], "communicatesContent": "They invent this theory to account for the crime. They then play up to the idea by leaving this bicycle in the park as proof of the existence of some outsider. The stain on the windowsill conveys the same idea. So does the card on the body, which might have been prepared in the house. That all fits into your hypothesis, Watson."}
valley-of-fear	event_34_4	event	\N	Holmes questions the shotgun, the alarm risk, and Mrs. Allen's possible inquiry	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1507", "sentence_1508", "sentence_1509", "sentence_1510", "sentence_1511"], "communicatesContent": "But now we come on the nasty, angular, uncompromising bits which won’t slip into their places. Why a cut-off shotgun of all weapons—and an American one at that? How could they be so sure that the sound of it would not bring someone on to them? It’s a mere chance as it is that Mrs. Allen did not start out to inquire for the slamming door. Why did your guilty couple do all this, Watson?"}
valley-of-fear	event_34_5	event	\N	Watson says he cannot explain it	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1512"], "communicatesContent": "I confess that I can’t explain it."}
valley-of-fear	event_34_6	event	\N	Holmes challenges the probability of a guilty couple removing a wedding ring and hiding a bicycle	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1513", "sentence_1514", "sentence_1515", "sentence_1516", "sentence_1517", "sentence_1518", "sentence_1519", "sentence_1520"], "communicatesContent": "And then again, if a woman and her lover conspire to murder a husband, are they going to advertise their guilt by ostentatiously removing his wedding ring after his death? Does that strike you as very probable, Watson? And once again, if the thought of leaving a bicycle concealed outside had occurred to you, would it really have seemed worth doing when the dullest detective would naturally say this is an obvious blind, as the bicycle is the first thing which the fugitive needed in order to make his escape. I can conceive of no explanation. And yet there should be no combination of events for which the wit of man cannot conceive an explanation. Simply as a mental exercise, without any assertion that it is true, let me indicate a possible line of thought. It is, I admit, mere imagination; but how often is imagination the mother of truth?"}
valley-of-fear	event_34_7	event	\N	Holmes proposes a hypothetical guilty secret, murder by an avenger, and the taking of the wedding ring	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1521", "sentence_1522", "sentence_1523", "sentence_1524"], "communicatesContent": "We will suppose that there was a guilty secret, a really shameful secret in the life of this man Douglas. This leads to his murder by someone who is, we will suppose, an avenger, someone from outside. This avenger, for some reason which I confess I am still at a loss to explain, took the dead man’s wedding ring. The vendetta might conceivably date back to the man’s first marriage, and the ring be taken for some such reason."}
valley-of-fear	event_34_8	event	\N	Holmes proposes that Barker and the wife arrive, are warned, and let the assassin go	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1525", "sentence_1526", "sentence_1527", "sentence_1528"], "communicatesContent": "Before this avenger got away, Barker and the wife had reached the room. The assassin convinced them that any attempt to arrest him would lead to the publication of some hideous scandal. They were converted to this idea, and preferred to let him go. For this purpose they probably lowered the bridge, which can be done quite noiselessly, and then raised it again."}
valley-of-fear	event_34_9	event	\N	Holmes proposes the assassin escapes on foot and leaves the bicycle concealed	33	{"section": "section_34", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1529", "sentence_1530", "sentence_1531"], "communicatesContent": "He made his escape, and for some reason thought that he could do so more safely on foot than on the bicycle. He therefore left his machine where it would not be discovered until he had got safely away. So far we are within the bounds of possibility, are we not?"}
valley-of-fear	event_35_1	event	\N	Holmes proposes to go up to the study and sit there alone.	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1542", "sentence_1544", "sentence_1546"], "communicatesContent": "I think that an evening alone in that study would help me much."}
valley-of-fear	event_35_2	event	\N	Holmes borrows Watson's umbrella.	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1550", "sentence_1551", "sentence_1552", "sentence_1556"], "communicatesContent": "By the way, you have that big umbrella of yours, have you not?"}
valley-of-fear	event_35_3	event	\N	Watson states that the umbrella is here.	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1551"], "communicatesContent": "It is here."}
valley-of-fear	event_35_4	event	\N	Holmes receives the umbrella from Watson.	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1552", "sentence_1556"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_35_5	event	\N	Inspector MacDonald and White Mason return from Tunbridge Wells exultant and report progress.	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "inspector_mac", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1558"], "communicatesContent": "We have made a great advance in our investigation."}
valley-of-fear	event_41_4	event	\N	Holmes calls for immediate action	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1813", "sentence_1814", "sentence_1815"], "communicatesContent": "Now!"}
valley-of-fear	event_35_6	event	\N	MacDonald reports that the bicycle has been identified and a description of the man has been obtained.	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "inspector_mac", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1559", "sentence_1560"], "communicatesContent": "We’ve had the bicycle identified, and we have a description of our man."}
valley-of-fear	event_35_7	event	\N	MacDonald explains how the bicycle was traced to Hargrave at the Eagle Commercial.	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "inspector_mac", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1563", "sentence_1564", "sentence_1565", "sentence_1566", "sentence_1567", "sentence_1568", "sentence_1569", "sentence_1570"], "communicatesContent": "We took the bicycle over with us and showed it at the hotels. It was identified at once by the manager of the Eagle Commercial as belonging to a man named Hargrave, who had taken a room there two days before."}
valley-of-fear	event_35_8	event	\N	Holmes praises the inspectors for practical work.	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1571", "sentence_1572", "sentence_1573"], "communicatesContent": "You have indeed done some solid work while I have been sitting spinning theories with my friend!"}
valley-of-fear	event_35_9	event	\N	Watson remarks that the events may fit Holmes's theories.	34	{"section": "section_35", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1574"], "communicatesContent": "But this may all fit in with your theories."}
valley-of-fear	event_36_1	event	\N	MacDonald states that there was little to identify the man	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1578", "sentence_1579", "sentence_1580", "sentence_1581"], "communicatesContent": "So little that it was evident that he had carefully guarded himself against identification. There were no papers or letters, and no marking upon the clothes. A cycle map of the county lay on his bedroom table. He had left the hotel after breakfast yesterday morning on his bicycle, and no more was heard of him until our inquiries."}
valley-of-fear	event_36_2	event	\N	MacDonald reports the man's description	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1588", "sentence_1589", "sentence_1590", "sentence_1591", "sentence_1595"], "communicatesContent": "Here we have it so far as they could give it. They don’t seem to have taken any very particular stock of him; but still the porter, the clerk, and the chambermaid are all agreed that this about covers the points. He was a man about five foot nine in height, fifty or so years of age, his hair slightly grizzled, a grayish moustache, a curved nose, and a face which all of them described as fierce and forbidding. He was dressed in a heavy gray suit with a reefer jacket, and he wore a short yellow overcoat and a soft cap."}
valley-of-fear	event_36_3	event	\N	MacDonald describes the shotgun and how it could be carried	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1596", "sentence_1597", "sentence_1598", "sentence_1599"], "communicatesContent": "It is less than two feet long. It could very well have fitted into his valise. He could have carried it inside his overcoat without difficulty."}
valley-of-fear	event_36_4	event	\N	MacDonald outlines his reconstruction of Hargrave's movements and murder	35	{"section": "section_36", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1601", "sentence_1602", "sentence_1603", "sentence_1604", "sentence_1605", "sentence_1606", "sentence_1607", "sentence_1608", "sentence_1610", "sentence_1611", "sentence_1612", "sentence_1613", "sentence_1614", "sentence_1615", "sentence_1616", "sentence_1617", "sentence_1618", "sentence_1619", "sentence_1620", "sentence_1621"], "communicatesContent": "When we have got our man, we shall be better able to judge. But, even as it stands, we have surely gone a long way. We know that an American calling himself Hargrave came to Tunbridge Wells two days ago with bicycle and valise. In the latter was a sawed-off shotgun; so he came with the deliberate purpose of crime. Yesterday morning he set off for this place on his bicycle, with his gun concealed in his overcoat. No one saw him arrive, so far as we can learn; but he need not pass through the village to reach the park gates, and there are many cyclists upon the road. Presumably he at once concealed his cycle among the laurels where it was found, and possibly lurked there himself, with his eye on the house, waiting for Mr. Douglas to come out. The shotgun is a strange weapon to use inside a house; but he had intended to use it outside, and there it has very obvious advantages, as it would be impossible to miss with it, and the sound of shots is so common in an English sporting neighbourhood that no particular notice would be taken. Mr. Douglas did not appear. He left his bicycle and approached the house in the twilight. He found the bridge down and no one about. He took his chance, intending, no doubt, to make some excuse if he met anyone. He met no one. He slipped into the first room that he saw, and concealed himself behind the curtain. Thence he could see the drawbridge go up, and he knew that his only escape was through the moat. He waited until quarter-past eleven, when Mr. Douglas upon his usual nightly round came into the room. He shot him and escaped, as arranged. He was aware that the bicycle would be described by the hotel people and be a clue against him; so he left it there and made his way by some other means to London or to some safe hiding place which he had already arranged."}
valley-of-fear	event_37_1	event	\N	Holmes returns from his solitary excursion at night	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1638"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_37_2	event	\N	Holmes speaks with Watson in the bedroom about sleeping beside a lunatic	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1642", "sentence_1643", "sentence_1644", "sentence_1646", "sentence_1647"], "communicatesContent": "would you be afraid to sleep in the same room with a lunatic, a man with softening of the brain, an idiot whose mind has lost its grip?"}
valley-of-fear	event_37_3	event	\N	Watson answers Holmes	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1646"], "communicatesContent": "Not in the least."}
valley-of-fear	event_37_4	event	\N	Holmes and the others find MacDonald and White Mason consulting in the police sergeant's parlour	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1649", "sentence_1650"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_37_5	event	\N	Holmes asks about the elusive bicyclist	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1652", "sentence_1653", "sentence_1654"], "communicatesContent": "Still on the track of the elusive bicyclist?"}
valley-of-fear	event_37_6	event	\N	Holmes asks for the latest news of the ruffian	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1654"], "communicatesContent": "What is the latest news of the ruffian?"}
valley-of-fear	event_37_7	event	\N	MacDonald reports that the bicyclist is reported from several places and has been arrested in three of them	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1655", "sentence_1656", "sentence_1657", "sentence_1658"], "communicatesContent": "He is at present reported from Leicester, Nottingham, Southampton, Derby, East Ham, Richmond, and fourteen other places. In three of them—East Ham, Leicester, and Liverpool—there is a clear case against him, and he has actually been arrested. The country seems to be full of the fugitives with yellow coats."}
valley-of-fear	event_41_5	event	\N	Holmes and the others run across the bridge and ring the bell	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1816"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_37_8	event	\N	Holmes advises MacDonald and White Mason to abandon the case	36	{"section": "section_37", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1661", "sentence_1662", "sentence_1663", "sentence_1664", "sentence_1665"], "communicatesContent": "I wish to give you a very earnest piece of advice. When I went into this case with you I bargained, as you will no doubt remember, that I should not present you with half-proved theories, but that I should retain and work out my own ideas until I had satisfied myself that they were correct. For this reason I am not at the present moment telling you all that is in my mind. On the other hand, I said that I would play the game fairly by you, and I do not think it is a fair game to allow you for one unnecessary moment to waste your energies upon a profitless task. Therefore I am here to advise you this morning, and my advice to you is summed up in three words—abandon the case."}
valley-of-fear	event_38_1	event	\N	MacDonald and White Mason stare at Holmes in amazement	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1666"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_38_2	event	\N	Holmes states that he considers the case hopeless but the arrival at the truth is not hopeless	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1669", "sentence_1670"], "communicatesContent": "I consider your case to be hopeless. I do not consider that it is hopeless to arrive at the truth."}
valley-of-fear	event_38_3	event	\N	Holmes says the cyclist is not an invention and that the investigators have his description, valise, and bicycle	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1671", "sentence_1673"], "communicatesContent": "But this cyclist. He is not an invention. We have his description, his valise, his bicycle."}
valley-of-fear	event_38_4	event	\N	Holmes says the man must be somewhere and can be found	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1674", "sentence_1676", "sentence_1677"], "communicatesContent": "The fellow must be somewhere. Why should we not get him? Yes, yes, no doubt he is somewhere, and no doubt we shall get him; but I would not have you waste your energies in East Ham or Liverpool. I am sure that we can find some shorter cut to a result."}
valley-of-fear	event_38_5	event	\N	The inspector accuses Holmes of holding something back and Holmes answers	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "inspector_mac", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1678", "sentence_1683"], "communicatesContent": "You are holding something back. It’s hardly fair of you, Mr. Holmes."}
valley-of-fear	event_38_6	event	\N	Holmes says he will hold information back only briefly and then return to London with his results	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1681", "sentence_1684"], "communicatesContent": "You know my methods of work, Mr. Mac. But I will hold it back for the shortest time possible. I only wish to verify my details in one way, which can very readily be done, and then I make my bow and return to London, leaving my results entirely at your service. I owe you too much to act otherwise; for in all my experience I cannot recall any more singular and interesting study."}
valley-of-fear	event_38_7	event	\N	Holmes says he spent some hours at the Manor House last night	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1688"], "communicatesContent": "Well, since you ask me, I spent, as I told you that I would, some hours last night at the Manor House."}
valley-of-fear	event_38_8	event	\N	Holmes produces a tract about the Manor House	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1691", "sentence_1692"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_38_9	event	\N	Holmes remarks that sympathy with the historical atmosphere of surroundings adds zest to an investigation and quotes the tract	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1693", "sentence_1696"], "communicatesContent": "It immensely adds to the zest of an investigation, my dear Mr. Mac, when one is in conscious sympathy with the historical atmosphere of one’s surroundings. Don’t look so impatient; for I assure you that even so bald an account as this raises some sort of picture of the past in one’s mind. Permit me to give you a sample. ‘Erected in the fifth year of the reign of James I, and standing upon the site of a much older building, the Manor House of Birlstone presents one of the finest surviving examples of the moated Jacobean residence—’"}
valley-of-fear	event_38_10	event	\N	Holmes says the tract mentions historical associations connected with the house	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1699", "sentence_1700"], "communicatesContent": "Well, I won’t read it verbatim, since you feel so strongly upon the subject. But when I tell you that there is some account of the taking of the place by a parliamentary colonel in 1644, of the concealment of Charles for several days in the course of the Civil War, and finally of a visit there by the second George, you will admit that there are various associations of interest connected with this ancient house."}
valley-of-fear	event_38_11	event	\N	Holmes states that breadth of view, interplay of ideas, and oblique uses of knowledge are essential and refers to himself as a connoisseur of crime	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1703", "sentence_1707"], "communicatesContent": "Is it not? Is it not? Breadth of view, my dear Mr. Mac, is one of the essentials of our profession. The interplay of ideas and the oblique uses of knowledge are often of extraordinary interest. You will excuse these remarks from one who, though a mere connoisseur of crime, is still rather older and perhaps more experienced than yourself."}
valley-of-fear	event_38_12	event	\N	The detective admits Holmes's experience and asks him to reach the point	37	{"section": "section_38", "speakerId": "inspector_mac", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1708", "sentence_1709"], "communicatesContent": "I’m the first to admit that. You get to your point, I admit; but you have such a deuced round-the-corner way of doing it."}
valley-of-fear	event_39_1	event	\N	Holmes calls at the Manor House the previous night	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1711"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_39_2	event	\N	Holmes does not see Barker or Mrs. Douglas at the Manor House	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1712"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_39_3	event	\N	Holmes visits Ames and is allowed to sit alone in the study	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1714"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_39_4	event	\N	Holmes looks for the missing dumb-bell in the study	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1721", "sentence_1724"], "communicatesContent": null}
a-case-of-identity	sentence_173	lexical	\N	\N	172	{"text": "“None.”", "section": "section_16", "entities": []}
valley-of-fear	event_39_5	event	\N	Holmes tells the inspector and White Mason that they are investigating the murder of John Douglas and should not trace the man on the bicycle	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1728", "sentence_1729", "sentence_1730", "sentence_1731", "sentence_1732", "sentence_1733", "sentence_1734", "sentence_1735", "sentence_1738", "sentence_1741", "sentence_1742", "sentence_1743", "sentence_1744", "sentence_1745", "sentence_1746", "sentence_1747", "sentence_1748", "sentence_1749", "sentence_1750", "sentence_1751", "sentence_1752", "sentence_1753"], "communicatesContent": "We are investigating the murder of Mr. John Douglas of Birlstone Manor. Don't trouble to trace the mysterious gentleman upon the bicycle. I will tell you exactly what to do, if you will do it. I recommend a nice, cheery country walk for both of you, and I want you to meet me here before dusk without fail."}
valley-of-fear	event_39_6	event	\N	Holmes asks the inspector and White Mason to write a note to Barker and dictates it	38	{"section": "section_39", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1753", "sentence_1754", "sentence_1755", "sentence_1756", "sentence_1757", "sentence_1758", "sentence_1759", "sentence_1760", "sentence_1761", "sentence_1762", "sentence_1763", "sentence_1764", "sentence_1765"], "communicatesContent": "Write a note to Mr. Barker: It is our duty to drain the moat, in the hope that we may find something which may bear upon our investigation. I have made arrangements, and the workmen will be at work early tomorrow morning diverting the stream."}
valley-of-fear	event_40_1	event	\N	Holmes tells the others to sign the paper and send it by hand at four o'clock	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1767", "sentence_1768", "sentence_1769", "sentence_1770"], "communicatesContent": "Now sign that, and send it by hand about four o’clock. At that hour we shall meet again in this room. Until then we may each do what we like; for I can assure you that this inquiry has come to a definite pause."}
valley-of-fear	event_40_2	event	\N	The group reassembles in the evening	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1771"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_40_3	event	\N	Holmes asks the others to put everything to the test and start the expedition	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1773", "sentence_1774", "sentence_1775"], "communicatesContent": "I am asking you now to put everything to the test with me, and you will judge for yourselves whether the observations I have made justify the conclusions to which I have come. It is a chill evening, and I do not know how long our expedition may last; so I beg that you will wear your warmest coats. It is of the first importance that we should be in our places before it grows dark; so with your permission we shall get started at once."}
valley-of-fear	event_40_4	event	\N	The party walks along the outer bounds of the Manor House park	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1776"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_40_5	event	\N	The party slips through a gap in the rails	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1776", "sentence_1777"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_40_6	event	\N	The party follows Holmes to the shrubbery opposite the main door and drawbridge	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1777"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_40_7	event	\N	Holmes crouches behind the screen of laurels and the others follow	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1779"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_40_8	event	\N	Holmes tells the others to remain patient and keep quiet	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1782"], "communicatesContent": "Possess our souls in patience and make as little noise as possible."}
valley-of-fear	event_40_9	event	\N	Holmes explains why he staged the scene and asks for patience	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1785", "sentence_1786", "sentence_1787", "sentence_1788", "sentence_1789", "sentence_1790", "sentence_1791", "sentence_1792", "sentence_1793"], "communicatesContent": "Watson insists that I am the dramatist in real life. Some touch of the artist wells up within me, and calls insistently for a well-staged performance. Surely our profession, Mr. Mac, would be a drab and sordid one if we did not sometimes set the scene so as to glorify our results. The blunt accusation, the brutal tap upon the shoulder—what can one make of such a denouement? But the quick inference, the subtle trap, the clever forecast of coming events, the triumphant vindication of bold theories—are these not the pride and the justification of our life’s work? At the present moment you thrill with the glamour of the situation and the anticipation of the hunt. Where would be that thrill if I had been as definite as a timetable? I only ask a little patience, Mr. Mac, and all will be clear to you."}
valley-of-fear	event_40_10	event	\N	The London detective remarks on the cold and the hoped-for conclusion	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "london", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1794"], "communicatesContent": "Well, I hope the pride and justification and the rest of it will come before we all get our death of cold."}
valley-of-fear	event_40_11	event	\N	The vigil continues through a long, bitter wait	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1795", "sentence_1796", "sentence_1797", "sentence_1798", "sentence_1799"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_40_12	event	\N	The inspector asks how long the watch will last and what they are watching for	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "inspector_mac", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1800", "sentence_1801", "sentence_1802"], "communicatesContent": "How long is this to last? And what is it we are watching for?"}
valley-of-fear	event_40_13	event	\N	Holmes answers that he does not know the duration and identifies their watch as waiting for the object of the surveillance	39	{"section": "section_40", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1803", "sentence_1804", "sentence_1805"], "communicatesContent": "I have no more notion than you how long it is to last. If criminals would always schedule their movements like railway trains, it would certainly be more convenient for all of us. As to what it is we— Well, that's what we are watching for!"}
valley-of-fear	event_41_1	event	\N	A man opens the study casement and peers out into the gloom	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1808", "sentence_1809"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_2	event	\N	The man stirs up the moat with something in his hand	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1810", "sentence_1811"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_3	event	\N	The man hauls a large round object in through the open casement	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1812"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_7	event	\N	Holmes rushes into the room and brushes Ames aside	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1818"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_8	event	\N	Holmes notes the oil lamp as the source of the seen glow	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1819"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_9	event	\N	Cecil Barker holds the oil lamp toward the entrants and protests	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "cecil_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1820", "sentence_1821", "sentence_1822", "sentence_1823", "sentence_1824"], "communicatesContent": "What the devil is the meaning of all this? What are you after, anyhow?"}
valley-of-fear	event_41_10	event	\N	Holmes seizes the sodden bundle from under the writing table	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1825", "sentence_1826"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_11	event	\N	Barker questions how Holmes knew about the bundle	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "cecil_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1827", "sentence_1828", "sentence_1829"], "communicatesContent": "How in thunder came you to know anything about it?"}
valley-of-fear	event_41_12	event	\N	Holmes states that he put the bundle there	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1830", "sentence_1831", "sentence_1832", "sentence_1833"], "communicatesContent": "I put it there. Perhaps I should have said 'replaced it there.'"}
valley-of-fear	event_41_13	event	\N	Holmes explains how he inferred the bundle had been sunk in the water and later recovered it with Ames's help	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1834", "sentence_1835", "sentence_1836", "sentence_1837"], "communicatesContent": "I was struck by the absence of a dumb-bell. When water is near and a weight is missing it is a fair supposition that something has been sunk in the water. With Ames's help and Watson's umbrella, I fished up and inspected this bundle last night."}
valley-of-fear	event_41_14	event	\N	Holmes explains that the bundle was hidden so that the hider would withdraw it when darkness allowed	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1838", "sentence_1839", "sentence_1840"], "communicatesContent": "It was important to prove who placed it there. The moat was announced as being dried to-morrow so whoever hid the bundle would withdraw it when darkness enabled him to do so."}
valley-of-fear	event_41_15	event	\N	Holmes places the wet bundle on the table and opens it	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1841"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_16	event	\N	Holmes extracts a dumb-bell from the bundle and sets it aside	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1842"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_17	event	\N	Holmes removes a pair of boots from the bundle	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1843", "sentence_1844"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_18	event	\N	Holmes lays a sheathed knife on the table	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1845"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_41_19	event	\N	Holmes unravels a bundle of clothing from the bundle	40	{"section": "section_41", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1846"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_42_1	event	\N	Holmes holds the overcoat toward the light	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1848"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_42_2	event	\N	Holmes describes the overcoat’s inner pocket and the tailor’s tab	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1849", "sentence_1850"], "communicatesContent": "Here, as you perceive, is the inner pocket prolonged into the lining in such fashion as to give ample space for the truncated fowling piece. The tailor’s tab is on the neck—‘Neal, Outfitter, Vermissa, U.S.A.’"}
valley-of-fear	event_42_3	event	\N	Holmes states that Vermissa is a flourishing little town in a coal and iron valley	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1851"], "communicatesContent": "I have spent an instructive afternoon in the rector’s library, and have enlarged my knowledge by adding the fact that Vermissa is a flourishing little town at the head of one of the best known coal and iron valleys in the United States."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_4	event	\N	Holmes suggests that V.V. may stand for Vermissa Valley and that the valley may be the Valley of Fear	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1852", "sentence_1853", "sentence_1854"], "communicatesContent": "I have some recollection, Mr. Barker, that you associated the coal districts with Mr. Douglas’s first wife, and it would surely not be too far-fetched an inference that the V.V. upon the card by the dead body might stand for Vermissa Valley, or that this very valley which sends forth emissaries of murder may be that Valley of Fear of which we have heard. So much is fairly clear."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_5	event	\N	Holmes yields the floor to Barker for an explanation	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1855"], "communicatesContent": "And now, Mr. Barker, I seem to be standing rather in the way of your explanation."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_6	event	\N	Barker reacts with anger, amazement, consternation, and indecision	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1856", "sentence_1857"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_42_7	event	\N	Barker speaks ironically to Holmes	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "cecil_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1858", "sentence_1859"], "communicatesContent": "You know such a lot, Mr. Holmes, perhaps you had better tell us some more."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_8	event	\N	Holmes responds that Barker should explain	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1860"], "communicatesContent": "I have no doubt that I could tell you a great deal more, Mr. Barker; but it would come with a better grace from you."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_9	event	\N	Barker says any secret is not his and refuses to give it away	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "cecil_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1861", "sentence_1862"], "communicatesContent": "Well, all I can say is that if there’s any secret here it is not my secret, and I am not the man to give it away."}
valley-of-fear	event_44_1	event	\N	Douglas approves that the speaker figured it out about right	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "douglas_of_birlstone_manor_house", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1919"], "communicatesContent": "Well, you figured it out about right."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_10	event	\N	The inspector warns Barker that he will be kept in sight until a warrant is obtained	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "inspector_mac", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1863"], "communicatesContent": "Well, if you take that line, Mr. Barker, we must just keep you in sight until we have the warrant and can hold you."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_11	event	\N	Barker defies the inspector	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "cecil_barker", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1864"], "communicatesContent": "You can do what you damn please about that."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_12	event	\N	Mrs. Douglas enters the room and speaks to Cecil Barker	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "mrs_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1866", "sentence_1867", "sentence_1868", "sentence_1869"], "communicatesContent": "You have done enough for now, Cecil. Whatever comes of it in the future, you have done enough."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_13	event	\N	Holmes urges Mrs. Douglas to trust the police and to ask Mr. Douglas to tell his story	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1870", "sentence_1871", "sentence_1872", "sentence_1873", "sentence_1874"], "communicatesContent": "Enough and more than enough. I have every sympathy with you, madam, and should strongly urge you to have some confidence in the common sense of our jurisdiction and to take the police voluntarily into your complete confidence. It may be that I am myself at fault for not following up the hint which you conveyed to me through my friend, Dr. Watson; but, at that time I had every reason to believe that you were directly concerned in the crime. Now I am assured that this is not so. At the same time, there is much that is unexplained, and I should strongly recommend that you ask Mr. Douglas to tell us his own story."}
valley-of-fear	event_42_14	event	\N	Mrs. Douglas cries out in astonishment	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1875"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_42_15	event	\N	A man emerges from the gloom of the corner and advances into the room	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1876"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_42_16	event	\N	Mrs. Douglas embraces the man and Barker takes his hand	41	{"section": "section_42", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1877", "sentence_1878"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_43_1	event	\N	Douglas returns and hands papers to Watson	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1881", "sentence_1883", "sentence_1884", "sentence_1885", "sentence_1886", "sentence_1887", "sentence_1888", "sentence_1889", "sentence_1890"], "communicatesContent": "I’ve heard of you. You are the historian of this bunch. Tell it your own way; but there are the facts. I’ve been cooped up two days, and I’ve spent the daylight hours in putting the thing into words. You’re welcome to them—you and your public. There’s the story of the Valley of Fear."}
valley-of-fear	event_43_2	event	\N	Holmes says he wants Douglas's story of the present	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1891", "sentence_1892"], "communicatesContent": "That’s the past, Mr. Douglas. What we desire now is to hear your story of the present."}
valley-of-fear	event_43_3	event	\N	Douglas asks to smoke and explains his fear of tobacco smell	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1893", "sentence_1894", "sentence_1895", "sentence_1896"], "communicatesContent": "You’ll have it, sir. May I smoke as I talk? Thank you, Mr. Holmes. You’re a smoker yourself, if I remember right, and you’ll guess what it is to be sitting for two days with tobacco in your pocket and afraid that the smell will give you away."}
valley-of-fear	event_43_4	event	\N	Douglas leans against the mantelpiece and smokes Holmes's cigar	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1897"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_43_5	event	\N	Douglas says he has heard of Holmes and expects to bring fresh information	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1898", "sentence_1899", "sentence_1900"], "communicatesContent": "I’ve heard of you, Mr. Holmes. I never guessed that I should meet you. But before you are through with that, you will say I’ve brought you something fresh."}
valley-of-fear	event_43_6	event	\N	MacDonald reacts in amazement to Douglas's appearance	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1901", "sentence_1902", "sentence_1903", "sentence_1904", "sentence_1905"], "communicatesContent": "Well, this fairly beats me! If you are Mr. John Douglas of Birlstone Manor, then whose death have we been investigating for these two days, and where in the world have you sprung from now? You seemed to me to come out of the floor like a jack-in-a-box."}
valley-of-fear	event_43_7	event	\N	Holmes explains why he expected Douglas to be concealed under the roof	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1906", "sentence_1907", "sentence_1908"], "communicatesContent": "you would not read that excellent local compilation which described the concealment of King Charles. People did not hide in those days without excellent hiding places, and the hiding place that has once been used may be again. I had persuaded myself that we should find Mr. Douglas under this roof."}
valley-of-fear	event_43_8	event	\N	MacDonald accuses Holmes of playing a trick and wasting the search	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "macdonald", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1909", "sentence_1910", "sentence_1911"], "communicatesContent": "How long have you been playing this trick upon us, Mr. Holmes? How long have you allowed us to waste ourselves upon a search that you knew to be an absurd one?"}
valley-of-fear	event_43_9	event	\N	Holmes explains that he formed his views last night and sent the inspectors away for the day	42	{"section": "section_43", "speakerId": "holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1912", "sentence_1913", "sentence_1914", "sentence_1915", "sentence_1916", "sentence_1917", "sentence_1918"], "communicatesContent": "Not one instant, my dear Mr. Mac. Only last night did I form my views of the case. As they could not be put to the proof until this evening, I invited you and your colleague to take a holiday for the day. Pray what more could I do? When I found the suit of clothes in the moat, it at once became apparent to me that the body we had found could not have been the body of Mr. John Douglas at all, but must be that of the bicyclist from Tunbridge Wells. No other conclusion was possible. Therefore I had to determine where Mr. John Douglas himself could be, and the balance of probability was that with the connivance of his wife and his friend he was concealed in a house which had such conveniences for a fugitive, and awaiting quieter times when he could make his final escape."}
valley-of-fear	event_45_9	event	\N	Douglas turns up his coat and cuff to show a mark on his forearm	44	{"section": "section_45", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1975"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_44_2	event	\N	The speaker says he tried to evade British law and shake pursuers off his track	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1920", "sentence_1921", "sentence_1922"], "communicatesContent": "I thought I’d dodge your British law; for I was not sure how I stood under it, and also I saw my chance to throw these hounds once for all off my track. Mind you, from first to last I have done nothing to be ashamed of, and nothing that I would not do again; but you’ll judge that for yourselves when I tell you my story. Never mind warning me, Inspector: I’m ready to stand pat upon the truth."}
valley-of-fear	event_44_3	event	\N	The speaker says the bundle of papers contains the story	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1923", "sentence_1924"], "communicatesContent": "I’m not going to begin at the beginning. That’s all there, and a mighty queer yarn you’ll find it."}
valley-of-fear	event_44_4	event	\N	The speaker says some men hate him and want to know they have got him	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1925", "sentence_1926"], "communicatesContent": "It all comes down to this: That there are some men that have good cause to hate me and would give their last dollar to know that they had got me. So long as I am alive and they are alive, there is no safety in this world for me."}
valley-of-fear	event_44_5	event	\N	The speaker says men chased him from Chicago to California and out of America, and that he married and settled down	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1927"], "communicatesContent": "They hunted me from Chicago to California, then they chased me out of America; but when I married and settled down in this quiet spot I thought my last years were going to be peaceable."}
valley-of-fear	event_44_6	event	\N	The speaker says he never explained the situation to his wife	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1928", "sentence_1929", "sentence_1930", "sentence_1931", "sentence_1933"], "communicatesContent": "I never explained to my wife how things were. Why should I pull her into it? She would never have a quiet moment again; but would always be imagining trouble. I fancy she knew something, for I may have dropped a word here or a word there; but until yesterday, after you gentlemen had seen her, she never knew the rights of the matter. She knows everything now, and I would have been a wiser man if I had told her sooner."}
valley-of-fear	event_44_7	event	\N	The speaker says Barker and his wife told what they knew	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1932"], "communicatesContent": "She told you all she knew, and so did Barker here; for on the night when this thing happened there was mighty little time for explanations."}
valley-of-fear	event_44_8	event	\N	The speaker says he acted for the best and takes his wife's hand	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1934"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_44_9	event	\N	The speaker says he saw a man in Tunbridge Wells and recognized him as his worst enemy	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": "john_douglas", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1935", "sentence_1936", "sentence_1937"], "communicatesContent": "Well, gentlemen, the day before these happenings I was over in Tunbridge Wells, and I got a glimpse of a man in the street. It was only a glimpse; but I have a quick eye for these things, and I never doubted who it was. It was the worst enemy I had among them all—one who has been after me like a hungry wolf after a caribou all these years."}
valley-of-fear	event_44_10	event	\N	The speaker returns home and makes ready for trouble	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1938", "sentence_1939", "sentence_1940"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_44_11	event	\N	The speaker stays on guard and does not go into the park	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1941", "sentence_1942"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_44_12	event	\N	The speaker thinks the drawbridge being up made him feel safer in the evenings	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1943"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_44_13	event	\N	The speaker makes a round in his dressing gown and enters the study	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1944", "sentence_1945"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_44_14	event	\N	The speaker senses danger in the study	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1945", "sentence_1946", "sentence_1947"], "communicatesContent": null}
valley-of-fear	event_44_15	event	\N	The speaker spots a boot under the window curtain	43	{"section": "section_44", "speakerId": null, "sourceNodes": ["sentence_1948"], "communicatesContent": null}
