a-case-of-identity	event_18_3	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes remarks that the maiden is an interesting study	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_214"], "communicatesContent": "Quite an interesting study, that maiden."}
a-case-of-identity	event_18_4	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes says he found the maiden more interesting than her problem and cites parallel cases in Andover and The Hague	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_215", "sentence_216", "sentence_217", "sentence_218"], "communicatesContent": "I found her more interesting than her little problem, which, by the way, is rather a trite one. You will find parallel cases, if you consult my index, in Andover in '77, and there was something of the sort at The Hague last year. Old as is the idea, however, there were one or two details which were new to me. But the maiden herself was most instructive."}
a-case-of-identity	event_18_5	event	\N	Watson remarks that Holmes appeared to read much that was invisible to him	17	{"section": "section_18", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_219"], "communicatesContent": "You appeared to read a good deal upon her which was quite invisible to me."}
a-case-of-identity	event_19_1	event	\N	Holmes remarks on the importance of small visual details	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_220", "sentence_221", "sentence_222"], "communicatesContent": "Not invisible but unnoticed, Watson. You did not know where to look, and so you missed all that was important. I can never bring you to realise the importance of sleeves, the suggestiveness of thumb-nails, or the great issues that may hang from a boot-lace."}
a-case-of-identity	event_19_2	event	\N	Holmes asks Watson what he gathered from the woman's appearance and tells him to describe it	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_223", "sentence_224"], "communicatesContent": "Now, what did you gather from that woman’s appearance? Describe it."}
a-case-of-identity	event_19_3	event	\N	Watson describes the woman's appearance	18	{"section": "section_19", "speakerId": "watson", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_225", "sentence_226", "sentence_227", "sentence_228", "sentence_229", "sentence_230"], "communicatesContent": "Well, she had a slate-coloured, broad-brimmed straw hat, with a feather of a brickish red. Her jacket was black, with black beads sewn upon it, and a fringe of little black jet ornaments. Her dress was brown, rather darker than coffee colour, with a little purple plush at the neck and sleeves. Her gloves were greyish and were worn through at the right forefinger. Her boots I didn’t observe. She had small round, hanging gold earrings, and a general air of being fairly well-to-do in a vulgar, comfortable, easy-going way."}
a-case-of-identity	event_20_1	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes praises Watson's progress and instructs him to focus on details	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_232", "sentence_233", "sentence_234", "sentence_235"], "communicatesContent": "’Pon my word, Watson, you are coming along wonderfully. You have really done very well indeed. It is true that you have missed everything of importance, but you have hit upon the method, and you have a quick eye for colour. Never trust to general impressions, my boy, but concentrate yourself upon details."}
a-case-of-identity	event_20_2	event	\N	Sherlock Holmes describes how he examines a woman's clothing and a man's trousers	19	{"section": "section_20", "speakerId": "sherlock_holmes", "sourceNodes": ["sentence_236", "sentence_237"], "communicatesContent": "My first glance is always at a woman’s sleeve. In a man it is perhaps better first to take the knee of the trouser."}
